    [1;34m"body"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"## Problem\n\n#3494 added `--pipeline <variant>` to `stella run` alone. Two things it deliberately did not do, both of which a user will reach for immediately:\n\n### 1. Every other execution surface still has only `--no-pipeline`\n\n`stella chat`/deck, `stella goal`, `stella fleet` and `stella arena` all take `no_pipeline: bool` (`crates/stella-cli/src/cli.rs:512,585,607,781`; `crates/stella-cli/src/arena.rs:73`) and cannot name a wrapper. `doc:pipeline-as-plugins` §7 plans `--pipeline <variant>` to *replace* `--no-pipeline` everywhere (the #3381 flag inversion); #3494 shipped the additive half on one command so the dispatcher had a driver at all.\n\n`crate::wrapper_plugin::PipelineChoice` is the type the inversion needs — it already refuses the \"both flags\" combination and accepts `classic` by name — so the remaining work is threading it through the other four commands and then retiring the boolean.\n\n### 2. A wrapper's verdict does not decide the exit status\n\n`crates/stella-cli/src/wrapper_plugin.rs::run_wrapped` returns the **last turn's** result. A `DispatchReport` whose outcome is `Outcome::Unmet` is printed to stderr and exits `0`. `--require-verified` is wired to the staged pipeline's ladder, not to a plugin's declared rule, so there is no way to make a plugin's refusal fail a delivery gate.\n\nThat was left alone on purpose — making a third party's manifest able to fail a user's build is a decision with its own blast radius, and it should be an explicit flag rather than a side effect of installing a plugin — but the gap is real and undocumented outside this issue.\n\n## Definition of done\n\n- Every execution surface takes `PipelineChoice`, and `--no-pipeline` is either an alias of `--pipeline raw` or removed with a deprecation, per #3381.\n- A named flag (or an extension of `--require-verified`) makes a wrapper's `Outcome::Unmet` a non-zero exit, with a witness test for both directions.\n- `website/content/docs/commands/*.mdx` updated for whichever surfaces gain the flag.\n\nRefs #3494, #3381.\n"[m[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"number"[m[1;37m:[m 3554[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"title"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"--pipeline &lt;variant&gt; is on `stella run` only, and a plugin's Unmet verdict does not affect the exit status"[m
  [1;37m}[m[1;37m,[m
  [1;37m{[m
    [1;34m"body"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"## Problem\n\n`stella run --pipeline <variant>` (#3494) drives the raw step loop in the **shared work tree**. So `crates/stella-cli/src/wrapper_plugin.rs::run_wrapped` sends `RoundInput { candidate: None, .. }` and `RawTurnDriver` reports `TamperFinding::NotChecked` about its own check.\n\nBoth are honest — this host really has no candidate worktree and really took no authoring-time identity snapshot — but the consequence is a whole class of plugin that cannot work on the only shipped driver:\n\n- `judge` (`crates/stella-runtime/src/wrapper/verdict.rs`) reads `flip` first; under `FlipPolicy::Required`, `FlipObservation::Unobservable` → `UndecidedReason::FlipUnobservable`.\n- Even a plugin that *does* observe a flip is then denied credit, because `TamperPolicy::ArtifactIdentity` + `TamperFinding::NotChecked` → `UndecidedReason::TamperUnchecked`.\n\nSo every witness-flavoured wrapper — which is most of what `doc:pipeline-as-plugins` §7's Track B lists (vera, stella-candidates) — reports `Undecided` on every run. Only a measurement/budget oracle (`flip = \"not-applicable\"`) works today.\n\n## What exists to build on\n\n- `stella_plugin::CandidateGrant` + `TestPlan` + `TestBaseline` — the serializable subset a plugin acts on (#3498), already proven end-to-end in `crates/stella-runtime/tests/wrapper_socket.rs::a_plugin_acts_on_the_candidate_using_only_what_the_request_carried`.\n- `stella_pipeline::ports::CandidateHandles` — the host-side minting and path fencing, including canonicalisation. It is what a driver would call to produce a grant.\n- The tamper snapshot is host-side by design (#3499); `TamperPolicy::ArtifactIdentity` already assumes it.\n\n## Constraints\n\n- `stella-runtime` must not depend on `stella-pipeline` (`crates/stella-runtime/tests/no_pipeline_edge.rs`), so the grant is minted by the **driver** in `stella-cli` and passed in on `RoundInput`, never resolved inside the dispatcher.\n- `doc:pipeline-as-plugins` §7 wants `stella-cli`'s dependency on `stella-pipeline` cut eventually, so a driver reaching into `CandidateHandles` is a step sideways unless the minting moves with it. Decide that before wiring.\n\n## Definition of done\n\n`stella run --pipeline <variant>` mints a candidate workspace, passes the grant and the host's tamper finding, and a wrapper plugin whose oracle declares `flip = \"required\"` can reach `Verdict::Met`. A witness test asserts the flip is credited when it happens and refused when the witness artifacts changed.\n\nUntil then, `crates/stella-cli/src/wrapper_plugin.rs`'s module docs state this gap and must keep stating it.\n\nRefs #3494, #3498, #3499.\n"[m[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"number"[m[1;37m:[m 3553[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"title"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"a wrapper plugin declaring flip = \"required\" always abstains under `stella run --pipeline`: no candidate workspace, no tamper snapshot"[m
  [1;37m}[m[1;37m,[m
  [1;37m{[m
    [1;34m"body"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"## Problem\n\n`crates/stella-cli/src/wrapper_plugin.rs::RawTurnDriver::run_turn` builds the `stella_plugin::TurnOutcome` a plugin's `after_turn` receives from the engine's own `TurnOutcome`, which carries only the final text and the cost. So:\n\n```rust\nWrapperTurnOutcome { completed, answer, ..Default::default() }  // tools: [], changed_files: []\n```\n\n`TurnOutcome::tools` and `TurnOutcome::changed_files` are therefore **always empty** on this path. A plugin reading either one cannot tell \"the turn dispatched no tools / changed no files\" from \"this host does not report them\", which is the exact silent-answer shape the plugin crate's whole posture refuses (`crates/stella-plugin/src/wire.rs`). A wrapper that gates its evidence on \"did the turn touch anything\" will grade every run as untouched.\n\n## Why it is not a one-liner\n\nBoth facts exist, but only as **events**, and by the time this seam sees the turn they have already been drained:\n\n- tool names ride `AgentEvent::ToolStart`, collected into `RendererOutcome::events` only for `OutputFormat::Json` (`crates/stella-cli/src/agent/persistence.rs::spawn_renderer`).\n- the tree delta is measured once, at the turn boundary, by `crate::turn_files::emit_shared_tree_changes` → `cfg.durability.snapshot_worktree()`, which is consumed there; calling it again afterwards reports nothing.\n\n## Repro / verify\n\nInstall a wrapper plugin whose `after_turn` echoes `changed_files` as a measurement, run `stella run --pipeline <variant> \"edit a file\"`, and observe the measurement is 0 while the file changed.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nEither the two lists are populated honestly from a measurement the driver can make at the same boundary `turn_files` measures at, **or** the wire distinguishes \"not reported by this host\" from \"empty\" (a `null` vs `[]` distinction, `PROTOCOL_VERSION` unchanged since it is additive) and this driver sends the former. A test asserts a turn that edited a file reports it, and its falsifier asserts a turn that edited nothing does not.\n\nRefs #3494.\n"[m[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"number"[m[1;37m:[m 3552[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"title"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"a wrapper plugin on the CLI path is handed an empty tools/changed_files list, indistinguishable from a turn that touched nothing"[m
  [1;37m}[m[1;37m,[m
  [1;37m{[m
    [1;34m"body"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"## Problem\n\n#3494 landed the host sequence (`stella_runtime::wrapper::WrapperDispatch`, `crates/stella-runtime/src/wrapper/dispatch.rs`) and wired **one** driver: `stella run --pipeline <variant>` (`crates/stella-cli/src/wrapper_plugin.rs`).\n\n`doc:wrapper-socket` §6 and `doc:pipeline-as-plugins` §0/§11 make the acceptance criterion three drivers, \"proven by a test that exercises all three, not by an argument that it should work\":\n\n1. `stella-cli` — **done** (#3494).\n2. `stella-serve` over HTTP — **not done**. Nothing in `crates/stella-serve/` references `WrapperDispatch` or `TurnWrapper`.\n3. A minimal embedded host linking `stella-engine` — **not done**. No such host exists in the tree.\n\nUntil 2 and 3 exist, §6 is unmet and the socket's \"no host assumed\" claim is an argument rather than a proof. **Do not let any doc claim §6 is satisfied.**\n\n## What exists to build on\n\n- `stella_runtime::wrapper::{WrapperDispatch, TurnDriver, TurnPrelude, DrivenTurn, RoundInput, DispatchReport}` — the sequence, already host-agnostic: nothing in it reads the environment, the cwd, a terminal or a git workspace, and `crates/stella-runtime/tests/no_ambient_reads.rs` holds that.\n- `crates/stella-runtime/tests/wrapper_dispatch.rs` — the sequence's witness, driven with a `/bin/sh` plugin and a recording `TurnDriver`. The three-driver test should reuse that plugin verbatim; \"unchanged plugin\" is the whole claim.\n- `crates/stella-cli/src/wrapper_plugin.rs::RawTurnDriver` — the reference `TurnDriver` implementation, ~40 lines around one `run_turn` call.\n\n## Constraints already discovered\n\n- **`TurnDriver` is `#[async_trait(?Send)]`.** `stella_core`'s own turn future is not `Send` (its speculative-tool machinery holds `Box<dyn Future>` without the bound), so a `Send` bound is one no in-process host can satisfy — `stella-cli`'s driver does not compile against it. A serve driver that wants to `tokio::spawn` its dispatch will hit this; the fix is in `stella-core`, not in the socket.\n- **Name collision.** `stella-serve` already has a `pub(crate) struct DrivenTurn` (`crates/stella-serve/src/session.rs:488`) for a similar-but-different idea. Implementing `TurnDriver` there needs an `as` rename on one of them.\n- `stella-serve` is its own binary and `stella-cli` does not link it, so a change there reaches no `stella` user — the test is the deliverable.\n\n## Definition of done\n\n- `stella-serve` implements `TurnDriver` over its `Engine::drive` loop and drives `WrapperDispatch` for a session whose request names a wrapper.\n- A minimal embedded host (a test binary or an example linking `stella-engine`) does the same.\n- One test exercises the **same** plugin fixture under all three drivers and asserts the same verdict from each.\n- `crates/stella-runtime/README.md`'s \"what has not landed\" paragraph and `doc:wrapper-socket` §6 are updated in the same PR.\n\nRefs #3494, #3380.\n"[m[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"number"[m[1;37m:[m 3551[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"title"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"wrapper socket §6: only stella-cli drives WrapperDispatch — stella-serve and an embedded stella-engine host still do not"[m
  [1;37m}[m[1;37m,[m
  [1;37m{[m
    [1;34m"body"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"## Problem\n\nEvery spawn plane in the workspace bounds a runaway child with two mechanisms,\nand only one of them exists off unix:\n\n- `kill_on_drop(true)` — portable, reaches **the direct child only**.\n- `pre_exec(setsid)` + `exec::GroupKillGuard` — reaches the whole tree, and is\n  `#[cfg(unix)]` in every one of its call sites\n  (`crates/stella-tools/src/exec.rs`, `hook_runner.rs`, `bash.rs`,\n  `custom.rs`, `crates/stella-cli/src/agent/tools.rs`, and now\n  `crates/stella-runtime/src/wrapper/subprocess.rs`).\n\nSo on Windows a hook, a custom tool, a `bash` tool call, or a plugin wrapper\nthat backgrounds work leaves that work running after the turn is reported\nfinished. The `Refs #3380` change that gave the plugin transport the group\nkill states the gap in its own module doc (\"It reaches the direct child and\nnothing else, which is all there is on non-unix\") rather than pretending\notherwise, and this issue is where that admission is tracked.\n\nThis is the *mechanism* gap. #3497 is the neighbouring but distinct problem\nthat the transport's tests are `#[cfg(unix)]` and so prove nothing on\nWindows; fixing this one does not close that one, and vice versa.\n\n## The shape of the fix\n\nWindows has the equivalent primitive: a **Job Object** with\n`JOB_OBJECT_LIMIT_KILL_ON_JOB_CLOSE`. Assign the child to a job at spawn,\nhold the job handle where `GroupKillGuard` holds the pid, and closing (or\nterminating) the job takes the whole tree — including grandchildren the child\nspawned, which is exactly what `kill_on_drop` cannot do.\n\nSuggested shape, so the platform split stays in one place rather than\nspreading a second `#[cfg]` through six call sites:\n\n- Give `exec::GroupKillGuard` a Windows implementation with the same three\n  methods (`arm` / `disarm` / `kill_now`) over a job handle instead of a pid,\n  so callers keep one name and drop their `#[cfg(unix)]`.\n- Give `exec::detach_into_own_process_group` a Windows body that creates and\n  assigns the job (name it for what it does on both platforms, or keep the\n  name and document the two meanings — do not add a second function callers\n  must choose between).\n- `windows-sys` is the usual dependency for this; it needs the justification\n  sentence AGENTS.md § \"No new dependencies casually\" requires, and it must be\n  a `[target.'cfg(windows)'.dependencies]` entry so no unix build pays for it.\n\n## Definition of done\n\n- A child that backgrounds a grandchild and then times out leaves no surviving\n  grandchild **on Windows**, proven by a test — the same assertion\n  `crates/stella-runtime/tests/wrapper_transport_limits.rs::a_timed_out_plugin_leaves_no_surviving_grandchild`\n  makes on unix (it records the grandchild's pid and then asserts the process\n  is gone, treating a zombie as gone).\n- The `#[cfg(unix)]` guards around the guard/`setsid` pairs at the six call\n  sites above are gone, or each remaining one names why that site is\n  deliberately unix-only.\n- The doc comments that currently say \"all there is on non-unix\"\n  (`crates/stella-runtime/src/wrapper/subprocess.rs`,\n  `crates/stella-tools/src/hook_runner.rs`) are updated in the same PR.\n\n## Constraints\n\n- CI does not currently run a Windows job, so the witness has to be written to\n  be *runnable* there before it can be *required* there. Say in the PR how it\n  was run.\n- Do not weaken the unix path to share code with Windows; the two primitives\n  are genuinely different and the shared surface is the guard's three methods,\n  not its internals.\n"[m[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"number"[m[1;37m:[m 3550[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"title"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"on Windows nothing kills a spawned tree: the process-group backstop is `#[cfg(unix)]` on every spawn plane"[m
  [1;37m}[m[1;37m,[m
  [1;37m{[m
    [1;34m"body"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"## Problem\n\n`crates/stella-tools/src/exec.rs` now exposes the `setsid` half of the\nprocess-group spawn policy as one function:\n\n```rust\n#[cfg(unix)]\npub fn detach_into_own_process_group(cmd: &mut Command)\n```\n\nIt was added (in the same change that fixed the plugin transport's two\ntransport defects, `Refs #3380`) so a spawn site *outside* `stella-tools` —\n`stella-runtime`'s plugin transport — could take the policy without taking a\n`libc` dependency and a fifth copy of the `unsafe` block. `crate::hook_runner`\nwas converted with it.\n\nThree in-crate/in-workspace copies of the identical block remain:\n\n- `crates/stella-tools/src/bash.rs:499` (`cmd.pre_exec(|| { libc::setsid(); Ok(()) })`)\n- `crates/stella-tools/src/custom.rs:656`\n- `crates/stella-cli/src/agent/tools.rs:653`\n\nEach is byte-for-byte the same policy, each carries its own `unsafe` block and\nits own SAFETY reasoning, and each already arms the shared\n`exec::GroupKillGuard` beside it — so the guard half of the policy is\nconverged and the `setsid` half is not. That asymmetry is the bug: the guard's\nown doc comment says \"every `pre_exec(setsid)` spawn site in the workspace\nmust use *this* guard rather than grow a second one\", and the same argument\napplies verbatim to the call that creates the group the guard kills.\n\n`detach_into_own_process_group`'s doc comment currently says these three \"are\ntracked for conversion\", which is this issue. If the conversion is rejected,\nthat sentence has to change in the same PR — a doc claiming a tracked plan\nthat does not exist is worse than no claim.\n\n## Definition of done\n\n- The three call sites above call `crate::exec::detach_into_own_process_group`\n  (or `stella_tools::exec::…` from `stella-cli`) instead of their own\n  `unsafe { cmd.pre_exec(…) }`.\n- No `pre_exec` + `libc::setsid` pair remains outside `exec.rs` in `crates/`\n  except `crates/stella-cli/src/daemon.rs` and\n  `crates/stella-tui/tests/deck_pty_smoke.rs`, which are deliberately\n  different: the daemon's closure also takes the liveness lock and opens the\n  split console and checks `setsid`'s return, and the TUI test's runs in a pty\n  harness. Neither is this policy; do not fold them in.\n- `grep -rn \"libc::setsid\" crates/` shows the intended set and nothing else.\n\n## Verify\n\nPure refactor — no witness test is expected; say so in the PR template. The\nexisting coverage is what proves it did not change behaviour:\n\n```\ncargo test -p stella-tools --lib -- bash:: custom:: hook_runner::\ncargo test -p stella-cli --lib -- agent::tools\n```\n\n`bash::tests::a_dropped_bash_call_kills_the_process_group`,\n`custom::tests::a_dropped_custom_tool_kills_the_process_group` and\n`crates/stella-cli/src/agent/tools/diff_baseline_tests.rs` (see #613) are the\ntests that fail if a conversion drops the `setsid` and leaves only\n`kill_on_drop`.\n\n## Constraints\n\n- `detach_into_own_process_group` takes `&mut tokio::process::Command`. A site\n  spawning a `std::process::Command` needs its own path — check before\n  converting; do not widen the helper to a generic without a second real\n  caller.\n- Keep each site's surrounding comment about *why* it needs a group (they\n  differ: a model-launched shell, a custom tool, a session tool), and delete\n  only the mechanical half.\n- `make lint` and `make format` before pushing; the `unsafe` removal must not\n  leave an unused `libc` import behind in the converted files.\n"[m[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"number"[m[1;37m:[m 3549[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"title"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"three spawn sites still hand-roll the `pre_exec(setsid)` block that `exec::detach_into_own_process_group` now owns"[m
  [1;37m}[m[1;37m,[m
  [1;37m{[m
    [1;34m"body"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"## Problem\n\n`plugins/stella-research/testdata/*.expected.json` are goldens: nine committed\n`BeforeTurnResponse` envelopes that\n`crates/stella-runtime/tests/research_plugin_conformance.rs` compares against.\nThere is **no committed way to regenerate them**.\n\nThe fixture workspace they were produced from is built by the harness in Rust\n(`fixture()` / `bulk_fixture()`), and the goldens were generated by a scratch\nscript that reproduced that tree in Python and is not in the repository. So the\nnext person who changes a contribution's wording has two options, both bad:\nhand-edit nine JSON files, or rewrite the scratch script and hope their fixture\nmatches the harness's.\n\nThe repo already has the right pattern for this and this is the one place that\ndoes not follow it: `BLESS=1 cargo test -p stella-tui --test deck_render_snapshots`\nregenerates the command deck's golden frames, and AGENTS.md's testing section\nsays what makes it safe — \"then **read the diff**: a golden blessed without\nlooking is a changelog, not a test\".\n\n## Definition of done\n\n- `BLESS=1 cargo test -p stella-runtime --test research_plugin_conformance`\n  rewrites `*.expected.json` from the same fixture the assertions use, so the\n  fixture has exactly one definition.\n- The bless path writes the same formatting the committed goldens carry\n  (2-space indent, trailing newline) so a regeneration diff is only the content\n  that changed.\n- It refuses to bless a vector that has a `.refusal.txt` sibling, matching the\n  one-grading-file rule `every_vector_is_graded_by_exactly_one_sibling` already\n  enforces.\n- The README's testing section names it.\n\nRefs #3380.\n"[m[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"number"[m[1;37m:[m 3548[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"title"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"plugins/stella-research's goldens have no regeneration path, so the next change to them will be hand-edited"[m
  [1;37m}[m[1;37m,[m
  [1;37m{[m
    [1;34m"body"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"## Problem\n\n`plugins/stella-research` reads exactly one path — `candidate.root`, from the\n`CandidateGrant` on its `before_turn` request — because that is the only path\nthe socket gives it and reaching for a working directory instead is the ambient\nauthority `doc:wrapper-socket` §6 exists to refuse.\n\nOn the shipping path there is no grant. `crates/stella-cli/src/wrapper_plugin.rs`\nsays so in its own module doc (\"the raw one-shot path works in the shared tree,\nso `RoundInput::candidate` is `None`\"), and points at \"#3494's successor\"\nwithout an issue number. This is that issue, scoped to the half a read-only\nplugin needs.\n\nSo today, with the plugin installed and `--pipeline research-v1` selected,\n`stella-research` is dispatched and contributes **nothing** — correctly, and\nuselessly. Its vector `06-no-candidate-grant` grades exactly that behaviour.\n\nA second, independent reason it contributes nothing on that path:\n`pre_turn_signals` publishes `questions: 0` (the raw path runs no triage), so\nthe `research` stage's declared condition `questions > 0` is false and the stage\nnever runs. Both have to close for the plugin to do anything in a real run.\n\n## The design question, which is not \"mint a worktree\"\n\n`CandidateGrant` names a *candidate workspace* — a snapshot the host created for\na turn to work in, addressable by `CandidateHandle` for create/run-test/seal/\nadopt/remove (`stella_protocol::candidate::CandidateOp`). A `before_turn`\nread-only plugin wants something weaker and cheaper: **the tree it may read**,\nwith no worktree, no handle operations, and no write.\n\nOptions:\n\n1. Mint a `CandidateGrant` over the shared workspace root with a handle that\n   answers `Root` and refuses everything else. Reuses the type; the honesty\n   cost is that \"candidate\" then sometimes means \"the tree you are in\".\n2. A distinct read grant on `BeforeTurnRequest` (e.g. `workspace:\n   Option<ReadGrant>`) carrying a canonical root and nothing else. Truthful,\n   additive, and one more concept.\n3. Leave `before_turn` blind before a candidate exists, and accept that\n   read-only stages can only run in a pipeline that already made one.\n\n## Definition of done\n\n- A read-only `before_turn` plugin driven by `stella-cli` receives a workspace\n  it may read, or the impossibility is documented in `doc:wrapper-socket` §5\n  where a plugin author meets it.\n- A run with `--pipeline research-v1` produces a contribution that reaches the\n  turn, witnessed end-to-end rather than argued (the shape is in\n  `crates/stella-runtime/tests/research_plugin_dispatch.rs`, which supplies the\n  grant itself because no host does).\n- The `questions` half is resolved too: either the wrapper path gets real triage\n  signals, or the manifest's condition is revisited alongside #3538.\n\nRefs #3380, #3494, #3498.\n"[m[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"number"[m[1;37m:[m 3547[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"title"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"A read-only before_turn plugin has no workspace to read: the CLI driver passes candidate: None, and CandidateGrant means a worktree"[m
  [1;37m}[m[1;37m,[m
  [1;37m{[m
    [1;34m"body"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"## State after the D2+D3 slice\n\n`doc:pipeline-as-plugins` §10 splits into D1–D6. What is done and what is not, verified rather than assumed:\n\n- **D1 — done** (5c5c325). The decision core is `crates/stella-autonomy`.\n- **D2 — done.** The host-verb surface is declared as data (`crates/stella-autonomy/src/surface.rs`, `HOST_SURFACE` + `HOST_SURFACE_VERSION`), printed by `stella self-driving surface [--format json]`, and checked against the shipping clap tree in both directions (`crates/stella-cli/src/self_driving_cmd/surface.rs::surface_matches_the_argument_tree`, over `stella_autonomy::surface_drift`).\n- **D3 — done as far as D-3 requires.** The grant is expressible and showable before install: `plugins/stella-selfdriving/plugin.toml`, rendered by the real `stella plugin install` and witnessed by `crates/stella-cli/tests/self_driving_consent.rs`.\n- **D4 — verified, no change needed.** `/api/self-driving` and `/api/self-driving-run` were driven against a state directory written by the current binary and both render (loops, runs, cycles, trajectory, self-improvement signals).\n- **D5 — green, assertions intact.** `bash scripts/test-self-driving.sh` reports 60/60.\n- **D6 — not started**, because nothing moved yet.\n\n## What is left\n\n**The extraction itself has not happened.** `scripts/self-driving.sh` (987 lines) is still the working driver, and §10's rule is that it is not deleted until its replacement is proven. `plugins/stella-selfdriving/` currently holds only a manifest and a README: installing it copies a declaration and starts nothing.\n\n**The declared grant binds to nothing.** `plugins/stella-selfdriving/plugin.toml` declares `bash`/`write_file`/`process_spawn` at destructive/high, and no `AuthzGate` rule is derived from it under `Principal::Plugin`. That is §A4's loader work, not this slice's. Until it lands, the consent prompt is an accurate description with no enforcement behind it — which is exactly what the prompt's own \"claimed limit\" sentence tells the user, but it is a gap and not a design.\n\n**D6's carry list is untouched**: `stella-home`'s four `self_driving` resolvers (including `resolve_legacy_self_driving_roots`, the `fullauto` → `self-driving` migration) and the `/self-driving*` slash-command install path (`cmd_install_commands` in `scripts/self-driving.sh`, sources at `scripts/self-driving/commands/`) all still assume the in-repo shell driver.\n\n## Definition of done\n\n- A plugin package that actually drives the loop, binding only to verbs `stella self-driving surface` declares — no verb reached by any other route.\n- `scripts/test-self-driving.sh` green against the plugin path with **every assertion intact**; if one has to be relaxed, behaviour changed and that is a bug in the move, not in the test.\n- `/api/self-driving` still renders, driven for real, not argued.\n- `stella-home`'s resolvers and the slash-command install path carried, legacy migration included.\n- The shell driver deleted only after the above, in its own commit.\n\n## Constraints already discovered\n\n- The loop is **three** programs holding different powers, and a check that assumes one file is wrong: the shell driver holds `brew`, the EC2 rig and the daemon; the binary's `self-driving` verbs hold the `gh` reads that rank the queue (`self_driving_cmd.rs::demand`); the slash commands hold the `gh` writes that file a finding (`scripts/self-driving/commands/self-driving/tickets.md`). See the table in `crates/stella-cli/tests/self_driving_consent.rs`.\n- The daemon runs `stella run` per cycle under `SELF_DRIVING_BUDGET_USD`, and `daemon stop` kills the child's whole process group — a move that loses the process-group kill reintroduces a stop that does not stop while spend keeps climbing (the comment at `scripts/self-driving.sh::daemon_loop` records the measurement).\n- Adding a verb means adding a `HOST_SURFACE` row in the same PR or the build fails; removing one fails it too. Bump `HOST_SURFACE_VERSION` only on a removal, a rename, or an `Emits` change.\n\nRefs #3380. See also #3537 (the consent prompt has no word for a host plugin)."[m[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"number"[m[1;37m:[m 3546[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"title"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"Track D remainder: the self-driving driver still lives in scripts/, and its declared grant binds to no gate"[m
  [1;37m}[m[1;37m,[m
