    [1;34m"body"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"## Problem\n\n`doc:wrapper-socket` §5 says `after_turn` **may** \"gather evidence — run a test,\nread a diff, author a witness, **spend a declared model role's call and return\nthe parsed assessment as evidence**\", and §7 says how that is meant to stay\nsafe: \"It does not give a plugin an `Engine`, a provider, or a credential …\nevery model call is made by the host\".\n\nRead the wire and neither half exists:\n\n- `AfterTurnRequest` / `AfterTurnResponse`\n  (`crates/stella-plugin/src/wire.rs`) carry a request for evidence and an\n  `ObservedEvidence` back. There is no field in which a plugin can ask for a\n  completion, and no mechanism by which the host would make one on its behalf.\n- `BeforeTurnResponse::role` is a role intent **for the turn the host is about\n  to run** — not a call the plugin gets the result of.\n- Every point is one host-initiated request and one plugin response, so there\n  is no shape in which a plugin could ask and be answered.\n\nSo a plugin that needs a model call has exactly two options today, and both are\nwrong: reach for a credential itself (invariant 3, and #3512 shows the\n`[runtime].env` route is already refused), or not do the work.\n\n## What it blocks, concretely\n\nThe extraction order in `doc:pipeline-as-plugins` §7 runs through stages that\nare model calls by definition:\n\n- **stella-research** (#3380 §7.1, landed as `plugins/stella-research`) replaces\n  a fan-out of read-only sub-agents. The shipped plugin does deterministic\n  grounding instead and says so — it cannot do the thing it replaces.\n- **stella-plan** (§7.2) is a model call and nothing else.\n- **stella-goal** (§3) spends a verifier call, which §5 names as the example.\n\n## Options\n\n1. **A callback channel** — the plugin's response may include a request for one\n   role call, and the host re-invokes the point with the result. Additive, but\n   it makes a point re-entrant, and re-entrancy needs a bound.\n2. **Host-resolved role output on the request** — the host makes the declared\n   call *before* dispatching the point and hands the parsed result in. Keeps one\n   request/one response, at the cost of the host deciding when to spend.\n3. **Withdraw the claim** — §5 stops saying `after_turn` may spend a call, and\n   the extraction plan records that model-calling stages stay in the binary.\n\n## Definition of done\n\n- One of the three is decided and written into `doc:wrapper-socket` §5 rather\n  than left as a sentence the code does not implement.\n- If a mechanism ships: the spend is visible on the receipt against the declared\n  role (which is §5's own stated reason for preferring it to a `judge`), and a\n  witness test shows a plugin causing exactly one call it declared.\n\nRefs #3380, #3492, #3496.\n"[m[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"number"[m[1;37m:[m 3541[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"title"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"doc:wrapper-socket says after_turn may \"spend a declared model role's call\", but nothing on the wire lets a plugin ask for one"[m
  [1;37m}[m[1;37m,[m
  [1;37m{[m
    [1;34m"body"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"## Problem\n\n`doc:pipeline-as-plugins` §3 lists **stella-research** as replacing \"research\nsub-agents, **recall**\". Track B's first extraction\n(`plugins/stella-research`) delivers the first half and explicitly declines the\nsecond, because there is nothing on the wire it could use.\n\nRecall today is `ContextRecallPort::recall(goal)` →\n`Recall { frames: Vec<RecalledFrame>, .. }`\n(`crates/stella-pipeline/src/ports.rs`), fanned out through the CGP host over\n`.stella/private/context.db` and `codegraph.db`: materialized memories,\nepisodes, facts, code-graph symbols. A plugin gets none of it —\n`BeforeTurnRequest` carries `goal`, `stage`, `round`, `candidate` and published\nsignals, and the candidate grant is a filesystem root, not a context plane.\n\nTwo directions are possible and they are not the same feature:\n\n1. **Read**: the host retrieves and hands the frames to the plugin, which then\n   decides what to contribute. Cheap, but it makes the host do the retrieval\n   the plugin was extracted to own.\n2. **Ask**: the plugin asks the host to recall — a request/response the plugin\n   initiates. Nothing in the socket is shaped like that today; every point is\n   one host-initiated request and one plugin response.\n\nDirection 2 is the one that would let a retrieval plugin exist at all, and it\nis a real addition to `doc:wrapper-socket`, not a use of it.\n\n## Why it matters beyond this plugin\n\n`stella-plan` (Track B.2) reads recall too — `build_planner_prompt` takes the\nframes — so the same gap blocks the second extraction. Whatever is decided here\ndecides that one.\n\n## Definition of done\n\n- A decision recorded in `doc:wrapper-socket`: either the socket gains a way for\n  a plugin to reach the context plane, or §3's table stops listing recall under\n  stella-research and says where it goes instead.\n- If the socket grows it: `plugins/stella-research` answers\n  `StageName::Recall` with a contribution instead of the deliberate empty one\n  its `contribute()` returns today, with a vector grading it.\n\nRefs #3380.\n"[m[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"number"[m[1;37m:[m 3540[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"title"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"The context plane has no wire representation, so the `recall` half of the stella-research extraction cannot leave the binary"[m
  [1;37m}[m[1;37m,[m
  [1;37m{[m
    [1;34m"body"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"## Problem\n\nThe built-in research stage answers **triage's questions**: triage names up to\n`crate::triage::MAX_RESEARCH_QUESTIONS` self-contained questions\n(`crates/stella-pipeline/src/triage.rs::parse_research_questions`) and\n`Pipeline::research_stage` fans them out as read-only sub-agents, one per\nquestion (`crates/stella-pipeline/src/pipeline/research_stage.rs`).\n\n`BeforeTurnRequest` (`crates/stella-plugin/src/wire.rs`) carries `goal`,\n`stage`, `round`, `candidate` and `published: Vec<PublishedSignal>`. The only\nthing it says about research questions is `Signal::Questions`, which is a\n`u64` **count**. The question *text* does not cross the wire in any form.\n\nSo `plugins/stella-research` — Track B's first extraction (#3380 §7) — cannot\ndo the thing the stage it replaces does. It re-derives search terms from the\ngoal string instead (`search_terms` in `plugins/stella-research/main.py`),\nwhich is a strictly weaker input: triage's questions are the product of a model\ncall that already read the goal *and* the task class.\n\n## Why this is not just \"add a field\"\n\n`published` is typed by the closed `Signal` vocabulary, whose whole defence is\nthat a stage cannot invent a fact for a later stage to read. Question text is\nnot a signal — it is a payload — so it needs either:\n\n1. a first-class optional field on `BeforeTurnRequest` (e.g.\n   `questions: Vec<String>`, additive, `PROTOCOL_VERSION` unchanged); or\n2. a general \"stage input\" channel, which is the larger and more dangerous\n   design (an untyped bag reopens exactly what the closed vocabulary closed).\n\nOption 1 is small, additive and honest about being research-specific. Its cost\nis one field the wire carries for one stage; weigh that against a plugin that\ncannot see its own input.\n\n## Definition of done\n\n- A `before_turn` request at `StageName::Research` carries the questions triage\n  named, or the reason it deliberately does not is written down in\n  `doc:wrapper-socket` §5.\n- `plugins/stella-research/main.py` answers those questions rather than terms\n  it guessed from the goal, and a vector in\n  `plugins/stella-research/testdata/` grades it.\n- The field is additive: a plugin written before it existed still loads and\n  answers (the deny-unknown-fields rule means the *plugin* must tolerate it, so\n  the reference plugin's `BEFORE_TURN_REQUEST_FIELDS` set is part of this\n  change).\n\nRefs #3380.\n"[m[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"number"[m[1;37m:[m 3539[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"title"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"before_turn carries a count of triage's research questions but never the questions, so an extracted research plugin cannot answer them"[m
  [1;37m}[m[1;37m,[m
  [1;37m{[m
    [1;34m"body"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"## Problem\n\n`plugins/stella-research/plugin.toml` (Track B's first extraction, #3380 §7)\ndeclares the classic stage order as a `[wrapper]` variant. Exactly **one** of\nits stages could be given its real gate:\n\n- `research` → `if = \"questions > 0\"`, which is precisely\n  `Pipeline::research_stage`'s `questions.is_empty()` early return.\n\nEvery other gate the built-in pipeline actually takes is a **conjunction**, and\nthe grammar in `crates/stella-plugin/src/wrapper.rs` has none — a condition is\none boolean signal (optionally `no-`-prefixed) or one count comparison:\n\n- the conversational fast path (`crates/stella-pipeline/src/pipeline.rs`, the\n  `if assessment.conversational` return) skips plan, execute, witness and\n  verify together;\n- the authored-witness decision is a five-term conjunction in the same file\n  (`!conversational && test_command.is_none() && responsibility_enabled(..) &&\n  wants_witness() && verifies_unconditionally() && can_author_independent_witness()`);\n- `plan` runs on `task_class.plans() && responsibility_enabled(Plan)`.\n\nSo a manifest can declare a stage **unconditional** (over-claiming: it says a\nchat turn plans) or pick one conjunct (declaring a weaker rule as if it were\nthe rule). stella-research chose unconditional and says so in a comment, which\nis the least-bad option and not a good one.\n\n## Why it is not urgent today, and exactly when it becomes urgent\n\n`WrapperDispatch::open_round` (`crates/stella-runtime/src/wrapper/dispatch.rs`)\nuses the resolved `StageProgram` only to decide **where a plugin is asked to\ncontribute**; the host runs the turn. So an over-declared stage costs a\n`before_turn` call today, not a plan.\n\nThe moment a host drives its stage order *from* a `StageProgram` — which is what\n`StageName`'s own docs (\"the stages that run, in the order they run\") promise —\nan unconditional `plan` declaration runs a planner for `hi`. That is the bug\nthis issue exists to prevent, and it must be closed before that wiring.\n\n## Options\n\n1. **Widen the grammar with conjunction only** — `a && b`, no disjunction, no\n   parentheses, no precedence to argue about. Keeps \"a Turing-complete\n   condition in a manifest is a second program with no gate on it\"\n   (`wrapper.rs:16-21`) intact; it stays a closed, enumerable rule a reviewer\n   diffs.\n2. **Add a `Signal` per composite decision** — e.g. `authors-witness`,\n   `runs-plan` — so the host publishes the answer it already computed and the\n   manifest reads one name. Narrower, and it moves the conjunction into the\n   host where it already lives.\n\nOption 2 is closer to the existing design (`Signal` is explicitly the place\nexpressiveness grows, \"never a richer condition syntax\"), and it is what\n`Signal`'s own doc comment argues for. Option 1 is cheaper for authors. This\nneeds a decision, not a default.\n\n## Definition of done\n\n- A `[wrapper]` variant can state the built-in's conversational fast path and\n  its authored-witness gate without over-claiming.\n- `plugins/stella-research/plugin.toml`'s comment block naming this gap is\n  replaced by the real conditions, and\n  `crates/stella-runtime/tests/research_plugin_conformance.rs`'s\n  `the_shipped_manifest_declares_before_turn_and_nothing_stronger` asserts them.\n- The grammar stays closed and load-checked: an unparsable or ungrounded\n  condition is still a load error with a reason.\n\nRefs #3380, #3381.\n"[m[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"number"[m[1;37m:[m 3538[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"title"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"The closed condition grammar has no conjunction, so no [wrapper] variant can transcribe the built-in pipeline's own stage gates"[m
  [1;37m}[m[1;37m,[m
  [1;37m{[m
    [1;34m"body"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"## Problem\n\n`stella_plugin::consent::grade_sentence` renders `Participation::None` as:\n\n> none — it never runs inside a turn. It is a content bundle (skills, commands, agents, tools)\n\nThat sentence is correct for the case it was written for and wrong for the one `doc:pipeline-as-plugins` §10 settled. §10's option 2 makes self-driving a **host**: it never runs inside a turn *and* it is not a content bundle — it drives Stella from outside by calling the verbs `stella self-driving surface` declares. Both facts are true at once, and the vocabulary can only say the first.\n\nReproduce, after #3380's slice landed `plugins/stella-selfdriving/`:\n\n```\nstella plugin install plugins/stella-selfdriving\n```\n\nThe prompt reads \"Say in your turn loop: none — it never runs inside a turn. It is a content bundle (skills, commands, agents, tools)\" directly above a DESTRUCTIVE capability list covering `gh`, `brew`, an EC2 rig billed by the day, and a rewrite of a line in `~/.zshrc`. A user reading top-down is told \"content bundle\" and then handed the widest grant in the tree. Nothing is *false* — the package does ship slash commands — but the sentence steers away from what is actually being consented to.\n\n## Why this is not a D-3 escalation\n\nThe playbook's one escape hatch is \"the grant cannot be expressed, or cannot be shown before install\". Both hold: the capability list expresses it and `consent_text` shows it in full before the prompt (witnessed by `crates/stella-cli/tests/self_driving_consent.rs`). This is narrower — the *framing sentence* above the grant does not have a word for this shape.\n\n## Files\n\n- `crates/stella-plugin/src/consent.rs` — `grade_sentence`, and `loop_say` which calls it\n- `crates/stella-plugin/src/manifest.rs` — `Participation`, the `none < observer < steering < arbiter` ladder\n- `plugins/stella-selfdriving/plugin.toml` — the manifest that exposes the gap\n- `crates/stella-cli/tests/self_driving_consent.rs` — `the_prompt_shows_a_host_that_takes_no_say_in_the_turn` currently asserts the existing sentence and will need updating with any fix\n\n## Constraints already discovered\n\n- **`Participation` is a monotone ladder** and `includes()` is used as an ordering (`manifest.rs::validate`). \"Host\" is not a rung on that ladder — it is orthogonal to how much say a plugin has inside a turn, which is zero for both a content bundle and a host. A new variant is likely the wrong fix.\n- More promising: leave `Participation` alone and let the *rendering* say more when the manifest gives it something to say. A `none`-grade plugin with capabilities but no `skills`/`commands`/`agents`/`tools` content is not a content bundle, and the sentence could stop asserting it is.\n- `consent_text` is pure and deterministic by contract (a host diffs two renderings) — any fix must keep that.\n- `crates/stella-plugin` may not depend on `stella-core`, so nothing here can reference `Principal::Plugin` by type.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nA `none`-grade plugin that declares capabilities and ships no content bundle renders a framing sentence that does not call it a content bundle, with a witness test in `crates/stella-plugin/tests/install_consent.rs` that fails on the current rendering; `plugins/stella-selfdriving`'s prompt reads correctly top-down; `crates/stella-cli/tests/self_driving_consent.rs` updated in the same PR.\n\nRefs #3380."[m[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"number"[m[1;37m:[m 3537[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"title"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"The install-consent prompt has no word for a plugin that is a HOST, and calls it a content bundle"[m
  [1;37m}[m[1;37m,[m
