    [1;34m"body"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"The harbor adapter refuses a Stella trial with a message that does not say which environment variable tripped it. Diagnosing one instance cost a full cycle: a killed 5-task match, a 1-task control to prove it deterministic, and a hand-patched copy of the adapter to print the name.\n\n## What happened\n\nEvery Stella trial in a Terminal-Bench match died immediately after `stella init` with:\n\n```\nTrial pypi-server__9PpJ4U4 failed: selected provider credential remains in Docker's host environment\n```\n\nThe message names neither the variable nor the credential, so there is no way to act on it. It is also indistinguishable from an adapter bug, which is where the investigation went first.\n\nThe actual cause was operator environment: the host exported the same Voyage secret under two names — `VOYAGE_API_KEY` (recognised, and the one `EMBEDDING_CREDENTIAL_NAMES` forwards) and a duplicate `VOYAGE_AI_KEY`. `_is_credential_env_name` matches `_API_KEY` but not `_AI_KEY`, so the scrub left the duplicate in the Compose host environment and the guard then correctly caught its own scrub failing. `unset VOYAGE_AI_KEY` before launching resolves it with no loss of capability.\n\nThe guard is right to refuse — the secret genuinely would have reached Docker's host environment. The defect is that it refuses unactionably.\n\n## Files\n\n- `bench/harbor_adapter/stella_harbor/__init__.py` — the message is raised at two call sites (after health-check/before upload, and again before the anonymous-stdin handoff). Both compute `host_env` and already hold `credentials`, so the offending names are in scope at the raise.\n- `bench/harbor_adapter/stella_harbor/__init__.py::_is_credential_env_name` — the name-based filter with the `_AI_KEY` gap.\n- `bench/harbor_adapter/stella_harbor/credential_bundle.py::EMBEDDING_CREDENTIAL_NAMES`.\n\n## Repro\n\n```sh\nexport VOYAGE_API_KEY=<any value>\nexport VOYAGE_AI_KEY=\"$VOYAGE_API_KEY\"     # same value, unrecognised name\n# run any arenabench match with a stella seat\n```\n\nEvery trial fails at the guard. Deterministic — reproduced at 1 task / concurrency 1, so it is not a concurrency race.\n\n## Definition of done\n\n1. The `RuntimeError` names the offending variable(s) — the names only, never the value. Both call sites. The names are already computable at the raise:\n   `sorted(n for n, v in host_env.items() for c in credentials if c and c in v)`.\n2. A decision on the `_AI_KEY` gap, made explicitly rather than by omission. Either widen `_is_credential_env_name` to cover `_AI_KEY` (and audit for other near-miss suffixes), or state in a comment why an exact-name policy is deliberate and the duplicate is the operator's problem. Widening the filter changes what gets scrubbed, so it wants a reviewed answer, not a quiet edit.\n3. A witness test that fails on `main`: construct a host env carrying a selected credential under a name the filter does not recognise, assert the raised message contains that variable name and does not contain the secret value.\n\n## Constraints\n\n- Never render the credential value into the message, a log, or a test fixture. `_contains_credential` exists precisely to search without rendering; the new message must hold that line.\n- Sibling tests live in `bench/harbor_adapter/tests/test_embedding_credential.py`.\n\nFound while running a 5-task ArenaBench match on 2026-08-17."[m[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"number"[m[1;37m:[m 3533[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"title"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"bench adapter: credential-absence guard refuses without naming the offending variable, and its name-based scrub misses _AI_KEY"[m
  [1;37m}[m[1;37m,[m
