    [1;34m"body"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"## Problem\n\n`docs/wire/` now carries the wrapper socket's wire contract (`docs/wire/wrapper.wire.json`), generated by `crates/stella-plugin/src/bin/export_wrapper_wire.rs` from `crates/stella-plugin/src/wire_corpus.rs` and gate-checked by `scripts/check-wire-schema.sh`. That closes `doc:wrapper-socket` §3 commitment 2's headline case — a renamed field or a re-tagged variant is now a red gate on the author's machine.\n\nBut it is **not the same artifact as its two neighbours**. `agentevent.schema.json` and `serveframe.schema.json` are JSON Schema 2020-12, derived by `schemars` from `#[cfg_attr(feature = \"schema\", derive(JsonSchema))]` on the types. `wrapper.wire.json` is a *corpus*: every message serialized twice, once with every optional field populated (`full`) and once with every omissible field omitted (`minimal`).\n\nIt was built that way for one reason, stated in `wire_corpus.rs`'s module docs: `schemars` generates a schema from a `JsonSchema` impl, an impl can only be written where the type is defined, and `crates/stella-plugin/src/wire.rs` was held by a concurrent session at the time. Hand-writing a schema beside it would have been the second copy of the contract that `docs/wire/` exists to prevent.\n\n## What the corpus does not catch\n\nEverything below changes the wire contract and produces **zero diff** in `wrapper.wire.json`:\n\n- a widened or narrowed scalar — `pub round: u32` → `u64`, `TestPlan::timeout` style numeric fields;\n- a string field gaining (or losing) a format/pattern constraint;\n- an enum whose variant set grows *without* a corresponding successor-chain edit — this one is compiler-caught today (see below), but only because the corpus hand-maintains the chain.\n\nA `schemars` schema catches the first two structurally.\n\n## Definition of done\n\n1. Add `#[cfg_attr(feature = \"schema\", derive(schemars::JsonSchema))]` to every type in `crates/stella-plugin/src/wire.rs` reachable from `WrapperRequest` / `WrapperResponse`, plus the types they pull in: `ObservedEvidence` (`src/observed.rs`), `Signal` / `StageName` (`src/wrapper.rs`). `stella_protocol::candidate::CandidateHandle` already carries the derive under `stella-protocol`'s own `schema` feature.\n   - `WrapperRequest` / `WrapperResponse` have **hand-written `Deserialize` impls** over a two-key `Envelope` (#3500). `schemars` derives from the *shape*, not from `Deserialize`, so the derived schema describes the `#[serde(tag = \"point\", content = \"body\")]` framing the `Serialize` derive uses. Confirm that is what lands, and that the schema does not claim the envelope accepts unknown keys — it does not.\n2. Flip `crates/stella-plugin/Cargo.toml`'s `schema = []` to `schema = [\"dep:schemars\"]` and add `schemars = { workspace = true, optional = true }`. **This adds a second workspace dependency to a near-leaf crate** — the manifest's own comment says a second edge \"wants the same argument made again, not this one cited\". It is optional and off by default, which is the argument, but make it explicitly.\n3. Emit `docs/wire/wrapper.schema.json` (and `wrapper.d.ts` if the shared TypeScript printer, `stella_protocol::schema_export::typescript_declarations_with_header`, accepts the schema — `stella-serve` reuses it rather than duplicating one, and so should this).\n4. Add the new filenames to the `for artifact in …` loop in `scripts/check-wire-schema.sh`, and to `docs/wire/README.md`'s table.\n5. **Decide whether the corpus stays.** They are not redundant: the corpus pins actual bytes for the `full`/`minimal` pair, which is how an optional↔required flip becomes visible, and a schema states that more directly via `required`. Keeping both is defensible; dropping the corpus is defensible. Say which and why in the PR — do not let it rot as a second artifact nobody regenerates on purpose.\n\n## Constraints already discovered\n\n- `scripts/export-agentevent-schema.sh` is the single regeneration entry point (`make wire-schema-update` calls it) and already runs all three exporters. A fourth artifact from the same `export-wrapper-wire` bin needs no new script.\n- `.github/workflows/wire-schema.yml` and `.githooks/pre-push` carry the same path list by hand and both already include `crates/stella-plugin/`. No change needed there.\n- The corpus's totality is **compiler-enforced**: `wire_corpus.rs` enumerates every closed enum with a successor `match` (`E0004` on a new variant) and builds every struct with an exhaustive literal (`E0063` on a new field). This is not decoration — it fired for real during authoring, catching `AfterTurnRequest::stage` being added in a concurrent session. If the corpus is dropped in step 5, that enforcement goes with it and `schemars` does not replace it (a derive follows the type silently). Weigh that.\n- `wire_corpus.rs`'s unit tests sit behind the `schema` feature, so `cargo test --workspace` (and therefore `make gate`'s test step) **does not run them**. `stella-protocol`'s `schema_export` has the same shape. The exporter itself is exercised on every gate run by `scripts/check-wire-schema.sh`, so this is a gap in the module's own assertions, not in the guard. Worth fixing here or splitting out.\n\n## Verify\n\n```sh\nbash scripts/export-agentevent-schema.sh\n./scripts/check-wire-schema.sh              # green, and idempotent on a second run\n```\n\nThen the witness, in a detached worktree so the shared tree is untouched:\n\n```sh\ngit worktree add --detach /tmp/wt HEAD\n# in /tmp/wt: rename a field on the wire, e.g. on TurnOutcome::tools\n#   #[serde(rename = \"tool_calls\", default, skip_serializing_if = \"Vec::is_empty\")]\ncargo test -p stella-plugin        # still green — the round-trip tests are blind to this\n./scripts/check-wire-schema.sh     # must be red, naming the artifact and the key\ngit worktree remove --force /tmp/wt\n```\n\nThat exact rename was used to witness the corpus guard and is a good regression case for the schema one: `cargo test -p stella-plugin` passed all 128 tests while `check-wire-schema.sh` exited 1 with `docs/wire/wrapper.wire.json is stale` and a `-\"tools\"` / `+\"tool_calls\"` diff.\n\nRefs #3380. Follow-up to the PR that landed `wrapper.wire.json`."[m[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"number"[m[1;37m:[m 3532[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"title"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"Upgrade docs/wire/wrapper.wire.json from a message corpus to a derived JSON Schema"[m
  [1;37m}[m[1;37m,[m
