    [1;34m"body"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"## Problem\n\n`crates/stella-cli/src/plugin_cmd/roster.rs::read_tier` selects tier entries with\n`.filter(|path| path.is_dir())`. `Path::is_dir` **follows symlinks**, so\n`.stella/plugins/evil -> /somewhere/else` (or `~/.stella/plugins/evil -> …`) is read\nas an installed package: its `plugin.toml` loads, it appears in `stella plugin list`,\nand `PluginRoster::hook_routes()` emits routes for it with `${plugin_dir}` interpolated\nto the *link* path.\n\nThis is the one place the loader admits content it never copied. `install` refuses\nsymlinks explicitly (`copy_tree`, and the witness\n`plugin_cmd::tests::a_symlink_in_a_package_is_refused`), on the stated grounds that a\npackage is third-party content and a link is a request to run something the author\ndoes not ship. The tier reader does not hold the same line, so anything that can drop\na symlink into a tier — a `git clone` for the project tier (behind\n`STELLA_TRUST_PROJECT`), any process with write access for the user tier — gets a\nrouted plugin whose files live wherever the link points.\n\n`stella plugin remove <name>` then refuses it: `remove_plugin_dir`\n(`crates/stella-cli/src/plugin_cmd.rs`, added in #3380's fix) checks\n`symlink_metadata` and errors `\"… is a symlink, not an installed package — delete it\nby hand if you meant to\"`, deliberately, because `remove_dir_all` down a link would\ndelete whatever it names. So today the pair is inconsistent in the worst direction:\n**loadable and routable, but not uninstallable through the CLI.**\n\n## Files\n\n- `crates/stella-cli/src/plugin_cmd/roster.rs` — `read_tier` (the `is_dir` filter), and\n  `read_project_tier` above it for the trust gate that does *not* cover this.\n- `crates/stella-cli/src/plugin_cmd.rs` — `copy_tree` (install's refusal, the precedent),\n  `remove_plugin_dir` (remove's refusal).\n- `crates/stella-cli/src/plugin_cmd/tests.rs` — `a_symlink_in_a_package_is_refused` is the\n  shape a witness here should follow; `plant`/`plant_named` are the fixture helpers.\n\n## Repro\n\nUnix only. In a test, or by hand:\n\n```bash\nmkdir -p /tmp/ws/.stella/plugins /tmp/elsewhere/vera\ncat > /tmp/elsewhere/vera/plugin.toml <<'TOML'\nname = \"vera\"\n[loop]\nparticipation = \"arbiter\"\nhooks = [\"PreToolUse\", \"Stop\"]\n[requirements]\nr = \"the tests pass\"\n[runtime]\nargv = [\"python3\", \"${plugin_dir}/main.py\"]\ntimeout_secs = 30\nTOML\nln -s /tmp/elsewhere/vera /tmp/ws/.stella/plugins/vera\ncd /tmp/ws && STELLA_TRUST_PROJECT=1 stella plugin list      # `vera` is listed and dispatches\ncd /tmp/ws && stella plugin remove vera                      # refused: \"is a symlink, not an installed package\"\n```\n\n## What \"done\" looks like\n\nDecide the policy and make both halves agree. The likely answer is that a tier entry\nthat is a symlink is **not** a plugin: skip it in `read_tier` (via\n`symlink_metadata`, not `is_dir`) and push a notice naming it, in the shape of the\nother `read_tier` notices — silence here is indistinguishable from a broken plugin,\nwhich is the reason those notices exist. `remove` can then keep refusing it, since\nnothing routes it and no CLI verb claims to own it; if instead the decision is that a\nsymlinked tier entry is a legitimate \"install from a checkout\" affordance, then\n`remove` needs a spelling that unlinks it and the trust story for the project tier\nneeds stating, because the link's target was never consented to at install.\n\nConstraints:\n\n- Witness test required, in `crates/stella-cli/src/plugin_cmd/tests.rs`, `#[cfg(unix)]`\n  like the existing symlink witness: it must assert on `hook_routes()` (a route is an\n  argv a host spawns), not only on `plugins()`.\n- Do not weaken `remove_plugin_dir`'s symlink refusal into a `remove_dir_all` down the\n  link.\n- `make gate` locally is `cargo fmt` + `cargo clippy -p stella-cli --all-targets -- -D warnings`\n  + `cargo test -p stella-cli`.\n\n## Context\n\nFound while fixing #3380's loader defects (`remove` stopping at the first tier,\nresolving by directory name, and non-atomic install). Not fixed there because it\nchanges what the loader admits, which is a policy decision rather than a defect repair,\nand the scope of that change was the three audited defects. Related: #3509 (project-tier\ntrust gate), #3521 (nothing dispatches `PluginHookRoute` yet — when a host starts\nconsuming routes, this becomes a live execution path rather than a listing bug)."[m[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"number"[m[1;37m:[m 3530[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"title"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"Plugin loader: a symlinked directory inside a plugins tier is loaded and routed, but cannot be uninstalled"[m
  [1;37m}[m[1;37m,[m
