    [1;34m"body"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"## Problem\n\n#3512 moved the plugin credential refusal into the socket:\n`SubprocessWrapper::declare` (`crates/stella-runtime/src/wrapper/subprocess.rs`)\nfilters the resolved `Vec<(String, String)>` through `refuses_env_name` and\nreturns an `AdmittedWrapper { wrapper, refused }`.\n\nThe filter runs **exactly once, at construction**. The judgement it calls,\n`stella_tools::subprocess_env::is_sensitive_env_name`, is not a pure function\nof its argument: it also consults `REGISTERED_CREDENTIAL_ENV_VARS`, a\nprocess-global monotonic registry populated by\n`register_sensitive_env_names` from trusted settings\n(`crates/stella-tools/src/subprocess_env.rs:212-228`).\n\nSo the two orderings give different answers for the same manifest:\n\n- settings register `MY_VENDOR_KEY` → wrapper declared → the pair is refused.\n- wrapper declared → settings register `MY_VENDOR_KEY` → the wrapper already\n  holds the resolved pair and hands it to every child it spawns, forever.\n\nToday the second ordering is believed not to occur: settings are loaded before\nany plugin roster is composed, and nothing dispatches a wrapper at all yet\n(#3494). That is an argument from current call order, not a property — and the\nwhole point of #3512 was that a security property held by one caller's\ndiscipline is not a property.\n\n## Why it is not obviously \"just re-check at spawn\"\n\n`exchange` (same file) is the spawn site and it is `&self`, so re-filtering\nthere is cheap but would make the *report* stale: `AdmittedWrapper::refused`\nwas already returned to the caller and printed. A refusal discovered at spawn\nhas no surface to reach — which is the silent-filter failure mode #3512\nexisted to close, reintroduced one layer down.\n\nOptions worth weighing (not a decision):\n\n1. Re-filter in `exchange` and carry the newly refused names out through a\n   typed `WrapperError` variant, so a late registration fails the dispatch\n   loudly rather than leaking or silently dropping.\n2. Have `declare` take the credential predicate as a parameter, making the\n   time-of-check explicit at the call site and the socket a pure function of\n   its inputs (this also removes the crate's one read of process-global state\n   that `tests/no_ambient_reads.rs` does not cover, because it is not spelled\n   `std::env::var`).\n3. Declare the ordering as an invariant with a test that fails if a host can\n   observe the difference.\n\n## Files\n\n- `crates/stella-runtime/src/wrapper/subprocess.rs` — `declare`,\n  `refuses_env_name`, `exchange`.\n- `crates/stella-tools/src/subprocess_env.rs` — `is_sensitive_env_name`,\n  `register_sensitive_env_names`, `REGISTERED_CREDENTIAL_ENV_VARS`.\n- `crates/stella-runtime/tests/wrapper_env_refusal.rs` — where a witness for\n  whichever option is chosen belongs.\n\n## Verify / repro\n\nThere is no failing test today; the repro is a unit test that constructs a\nwrapper with a pair named by nothing in the static lists, then calls\n`register_sensitive_env_names([\"THAT_NAME\"])`, then spawns the child and asks\nit whether it can see the variable. It can. Note that the registry is\nprocess-global and monotonic, so such a test contaminates every other test in\nthe binary that asserts a name is *admitted* — it likely needs its own test\nbinary, which is also why this was not folded into #3512.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nA host cannot observe an admitted credential after that name has been\nregistered, and whichever mechanism achieves that reports the refusal to a\ncaller rather than dropping it. Constraints: `stella-runtime` must not read\nambient process state (`crates/stella-runtime/tests/no_ambient_reads.rs`);\ninvariant 5 (typed errors, no `String`); the CLI's consent-time report in\n`crates/stella-cli/src/plugin_cmd/process.rs` must keep agreeing with whatever\nthe socket does (asserted across the crate boundary by\n`what_install_corrects_is_exactly_what_the_socket_withholds` in\n`crates/stella-cli/src/plugin_cmd/tests.rs`).\n\nRefs #3512, #3494."[m[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"number"[m[1;37m:[m 3529[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"title"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"The socket's credential refusal is evaluated once at declare time, so a name registered later is admitted for the wrapper's whole life"[m
  [1;37m}[m[1;37m,[m
  [1;37m{[m
    [1;34m"body"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"## The outcome\n\nStella loses Terminal-Bench 2.1 tasks it can reason about, because the turn\ncosts too much wall clock per step and the run gets cut off. This epic collects\nevery issue whose fix is *seconds off the clock*, as opposed to a better answer.\n\n## The measurement that opened it\n\n#3272, on run `preregA2` at stock timeouts, restricted to the five tasks that\nsolved on some attempts and not others:\n\n| | n | mean steps | mean agent wall |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| wins | 6 | 59.3 | 845s |\n| losses | 9 | 48.6 | **1129s** |\n\nLosses spend 284s more wall clock while completing 11 fewer steps, and 5 of the\n9 terminate `interrupted` (the timeout) rather than `completed`. The agent is\nnot thinking its way to a wrong answer — it is running out of time.\n\nThat reframes a class of backlog items that were previously filed as\ncorrectness or ergonomics: they are the same defect measured in seconds.\n\n## Children\n\n- **#3272** — the finding itself: losses are clock-bound, not reasoning-bound.\n  Closing it means the per-step cost is characterised and the dominant term named.\n- **#3292** — loop detection is blind to varied-input, varied-output grinding\n  (62 bash calls, 62 distinct outputs, no detector fires, the turn ran to its\n  step ceiling and solved nothing). Unproductive grinding is pure clock.\n- **#3328** — the detector-side half: the output-keyed stagnation rung kills\n  *legitimate* loops over unstampable constant-output tools. The opposite error\n  of #3292 and, per its own title, to be decided jointly with it.\n- **#3307** — the afford-hinted 402 retry is per-request, so a persistently low\n  balance pays one wasted round-trip **every turn**. Dozens per bench trial.\n\n## Definition of done\n\n1. The per-step wall clock of a Stella turn is decomposed into named terms\n   (model latency, tool wall clock, harness overhead) from `stella-events.jsonl`\n   — `tool_start` → `tool_result` joins, not a surface summary.\n2. Each child above is closed or explicitly deferred with the seconds it was\n   worth measured, not estimated.\n3. A repeat of the #3272 measurement on the same task set shows the loss-vs-win\n   wall-clock gap moved, reported honestly including if it did not.\n\n## Constraints\n\n- CLAUDE.md § \"A bench conclusion comes from the trace, never from a surface\n  signal\" governs every number claimed here.\n- Never run the comparison with a budget or token cap, and never modify\n  timeouts — see #3256 for why every number recorded before this rule is not\n  leaderboard-eligible.\n"[m[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"number"[m[1;37m:[m 3528[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"title"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"Epic: Stella's turn is clock-bound — cut the wall clock a step costs"[m
  [1;37m}[m[1;37m,[m
  [1;37m{[m
    [1;34m"body"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"## Problem\n\n#3499 and #3501 landed the grammar changes Track C\n(macanderson/stella-examples#1) asked for, but the example plugins themselves\nwere not updated in the same change — they live in a separate repository. Until\nthey are, the published examples teach the redundant shape, and #3501's stated\ndefinition of done (\"the three manifests then differ in exactly two lines\") is\nnot yet demonstrated anywhere.\n\n## What changed in `stella` (commit `2c6190c`)\n\n1. `[oracle] command` is optional when `[runtime]` declares the process the\n   oracle is. The duplicated argv is now removable.\n2. `[oracle] tamper` is optional and defaults to `artifact-identity`; the field\n   names what the *host* does.\n3. `EvidenceSet.tamper` is gone from the plugin's wire type. A plugin returns\n   `ObservedEvidence` — `{\"flip\": …, \"measurements\": {…}}` — and a response\n   carrying `tamper` is now **refused** by the host's decoder, so any example\n   still sending it stops working.\n4. `[loop] points` must name the socket points a plugin answers, and a manifest\n   declaring `[oracle]` must include `after_turn` or it will not load.\n\nItem 3 is a breaking change for any plugin written against the shipped shape;\nitems 1, 2 and 4 are why the manifests can now be near-identical.\n\n## Work\n\nIn `macanderson/stella-examples`:\n\n- Drop `[oracle] command` from all three manifests (Rust, Python, TypeScript)\n  and let `[runtime]` name the process once.\n- Drop the `tamper` line; add `points = [\"after_turn\"]` (plus `before_turn`\n  where the plugin answers it).\n- Stop emitting `\"tamper\"` in the `after_turn` response, in all three\n  languages, and update any recorded wire vectors under `plugins/testdata/`.\n- Delete `plugins/ci/check-manifests-identical.py`'s two-argv special case —\n  the assertion \"the two argvs must name the same program\" has nothing left to\n  compare.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nThe three manifests differ in exactly two lines (one `[runtime] argv` each),\n`check-manifests-identical.py` needs no special case, and all three plugins\nround-trip their `after_turn` response through the current\n`stella_plugin::ObservedEvidence`.\n\nRefs #3499, #3501, #3380. Upstream: macanderson/stella-examples#1.\n"[m[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"number"[m[1;37m:[m 3523[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"title"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"Track C's three example plugins still carry the manifest redundancies #3499/#3501 removed"[m
  [1;37m}[m[1;37m,[m
  [1;37m{[m
    [1;34m"body"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"## Problem\n\n#3501 gave a manifest `[loop] points` and `LoopGrant::permits_point`\n(`crates/stella-plugin/src/manifest.rs`), which the crate documents as the\nauthoritative filter behind \"an undeclared point is never dispatched\" — the\nsame claim `permits_hook` makes for hooks. **Nothing consults it yet.**\n\n`stella_runtime::wrapper::SubprocessWrapper` / `InProcessWrapper` dispatch\n`before_turn` and `after_turn` unconditionally: they hold an argv and a\ntimeout, not a manifest, so a host that builds one from an installed plugin can\ncall a point that plugin never declared. `PluginRoster` has `hook_routes()` and\nno equivalent for points, so there is no place where the declaration is turned\ninto the set of permitted dispatches.\n\nToday that is only latent — no host dispatches a wrapper point at all (#3521 is\nthe same gap for hooks) — but the filter shipped as a promise, and a promise\nnothing enforces is exactly the \"manifest that quietly does nothing\" this crate\nexists to prevent, arriving from the host's side instead of the plugin's.\n\n## Files\n\n- `crates/stella-plugin/src/manifest.rs` — `LoopGrant::points`,\n  `LoopGrant::permits_point`, and the load rules beside them\n  (`PointsRequireSteering`, `DuplicatePoint`, `OracleRequiresAfterTurn`).\n- `crates/stella-cli/src/plugin_cmd/roster.rs` — `PluginRoster::hook_routes`\n  is the shape to copy; a `point_routes()` would sit beside it, gated on\n  `[runtime]` exactly as hook routes are.\n- `crates/stella-runtime/src/wrapper/mod.rs` — `TurnWrapper`, and `admissible`,\n  which is where value-level enforcement of a load-time rule already lives.\n\n## Constraints already discovered\n\n- The roster is recomputed from disk on every load by design; routes must not\n  be cached across an install/remove (`roster.rs` module docs).\n- An in-process Rust wrapper (`InProcessWrapper`) has no `[runtime]` block, so\n  a point-route builder must not require one the way `hook_routes` does — or\n  the fast path silently loses its dispatches.\n- `admissible()` already refuses an undeclared role and a mistyped signal from\n  a `before_turn` response; refusing a dispatch to an undeclared point is the\n  same idea one step earlier, and could plausibly live in the same module.\n\n## Definition of done\n\n- Exactly one path decides whether a plugin is dispatched at a point, and it\n  answers from `permits_point`.\n- A witness test: a manifest declaring only `after_turn` is never dispatched at\n  `before_turn` **through the host path** (the manifest-level half is\n  `an_undeclared_point_is_never_dispatched` in `manifest.rs` and already\n  passes, so the new test must fail without the host wiring).\n\nRefs #3501, #3521, #3380.\n"[m[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"number"[m[1;37m:[m 3522[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"title"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"Nothing routes wrapper-point dispatch through LoopGrant::permits_point — the filter exists, the chokepoint does not"[m
  [1;37m}[m[1;37m,[m
  [1;37m{[m
    [1;34m"body"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"## Problem\n\n`PluginRoster::hook_routes()` (`crates/stella-cli/src/plugin_cmd/roster.rs`) produces the set of dispatches a host is permitted to make, and its only consumer today is `plugin_cmd::list`, which **prints** them. No code path spawns a plugin's `[runtime]` process for a `HookEvent`. The routes are scaffolding with no wiring — invariant #10's shape, and AGENTS.md's \"unwired code with no tracking issue\" rule is why this issue exists.\n\nThat matters now because #3509 just put the project tier behind `settings::project_code_execution_trusted()`, and the gate lives in `roster::read_project_tier` — i.e. **inside** `PluginRoster::load`. A future host that reaches for `stella_home::resolve_project_plugins_dir()` directly, or that reads the tier before composing a roster, re-opens the arbitrary-code-execution-on-clone hole with no test failing. The gate is only as good as the chokepoint being the only door.\n\n## Files\n\n- `crates/stella-cli/src/plugin_cmd/roster.rs` — `PluginRoster::load`, `read_project_tier`, `hook_routes`.\n- `crates/stella-cli/src/plugin_cmd/process.rs` — `prepare_command`, which turns a route into a `Command` (already withholds model credentials).\n- `crates/stella-core/src/hooks.rs` — where the engine's own hook matching lives, for the shape the plugin dispatch has to sit beside.\n- `stella_home::resolve_project_plugins_dir` — the function a future host must **not** call directly.\n\n## Constraints already discovered\n\n- The roster is recomputed from disk on every load by design (see `roster.rs`'s module docs) — a host must not cache routes across an install/remove.\n- Every route's `principal` is `Principal::Plugin`, never `Principal::User`; #3482 tracks the fact that nothing yet binds that principal to an `AuthzGate` rule, and this wiring is where the binding becomes load-bearing.\n- `hook_routes()` is documented as \"the authoritative routing filter\" — nothing downstream re-checks `permits_hook`, so a host must dispatch only what it is handed.\n\n## Definition of done\n\n- A host path dispatches plugin hook routes, sourced from `PluginRoster::load` and nothing else.\n- A witness test that a plugin planted in an **untrusted** project tier produces no spawn on that path — i.e. the #3509 gate is proven at the dispatch site, not only at the roster.\n- If direct reads of the plugins tier need to be prevented structurally, consider narrowing `resolve_project_plugins_dir`'s visibility or adding a grep guard, and say which was chosen.\n\nRefs #3509, #3482.\n"[m[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"number"[m[1;37m:[m 3521[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"title"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"Nothing dispatches PluginHookRoute yet — the eventual host wiring must consume PluginRoster::load, not re-read the plugins tier"[m
  [1;37m}[m[1;37m,[m
  [1;37m{[m
    [1;34m"body"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"## Problem\n\nTwo functions resolve the same directory (`~/.stella/plugins`) through two different accessors:\n\n- `crates/stella-cli/src/plugin_cmd.rs::tier_dir` → `crate::paths::stella_root()`\n- `crates/stella-cli/src/plugin_cmd/roster.rs::PluginRoster::load` → `crate::paths::user_extension_root()` (changed in #3509)\n\nThey are the same value in production (`UserPaths::from_environment` sets `extensions_visible: true`), so this is not a live bug. It is divergent in a **test** build, where `UserPaths::test_default()` sets `extensions_visible: false`: `load` correctly answers \"no user tier\", while `tier_dir` still resolves against the developer's real `~/.stella` and would have `stella plugin install --scope user` write into it.\n\nNo test does that today, which is the only reason it has not bitten. `crates/stella-cli/src/paths.rs`'s own doc on `extensions_visible` states the policy this violates: a suite that reads the developer's real user-scope extension directory \"passes or fails for reasons that have nothing to do with the code under test — and would then disagree with CI, where that directory does not exist.\"\n\n## Files\n\n- `crates/stella-cli/src/plugin_cmd.rs` — `tier_dir`, the `PluginScope::User` arm.\n- `crates/stella-cli/src/plugin_cmd/roster.rs` — `PluginRoster::load`, for the shape to match.\n- `crates/stella-cli/src/paths.rs` — `stella_root` / `user_extension_root` / `extensions_visible` and their doc comments.\n\n## Verify\n\nAdd a test that installs with `--scope user` under `crate::paths::test_user_home(tmp)` and asserts the destination is under `tmp`, plus one that asserts the install is refused (or resolves to nothing) with no home installed. The second fails today.\n\nNote that `tier_dir`'s `None` arm returns a real user-facing error message (\"no home directory is discoverable … use --scope project\"), so switching the accessor changes what a test build sees from that arm — decide deliberately whether an un-redirected test should get that error or a real path.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nOne accessor is used for the user plugin tier on both the install and the load path, with a comment saying which and why, and a test pinning that an un-redirected unit test cannot reach the developer's own `~/.stella/plugins` from either.\n\nFound while fixing #3509.\n"[m[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"number"[m[1;37m:[m 3520[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"title"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"plugin install resolves the user tier through paths::stella_root while the loader resolves it through paths::user_extension_root"[m
  [1;37m}[m[1;37m,[m
  [1;37m{[m
    [1;34m"body"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"## Problem\n\nThe README states the project-trust boundary as:\n\n> \"project-scope hooks and credential-routing fields don't load from a cloned repo until you set `STELLA_TRUST_PROJECT=1`. That's Stella refusing to let a repo you just cloned run arbitrary commands on your machine.\"\n\nThat sentence enumerates two of the **four** surfaces the flag now gates. `.stella/mcp.toml` has been gated by `agent::load_mcp_plan` for some time, and `<workspace>/.stella/plugins/` is gated as of #3509 (`plugin_cmd::roster::read_project_tier`). The enumeration reads as exhaustive, so a reader auditing their own exposure gets an under-count of what the flag protects — and, worse, the next surface added has nothing telling its author the sentence exists.\n\nThis is the shared-cell drift shape AGENTS.md warns about: one prose list that every gating PR must remember to write, with no guard checking it.\n\n## Files\n\n- `README.md` — the sentence above (grep for `STELLA_TRUST_PROJECT`).\n- `crates/stella-cli/src/settings.rs::project_code_execution_trusted` — the doc comment there is the closest thing to a canonical list and is itself missing plugins.\n- Call sites, i.e. the real list: `crates/stella-cli/src/settings/merge.rs` (hooks, context sources, credential routing), `crates/stella-cli/src/agent.rs::load_mcp_plan` (`.stella/mcp.toml`), `crates/stella-cli/src/plugin_cmd/roster.rs::read_project_tier` (`.stella/plugins/`).\n\n## Definition of done\n\n- The README sentence names all four surfaces, or names none of them and points at one document that does.\n- `project_code_execution_trusted`'s doc comment is the single normative enumeration, and the README cites it rather than restating it.\n- Consider whether a guard is warranted: a `grep` for `project_code_execution_trusted()` call sites compared against the documented list would catch the next omission the way `check-gate-parity.sh` catches the `GATE_STEPS` one. If a guard is judged not worth it, say so in the PR so the next reader knows it was decided rather than missed.\n\nFound while fixing #3509.\n"[m[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"number"[m[1;37m:[m 3519[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"title"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"README's project-trust promise names hooks and credential routing but not plugins or .stella/mcp.toml"[m
  [1;37m}[m[1;37m,[m
  [1;37m{[m
    [1;34m"body"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"## Problem\n\n#3500's fix gave `WrapperRequest`/`WrapperResponse` a hand-written `Deserialize` over a two-key `Envelope` with `deny_unknown_fields`, because serde has no `deny_unknown_fields` for an adjacently tagged enum. The envelope holds `body` as a `serde_json::Value` until `point` has been read — serde's own `Content` buffer is private, and holding the body is what keeps the two keys order-free instead of \"point must come first\".\n\nThat costs two things, both small and both real:\n\n1. **Body decode errors lose their position.** `decode_body` re-deserializes out of a `Value` and reports through `serde::de::Error::custom`, so a plugin author gets `unknown field \\`system_prompt\\`, expected one of …` without the `at line N column M` that a direct `from_slice` would have carried. The field name survives (asserted in `an_unknown_envelope_key_is_refused_rather_than_ignored`), which is why this is a papercut and not a regression in what is *decidable* — but position is the half a plugin author actually navigates by.\n2. **One extra allocation and parse pass per message**, on a path that carries `changed_files` lists and answer text.\n\nFiles: `crates/stella-plugin/src/wire.rs` (`Envelope`, `decode_body`, the two `Deserialize` impls) and `crates/stella-plugin/Cargo.toml`, where `serde_json` moved from `[dev-dependencies]` to `[dependencies]` for this. The move left `Cargo.lock` unchanged (the lockfile does not distinguish dev deps), and `stella-plugin` still performs no I/O.\n\n## What \"done\" could look like\n\nAny of these, in rough order of preference:\n\n- A small private `Content`-style buffer in this crate — enough to hold one JSON-ish body and replay it into a `Deserializer` — which drops the `serde_json` dependency again and keeps positions. This is what serde does internally; the question is whether ~150 lines of it is worth owning.\n- A visitor that takes the precise seed when `point` precedes `body` (the order the host itself always writes, and the common case) and falls back to the buffer only when it does not, so the common path keeps its positions.\n- Decide the papercut is acceptable and close this, having written down why — the honest outcome if a buffer of our own turns out to be the worse trade.\n\n## Verify\n\nExtend `crates/stella-plugin/tests/wire_contract.rs`: an envelope whose body has an unknown field reports a position, and `an_unknown_envelope_key_is_refused_rather_than_ignored`'s both-orders assertion still passes.\n\nRefs #3500, #3380."[m[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"number"[m[1;37m:[m 3518[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"title"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"stella-plugin: the strict envelope decoder buffers the body through serde_json::Value, so a malformed body loses its line and column"[m
  [1;37m}[m[1;37m,[m
  [1;37m{[m
    [1;34m"body"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"## Problem\n\nThere are two evaluators for `[[oracle.checks]]` and they can drift.\n\n- `Oracle::unmet` (`crates/stella-plugin/src/evidence.rs`) is documented as \"the whole evaluator for the evidence half of the grammar\". It has **no production caller**: `grep -rn '\\.unmet(' crates/ --include=*.rs` finds only `crates/stella-plugin/tests/non_witness_done.rs`.\n- `judge` (`crates/stella-runtime/src/wrapper/verdict.rs`) is the only place a verdict is actually made, and it walks `oracle.checks` itself — `check.rule()`, `evidence.measurements.get(...)`, `parsed.holds(reported)` — rather than calling `Oracle::unmet`.\n\nSo the evaluator that the plugin crate calls canonical is dead, and the one that binds is a second copy in another crate. A change to one is invisible to the other; the tests in `non_witness_done.rs` and `evidence.rs` would stay green while the shipped verdict path diverged.\n\nThis is not merely tidiness. #3510 was exactly a defect in how `judge` composes checks with the flip policy, and the \"canonical\" evaluator has no opinion about that composition at all — its doc comment even says an empty answer \"is not by itself 'done' whenever the flip policy also has something to say\", which is the caller's problem and there is no caller.\n\n## Why they are not trivially the same function\n\nThe divergence is real and has to be designed, not just deleted:\n\n- `Oracle::unmet` returns `Err(ManifestError::MeasurementNotReported)` for a missing number; `judge` must **abstain** (`UndecidedReason::MeasurementMissing`) because it is total and has no `Result` (`doc:wrapper-socket` §4, and the module doc of `verdict.rs`).\n- `Oracle::unmet` returns `UnmetCheck { requirement, rule, reported }`; `judge` needs `UnmetRequirement { requirement, statement, because: UnmetBecause::Budget { .. } }`, carrying the requirement's *statement* from `[requirements]`, which the oracle does not hold.\n- `judge` iterates requirements (a `BTreeMap`, for byte-stable ordering — invariant 7) and filters checks per requirement; `Oracle::unmet` iterates checks in declaration order.\n- `judge` must interleave the per-requirement flip conjunct (#3510).\n\n## What \"done\" looks like\n\nOne evaluator for \"does this check hold against these reported numbers\", with the two callers differing only in how they *report*. Concretely, one plausible shape (the design is open):\n\n- Keep the pure per-check kernel in `stella-plugin` (it is the grammar's own semantics and belongs beside `MeasurementRule::holds`), returning a small total enum — held / failed-with-value / measurement-missing / unreadable — with no `Result`.\n- `Oracle::unmet` becomes a thin fold over it that keeps its current `Result` shape for its existing callers.\n- `judge` folds the same kernel into `UnmetRequirement`/`UndecidedReason`, so `verdict.rs` no longer re-derives `check.rule()` + `holds`.\n\nEither that, or `Oracle::unmet` is deleted as unwired and its tests moved onto the kernel — but \"delete it\" needs a decision, because it is `pub` API of `stella-plugin`.\n\n## Verify\n\n- `grep -rn '\\.unmet(' crates/ --include=*.rs` shows a production caller, or `Oracle::unmet` is gone.\n- `crates/stella-runtime/src/wrapper/verdict.rs` no longer calls `check.rule()` / `MeasurementRule::holds` directly.\n- `cargo test -p stella-plugin -p stella-runtime` green, in particular `crates/stella-runtime/tests/wrapper_verdict.rs` — its exhaustive pass, `a_check_can_only_narrow_the_met_set`, and `a_check_does_not_excuse_a_requirement_from_the_flip_or_the_tamper_exclusion` all pin the behaviour that must not change.\n- A witness for the drift itself: a test that would fail if the two evaluators disagreed on the same `(check text, reported map)` — e.g. a shared table both are run against.\n\n## Constraints\n\n- `stella-plugin` is a near-leaf: `stella-protocol` is its only workspace dependency. Nothing may be added to it that pulls in another crate.\n- `judge` must stay synchronous, total and I/O-free — no `Result` may appear in its signature (`crates/stella-runtime/src/wrapper/verdict.rs` module doc, `doc:wrapper-socket` §4).\n- `Oracle::unmet` is `pub`; changing or removing it is a public API change to `stella-plugin`.\n\nFound while fixing #3510 (`fix(stella-runtime): an oracle check narrows done, it never widens it`). Refs #3510, #3380."[m[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"number"[m[1;37m:[m 3515[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"title"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"judge re-implements Oracle::unmet, so the check evaluator exists twice and only one copy decides anything"[m
  [1;37m}[m[1;37m,[m
  [1;37m{[m
    [1;34m"body"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"## Three consent defects found by the authority lens of the socket audit\n\nThey share a root: **install records nothing about what was consented to**, and the consent text describes fewer powers than the plugin actually receives.\n\n---\n\n### 1. Self-escalation — a plugin rewrites its own grant\n\nA user installs `vera` as `participation = \"observer\"`, no hooks, `env = []`, and consents to that text.\n\n`vera`'s process — which runs as the user and owns its install directory — writes `~/.stella/plugins/vera/plugin.toml` with `participation = \"arbiter\"`, `hooks = [\"PreToolUse\",\"Stop\"]`, `env = [\"GITHUB_TOKEN\"]`.\n\nNext run, `read_tier` (`crates/stella-cli/src/plugin_cmd/roster.rs:263-273`) parses the new file and `hook_routes()` emits the new routes. `LoopGrant::permits_hook` — \"the authoritative filter\" — faithfully authorises grants **no human ever saw**. `install` refusing to overwrite an existing directory does not help, because nothing re-enters `install`.\n\n**Fix:** write a **consent receipt** at install (digest of the consented `plugin.toml`, scope, timestamp); `read_tier` refuses — with a notice pointing at re-consent — any plugin whose manifest no longer matches its receipt. This also fixes the deeper problem that \"installed\" currently means nothing more than \"a directory with a `plugin.toml` exists\" (see #3509).\n\n---\n\n### 2. The consent prompt never shows the model tier\n\n`consent_text` renders **no `[roles]` information at all**. A manifest with `[roles.triage] tier = \"premium\"` renders one line — *\"spends model calls on these stages as child turns: triage, verify\"* — and the word `premium` never appears.\n\nMeanwhile `WrapperError::UndeclaredRole` justifies its refusal with *\"the roles a human consented to at install are the roles a wrapper may spend\"* — citing a consent document that **does not contain them**. The user is never shown which tier their money goes to.\n\n**Fix:** render each `[roles.<name>]` and its `tier` in `loop_say`, so the enforcement cites text the user actually read.\n\n---\n\n### 3. The consent prompt never discloses what data leaves the process\n\nThe document enumerates *powers* (grade, hooks, argv, env, capabilities) but never the *data* the plugin's process receives. The wire contract ships, on every turn:\n\n- `goal` — the user's prompt (`crates/stella-plugin/src/wire.rs:188`)\n- `turn.answer` — the model's full reply (`wire.rs:360`)\n- `turn.tools`, `turn.changed_files` (`wire.rs:375`)\n\nas JSON on a third party's stdin. A plugin with no `[[capabilities]]` renders *\"It asks for no tool capabilities.\"* — which reads as harmless.\n\n**A prompt-exfiltrating plugin is indistinguishable from a benign one at the consent prompt.** For a BYOK, no-telemetry product whose central promise is that prompts never leave the machine, this is the disclosure that matters most.\n\n**Fix:** a fixed disclosure block in `consent_text` for any manifest carrying `[wrapper]` or `[runtime]`, naming exactly what crosses to the child at each granted point — **derived from the wire types** so it cannot drift as fields are added.\n\n---\n\n## Definition of done\n\n- Witness: a plugin whose manifest changed after install is refused until re-consented.\n- Witness: `consent_text` for a manifest with `[roles]` contains the tier; for one with `[wrapper]`, contains the data-disclosure block.\n- Both fail today.\n\nFound by the blocking three-lens socket audit (authority lens). Related: #3509, #3512. Refs #3380."[m[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"number"[m[1;37m:[m 3514[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"title"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"A plugin can widen its own grant by rewriting its manifest, and the consent prompt hides both the model tier and the data that leaves the process"[m
  [1;37m}[m[1;37m,[m
