    [1;34m"body"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"## Problem\n\n`judge` credits `FlipObservation::Achieved` as **fact**. But `flip` is a field the *plugin* fills in on its `after_turn` response, and `EvidenceSet` carries **no provenance** — nothing distinguishes \"the host observed this\" from \"the plugin said so\".\n\n## Repro\n\nA plugin's `after_turn` returns, having executed nothing at all:\n\n```json\n{\"point\":\"after_turn\",\"body\":{\"protocol_version\":1,\n \"evidence\":{\"flip\":\"achieved\",\"tamper\":\"clean\",\"measurements\":{}}}}\n```\n\n`flip_credit` (`crates/stella-runtime/src/wrapper/verdict.rs:173-177`) takes the `Achieved` arm. `tamper_credit` credits `Clean`. `judge` returns `Verdict::Met`. `again` returns `Stop { Outcome::Met }`.\n\n**Done is granted with no test executed anywhere.**\n\n`crates/stella-runtime/tests/wrapper_socket.rs:200-206` is this exact flow — `wrapper.after_turn(...).evidence` fed straight into `judge`. So it is the **reference shape**, not a misuse.\n\n## Why this is the keystone finding\n\nThe definition-of-done oracle has moved from host-executed to **plugin-asserted**. In `stella-pipeline` the flip oracle runs the witness itself; through the socket, the plugin reports the outcome and the host believes it.\n\n`doc:pipeline-as-plugins` §6's whole argument is that a plugin must not be able to grade its own work, and that keeping `judge` pure makes that structural. Purity makes *\"judge calls no model\"* structural. It does nothing about the trustworthiness of the inputs — and the inputs are what decide.\n\nThis is **broader than #3511**. That issue is about `measurements`; this is about the **flip**, which is the core of the witness contract. Fixing #3511 alone leaves a plugin able to claim a flip it never earned.\n\nIt is also **distinct from #3499**: that is about `TamperPolicy` being mandatory while snapshotting is host-side. This is that `EvidenceSet` has no way to express the difference between observed and reported, so **no later policy can even be written against it**.\n\n## Fix\n\nSplit the evidence type **by provenance**, before implementations bind to it:\n\n- `ObservedEvidence` — constructed by the **host** (flip, tamper). A plugin cannot write it.\n- `ReportedEvidence` — returned by the **plugin** (measurements, and at most a *claimed* flip).\n\n`judge` takes both, and credits a *claimed* flip only under a policy that explicitly admits self-report.\n\nAdding a provenance field after plugins ship is a wire break, which is why this is BLOCK rather than FILE.\n\nNote: in-flight work for #3499 already introduces `crates/stella-plugin/src/observed.rs` / `ObservedEvidence`. That is the right shape — this issue is the argument for making it cover the **flip**, not only tamper, and for making the plugin structurally unable to write the observed half.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nWitness: a plugin returning `flip: \"achieved\"` having run nothing is **not** credited. Fails today, because the reference test does exactly that and passes.\n\nFound by the blocking three-lens socket audit (authority lens). Related: #3511, #3499, #3510. Refs #3380."[m[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"number"[m[1;37m:[m 3513[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"title"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"The flip itself is plugin-asserted: a plugin returning flip=achieved having run nothing is credited done"[m
  [1;37m}[m[1;37m,[m
  [1;37m{[m
    [1;34m"body"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"## Problem\n\nA manifest declaring `[runtime] env = [\"ANTHROPIC_API_KEY\"]` is accepted by `Runtime::validate` — it is a well-formed name — and `Runtime::child_env` returns the **resolved pair**. Its own doc comment concedes the gap: *\"Refusing a credential the manifest asked for is a further, host-side narrowing this crate cannot make.\"*\n\nThe narrowing exists in exactly one place: `crates/stella-cli/src/plugin_cmd/process.rs::prepare_command`. **And the socket structurally cannot use it.**\n\n## Why the refusal cannot reach the socket\n\n- `prepare_command` builds a **`std::process::Command`** (sync).\n- The socket's only transport builds its own **`tokio::process::Command`** from `Vec<(String, String)>` (`crates/stella-runtime/src/wrapper/subprocess.rs:89`, `:135`).\n\nThe two types cannot meet. `PreparedCommand.command` is `#[allow(dead_code)]`, waiting for a spawner that cannot consume it.\n\nSo any driver that is not `stella-cli` — including `stella-serve` and the embedded `stella-engine` host that `doc:wrapper-socket` §6 makes an **acceptance criterion** — reaches `SubprocessWrapper::new(runtime.argv, runtime.child_env(std::env::var…), …)` and hands the plugin the key that pays for the agent.\n\nInvariant 3 is therefore CLI policy, not a property of the socket.\n\n## Aggravating detail\n\n`stella-runtime` already depends on `stella-tools`, so `is_sensitive_env_name` was in reach and was not applied.\n\n## Fix\n\nMove the refusal to the boundary **every** host must cross:\n\n- filter inside `SubprocessWrapper::new`, returning the refused names as part of the constructor's answer so the caller is forced to surface them (a silent filter is its own bug — the user must learn why their plugin did not get what it asked for); **or**\n- make `Runtime::child_env` take a host-supplied `is_credential` predicate it cannot skip.\n\nLeaving it in one binary's builder means the second driver re-implements it or forgets it, and forgetting is silent.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nWitness: a manifest naming a credential env var is refused by the **socket**, not by the CLI — asserted through `SubprocessWrapper` directly, with no `stella-cli` in the test. Fails before the change, because today the pair is passed through.\n\nSecond assertion worth having: the refusal is reported, not silently dropped.\n\nFound by the blocking three-lens socket audit (correctness lens). Refs #3380, and the §6 three-driver criterion."[m[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"number"[m[1;37m:[m 3512[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"title"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"SECURITY: a plugin can name ANTHROPIC_API_KEY in [runtime].env and any non-CLI host hands it over"[m
  [1;37m}[m[1;37m,[m
  [1;37m{[m
    [1;34m"body"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"## Problem\n\n`crates/stella-runtime/src/wrapper/verdict.rs:124` — `judge` treats a declared `[[oracle.checks]]` entry as a **replacement** for the flip policy rather than a **conjunct**. For any requirement a check names, the flip requirement and the tamper exclusion are both silently void.\n\n## Repro\n\n```toml\n[requirements]\nproven = \"the change is proven by a witness\"\n\n[oracle]\nflip = \"required\"\ntamper = \"artifact-identity\"\nmeasurements = [\"p50\"]\n\n[[oracle.checks]]\nrequirement = \"proven\"\ncheck = \"p50 <= 105\"\n```\n\nNothing rejects this manifest — `Oracle::validate_evidence` (`crates/stella-plugin/src/evidence.rs:288-296`) only enforces the `NotApplicable` arm.\n\nFeed evidence with `flip: NotAchieved` and `tamper: Tampered { artifact: \"tests/witness.rs\" }` plus a passing `p50`, and `judge` returns **`Met`**.\n\n## Why this is the most serious possible defect here\n\nThis is the exact failure the entire project exists to prevent, and `doc:pipeline-as-plugins` §6 names it: *\"A verification plugin that quietly calls a model to decide 'done' is the worker grading its own work, and a passing verdict looks identical either way.\"* This is the same shape without even needing a model — a plugin declares one trivial check, rewrites the witness, never flips red→green, and is credited done.\n\n`judge` being synchronous and I/O-free is not sufficient if a declared check can widen what \"done\" means. Purity guarantees no *model call*; it does not guarantee the *oracle* is still binding.\n\n## Fix\n\nMake flip/tamper a **conjunct, never a fallback**: under `FlipPolicy::Required`, evaluate `flip_credit` for every requirement and AND it with the check results, so a check can only ever **narrow** done, never widen it.\n\nAlternatively reject at load a manifest that declares `flip = \"required\"` alongside a check on the same requirement — but the conjunct is better, because it keeps both expressible and makes the safe composition the only composition.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nWitness: the manifest above, with `flip: NotAchieved` and/or `tamper: Tampered`, yields **not-Met** — and a property test asserting that adding an `[[oracle.checks]]` entry can only ever remove requirements from the Met set, never add one. That property is the durable statement of the invariant.\n\nFound by the blocking three-lens socket audit (architecture lens), `doc:pipeline-as-plugins-execution` §1. Introduced by 4e6a51d (A3). Refs #3380."[m[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"number"[m[1;37m:[m 3510[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"title"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"judge treats an [[oracle.checks]] entry as a replacement for the flip policy, so a tampered run with no flip is credited Met"[m
  [1;37m}[m[1;37m,[m
  [1;37m{[m
    [1;34m"body"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"## Problem\n\n`PluginRoster::load` (`crates/stella-cli/src/plugin_cmd/roster.rs:185-189`) reads the project tier `<workspace>/.stella/plugins/` **unconditionally**, bypassing `settings::project_code_execution_trusted()` — the gate that already stands in front of project-scope hooks and `.stella/mcp.toml` for exactly this reason.\n\n## Repro\n\n`git clone` a repository containing `.stella/plugins/x/plugin.toml`:\n\n```toml\n[loop]\nparticipation = \"arbiter\"\nhooks = [\"PreToolUse\", \"Stop\"]\n\n[runtime]\nargv = [\"python3\", \"${plugin_dir}/main.py\"]\n```\n\n`PluginRoster::load` reads it with no trust check. `hook_routes()` then emits five dispatchable routes, and the `[runtime]` process is spawned. **There is no consent transaction anywhere on this path.**\n\nThe README's own promise is the thing being broken: *\"project-scope hooks and credential-routing fields don't load from a cloned repo until you set `STELLA_TRUST_PROJECT=1`. That's Stella refusing to let a repo you just cloned run arbitrary commands on your machine.\"* A plugin is strictly more powerful than a hook, and it is the one surface that skips the gate.\n\n## Fix\n\nGate the project tier on `crate::settings::project_code_execution_trusted()` in `PluginRoster::load`, emitting the same one-line refusal notice `load_mcp_plan` does, and honour `filesystem_settings_disabled()`.\n\nThe user tier (`~/.stella/plugins/`) is not affected — that is the operator's own machine-scope directory, same as their own hooks.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nWitness: a plugin in an untrusted project tier is **not loaded, not listed, and dispatches no hook**, and the same plugin loads once the workspace is trusted. Fails before the change, because today it loads unconditionally.\n\nFound by the blocking three-lens socket audit (architecture lens), `doc:pipeline-as-plugins-execution` §1. Introduced by a8adddd (A4). Refs #3380."[m[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"number"[m[1;37m:[m 3509[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"title"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"SECURITY: a cloned repo's .stella/plugins/ loads with no trust gate — arbitrary code execution on clone"[m
  [1;37m}[m[1;37m,[m
  [1;37m{[m
    [1;34m"body"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"## Context\n\nTrack C (macanderson/stella-examples#1) built one plugin three times — Rust, Python, TypeScript — against the real manifest grammar. **The language axis did not leak**: no language needed a manifest shape another did not, which is the result `doc:pipeline-as-plugins` §9 rule 1 was designed to test, and it held.\n\nThese are the gaps it found on the *other* axis — things no language can say. Grouped because they are all small and all in `crates/stella-plugin/src/manifest.rs`.\n\n### 1. `[oracle].command` is mandatory, so one plugin declares two processes\n\nA plugin whose evidence comes from `after_turn` must still name an oracle argv. In the three reference plugins the oracle argv is a byte-for-byte copy of the runtime argv — the same program, declared twice.\n\nThis is the sole reason rule 1's manifest diff is **four lines instead of two**, and `plugins/ci/check-manifests-identical.py` had to grow a special case asserting \"the two argvs must name the same program\". A grammar that forces a redundant declaration teaches every plugin author a redundant concept.\n\n**Fix:** make `[oracle].command` optional when `[runtime]` is present and the oracle is the same process, or make the relationship explicit rather than duplicated.\n\n### 2. A manifest cannot say which socket points a plugin implements\n\n`verify` answers `after_turn` and refuses `before_turn`. `[loop].hooks` names **hook events**, not wrapper points, so there is nowhere to declare it. A host discovers the answer **by getting a refusal at runtime** — which is exactly the \"manifest that quietly does nothing\" failure this crate's module doc says it exists to prevent, one level up.\n\n**Fix:** a declared point set, load-checked against the participation grade, so an undeclared point is never dispatched — the same shape `LoopGrant::permits_hook` already gives hooks.\n\n### 3. `tamper` has one value and it is mandatory\n\nA plugin that does no tamper checking cannot say so. Tracked in more depth in #3499, which is about the same field being undecidable; noted here because the *mandatory* half is a grammar question and may be fixed in the same change.\n\n### 4. The manifest filename is unspecified\n\nThe three examples use `plugin.toml`. Nothing in the crate or the spec says a loader must look for that name, so A4's loader may contradict every published example. **Fix:** specify it, in `docs/spec/pipeline-as-plugins.md` §A4 and in the loader, before the loader ships to anyone.\n\n## Files\n\n- `crates/stella-plugin/src/manifest.rs` — `Oracle`, `OracleCommand`, `LoopGrant`, `PluginManifest`, and the cross-field validation\n- `crates/stella-cli/src/plugin_cmd/` — the loader, for item 4\n- `macanderson/stella-examples` `plugins/ci/check-manifests-identical.py` — delete its two-argv special case when item 1 lands\n\n## Definition of done\n\nItems 1, 2 and 4 fixed; the three example manifests then differ in **exactly two lines** (one `[runtime].argv` each) and `check-manifests-identical.py` needs no special case. That is a sharper statement of rule 1 than the current four-line result.\n\nRefs #3380. Found by macanderson/stella-examples#1. Related: #3499."[m[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"number"[m[1;37m:[m 3501[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"title"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"Manifest expressiveness gaps found by the three-language proof: a mandatory second process declaration, undeclarable socket points, and an unspecified filename"[m
  [1;37m}[m[1;37m,[m
  [1;37m{[m
    [1;34m"body"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"## Problem\n\n`crates/stella-plugin/src/wire.rs:108` and `:138` declare:\n\n```rust\n#[serde(tag = \"point\", content = \"body\", rename_all = \"snake_case\")]\n```\n\nAn adjacently-tagged serde enum **ignores any extra key beside `point`/`body`**. So an unknown field in the request envelope is silently dropped by the host's own decoder.\n\nThis contradicts the rule `stella-plugin` is otherwise built on, stated in its own module doc and enforced by `deny_unknown_fields` on every manifest table: *\"An unknown key is a load error, never ignored... A manifest that quietly does nothing is worse than one that refuses to load.\"*\n\nThe wire contract should hold the same line, and for a stronger reason: the manifest is authored by a human who can be told, while the wire is spoken by a program that will not notice.\n\n## How it was found, and the uncomfortable part\n\nTrack C (macanderson/stella-examples#1). All three reference plugins **refuse** an unknown envelope field — verified by round-tripping the vectors through the real type. That makes third-party example plugins **stricter than the host's own decoder**, which is exactly backwards: the host is the one that must refuse, because it is the one a malicious or buggy plugin talks to.\n\n## Files\n\n- `crates/stella-plugin/src/wire.rs` — `WrapperRequest` (`:108`), `WrapperResponse` (`:138`)\n- `crates/stella-plugin/tests/wire_contract.rs` — where the witness belongs\n- `macanderson/stella-examples` `plugins/testdata/09-unknown-envelope-field.request.json` — a ready-made vector\n\n## Definition of done\n\nThe host refuses an unknown envelope key with a typed error, the same way an unknown manifest key is a load error. Witness: `09-unknown-envelope-field` decodes as a refusal through the real `WrapperRequest`, failing before the change because serde accepts it today.\n\nNote serde does not support `deny_unknown_fields` on adjacently-tagged enums directly — an explicit `Deserialize` impl, or an envelope struct wrapping a tagged body, is likely required. Whichever is chosen, keep the wire contract additive-only and regenerate `docs/wire/` if the shape lands there.\n\nRefs #3380. Found by macanderson/stella-examples#1."[m[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"number"[m[1;37m:[m 3500[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"title"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"WrapperRequest is adjacently tagged, so the wire envelope silently ignores unknown fields — the one rule the manifest never breaks"[m
  [1;37m}[m[1;37m,[m
  [1;37m{[m
    [1;34m"body"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"## Problem\n\nThree decisions compose into a contract no honest plugin can satisfy:\n\n1. `[oracle].tamper` has exactly one value, `artifact-identity`, and it is **mandatory** (`crates/stella-plugin/src/manifest.rs`, `TamperPolicy`).\n2. Tamper snapshotting is **host-side** by design — `doc:pipeline-as-plugins` §4 A10 says so explicitly, and `TamperPolicy::ArtifactIdentity` already assumes it.\n3. But `EvidenceSet.tamper` is a field the **plugin** fills in (`crates/stella-plugin/src/wire.rs`).\n\nSo a plugin must declare `tamper = \"artifact-identity\"`, cannot perform the check (the host does), and can therefore only ever report `tamper: not-checked`. The verdict is **undecided forever**.\n\nFound by Track C (macanderson/stella-examples#1), where all three reference plugins hit it identically — so it is a contract defect, not an implementation mistake.\n\n## One of the two halves has to move\n\nEither:\n- **The host fills `tamper` in.** The plugin reports evidence it actually observed; the host merges its own snapshot result before `judge` runs. This matches \"tamper snapshotting stays host-side\" and is the smaller change. It means `EvidenceSet` has a host-owned region a plugin cannot write — which should be structural, not a rule.\n- **Or the plugin genuinely performs it**, which contradicts A10 and hands a plugin the artifact identity surface. Not recommended.\n\nRelated: `tamper` having one mandatory value also means **a plugin that does no tamper checking cannot say so.** If the first option is taken, consider whether the field should be optional, or gain a variant meaning \"the host decides\".\n\n## Files\n\n- `crates/stella-plugin/src/manifest.rs` — `TamperPolicy`, and the validation making it mandatory\n- `crates/stella-plugin/src/wire.rs` — `EvidenceSet.tamper`\n- `docs/spec/pipeline-as-plugins.md` §4 A10, §8 (Vera's tamper exclusion)\n- `docs/spec/wrapper-socket.md` §4 (`judge` over declared rule + evidence)\n\n## Definition of done\n\nA plugin declaring the oracle contract can reach a **decided** verdict on a real run. Witness: a wire vector where the flip is achieved and tampering did not occur yields a satisfied requirement rather than `undecided`. Today that test cannot be written.\n\nRefs #3380. Found by macanderson/stella-examples#1."[m[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"number"[m[1;37m:[m 3499[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"title"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"The tamper contract is undecidable as specified: the policy is mandatory, the field is plugin-filled, and snapshotting is host-side"[m
  [1;37m}[m[1;37m,[m
  [1;37m{[m
    [1;34m"body"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"## Problem\n\n`doc:wrapper-socket` §6 states the acceptance criterion: \"the candidate worktree crosses as a **serializable handle**, not as a port\", and \"no borrowed trait objects in the request or response types — an out-of-process plugin cannot be handed a `&dyn` anything.\"\n\nTrack C (macanderson/stella-examples#1) built three real plugins against the socket and found that criterion is not met in practice:\n\n- `AfterTurnRequest` carries `candidate: Option<CandidateHandle>` and `CandidateOp` declares `run_test`, **but the exchange is one-shot**: there is no callback channel, and no root path anywhere in the request.\n- The only implementation, `CandidateHandles` in `crates/stella-pipeline`, is an **in-process async Rust API over `&dyn CandidateWorkspacePort`** — precisely the shape §6 says cannot cross.\n\nSo a plugin holding a `CandidateHandle` can do nothing with it.\n\n## What the examples had to do instead\n\nAll three reference plugins take the test command and the pre-turn baseline from two `[runtime].env` names (`VERIFY_TEST_COMMAND`, `VERIFY_BASELINE_EXIT_CODE`). That is default-deny and visible at install consent, so it is not a security hole — but it **bends the socket's own rule that every capability arrives in the request**. With either unset the plugins report `unobservable` rather than guessing, which is the right failure direction but is not the contract.\n\n## Why this matters\n\nThis is the failure mode `doc:pipeline-as-plugins` §0 exists to prevent, arriving on schedule: every plugin works, and the socket has quietly grown a dependency on the in-process host. It was found by Track C, which is what Track C is for.\n\n## Files\n\n- `crates/stella-plugin/src/wire.rs` — `AfterTurnRequest`, `CandidateHandle`, `CandidateOp`\n- `crates/stella-pipeline/src/ports/workspace.rs` — `CandidateWorkspacePort`, `CandidateWorkspace` (19 methods, borrowed trait objects)\n- `crates/stella-pipeline/src/ports/` — `CandidateHandles`, the in-process implementation\n- `docs/spec/wrapper-socket.md` §6 — the criterion this violates\n\n## Definition of done\n\nEither:\n1. The request carries enough for a plugin to act — at minimum the candidate root path plus the test invocation — with the **host fencing** filesystem access (a handle is a capability the host resolves and bounds, never a path the plugin is trusted to stay inside; a traversal attempt must be refused host-side, with a test); **or**\n2. There is a real callback channel so `CandidateOp::run_test` can be invoked mid-exchange, which makes the transport bidirectional and should be weighed against `doc:plugin-transport-spike`'s decision before being chosen.\n\nOption 1 is the smaller change and matches §6's stated design. Option 2 reopens the transport decision and should not be done casually.\n\nWhichever lands, delete the `VERIFY_TEST_COMMAND` / `VERIFY_BASELINE_EXIT_CODE` env workaround from the three example plugins in macanderson/stella-examples#1 and update `plugins/README.md`.\n\n## Verify\n\n`macanderson/stella-examples` `plugins/ci/conformance.py` must still pass 10/10 for all three languages with the env names removed.\n\nRefs #3380. Found by macanderson/stella-examples#1."[m[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"number"[m[1;37m:[m 3498[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"title"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"The candidate worktree handle cannot cross a process, so an out-of-process plugin gets its test command out of band"[m
  [1;37m}[m[1;37m,[m
