    [1;34m"body"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"## Problem\n\n`crates/stella-runtime/tests/wrapper_socket.rs` — the only end-to-end proof\nthat the wrapper socket's wire path works — opens with `#[cfg(unix)]`, because\nevery plugin in it is a `/bin/sh` script. On Windows the file compiles to\nnothing and the whole socket is untested, including the parts that have nothing\nto do with shell scripts: the stdin/stdout exchange, the concurrent\nwrite/wait, `kill_on_drop` at the timeout, and `env_clear()`.\n\n`SubprocessWrapper` itself (`crates/stella-runtime/src/wrapper/subprocess.rs`)\nis written in portable `tokio::process` and is *expected* to work there. That\nexpectation is exactly the kind of claim this repo does not let stand without\nevidence.\n\n## Why it is not simply \"add a .cmd fixture\"\n\nThe plugin has to be something the host can start with an argv and that reads\nJSON on stdin. Candidates, in the order worth trying:\n\n1. **A tiny Rust helper binary** declared as a `[[bin]]` or an example in\n   `stella-runtime`, and located via `env!(\"CARGO_BIN_EXE_<name>\")`. Portable,\n   no interpreter assumption, and it doubles as the reference in-tree plugin a\n   docs page can point at. Note it must not become a *privileged* Rust path —\n   it speaks the same wire contract a Python plugin would\n   (`doc:pipeline-as-plugins` §5 commitment 2).\n2. A `python3` script gated on the interpreter being present — rejected as the\n   primary route because a skipped test is a green test.\n\n## Definition of done\n\n- The socket's end-to-end tests (participation, verdict, timeout, non-zero\n  exit, unreadable answer, point mismatch, protocol version, environment\n  default-deny) run on every platform CI builds for.\n- `#[cfg(unix)]` is gone, or survives only on the specific cases that are\n  genuinely shell-shaped, each with a comment saying why.\n- The Windows path is *run*, not argued.\n\nRefs #3380.\n"[m[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"number"[m[1;37m:[m 3497[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"title"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"the wrapper subprocess transport is unproven on Windows: its whole test file is #![cfg(unix)]"[m
  [1;37m}[m[1;37m,[m
  [1;37m{[m
    [1;34m"body"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"## Problem\n\n`BeforeTurnResponse::role` (added by #3380) is a role **intent** the host\nresolves against the user's BYOK providers, and\n`stella_runtime::wrapper::admissible` refuses an intent the manifest does not\ndeclare in `[roles]` — the roles a human consented to at install are the roles\na wrapper may spend.\n\nBut `[roles]` currently requires `[subloop]`:\n\n```rust\n// crates/stella-plugin/src/manifest.rs, validate()\nif let Some(roles) = &self.roles {\n    if self.subloop.is_none() {\n        return Err(ManifestError::RolesRequireSubloop);\n    }\n```\n\nwith the stated reason \"a role is only resolved for a subloop stage\". That was\ntrue when `[subloop]` was the only thing that could spend a model call. It is\nno longer: a `[wrapper]` naming a role intent at `before_turn` is a second\nconsumer, and today such a manifest has to declare a `[subloop]` it does not\nuse to get past load. `crates/stella-runtime/tests/wrapper_socket.rs`'s\nreference manifest does exactly that (`[subloop] stages = [\"triage\"]`), which\nis a fixture carrying a workaround.\n\n## Definition of done\n\n- `[roles]` is admissible when **either** `[subloop]` or `[wrapper]` is\n  declared, and still refused when neither is (the rule is \"a role must have\n  something that could resolve it\", not \"a role requires a subloop\").\n- `ManifestError::RolesRequireSubloop` is renamed/re-worded to say what it now\n  enforces; it is a public variant, so the rename is a compatible-direction\n  change to make in one PR.\n- A witness test: a `[wrapper]` + `[roles]` manifest with no `[subloop]` loads,\n  and a manifest with `[roles]` alone still does not.\n- The workaround `[subloop]` comes out of\n  `crates/stella-runtime/tests/wrapper_socket.rs`'s `MANIFEST`.\n\n## Constraints\n\n- `crates/stella-plugin/src/manifest.rs` is not a god file, but the validation\n  ordering is load-bearing and pinned by\n  `the_first_repeat_in_declaration_order_is_the_one_reported` — keep the pass\n  structure.\n- Widening a load rule is the compatible direction (accepting more later is\n  additive; refusing more later breaks shipped manifests), which is why this is\n  a widening and not a second table.\n\nRefs #3380.\n"[m[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"number"[m[1;37m:[m 3496[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"title"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"plugin: a wrapper cannot name a role intent without declaring an unrelated [subloop]"[m
  [1;37m}[m[1;37m,[m
  [1;37m{[m
    [1;34m"body"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"## Problem\n\n#3380 A3a+A3b landed the wrapper socket — the wire contract in\n`crates/stella-plugin/src/wire.rs` and the trait plus host functions in\n`crates/stella-runtime/src/wrapper/` — but **nothing in the shipping binary\ncalls it.** The only code that drives a whole turn through the socket is\n`crates/stella-runtime/tests/wrapper_socket.rs`, which plays the host itself.\n\nThis is scaffolding with a tracking issue rather than scaffolding with none\n(AGENTS.md § \"Nothing left behind\"), but until it is wired, `stella run` still\ngets its stage order from the hardcoded branches in\n`crates/stella-pipeline/src/pipeline.rs` and an installed wrapper plugin\nparticipates in nothing.\n\n## What exists to build on\n\n- `stella_runtime::wrapper::TurnWrapper` — the two async points.\n- `stella_runtime::wrapper::{SubprocessWrapper, InProcessWrapper}` — the two\n  transports. `SubprocessWrapper::new(argv, env, timeout)` is the one a loader\n  builds from a manifest's `[runtime]` block (`Runtime::child_env` returns\n  exactly the `env` pairs).\n- `stella_runtime::wrapper::{judge, again, admissible}` — the host's own\n  decisions, already total and I/O-free.\n- `stella_plugin::Wrapper::resolve` → `StageProgram` — which stages run.\n\n## Definition of done\n\n1. A host sequence (the natural home is `stella-cli`, since `stella-pipeline`\n   is the first plugin rather than the host) that, for each installed wrapper:\n   resolves the stage program, calls `before_turn` per stage, applies the\n   contributions as **volatile** messages (`VolatileContext::into_message` —\n   invariant 7), runs the turn, calls `after_turn`, then `judge` and `again`.\n2. `admissible` is called on every `before_turn` response before its\n   contribution is applied — an undeclared role intent or a mistyped published\n   signal must be refused, not logged.\n3. A `WrapperError` from either point is handled as an abstention:\n   `EvidenceSet::unobserved()` into `judge`, never silence read as success.\n4. A witness test that a wrapper plugin installed on disk changes a real run's\n   behaviour, not a test that plays host.\n\n## Constraints\n\n- The engine never learns plugins exist (`stella-core` must not depend on\n  `stella-plugin`); the host binds the grants.\n- `doc:wrapper-socket` §6's acceptance test — the same plugin under `stella-cli`,\n  `stella-serve` and an embedded host — is not satisfied by this issue alone,\n  but the host sequence should be written so it can be.\n\nRefs #3380.\n"[m[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"number"[m[1;37m:[m 3494[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"title"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"wrapper socket has no host caller: nothing in stella-cli or stella-pipeline dispatches before_turn/after_turn/judge/again"[m
  [1;37m}[m[1;37m,[m
