    [1;34m"body"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"## Problem\n\nA8 (#3380 track A) grew `Signal` to cover the facts the pipeline actually\nbranches on, including ones published *after* triage:\n\n| signal | publisher | live branch it transcribes |\n|---|---|---|\n| `mutating-actions`, `diff-lines` | `execute` | `LadderInputs::nothing_was_attempted`, the diff-budget conjunct of the `SubmitFast` rung (`crates/stella-pipeline/src/verify.rs`) |\n| `witness-authored` | `witness` | `witness.is_some()` in `Pipeline::verify_candidate` |\n| `flip-achieved`, `tests-red`, `tests-green` | `verify` | `flip.is_achieved()`, `touched_tests_passed == Some(false)` / `Some(true)` in `ladder_decision` |\n\nThe load-time graph check (`Wrapper::validate`) makes sure a condition only\nreads a signal an **earlier** stage publishes, and `Wrapper::resolve` refuses\nto read one whose publisher was skipped (`SignalNotProduced`). Both rules are\n*structural*: they bound which reads are legal in the declared order.\n\nNeither rule makes the value **fresh**. `stella_plugin::SignalValues` is one\nsnapshot of every signal, and `Wrapper::resolve` is a single up-front pass over\nit (`crates/stella-plugin/src/program.rs`). So a host that resolves the whole\nprogram before the first stage runs — which is exactly what\n`stella_pipeline::variant::signals` does, at the triage boundary — answers a\n`diff-lines > 0` condition from a snapshot taken before anything executed. The\nanswer is not an error and not a refusal: it is silently \"nothing happened\".\n\nToday this cannot bite, and that is asserted rather than assumed:\n`crates/stella-pipeline/tests/variant_program.rs::the_shipped_variant_reads_only_facts_the_triage_boundary_has`\nfails if the shipped `classic` manifest ever reaches for a post-triage signal.\nA hand-written variant has no such guard.\n\n## Files\n\n- `crates/stella-plugin/src/program.rs` — `SignalValues`, `Wrapper::resolve`.\n- `crates/stella-plugin/src/wrapper.rs` — `Signal::publisher`, `Wrapper::validate`.\n- `crates/stella-pipeline/src/variant.rs` — `signals()`, whose doc comment\n  states the triage-boundary contract this issue exists to remove.\n- `crates/stella-pipeline/tests/variant_program.rs` — the guard above.\n\n## What \"done\" looks like\n\nResolution becomes progressive, so a condition is evaluated when its\npublisher's output exists rather than before the run starts. One shape (not the\nonly one) is a `StageProgram` that advances stage by stage, taking the\npublisher's signal values at each boundary; another is a resolver that takes a\nsignal *oracle* the host answers on demand. Whichever is chosen must keep the\ntwo properties the current design is built on:\n\n- **Totality by the compiler.** `SignalValues` derives no `Default` on purpose:\n  adding a signal must break every construction site rather than read `false`\n  at one of them. A `HashMap<Signal, _>` would give that up.\n- **Purity.** `stella-core` never learns plugins exist and `stella-plugin` does\n  no I/O; the host gathers facts and hands them in.\n\nVerify with: a manifest gating a stage on `diff-lines > 0` resolves to\n*different* programs for a turn that changed 40 lines and one that changed\nnone, driven through the host rather than through a hand-built `SignalValues`.\nThen delete the `the_shipped_variant_reads_only_facts_the_triage_boundary_has`\nguard and the \"What the later stages' signals read here\" section of\n`variant::signals`'s doc comment in the same PR.\n\n## Constraints already discovered\n\n- `stella-plugin` is a near-leaf: `stella-protocol` is its only workspace\n  dependency and must stay that way.\n- `crates/stella-pipeline/src/pipeline.rs` is a grandfathered god file at its\n  ceiling — new logic goes in `src/variant.rs` or another sibling module.\n- Binding a resolved program to the pipeline's branches at all is #3380's\n  interception points; this issue is only about the values a condition is\n  answered from.\n\nRefs #3480, #3408, #3381, #3380.\n"[m[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"number"[m[1;37m:[m 3491[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"title"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"wrapper resolution is one up-front snapshot, so a condition on an execute/witness/verify signal reads a value that stage has not produced"[m
  [1;37m}[m[1;37m,[m
  [1;37m{[m
    [1;34m"body"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"## Problem\n\nThe D-1 falsifier (`doc:pipeline-as-plugins` §6.1) added the evidence half of `[oracle]`: a plugin declares the numbers its oracle reports (`measurements`) and a rule per requirement (`[[oracle.checks]]`), and `Oracle::unmet` decides them purely (`crates/stella-plugin/src/evidence.rs`).\n\n**Nothing calls it.** Like `Wrapper::resolve` before `stella-pipeline`'s `variant` module, this is a complete, tested load-time-and-evaluation contract with no host on the other end: the four wrapper interception points are #3380 and structured verdicts are A6. So today a `flip = \"not-applicable\"` arbiter loads, renders its budgets at install consent, and holds nothing open, because no host runs its oracle, decodes its numbers, or turns an unmet check into a `done: false`.\n\nFiled per AGENTS.md § \"Nothing left behind\": unwired code with no tracking issue is the exact failure this rule exists to prevent.\n\n## What \"done\" looks like\n\nWhen the Stop-gate host lands (A3 socket + A6 structured verdicts):\n\n1. The host runs `[oracle] command` inside its subprocess budget and decodes the oracle's reported measurements into a `BTreeMap<String, u64>` — the wire shape for that report is **undecided and part of this issue**; it must be declared alongside A5's `[runtime]` block, not invented at the call site.\n2. It calls `Oracle::unmet(&reported)` and treats a non-empty answer as the unmet requirements of the hold, bounded by `LoopGrant::max_holds` (see #3487).\n3. `ManifestError::MeasurementNotReported` must surface as an **operational failure of the oracle**, never as a met budget — the crate refuses to read a missing number as zero and the host must not undo that.\n4. `UnmetCheck`'s `Display` (`\"<requirement>: <measurement> was N, budget <op> M\"`) is the attributable hold message; a hold that does not name the requirement and the budget is the unattributable hold `[requirements]` exists to prevent.\n\n## Files\n\n- `crates/stella-plugin/src/evidence.rs` — `Oracle::unmet`, `UnmetCheck`, `MeasurementRule`.\n- `crates/stella-plugin/tests/non_witness_done.rs`, `crates/stella-plugin/tests/fixtures/perf-budget.toml` — the reference plugin whose definition of done this must actually enforce.\n- `crates/stella-pipeline/src/variant.rs` — the precedent for a host consuming this crate's pure answers.\n\n## Constraints\n\n- `stella-plugin` stays a near-leaf: no I/O here. Running the process and decoding its output is the host's.\n- The plugin never grades its own work (#2584): the host evaluates the declared rule; the oracle only reports numbers.\n- Related: #3380 (socket), #3487 (`max_holds` wiring), #3488 (measurement units).\n\n## Verify\n\nAn integration test in the host crate where a fixture oracle reporting a blown budget produces a hold citing `hot-path-within-budget`, and the same oracle reporting a value inside budget lets the turn end.\n"[m[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"number"[m[1;37m:[m 3489[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"title"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"plugin: `Oracle::unmet` has no host caller — wire measurement checks into the Stop-gate hold when A6 lands"[m
  [1;37m}[m[1;37m,[m
  [1;37m{[m
    [1;34m"body"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"## Problem\n\n`[[oracle.checks]]` (added by the D-1 grammar falsifier, `doc:pipeline-as-plugins` §6.1) states a verdict rule as data:\n\n```toml\n[[oracle.checks]]\nrequirement = \"hot-path-within-budget\"\ncheck = \"agent-loop-step-p50-pct-of-baseline <= 105\"\n```\n\nThe literal is parsed as `u64` (`MeasurementRule::value`, `crates/stella-plugin/src/evidence.rs`), matching the wrapper condition grammar's `<count-signal> <op> <integer>`. Real measurements are not non-negative integers:\n\n- **Fractional.** A microbenchmark budget of \"no more than 0.5% slower\" is not expressible in percent. The reference fixture (`crates/stella-plugin/tests/fixtures/perf-budget.toml`) works around it by reporting *percent of baseline* — `100` unchanged, `105` five percent slower — which caps resolution at one percent.\n- **Signed.** \"p50 improved by 3%\" has no representation; the fixture's unit dodges this by putting improvement below `100`.\n\nEvery plugin author will hit this and each will invent their own unit (basis points, per-mille, microseconds). That is a silent interoperability tax on a grammar whose whole point is that a Python author and a Rust author write the identical artifact.\n\n## Why it was not just widened to `f64`\n\nDeliberate, not an oversight: a float in a completion gate brings `NaN` (which makes every comparison false, so a broken oracle would silently *pass* a `<=` budget), `-0.0`, and equality/rounding hazards into the one code path that decides whether a turn may end. The falsifier did not need it (see §6.1), and CLAUDE.md's rule is to widen only what the falsifier needed.\n\n## What \"done\" looks like\n\nPick **one** and implement it with load-time validation and tests:\n\n1. **A declared unit per measurement** — `measurements = [{ name = \"p50\", unit = \"basis-points\" }]` with a closed `Unit` enum, so the integer stays but its meaning is stated and renderable in `consent_text`.\n2. **A fixed-point literal** — accept `<= 105.5` and store scaled integers (value + scale), keeping the comparison integral and total.\n3. **Decide the status quo is correct** and say so in the grammar's docs plus a rejection error that names the workaround, so an author writing `<= 0.5` gets told what to do instead of \"not a non-negative whole number\".\n\nOption 3 is a legitimate outcome; what is not legitimate is leaving it undecided while plugins start shipping units.\n\n## Files\n\n- `crates/stella-plugin/src/evidence.rs` — `MeasurementRule`, `OracleCheck::rule`, `Oracle::unmet`, `Oracle::validate_evidence`.\n- `crates/stella-plugin/src/error.rs` — `UnparsableCheck` is the error an author currently sees.\n- `crates/stella-plugin/tests/fixtures/perf-budget.toml`, `crates/stella-plugin/tests/non_witness_done.rs` — the falsifier fixture and its tests; the fixture header documents the workaround and must be updated with whatever is chosen.\n- `docs/spec/pipeline-as-plugins.md` §6.1 records the finding.\n\n## Constraints\n\n- The grammar stays **closed**: a named unit or a fixed-point literal is a reviewable addition; an expression language is not.\n- Whatever is chosen must round-trip byte-for-byte through TOML and JSON (invariant 4) and must be visible in `consent_text` — a budget a user cannot read before installing is not meaningfully declared.\n- `stella-plugin` is a near-leaf: no I/O, no new workspace dependency.\n\n## Verify\n\n`cargo test -p stella-plugin`, plus a case proving the previously-inexpressible budget now loads and decides in both directions.\n"[m[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"number"[m[1;37m:[m 3488[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"title"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"plugin: an `[[oracle.checks]]` budget can only be a non-negative integer — decide the unit story before a real perf plugin ships"[m
  [1;37m}[m[1;37m,[m
  [1;37m{[m
    [1;34m"body"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"## Problem\n\n#3380 A7 made the `Stop`-hold bound real on the **engine** side:\n\n- `crates/stella-core/src/driver/config.rs` now carries\n  `EngineConfig::stop_hold_allowance: Option<u32>`, `DEFAULT_STOP_HOLDS` (3),\n  `STOP_HOLD_CEILING` (8), `clamp_stop_holds()` and `EngineConfig::stop_holds()`;\n- `driver/user_hooks.rs::stop_hook_feedback` spends that allowance instead of\n  the old hardcoded `MAX_STOP_CONSULTS = 3`.\n\n**Nothing sets the field.** `LoopGrant::max_holds`\n(`crates/stella-plugin/src/manifest.rs`) is still read only by load-time grade\nvalidation, so a verification plugin declaring `max_holds = 5` still gets 3\nrounds. `clamp_stop_holds` and `STOP_HOLD_CEILING` are public specifically so a\nhost can clamp and *report* the narrowing, and today they have no caller.\n\nThis is the deliberate half of the invariant-#1 split: `stella-core` must never\ndepend on `stella-plugin`, so the engine takes a plain number and the **host**\ndoes the manifest read.\n\n## What \"done\" looks like\n\nThe host that assembles an engine from a plugin manifest — `stella-cli`'s\nengine assembly (`crates/stella-cli/src/agent/engine.rs::tuned_engine_config`\nis where the other catalog-derived config values land), and/or\n`stella-runtime`'s `RuntimeSpec`/`RuntimeBuilder` — reads\n`LoopGrant::max_holds` off the loaded manifest, passes it through\n`stella_core::driver::clamp_stop_holds`, sets `stop_hold_allowance`, and emits a\none-line boot notice when the clamp narrowed the ask (the same posture a pinned\nreasoning effort against an `Unsupported` provider takes — never a silent drop,\nAGENTS.md invariant #8).\n\nWitness: a manifest declaring `max_holds = 5` produces an `EngineConfig` whose\n`stop_holds()` is 5, and one declaring `max_holds = 1000` produces 8 *and* the\nnotice.\n\n## Verify\n\n`cargo test -p stella-core --lib driver::tests::user_hooks::verdicts` already\ncovers the engine half (a host-supplied allowance buys the rounds; an ask above\nthe ceiling is clamped). This issue is the manifest → host → config leg.\n\n## Refs\n\nFollow-on from #3380 A7. Depends on the plugin loader slice of\n`doc:pipeline-as-plugins` §4 existing (a host that loads a manifest at all).\n"[m[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"number"[m[1;37m:[m 3487[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"title"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"Wire `LoopGrant::max_holds` to `EngineConfig::stop_hold_allowance` — the engine seam exists, no host sets it"[m
  [1;37m}[m[1;37m,[m
  [1;37m{[m
    [1;34m"body"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"## Problem\n\n`crates/stella-core/src/driver/user_hooks.rs::stop_hook_feedback` matches\n`HookDecision::RequireApproval` and answers it with a diagnostic\n(\"does not apply at a turn boundary; completion allowed\"), then lets the\ncompletion stand. `doc:pipeline-as-plugins` §4 A6 names the real use case this\nblocks: a paid verification plugin that must ask *\"verification budget\nexhausted, continue?\"* has no way to get an answer.\n\nThe blocker is structural, not an oversight (the comment at the site now says\nso). The engine's only path to a human is\n`crates/stella-core/src/hooks/decision.rs::ApprovalRoute`, whose request type is\n\n```rust\npub struct ApprovalRouteRequest {\n    pub tool: String,        // <- there is no tool at a turn boundary\n    pub read_only: bool,     // <- nor a read_only bit\n    pub reason: String,\n}\n```\n\nand whose production implementation\n(`crates/stella-tools/src/hook_bridge.rs::BrokerApprovalRoute` over the #2676\n`ApprovalBroker`) keys its emit-before-park / TTL / `approval.*` audit events on\nthat tool name. Asking through it today means inventing a fake tool, which\nwould put a lie in the audit trail.\n\n## What \"done\" looks like\n\n`ApprovalRouteRequest` grows a *subject* rather than a tool: something like\n`ApprovalSubject::Tool { name, read_only }` | `ApprovalSubject::TurnCompletion\n{ final_text_digest }` (exact shape is the design question), with\n\n- the broker and its audit events keyed off the subject, not a bare tool name;\n- every asking surface updated — `crates/stella-tools/src/hook_bridge.rs`,\n  `crates/stella-tools/src/registry/approval.rs`, `crates/stella-cli/src/approval.rs`,\n  the Command Deck's approval card (#2789), and `stella-serve`;\n- `stop_hook_feedback` parking on the route and branching: **approved** lets the\n  completion stand, **denied** holds the turn open with the refusal's reason as\n  the model's next observation (the same tail-message path a `deny` takes);\n- the no-route (headless) posture staying **fail-open** —\n  `user_hooks.rs` module docs §\"The Stop gate\" explain why failing closed at a\n  turn boundary *is* the compact→error→stop-hook→retry spiral, not safety;\n- a witness test: a Stop hook returning `require_approval` against a scripted\n  route resolves both ways and the turn ends differently in each.\n\n## Constraints already discovered\n\n- Invariant #1: `stella-core` must not reach `stella-tools`; the route stays a\n  port and the broker stays above it.\n- Invariant #4: `ApprovalRouteRequest`/`ApprovalRouteResolution` round-trip\n  through serde; the round-trip test in `hooks/decision.rs` must be extended,\n  not replaced.\n- `crates/stella-core/src/bus.rs` and `driver.rs` are grandfathered god files\n  sitting on their ceilings — new lines go in siblings.\n\n## Refs\n\nLeft behind deliberately by the #3380 A6/A7 slice\n(`feat(stella-core,stella-protocol): structured denials, and a plugin's hold\nallowance becomes real`), which sized this as larger than that change.\nRelated: #2676, #3288, #2789, #2793.\n"[m[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"number"[m[1;37m:[m 3486[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"title"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"A Stop hook's `require_approval` still dead-ends at a turn boundary — ApprovalRouteRequest is tool-shaped"[m
  [1;37m}[m[1;37m,[m
  [1;37m{[m
    [1;34m"body"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"## Problem\n\n#3380 A10 added `stella_pipeline::ports::handle::CandidateHandles`: the six-operation, serializable-handle view over `CandidateWorkspacePort`/`CandidateWorkspace` (create, root, run-test, seal, adopt, remove) plus the host-side path fence. It is fully tested but **nothing in the shipping path constructs one** — the pipeline still drives `CandidateWorkspacePort::create` directly (`crates/stella-pipeline/src/pipeline/fanout_stage.rs`, `witness_stage.rs`, `pipeline.rs`).\n\nThat is deliberate for the slice — the consumer is the wrapper socket's `after_turn` dispatch (A3, `doc:wrapper-socket` §5), which does not exist yet — but unwired code with no tracking issue is exactly what AGENTS.md § \"Nothing left behind\" forbids, so this is the tracking issue.\n\n## What \"done\" looks like\n\nThe socket's `after_turn` request carries a `stella_protocol::CandidateHandle`, and the host resolving it uses **the same** `CandidateHandles` table the fan-out registered its workspaces into — not a second table and not a second creation path. `CandidateHandles::register` exists for precisely this: a caller that already created workspaces hands them to the table instead of creating a parallel set.\n\nConcretely:\n\n1. The fan-out constructs one `CandidateHandles` over the port it already borrows and `register`s each workspace it creates, keeping the `Box<dyn CandidateWorkspace>` reachable both ways.\n2. The A3 dispatch resolves plugin-supplied handles through it, and every plugin-supplied path through `CandidateHandles::resolve_path`.\n3. `crates/stella-pipeline/src/pipeline.rs` is a grandfathered god file **at its ceiling** — the wiring cannot add lines there; it goes in `pipeline/fanout_stage.rs` or a sibling.\n\n## Verify\n\nA test that a handle registered by the fan-out serves `root`/`run_test` for the same workspace the pipeline is executing in, alongside the existing `crates/stella-pipeline/src/ports/handle/tests.rs`.\n\nRefs #3380.\n"[m[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"number"[m[1;37m:[m 3485[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"title"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"pipeline: CandidateHandles has no production caller — wire the fan-out's workspaces through the handle table"[m
  [1;37m}[m[1;37m,[m
  [1;37m{[m
    [1;34m"body"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"## Problem\n\n`CandidateHandles::register` (`crates/stella-pipeline/src/ports/handle.rs`, #3380 A10) mints handles as `candidate-1`, `candidate-2`, … — a monotonic ordinal, deliberately deterministic so a run stays replayable. `stella_protocol::CandidateHandle` documents this: it is a **name**, not a bearer token.\n\nThe consequence is that a caller holding one handle can spell every other handle the same host minted. In a best-of-N fan-out that means an out-of-process plugin given a handle to candidate 2 can `adopt` candidate 1 — deliver a losing candidate's tree to the user — or `remove` it. Today nothing dispatches handles to a plugin at all (see the A3 socket work), so this is a hole being closed before it is opened, not a live exploit.\n\n## What \"done\" looks like\n\nA handle resolves only for the principal it was issued to. Concretely, one of:\n\n- the table keys on `(principal, handle)` and every op takes the principal from the dispatch context, or\n- the id carries an unguessable random component and stays out of the replay stream (which trades determinism — decide that explicitly rather than by accident).\n\nThe first is preferred: it keeps the value deterministic and puts the authority where #3380 A1 (plugin identity) already has to put it.\n\n## Files\n\n- `crates/stella-pipeline/src/ports/handle.rs` — `CandidateHandles`, `register`, `lookup`.\n- `crates/stella-protocol/src/candidate.rs` — `CandidateHandle`'s doc comment states the current property; update it in the same PR.\n- Tests: `crates/stella-pipeline/src/ports/handle/tests.rs::a_forged_or_retired_handle_resolves_to_nothing` is the shape to extend — a handle minted for principal A must be `UnknownHandle` for principal B.\n\nBlocked on A1 landing a principal to key against. Refs #3380.\n"[m[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"number"[m[1;37m:[m 3484[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"title"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"pipeline/plugin: a candidate handle is a guessable ordinal — bind it to the principal it was issued to"[m
  [1;37m}[m[1;37m,[m
  [1;37m{[m
    [1;34m"body"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"## Problem\n\n`CandidateHandles::resolve_path` (`crates/stella-pipeline/src/ports/handle.rs`, added for #3380 A10) fences a caller-supplied relative path against a candidate workspace root in two layers: a pure lexical refusal (`fence_lexical` — absolute, `..`, NUL, backslash) and an on-disk containment check (`fence_on_disk` — canonicalise the deepest existing ancestor, require it under the canonical root).\n\nThe answer it returns is a `PathBuf`, and it is only true **at the instant of the check**. Anything that runs between the check and the use — including the plugin that asked, which by construction has the root path from `CandidateOp::Root` and can write inside it — can replace a component with a symlink and turn an accepted path into an escape. Classic TOCTOU.\n\nThis is stated in the module docs rather than hidden, and it is not a regression: nothing in the tree resolved plugin-supplied paths at all before A10. But it is the residual hole in what is otherwise the security half of that slice.\n\n## What \"done\" looks like\n\nThe fence hands back something that cannot be re-pointed after the check — an `openat(O_NOFOLLOW)`-style resolution walked component by component from a directory file descriptor pinned at the root, with the *descriptor* (not the path) handed to the use site. That is a substrate change, not a caller change: `CandidateWorkspace` would need to grow an \"open inside me\" capability rather than exposing a root string, and the Windows story (`FILE_FLAG_OPEN_REPARSE_POINT`) needs a decision or a declared gap.\n\nConstraints already discovered:\n\n- `stella-pipeline` may do filesystem I/O (precedent: `src/replay/golden.rs`) — invariant 2 is about `stella-core`, not this crate. So the resolution may live here, but the *descriptor* has to reach whatever spawns the process.\n- Do not add a second copy of the containment logic: `fence`/`fence_lexical`/`fence_on_disk` are the one implementation and the lexical layer stays valuable regardless (it refuses malformed input without a syscall).\n- Tests to keep passing / extend: `crates/stella-pipeline/src/ports/handle/tests.rs` — `a_symlink_out_of_the_candidate_root_is_refused` and `the_lexical_fence_refuses_what_cannot_name_an_inside_path`. A witness for this issue is a test that swaps a component between resolve and use and still gets refused.\n\n## Verify\n\n`cargo test -p stella-pipeline --lib ports::handle`\n\nRefs #3380.\n"[m[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"number"[m[1;37m:[m 3483[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"title"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"pipeline: the candidate-handle path fence is TOCTOU — resolve once and hold, not check-then-use"[m
  [1;37m}[m[1;37m,[m
  [1;37m{[m
    [1;34m"body"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"## Problem\n\n`doc:pipeline-as-plugins` §4 A1 shipped the **vocabulary** for plugin authority and its consent rendering, deliberately stopping short of the loader (§A4). The residue is two unwired seams, both of which are safe today only because no loader exists:\n\n1. **`stella_core::ports::Principal::Plugin(String)`** (`crates/stella-core/src/ports/authz.rs`) is constructed nowhere in the workspace. Every other variant has a real construction site (`crates/stella-cli/src/subsession.rs:732`, `crates/stella-cli/src/fleet_cmd.rs:729`, `crates/stella-cli/src/candidate_ws.rs:498`, `crates/stella-serve/src/routes.rs:118`); this one does not, because the thing that would construct it — a plugin loader — is §A4.\n\n2. **A declared capability is shown but not enforced.** `Capability` (`crates/stella-plugin/src/consent.rs`) carries `tool`, `risk`, `purpose` and `scope`, and `consent_text` renders all four. Nothing turns an accepted consent into an `AuthzGate` rule. `consent_text` is honest about the half of this that a user could otherwise be misled by — it labels `scope` as *the plugin's claim* and states that the gate enforces the tool and the grade, not the claim — but \"the gate enforces the tool and the grade\" is currently true only in the sense that no plugin can call anything at all.\n\n## Why it matters\n\nThe whole point of `Principal::Plugin` is that a gate can refuse an installed plugin independently of its operator. Until a loader constructs it and installs a gate keyed on it, an installed plugin would run as whatever principal the host already passes — which for `stella-cli` today is the constant `Principal::User` (`crates/stella-cli/src/agent.rs:362`, `:1657`, `:1672`). That is precisely the marketplace defect §A1 exists to prevent, and it will be introduced silently by the first loader that forgets.\n\n## Files involved\n\n- `crates/stella-core/src/ports/authz.rs` — `Principal::Plugin`, `AuthzGate`, `RiskCeiling`.\n- `crates/stella-plugin/src/consent.rs` — `Capability`, `highest_risk`, `consent_text`, `validate_capabilities`.\n- `crates/stella-plugin/src/manifest.rs` — `PluginManifest::capabilities`.\n- `crates/stella-plugin/tests/install_consent.rs`, `crates/stella-plugin/tests/fixtures/self-driving.toml` — the widest grant, written out.\n- `crates/stella-cli/src/agent/tool_stack.rs` — where a session's principal and gate are bound today.\n- `crates/stella-home/` — `.stella/plugins/` and `~/.stella/plugins/` resolvers (do not exist yet).\n\n## Definition of done\n\n- A loader constructs `Principal::Plugin(manifest.name)` for calls originating from an installed plugin, and a test proves a plugin-originated call is *not* attributed to `Principal::User`.\n- Accepting the consent prompt produces an `AuthzGate` whose rules are derived from the accepted `[[capabilities]]` list: a tool absent from the list is denied, and a tool graded above what was accepted is denied. Both directions witnessed.\n- The `scope` field either (a) becomes gate-matched against the call's `input` — `AuthzGate::check` already receives it — with the consent wording changed to say so, or (b) stays a declared claim and the wording stays as it is. Decide explicitly; do not let the wording and the enforcement drift apart.\n- Uninstall actually removes the grant. Hook settings currently *concatenate* across scopes and no scope can remove another's entries (`crates/stella-cli/src/settings/merge.rs:47`, `:171-172`) — correct for operator hooks, wrong for plugins (§A4).\n\n## Constraints already discovered\n\n- `stella-core` must never depend on `stella-plugin`, and `stella-plugin` must never depend on `stella-core` (both crate READMEs, #3245 open question 3). The loader is the only place both are in scope. `consent_text` deliberately does not spell the string `plugin:` for this reason — `Principal::label()` owns it.\n- `stella-plugin` is pure: no I/O, no environment. Reading a manifest off disk and prompting belong to the host.\n- Invariant #9 (single-purpose tools) governs any new CLI verb: `stella plugin install` / `list` / `remove` are three tools, not one with a mode flag.\n\nRefs #3380, #1400.\n"[m[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"number"[m[1;37m:[m 3482[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"title"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"Nothing constructs Principal::Plugin or binds a declared [[capabilities]] entry to an AuthzGate rule"[m
  [1;37m}[m[1;37m,[m
  [1;37m{[m
    [1;34m"body"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"## Problem\n\nTwo files now carry the same stage order under the same variant id `classic`:\n\n- `crates/stella-plugin/tests/fixtures/wrapper-staged-v1.toml` — a **test\n  fixture** added by #3381, asserted against by\n  `crates/stella-plugin/tests/wrapper_stages.rs::todays_stage_order_loads_as_a_manifest`.\n- `crates/stella-pipeline/variants/classic.toml` — the **shipped** manifest\n  added by the #3408 reader slice, embedded with `include_str!` from\n  `crates/stella-pipeline/src/variant.rs` and asserted against by\n  `crates/stella-pipeline/tests/variant_program.rs`.\n\nThey are two copies of one claim (\"this is the order the pipeline runs\"), in\ntwo crates, with no guard tying them together. The next edit to either — a new\nstage, a changed condition — leaves the other stale and green. This is the\nshared-cell drift shape AGENTS.md keeps warning about, in a new place.\n\nThe duplication was accepted deliberately in the #3408 slice rather than fixed\nthere: `stella-plugin` must not depend on `stella-pipeline`, and reaching\nacross crates with `include_str!(\"../../stella-pipeline/variants/classic.toml\")`\nwould be a hidden path coupling that no gate checks and that breaks silently on\na move.\n\n## Options (pick one, argue for it)\n\n1. **Repoint the fixture at a distinct shape.** The fixture's job in\n   `wrapper_stages.rs` is to prove the load rules against a realistic manifest,\n   not to be the canonical order — rename it and let it drift on purpose, so\n   there is exactly one copy of \"today's order\" in the tree\n   (`variants/classic.toml`). Cheapest, and removes the shared cell entirely.\n2. **Make the pipeline's copy the only one** and have `wrapper_stages.rs`'s\n   order assertion move to `crates/stella-pipeline/tests/variant_program.rs`,\n   which already makes it against `replay.rs`'s `stage_transition_legal`.\n3. **A gate step** comparing the two files. Least attractive: it is a guard\n   invented to protect a duplication that has no reason to exist.\n\n## Verify\n\n`cargo test -p stella-plugin -p stella-pipeline`, and confirm that changing the\nstage list in one file fails at least one test.\n\nRefs #3408, #3381.\n"[m[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"number"[m[1;37m:[m 3481[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"title"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"stella-plugin: `tests/fixtures/wrapper-staged-v1.toml` now duplicates the shipped `crates/stella-pipeline/variants/classic.toml`"[m
  [1;37m}[m[1;37m,[m
  [1;37m{[m
    [1;34m"body"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"## Problem\n\n`crates/stella-pipeline/variants/classic.toml` (added in the #3408 reader slice)\ndeclares the stage **order** the staged pipeline runs today, and the ceremony\nconditions the closed condition grammar can express (`questions > 0`, `plans`,\n`no-test-command`, `verifies`). It does **not** reproduce three of today's\ngates, because each is a conjunction of facts the manifest has no name for:\n\n1. **The conversational fast path is an early `return`, not a skipped stage.**\n   `crates/stella-pipeline/src/pipeline.rs` (`if assessment.conversational {\n   return self.run_conversational(...) }`) leaves the pipeline after triage.\n   A stage list can skip a stage; it cannot say \"stop here\". Resolving\n   `classic` with `conversational: true` today still yields the full program.\n2. **`witness` is gated on more than `no-test-command`.** The real branch is\n   `!conversational && config.test_command.is_none() &&\n   responsibility_enabled(ModelCallRole::WitnessAuthor) &&\n   assessment.wants_witness() && class.verifies_unconditionally() &&\n   can_author_independent_witness()`.\n3. **`verify` is gated on more than `verifies`.** `TaskClass::SimpleLookup`\n   still verifies when the zero-diff guard trips (it \"unexpectedly touched\n   files\"), which the manifest's `verifies` signal does not carry.\n\nThe manifest's own header comment says all three out loud, so this is a\ndeclared gap rather than a silence — but it is a gap, and it is the thing that\nstops the resolved program from being able to *drive* the run.\n\n## Why not just widen the `if` grammar\n\n`doc:turn-loop-wrappers` §9.4 is explicit: the grammar is a small closed set of\nnamed predicates, because a Turing-complete condition in a manifest is a second\nprogram with no gate on it. Adding `&&` is the wrong fix. The documented escape\nhatch (`crates/stella-plugin/src/wrapper.rs`, `Condition`'s doc comment) is a\n**new named signal the host publishes** — a reviewable addition, one at a time.\n\n## What \"done\" looks like\n\n- New `Signal` variants for the facts above — likely `authors-witness` (the\n  host's already-computed conjunction) and `verifies-this-turn`, plus whatever\n  shape the conversational early return needs (it may need a stage-list\n  concept, e.g. a terminal stage, rather than a signal — decide and say which).\n- Each new variant needs: a `Signal::kind()`, a `Signal::publisher()`, a field\n  in `stella_plugin::SignalValues` (adding one is deliberately a compile error\n  at every construction site — that is the totality guarantee, not an\n  inconvenience to route around), and a value in\n  `stella_pipeline::variant::signals`.\n- `crates/stella-pipeline/variants/classic.toml` updated to use them, and its\n  header's \"what this does not express\" section shortened accordingly.\n- `crates/stella-pipeline/tests/variant_program.rs` gains a case per new gate,\n  from both sides.\n\n## Constraints already discovered\n\n- `stella-plugin` is a near-leaf: `stella-protocol` is its only workspace\n  dependency and must stay that way. The signals' *inputs* cross the boundary\n  (the host fills `SignalValues`), never the dependency.\n- `stella-core` must never depend on `stella-plugin` (invariant 1).\n- `crates/stella-pipeline/src/pipeline.rs` is a grandfathered god file **at**\n  its ceiling in `scripts/file-size-baseline.txt`; new logic goes in\n  `src/variant.rs` or another sibling module.\n- The final binding of a resolved program to the pipeline's branches is #3408 /\n  #3380 (the four wrapper interception points), not this issue. This one is\n  only about the manifest being able to *say* what the branches do.\n\nRefs #3408, #3381, #3380.\n"[m[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"number"[m[1;37m:[m 3480[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"title"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"wrapper manifest: the shipped `classic` variant cannot express three of today's stage gates — publish the missing host signals"[m
  [1;37m}[m[1;37m,[m
  [1;37m{[m
    [1;34m"body"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"## Problem\n\nThe `/models` dialog's role table is an **extension point**, but its vocabulary\nis closed in code. `EngineRole` (`crates/stella-tui/src/envelope.rs:993`) is a\nsix-variant enum — `Default`, `Worker`, `Verifier`, `Triage`, `Research`,\n`Plan` — with a hand-written `ALL` that `EngineConfigState::agents` is\ndocumented to mirror exactly (\"Exactly one entry per `EngineRole::ALL` slot, in\nthat order\", `envelope.rs:1138`).\n\nUnder the plugin architecture that is backwards. Core Stella is a bare loop:\nminimal tools, **one model end to end**. Of those six roles, exactly one —\n`Default` — belongs to core. The rest are policy that is leaving:\n\n| Role | Future owner |\n|---|---|\n| `Default` | core |\n| `Worker`, `Verifier` | **Vera** (the verification plugin) |\n| `Triage`, `Research`, `Plan` | a future `stella-plan` plugin |\n\nSo the table must **stay** — it is where a plugin advertises the model it will\nsend — but its rows must come from *registered plugins*, not from a compile-time\nenum. Today installing a plugin that wants its own model row is a code change to\n`stella-tui`, which defeats the point of a CLI-installable plugin marketplace.\n\n## Relationship to #2456\n\n#2456 asked the same underlying question for the pipeline's roster and was\nclosed NOT_PLANNED on 2026-08-10, three days before the Vera directive. Its\nanalysis is still the best survey of the cost and is worth reading, but its plan\ntargets the old architecture (roster inside `stella-pipeline`, resolution\nthrough a closed `stella_protocol::Role`). Do not reopen it — the requirement\nhas inverted. It was optional then; plugin-contributed roles make it mandatory\nnow.\n\nThe cost estimate from #2456 is the part to carry forward: `AgentEngineAgents`\n(`crates/stella-cli/src/settings.rs`) is a fixed struct with named `Option`\nfields and `EngineAgentKind` is a closed enum with a hand-written `ALL` —\n**235 references across 16 files in `crates/stella-cli/`** at the time it was\nmeasured. Re-measure before planning.\n\n## What to build\n\n1. **A role id is an open string with provenance**, not an enum variant. A row\n   knows which plugin declared it, so the dialog can group and attribute, and so\n   an uninstalled plugin's rows disappear rather than render dead.\n2. **Core declares exactly one role** (`default`). Every other row is\n   contributed.\n3. **Fail-closed resolution survives.** An unknown role id must still refuse and\n   list the ids that would have worked. #2456's warning is the reason: a typo'd\n   role name must never silently resolve to the worker grading its own work.\n4. **The dialog keeps rendering, not resolving.** `views/models_card.rs` is\n   explicit that every cell arrives pre-rendered on\n   `EngineConfigState::roles`, resolved driver-side. Keep that split; only the\n   *arity* of the list changes.\n\n## Ordering\n\nThis is downstream of the wrapper socket (#3380): a plugin cannot contribute a\nrole until a plugin can participate in the turn loop at all. The manifest half\n(#3381 / PR #3409, `crates/stella-plugin/src/wrapper.rs`) already parses a\n`[wrapper]` block and is the natural place for a role declaration to live, but\nit is currently inert — nothing binds a stage to the loop (#3408).\n\nSuggested sequence: #3380 → #3408 → this.\n\n## Definition of done\n\n- A plugin manifest declaring a role causes a row to appear in `/models`\n  attributed to that plugin, with no change to `stella-tui`.\n- Uninstalling that plugin removes the row.\n- An unknown role id refuses with an error naming the valid ids.\n- A configuration declaring no plugins produces a single-row table\n  (`default`) and a byte-identical engine setup to today's default path.\n- `make gate` green.\n\n## Constraints\n\n- `crates/stella-tui/src/views/engine.rs` and `src/views/session.rs` are\n  grandfathered god files closed to growth — new logic lands in sibling\n  submodules.\n- The deck has golden frame tests\n  (`crates/stella-tui/tests/deck_render_snapshots.rs`,\n  snapshots under `tests/snapshots/deck/`). Regenerate with\n  `BLESS=1 cargo test -p stella-tui --test deck_render_snapshots` and **read the\n  diff** — a table that loses rows should be visible in it.\n\nRefs #3380, #3408, #3246, #2456\n"[m[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"number"[m[1;37m:[m 3472[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"title"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"The /models role table must be plugin-populated: EngineRole is closed at six, core owns only one"[m
  [1;37m}[m[1;37m,[m
