    [1;34m"body"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"## Problem\n\n`read_file` is a catalog built-in again. The witness-author mount in `crates/stella-cli/src/candidate_ws/witness_tools.rs` **also** serves a tool called `read_file` — its own, different implementation, with its own payload ceiling and a credential denylist (it refuses `.env.local`, `.git/config`, and friends) that the catalog tool does not carry.\n\nSo one name now denotes two different tools depending on which executor answers. The test `the_out_of_catalog_surface_is_exactly_the_declared_exemption` was written to force exactly this decision — its doc says \"an exempt name joining the catalog fails here too, so the exemption is retired instead of going stale\" — and it fired.\n\nIt was resolved by **declaring** the collision (a new `SHADOWED` list beside `EXEMPT`) rather than deleting the exemption, because deleting it would assert a sameness that is false. That records the situation; it does not resolve it.\n\n## Why it matters\n\n`crates/stella-tools/src/contracts.rs` resolves trust **by name**: `contract_for` gives any name in `catalog::ALL_NAMES` a reviewed `Provenance::Builtin` contract. Its own module doc argues this is sound only because a name cannot be squatted (`custom::RESERVED_NAMES` is aliased to `ALL_NAMES`, MCP tools are namespaced).\n\nThe witness mount is not squatting through either of those doors — it is a separate `ToolExecutor` that never enters a `ToolRegistry`, so `contract_for` is not consulted on its dispatch path today. The hazard is therefore **latent, not live**: it becomes real the moment anything routes that mount's schemas through contract resolution, at which point a tool with a credential denylist and a different cap silently inherits the catalog `read_file`'s reviewed grade.\n\n## Files\n\n- `crates/stella-cli/src/candidate_ws/witness_tools.rs` — the mount; `read_file` at :76, schema at :345, the `SHADOWED` declaration in the test at the bottom\n- `crates/stella-tools/src/contracts.rs` — the by-name trust boundary and its soundness argument\n- `crates/stella-tools/src/read.rs` — the catalog tool\n\n## Options\n\n1. **Rename the mount's tool** (e.g. `witness_read_file`) and move it back to `EXEMPT`. Cheapest, removes the collision entirely; costs one prompt-visible name change for the witness author.\n2. **Have the mount serve the real `stella_tools::read::ReadFile`**, wrapped in whatever extra denial it needs. Removes the second implementation; requires checking that the catalog tool's caps and the mount's denylist compose.\n3. **Keep the collision** and make `contract_for` refuse to grade a schema that did not come from the registry, so the by-name rule cannot be reached from a foreign mount.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nOne option chosen and implemented, the `SHADOWED` list removed (or the test rewritten to assert whatever invariant replaces it), and `contracts.rs`'s soundness argument updated to state the new situation. Related: #3237 (retired built-in names are no longer reserved against custom-tool shadowing) is the same by-name-trust family and should be read alongside.\n"[m[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"number"[m[1;37m:[m 3469[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"title"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"The witness mount serves its own read_file under a now-catalog name — reconcile the collision before by-name trust reaches it"[m
  [1;37m}[m[1;37m,[m
