    [1;34m"body"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"## Problem\n\n`bash` is registered again as a built-in, and it ships with **no per-command boundary at all**. This is stated honestly in the module header (`crates/stella-tools/src/bash.rs`), not hidden — but it is a gap, and this issue is where it is tracked.\n\nTwo mechanisms used to sit on this spawn and neither was restored:\n\n1. `bash/confine.rs` (882 lines at `54233fd68^`) — a literal **audit of the command text** before the spawn: an isolated candidate's shell was refused a command naming the graded tree it delivers into, and every shell was refused one that rewrites Stella's own git refs.\n2. `bash/contain.rs` (574 lines) — an **OS write ban** on the graded tree specifically (#2931), because a text audit cannot survive `chr(47)` and a bench worker proved it.\n\nThey were not restored because both were armed by exactly one caller — the best-of-N candidate-workspace registry, which used `RegistryOptions` and `Bash::confined_to(...)`. Neither exists today (`ToolRegistry::new(root)` is the whole constructor), so restoring them verbatim would have landed ~1,450 lines of code with no caller. That is the failure mode the \"nothing left behind\" rule exists to prevent, so the gap is filed rather than papered over.\n\n## Why it matters\n\nThe loss this originally fixed was concrete and expensive: on `log-summary-date-ranges`, a worker script hardcoded a path into the graded tree, the write succeeded, the run aborted before adoption, and the grader read the leaked wrong intermediate — a solved task scoring zero. On `build-cython-ext`, a worker concluded the graded tree was its own workspace under another name and ran `rm -rf /app/pyknotid`, failing the exact two grader tests.\n\nToday the only things bounding a `bash` call are the registry's `tool.call.requested` policy chain and the operator's `\"tools\": {\"bash\": \"off\"}` switch. Session isolation is whatever container the whole process runs in (`docs/spec/remote-sandboxes.md` §2).\n\n## Files\n\n- `crates/stella-tools/src/bash.rs` — the tool; its header states the current absence\n- `git show 54233fd68^:crates/stella-tools/src/bash/confine.rs`\n- `git show 54233fd68^:crates/stella-tools/src/bash/contain.rs`\n- Both had **zero non-test `crate::` dependencies** (std only), so they port cleanly; the work is deciding what arms them.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nEither:\n\n- (a) a caller exists that needs a confined shell (a candidate-workspace or graded-tree mount), and `confine`/`contain` are restored **with** that caller and its witness tests, including the two regression shapes named above; or\n- (b) a reasoned decision that per-command confinement is not the right layer — that the container is — recorded in `docs/spec/remote-sandboxes.md`, with `bash.rs`'s header updated to cite it.\n\nDo not restore the modules without a caller.\n"[m[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"number"[m[1;37m:[m 3468[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"title"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"bash ships with no per-command boundary: decide whether confine/contain come back, or that the container is the layer"[m
  [1;37m}[m[1;37m,[m
  [1;37m{[m
    [1;34m"body"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"## Problem\n\nPR #3464 updated all 24 crate READMEs for the plugin-era product state: one turn loop, wrappers are plugins, verification leaving the engine for an installable plugin (#3246, `doc:turn-loop-wrappers`), and the engine embeddable through the Rust ports or the HTTP surface (`doc:engine-embedding`).\n\nThe **root `README.md`** was deliberately left out, and it is now the only surface that still states the old product claim without qualification. It leads with the witness-test contract — \"a task is **done** only when a witness test proves it — verified done, not claimed done\" — as an unconditional property of Stella. Once #3381 inverts the flag (raw loop default, `--pipeline <variant>` opt-in) and #3246 moves the staged pipeline out of the workspace, a plain `stella run` does not verify its own work unless the user installed the plugin. The sentence becomes false for the default path.\n\nThis was not folded into #3464 for two reasons, both worth preserving:\n\n1. It is a **user-facing marketing/positioning claim**, not an internal architecture note. How to say \"verification is becoming opt-in\" to users — without making it read as a capability being taken away — is a maintainer's call, not a docs-cleanup call (CLAUDE.md: \"'now' vs 'right' is the maintainer's call\").\n2. The root README is mirrored in prose by `website/content/docs/**`, `docs/why-stella.md`, and the decks under `website/public/presentations/`. Changing one copy and not the others swaps one stale claim for an inconsistent set.\n\n## Files involved\n\n- `README.md` (repo root) — the witness-test claim in the opening section and the feature table.\n- `docs/why-stella.md` — currently cited by nothing (`make doc-report` lists it as unadopted), so it drifts silently.\n- `website/content/docs/**` — the published docs; `description:` frontmatter also feeds `llms.txt`.\n- `website/public/presentations/turn-loop/` — the deck #3377 landed, which `doc:turn-loop-wrappers` was written from.\n- For reference on what the crates now say: `crates/stella-pipeline/README.md` § \"Direction — this crate becomes a plugin\" and `crates/stella-core/README.md` § \"Direction — one loop, zero built-in wrappers\" (both from #3464).\n\n## Constraints already discovered\n\n- The claim is not *wrong today*: the pipeline is still the default `stella run` path and `--no-pipeline` still opts out (`crates/stella-cli/src/cli.rs:596`). So this is a \"state it as a trajectory\" edit, not a correction — and it must not overclaim in the other direction either.\n- `doc:turn-loop-wrappers` §9.6 gates the default flip on a side-by-side bench on the same task panel, **reported even when the raw loop is worse**, because every published Stella benchmark number was produced with the pipeline as the default. Any user-facing wording should not pre-announce an outcome that bench has not returned.\n- `check-brand-case` covers `website/content/docs/**` prose: the wordmark is lowercase in prose, and a genuine identifier is exempted by writing it as an inline code span.\n- `docs/**` and `*.md` are ignored by `ci.yml` and covered by `docs-guards.yml` instead; `website/` changes additionally hit `deck-fit.yml` if a deck slide moves.\n\n## Definition of done\n\n- The root `README.md` states the verification contract accurately for both paths: what the raw loop guarantees (it runs the turn and reports what it changed) and what the verification wrapper adds when installed — with the witness contract presented as the wrapper's contract rather than the engine's.\n- The same framing reaches `docs/why-stella.md` and the published docs, so the copies agree.\n- The embedding story is stated once, in the user's words: Stella is usable as the AI engine inside another application, in process through the Rust ports or over HTTP via `stella-serve`.\n- No claim about the raw loop's *relative quality* until the §9.6 bench has run.\n- `make doc-links brand-case` green; `docs-guards.yml` green.\n\n## Related\n\n- #3246 — plugin platform sequencing (the extraction this documents).\n- #3380 — the wrapper contract.\n- #3381 — the manifest, the variant column, and the `--pipeline` flag inversion.\n- #3464 — the crate-README half of this change.\n"[m[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"number"[m[1;37m:[m 3465[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"title"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"docs: the root README and website still claim unconditional witness-verified done — restate it as the wrapper's contract"[m
  [1;37m}[m[1;37m,[m
