    [1;34m"body"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"## Problem\n\nRust doc comments on the `AgentEvent` payload types are exported **verbatim** into the generated public TypeScript wire contract, `docs/wire/agentevent.d.ts`. Nothing checks what that prose contains, so a Rust-only construct in a doc comment silently becomes part of a consumer-facing artifact.\n\nThe concrete hazard is intra-doc link targets. Writing the ordinary rustdoc form:\n\n```rust\n/// The artifact id, matching the `artifact_id` its\n/// [`AgentEvent::MediaProgress`](super::AgentEvent::MediaProgress) events carried.\n```\n\nproduces this in the shipped `.d.ts`:\n\n```ts\n  /**\n   * The artifact id, matching the `artifact_id` its\n   * [`AgentEvent::MediaProgress`](super::AgentEvent::MediaProgress) events carried.\n   */\n  id: string;\n```\n\n`super::AgentEvent::MediaProgress` is meaningless to a TypeScript consumer and leaks the Rust module layout into the contract. Today `docs/wire/agentevent.d.ts` contains **zero** occurrences of `super::` (`rg -c \"super::\" docs/wire/agentevent.d.ts` → no matches), so the artifact is currently clean — this is about keeping it that way.\n\n`wire-schema` does not catch it. That guard compares the committed artifact against a freshly generated one, so a leaked path is a *matching* diff the moment someone runs `bash scripts/export-agentevent-schema.sh` and commits, which is exactly what the failure message instructs them to do:\n\n```\n  * You changed the wire format. Regenerate and commit the artifacts with the\n    change, so the diff is reviewable:\n        bash scripts/export-agentevent-schema.sh\n```\n\nA reviewer reading that diff sees a doc-comment change, which reads as noise, and the guard goes green.\n\n## Why this came up\n\nPR #3451. #3440 split the payload types out of `event.rs` into `event/payload.rs`; five `[`AgentEvent::X`]` intra-doc links stopped resolving because the type is not in scope in the child module, and `main` went red on `doc-warnings`.\n\nThe obvious fix — qualify each link as `(super::AgentEvent::X)` — is what produced the leak above. #3451 instead uses `#[cfg(doc)] use super::AgentEvent;`, which resolves the links while changing no rendered text and no artifact. But nothing stops the next person from reaching for the qualified form, and if they regenerate as instructed, nothing will tell them.\n\n## Suggested fix\n\nAdd a check to the wire-schema path (`scripts/check-wire-schema.sh` / whatever `make wire-schema` drives) that fails when the generated `.d.ts` contains a Rust path or Rust-only doc syntax. A minimal, high-signal version:\n\n- reject `super::`, `crate::`, and `Self::` anywhere in `docs/wire/agentevent.d.ts`\n- optionally reject a markdown link whose target matches `^[A-Za-z_]+::` generally, which covers `stella_protocol::…` too\n\nKeep it narrow enough not to fire on legitimate prose — the four patterns above do not currently appear in the file, so the guard starts green.\n\nPoint the failure message at the fix that works, so the next person does not rediscover it:\n\n> A Rust path reached the public TypeScript contract. Use `#[cfg(doc)] use super::X;` in the payload module and write the link bare (`[`X::Variant`]`) rather than qualifying it — see `crates/stella-protocol/src/event/payload.rs`.\n\n## Files\n\n- `docs/wire/agentevent.d.ts` — the generated artifact\n- `scripts/export-agentevent-schema.sh` — the generator\n- `crates/stella-protocol/src/event/payload.rs` — the doc comments that feed it, and the `#[cfg(doc)]` precedent\n- whichever script `make wire-schema` invokes — where the new check belongs\n\n## Verify\n\n1. On `main` with #3451 merged, the new guard passes: `make wire-schema` exits 0.\n2. Temporarily rewrite one link in `payload.rs` to `[`AgentEvent::MediaProgress`](super::AgentEvent::MediaProgress)`, run `bash scripts/export-agentevent-schema.sh`, and commit the regenerated artifact. The guard must now **fail** — this is the witness, and it fails on the old code (no guard) and passes on the new.\n3. Revert.\n\n## Definition of done\n\n- A regenerated `agentevent.d.ts` containing a Rust module path fails `make wire-schema` with a message naming the `#[cfg(doc)]` alternative.\n- The guard is wired into `make wire-schema` so it runs in `make gate`, the pre-push hook's `guards` rung, and `.github/workflows/wire-schema.yml`.\n- A witness test as in \"Verify\" step 2 above.\n\n## Notes\n\n- Related but separate: `main` was red on `doc-warnings` at `b46eb1ac1` and #3440's own merge commit already carried the five unresolved links, so it landed red rather than breaking on composition. Three PRs merged within 80 seconds (04:43:37, 04:44:03, 04:44:56 on 2026-08-17), which is the auto-merge/`enforce_admins`-off path AGENTS.md describes. Not filed here — #3451 fixes the red; this issue is only about the missing guard.\n- A `doc-warnings` failure masks every gate step after it, which is how the `wire-schema` interaction stayed invisible until the rustdoc error was fixed.\n"[m[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"number"[m[1;37m:[m 3452[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"title"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"wire-schema: nothing stops a Rust module path leaking into docs/wire/agentevent.d.ts via a doc comment"[m
  [1;37m}[m[1;37m,[m
