    [1;34m"body"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"Found while fixing #3413.\n\n## What is true today\n\nOnly one driver emits the shared-tree `FileChange` events #3413 added: `agent::run_turn` (`crates/stella-cli/src/agent.rs`), via `crate::turn_files::emit_shared_tree_changes`. The other four turn drivers do not:\n\n- `crates/stella-cli/src/command_deck.rs:3891` — the deck's lead turn\n- `crates/stella-cli/src/subsession.rs:758`\n- `crates/stella-cli/src/fleet_cmd.rs:951`\n- `crates/stella-cli/src/agent/goal.rs:198` (goes through `run_turn`, so likely already covered — verify)\n\nSo the Files tab is populated for `stella run`/chat and still empty for a deck session, a fleet worker and a subsession.\n\nThis is the same shape as #2177: a projection wired at one driver and universal on the read side. `crates/stella-cli/src/durability.rs`'s `every_turn_boundary_in_the_crate_marks_the_turn_it_ended` is the existing fence for exactly this class of omission on the turn-mark side.\n\n## What to do\n\nAdd the `emit_shared_tree_changes(cfg, &tx)` call at each driver, immediately before the point where the turn's event channel closes — the events must land inside the turn they describe, so ordering is not optional.\n\nWatch the god-file ratchet: `crates/stella-cli/src/command_deck.rs` is grandfathered and closed to growth (currently at its 4010-line ceiling in `scripts/file-size-baseline.txt`), so its call has to be paid for by an extraction into a sibling module, the way #3413 paid for `agent.rs`'s by moving the JSON summary block into `agent/summary.rs`.\n\nBeware double-counting: `snapshot_worktree` is stateful (each call measures against the previous snapshot), so two drivers both calling it within one turn would split that turn's changes across two event bursts. Check whether any of these drivers nest.\n\n## Definition of done\n\n- Every turn driver in `stella-cli` emits shared-tree file changes.\n- A fence test in the shape of `every_turn_boundary_in_the_crate_marks_the_turn_it_ended` that fails when a driver ends a turn without emitting, so the next driver added cannot silently omit it.\n\nRefs #3413, #2177\n"[m[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"number"[m[1;37m:[m 3421[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"title"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"Shared-tree FileChange is wired at one turn driver only — the deck, fleet and subsession turns still report no file changes"[m
  [1;37m}[m[1;37m,[m
  [1;37m{[m
    [1;34m"body"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"Found while fixing #3413, out of scope there.\n\n## What is true today\n\n`WorkJournal::record` (`crates/stella-store/src/work_journal.rs`) — the method that commits the agent's *file content* into the session's durable history — has **no production caller**:\n\n```\nrg -n \"journal\\.record\\(\" crates/    # every hit is a test\ngit log --oneline -S\"journal.record(\" --all -- crates/\n```\n\nThe last production caller was deleted by the 12-tool purge (#3244, commit `e0ed0e16d`), which removed the file-CRUD tools and their read/write ledger. `record_checkpoint` still runs (resume frames), so the session ref exists — but its trees contain only reserved `.stella-journal/` blobs.\n\nThe consequence is that `WorkJournal::changed_paths_at_turn` filters every path out, so `crate::turn_diff::record_turn_diff` writes nothing, so **`session_turn_diffs` is empty in production** and the observatory's Sessions-view turn drill is permanently blank. That is precisely the failure #2177 fixed once already (\"nothing to distinguish 'this turn changed nothing' from 'this surface never records diffs at all'\") — it has regressed by a different route.\n\nNote `SessionDurability::mark_turn_end` (`crates/stella-cli/src/durability.rs:206`) returns early when `session_tip()` is `None`, so on a session with no checkpoint the turn is never even marked.\n\n## The fix is probably already sitting there\n\n#3413 added `WorkJournal::snapshot_worktree` (`crates/stella-store/src/work_journal/snapshot.rs`), which commits the whole work tree at each turn boundary on its own ref `refs/stella/<session>/tree`, with its own index, and returns measured per-path deltas. That is a real production producer of exactly the tree lineage `changed_paths_at_turn` wants — it is simply on a different ref.\n\nSo the likely repair is to point the per-turn diff projection at the snapshot lineage instead of the session head. Concretely, decide between:\n\n1. **Mark turns on the snapshot ref.** `mark_turn_end` marks `session_tip()`; have it mark the snapshot tip (or both), and teach `changed_paths_at_turn`/`read_at_turn` to resolve against that lineage.\n2. **Have `turn_diff` consume `snapshot_worktree`'s return value directly**, since it already carries paths, kinds and counts, and stop routing through the turn refs for this projection.\n\n(1) keeps `read_at_turn`'s content-replay working (the diff hunks need both sides' file content, which the snapshot lineage does have). (2) is smaller but loses the replay side. Read `crates/stella-cli/src/turn_diff.rs`'s module doc before choosing — it states the #1870 boundary ruling that the observatory must stay a pure artifact reader, which both options preserve.\n\n## Constraints\n\n- `turn_diff.rs`'s `MAX_DIFF_FILES` / `MAX_FILE_BYTES` caps and the \"no row for a turn that changed nothing\" rule must survive.\n- `crate::durability`'s `every_turn_boundary_in_the_crate_marks_the_turn_it_ended` is the fence that keeps every driver marking; do not break it.\n- Retention: `recorded_sessions` was widened in #3413 to read both `/head` and `/tree` tips. If turn marking moves, re-check `session_refs` and `prune` still reach everything.\n\n## Definition of done\n\n- A witness test: a turn that mutates a file on the engine-only path leaves a `session_turn_diffs` row with non-empty hunks; the test fails on `main`.\n- `WorkJournal::record` is either given a production caller or documented as dead and removed — an unwired method with no tracking issue is the thing AGENTS.md § \"Nothing left behind\" forbids, and this issue is that tracking.\n\nRefs #3413, #1870, #2177, #3244\n"[m[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"number"[m[1;37m:[m 3420[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"title"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"session_turn_diffs is empty in production: WorkJournal::record lost its only caller in the tool purge"[m
  [1;37m}[m[1;37m,[m
  [1;37m{[m
    [1;34m"body"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"## Context\n\n#3396 gave the usage hub (`~/.stella/usage.db`) its first migration ladder\n(`crates/stella-store/src/usage/schema.rs`, `HUB_MIGRATIONS`, stepped over\n`PRAGMA user_version`) and used it to back-propagate the door-only `kind`\nvocabulary (#3388) plus the `role` split (#3395). Two residues came out of\nbuilding it that are deliberately **not** in that PR.\n\n## 1. `tool_usage_rollup` is the one table that is not idempotent under re-sync\n\nThis is the reason #3396 rewrote values in place rather than rewinding the\nreplication cursor and re-replicating.\n\n`UsageStore::sync_execution` (`crates/stella-store/src/usage.rs`) is\n`INSERT OR REPLACE` on `execution_rollup`, so that table is idempotent — but it\nfolds the tool histogram into `tool_usage_rollup` **additively**, guarded only\nby an `already` probe on `execution_rollup`. So a re-sync of an execution whose\nrollup row was deleted, or a rewind via `rewind_telemetry_cursor`, double-counts\nthe tool-day buckets. `Store::sync_to_usage`'s own doc already warns that \"a\nbackfill must run against a fresh aggregate\", which is a documented landmine\nrather than a fixed one.\n\n**What to build:** make the tool-day fold idempotent per `(project_id,\nexecution_id)` — e.g. record which executions have been folded, or make the\nrollup derivable rather than accumulated — so \"re-sync\" stops being an operation\nthat can silently corrupt a count. Then `rewind_telemetry_cursor` becomes a safe\nrepair tool instead of a hazard, which is what a future hub backfill will want.\n\n**Verify:** a test that syncs one execution, rewinds the cursor, syncs it again,\nand asserts `tool_usage_rollup` is unchanged. It fails today.\n\n## 2. A hub is only corrected if it is opened by a build carrying the migration\n\n`schema::apply` runs on `UsageStore::open_*`, so the fix reaches a hub the next\ntime any command touches it. That is the right mechanism, but it means a hub\nbelonging to a machine that stops running Stella keeps the old vocabulary\nforever, and nothing reports that.\n\nThis is probably acceptable and is recorded so the next person does not assume\notherwise. If it is not acceptable, the options are a `stella usage doctor`\nthat reports the hub's `user_version`, or surfacing it in `stella usage report`.\n\n**Definition of done:** either a decision that lazy-on-open is sufficient\n(written down where the ladder is defined), or a command that can report and\nforce it.\n\n## Files\n\n- `crates/stella-store/src/usage/schema.rs` — the ladder and `apply`.\n- `crates/stella-store/src/usage.rs` — `sync_execution`, the additive\n  `tool_usage_rollup` fold, `rewind_telemetry_cursor`.\n- `crates/stella-cli/src/usage_cmd.rs` — `sync` / `--all`, which walk forward\n  from `telemetry_sync_cursors.last_source_rowid` and do **not** re-visit\n  already-replicated executions.\n\nRefs #3388, #3395, #3396.\n"[m[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"number"[m[1;37m:[m 3411[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"title"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"store: tool_usage_rollup double-counts on re-sync, so a hub backfill cannot use the cursor rewind"[m
  [1;37m}[m[1;37m,[m
  [1;37m{[m
    [1;34m"body"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"## Problem\n\nThe `[wrapper]` manifest block landed in `crates/stella-plugin/src/wrapper.rs`\n(#3381, manifest half). It parses, validates, load-checks its stage graph, and\nships two fixture variants — and **nothing reads it**. `stella-pipeline` still\nruns the stage order as hardcoded branches in `Pipeline::run`.\n\nUnwired code with no tracking issue is the failure mode AGENTS.md's \"nothing\nleft behind\" rule exists to prevent, so this is that issue.\n\n## Why it could not be done in the same PR\n\nBinding a stage name to the loop needs the four wrapper interception points\n(`before_turn` / `after_turn` / `judge` / `again?`) of **#3380**, which do not\nexist anywhere in the tree — verified: no `Wrapper`/`TurnWrapper` trait and no\n`before_turn`/`after_turn`/`judge` item exists in `stella-core`,\n`stella-runtime`, `stella-pipeline`, or `stella-plugin`; every hit is prose in\n`docs/spec/turn-loop-wrappers.md`. #3380 is itself blocked by #3379.\n\n## What to build\n\n1. **A host-side evaluator.** `Condition` is a pure two-shape grammar; the\n   host supplies the published signal values and asks whether each stage runs.\n   `CompareOp::apply` is already the whole count evaluator. The evaluator\n   itself must stay a pure function (invariant 2) — the *values* come from the\n   host.\n2. **`stella-pipeline` reads the manifest** instead of branching. Today's\n   order ships as `wrapper-staged-v1.toml`'s equivalent under the variant id\n   `classic` — which is already the id\n   `crates/stella-cli/src/agent/persistence.rs::PIPELINE_VARIANT_CLASSIC`\n   writes, so the store side needs no change to stay honest.\n3. **Publish the gating signals that are still missing.** `Signal` publishes\n   five facts today (`test-command`, `conversational`, `questions`, `plans`,\n   `verifies`), each transcribing a live branch. `Pipeline::run` also gates on\n   `responsibility_enabled(ModelCallRole::…)`, `assessment.wants_witness()`,\n   and `can_author_independent_witness()` (`pipeline.rs:1137-1142`). Each is\n   either a new published `Signal` or a documented reason it is host-internal\n   and not manifest-visible. **Do not** widen the grammar to reach them — a\n   new signal is a reviewable addition, a richer expression syntax is not\n   (`doc:turn-loop-wrappers` §9.4).\n\n## Constraints already discovered\n\n- `crates/stella-pipeline/src/pipeline.rs` is **2783 lines and exactly at its\n  ceiling** in `scripts/file-size-baseline.txt` — closed to growth. The\n  manifest reader lands in a sibling module, never in that file.\n- `stella-core` must never depend on `stella-plugin` (the leaf rule, #3245\n  open question 3). The host binds; the engine never learns plugins exist.\n- The variant id is written **only when that manifest was the thing that\n  ran**; a default path writes the default's id, never a blank (#2952's\n  sub-shape).\n- `stella-plugin` is a leaf with no workspace-crate dependencies. Keep it that\n  way — the evaluator's *inputs* cross the boundary, not its dependencies.\n\n## Verify / definition of done\n\n- `stella-pipeline` runs its stage order from a manifest; no Rust branch\n  decides which stages run.\n- Both fixture variants (`crates/stella-plugin/tests/fixtures/wrapper-*.toml`)\n  actually run, not merely load — #3381's DoD asks for \"both run from the same\n  code\", which the manifest half can only half-prove.\n- A `GROUP BY pipeline_variant` over `.stella/private/store.db` returns\n  per-variant cost and outcome for a real run.\n- A witness test: the same goal under two variants takes two different stage\n  paths, with the variant id on each execution row matching the manifest that\n  ran.\n\nBlocked by #3380 (and transitively #3379). Refs #3381, #3388, #3245, #2889.\n"[m[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"number"[m[1;37m:[m 3408[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"title"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"Wire the [wrapper] manifest into stella-pipeline: the stage list is parsed but nothing reads it"[m
  [1;37m}[m[1;37m,[m
