    [1;34m"body"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"## Problem\n\nTwo problems that share one fix.\n\n**Iterating on a pipeline design means recompiling a crate.** The conditional\nskipping the deck describes — \"a cheap task should not pay for ceremony it\ncannot use\" — is hardcoded branches inside\n`crates/stella-pipeline/src/pipeline.rs`. That file is on the god-file list in\n`scripts/file-size-baseline.txt` and is **closed to growth**, so the current\ndesign cannot absorb another variant even if we wanted one.\n\n**We cannot say which pipeline design is better.** Every turn already writes an\nexecution row tagged with the door it came in by — `run`, `chat`, `deck`,\n`deck-sub`, `goal`, `pipeline`. Nothing records *which shape of wrapper* ran. So\n\"is the cheaper stage order better?\" is answerable only by somebody who was\nthere, which is the exact lineage failure #2889 was filed against.\n\n## What to build\n\n### 1. Stages as a manifest\n\nA wrapper plugin declares an ordered stage list with conditions, in TOML:\n\n```toml\n[wrapper]\nid = \"staged-v1\"\n\n[[wrapper.stages]]\nname = \"triage\"\n\n[[wrapper.stages]]\nname = \"plan\"\nif   = \"questions > 0\"\n\n[[wrapper.stages]]\nname = \"execute\"\n\n[[wrapper.stages]]\nname = \"witness\"\nif   = \"no-test-command\"\n\n[[wrapper.stages]]\nname = \"verify\"\n```\n\nEach stage is an independent unit with a typed input and a typed output, so a\nstage is testable on its own. The conditions become a line you can read and\nchange instead of a branch buried in a 2700-line file.\n\nTwo manifest rules carried from #3245 slice A\n(`crates/stella-plugin/src/manifest.rs`) — do not re-derive them:\n\n- An unknown key is a **load error**, never ignored.\n- A condition naming a signal the host does not publish is **also a load\n  error**. A manifest that quietly does nothing is worse than one that refuses\n  to load.\n\n### 2. One column on the execution row\n\nAdd the wrapper variant id to the executions table\n(`crates/stella-store/src/ddl.rs`), beside the existing door tag. That is the\nwhole A/B setup: cost, outcome, and verified-versus-unverified rate per variant\nis one `GROUP BY variant` against `.stella/private/store.db`.\n\nTwo honesty rules, because this is a measurement surface and CLAUDE.md's bench\nrules apply to it:\n\n- A variant id is written **only when that manifest was the thing that ran**. A\n  default or fallback path writes the default's id, never a blank — a missing\n  measurement must not render as a negative one (#2952's recurring sub-shape).\n- The column measures the wrapper, not the task. Two variants compared over\n  different task sets are confounded, and the query must say so rather than\n  hide it.\n\n### 3. Flip the default\n\nToday the pipeline is the default and `--no-pipeline` opts out\n(`crates/stella-cli/src/cli.rs` — four separate `no_pipeline` flags, on `run`,\nthe deck path, `goal`, and `fleet`). That is backwards once wrappers are\nplugins: the raw loop is the ground truth and a wrapper is the extra thing you\nasked for.\n\n- Every door hits the raw loop by default.\n- `--pipeline <variant>` opts in, and names which manifest.\n- `--no-pipeline` stays as a deprecated no-op alias so no script breaks the day\n  it lands.\n\nThis is also the signal check on the whole programme. If inverting the flag\nfeels unsafe, one of #3379 or #3380 is not finished — a raw loop that cannot be\nthe default is a raw loop the wrappers are propping up.\n\n## Files\n\n- `crates/stella-plugin/src/manifest.rs` — the `[wrapper]` and\n  `[[wrapper.stages]]` blocks.\n- `crates/stella-pipeline/` — today's hardcoded stage order becomes\n  `staged-v1.toml`, shipped as the default variant.\n- `crates/stella-store/src/ddl.rs` and the migration — the variant column.\n- `crates/stella-cli/src/cli.rs` — the four `no_pipeline` flags and the new\n  `--pipeline`.\n- `crates/stella-cli/src/agent/goal.rs`, `fleet_cmd.rs` — the round and per-task\n  callers of the flag.\n\n## Constraints already discovered\n\n- **Blocked by #3380.** A manifest describes stages against the four wrapper\n  points; without the contract there is nothing for a stage name to bind to.\n- The store's door tag and the variant id are **two columns, not one**. A door\n  says which command you typed; a variant says which wrapper shape ran. #3274's\n  requested-versus-granted lesson applies: conflating two facts into one column\n  is how the last limit died.\n- Adding a column is a schema migration with the usual cascade rules — see the\n  `Store::prune` note in AGENTS.md about the 13 tables keyed off\n  `executions.id`.\n- `--pipeline <variant>` is a **scoping** parameter, not a mode selector, so it\n  does not run afoul of invariant 9: it names which wrapper, it does not choose\n  between two different verbs.\n\n## Definition of done\n\n- Today's stage order ships as a manifest and nothing in Rust hardcodes it.\n- A second variant exists (even a trivially cheaper one) and both run from the\n  same code, proving the manifest is real and not a description of one path.\n- Every execution row carries a variant id, and a `GROUP BY variant` query over\n  a real run returns per-variant cost and outcome.\n- The raw loop is the default on all six doors; `--pipeline <variant>` opts in;\n  `--no-pipeline` is a documented deprecated no-op.\n- A witness test: a manifest with an unknown key, and a manifest naming an\n  unpublished condition signal, each fail to load with a named error.\n\nDesign: `doc:turn-loop-wrappers` §5. Blocked by #3380. Refs #2889, #2952, #3274,\n#3245.\n\n\n---\n\n## Appendix — resolutions from `doc:turn-loop-wrappers` §9 (2026-08-16)\n\nThree amendments. Nothing above is withdrawn.\n\n**1. A new blocker: `kind` already encodes the wrapper (§9.5).** This issue's\nconstraint \"the store's door tag and the variant id are two columns, not one\" is\nright, and was written believing `kind` was already door-only. It is not:\n`crates/stella-cli/src/agent.rs:281` writes `pipeline` and\n`crates/stella-cli/src/command_deck.rs:1514` writes `deck-pipeline` when a\nwrapper is on. Adding `pipeline_variant` beside that gives two columns one\nquestion and makes `GROUP BY` silently split the deck in two — confounding the\nmeasurement surface on arrival. Disentangling `kind` into door-only, with a\nstated backfill, is filed as **#3388** and blocks the column half of this issue.\n\n**2. The `if` field is two languages, and needs to be a closed grammar (§9.4).**\nThe sketch's `\"questions > 0\"` (an arithmetic comparison over a stage output)\nand `\"no-test-command\"` (a host fact) are two grammars in one field. A\nTuring-complete condition in a manifest is a second program with no gate on it.\nMake it a small **closed** set of named predicates over typed stage outputs and\nhost facts, evaluated by a pure function — which is also what makes this issue's\nexisting rule (\"a condition naming a signal the host does not publish is a load\nerror\") mechanically checkable rather than aspirational.\n\nAdd alongside it: **the stage graph is load-checked**. A stage whose typed input\nno prior stage produces is a load error. This issue already says each stage has\na typed input and output; checking the graph is what turns that from\ndocumentation into a gate, and it is what buys \"a new variant is a manifest file\nonly\" — the failure mode of a hand-written variant becomes a rejection with a\nreason instead of a wedged run at round three.\n\n**3. The flag inversion is a measured change, not only a signal check (§9.6).**\nFraming it as a signal check is good and stays. It is also the change that\nreaches users, and **every published Stella benchmark number was produced with\nthe pipeline as the default.** #3245 Test 2 and `doc:turn-lane-assembly` §10.4\nalready gate the *extraction* on a side-by-side bench on the same panel; the\nsame gate belongs on the *default flip*, one step earlier, with the result\nreported even when the raw loop is worse. Whether to spend that bench or flip\nungated is the maintainer's call — recorded here rather than decided.\n\nBlocked by #3380 and (for the column) #3388.\n"[m[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"number"[m[1;37m:[m 3381[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"title"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"Wrapper stages become a manifest, variants become a column, and --pipeline replaces --no-pipeline"[m
  [1;37m}[m[1;37m,[m
