    [1;34m"body"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"## Problem\n\nRoute B (the staged pipeline) and Route D (goal mode) are the same idea written\ntwice.\n\n- **Route B**: plan, execute, verify, verdict, with a revise loop.\n- **Route D**: rounds, work, an independent verifier, met or not-met.\n- **`stella monitor`** is Route D with the goal pinned to \"the latest CI run is\n  green\". The deck says outright that it is not a seventh route.\n\nStrip the vocabulary off all three and the shape is identical: **propose work,\nrun a turn, judge from evidence, decide continue-or-stop.**\n\nOne idea, three surfaces. Every fix has to be made in each of them, and when\nsomebody forgets, they drift. Two live examples in the backlog:\n\n- #2819 — `VerifierScopedTools` in `crates/stella-cli/src/agent/goal.rs` is goal\n  mode's own hand-rolled read-only verifier view, and it silently drops two\n  `ToolExecutor` methods the pipeline's equivalent does not. That is drift\n  between two copies of \"the judge sees a read-only tool stack\".\n- The deck's slide 22 names the structural version: `Engine::with_sleeper`\n  cannot carry `gate`, `steering` or `hooks` because those are private builder\n  fields, so a hand-rolled child engine drops all three. **Goal mode shipped\n  with exactly that bug.**\n\n## What to build\n\nOne wrapper contract with four interception points, and nothing else:\n\n| Point | When it runs | May do | May not do |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `before_turn` | before the loop is asked for a turn | add context, pick a plan, narrow scope, choose a model role | run the loop itself |\n| `after_turn` | once the turn's `Complete` lands | gather evidence — run a test, read a diff, author a witness | change the turn that just ran |\n| `judge` | after `after_turn` | turn evidence into a verdict | call a model |\n| `again?` | after `judge` | ask for another turn with a correction, or stop with an outcome | fake an ending the engine did not emit |\n\nThen:\n\n- **The staged pipeline becomes the first plugin using it.** `triage`, `recall`,\n  `research`, `plan`, `scope` are `before_turn`; `witness` is `after_turn`;\n  `verify` is `judge`; `revise` is `again?`.\n- **Goal mode becomes the second** — or the same wrapper with a different\n  `judge`. Its rounds are `again?`; its independent verifier is `judge`.\n- **Core keeps one loop, one wrapper socket, and zero built-in wrappers.**\n\n## Why `judge` calls no model\n\nThis is already the shipped rule, not a new one. #2584 removed the model verdict\nand the distress-guidance call **structurally** — `Roster::apply` rejects both\nkeys as `NotAssignable`, so no configuration restores them — and\n`ladder_decision` (`crates/stella-pipeline/src/verify.rs`) is terminal at all\nfive outcomes. The wrapper contract keeps that rule rather than re-arguing it.\nThe one surviving verifier-tier model call is the witness author, which lives in\n`after_turn` because it builds the measuring stick instead of replacing it.\n\n## The socket\n\nDo not invent a new plane. The hook surface already has the right vocabulary —\n`allow`, `modify`, `deny`, `require_approval` (`crates/stella-core/src/bus.rs`,\nplus the out-of-process user hooks from #2684). A wrapper is that plane given a\nturn-shaped set of names.\n\n`before_turn` is also where the steering plane (#3243) asks its one \"what could\nhelp here?\" question for a wrapped run. It is not a fifth selector.\n\n## Files\n\n- `crates/stella-core/src/driver.rs` — where the socket is called from.\n- `crates/stella-core/src/bus.rs`, `crates/stella-core/src/hooks.rs` — the\n  existing decision vocabulary the contract reuses.\n- `crates/stella-plugin/src/manifest.rs` — where a plugin declares which of the\n  four points it wants. #3245 slice A already landed the manifest crate as a\n  leaf with no workspace-crate dependencies; the four points join it there.\n- `crates/stella-pipeline/` — ported onto the contract, not rewritten.\n- `crates/stella-cli/src/agent/goal.rs` — its round loop and\n  `VerifierScopedTools` collapse into the contract (see #2819).\n\n## Constraints already discovered\n\n- **#3379 lands first.** A wrapper cannot be a pure client of the loop while the\n  pipeline still owns the loop's ending. That is the precondition, not a\n  parallel track.\n- **`stella-core` must never depend on `stella-plugin`.** The engine never\n  learns that plugins exist; the host binds a plugin's declared points to the\n  engine's socket. #3245 open question 3, and the reason `stella-plugin` is a\n  leaf.\n- **One blessed constructor.** A plugin may only obtain an engine through the\n  constructor that attaches every seam. That is what deletes the\n  `with_sleeper`-drops-three bug class, and it is why #3274's lane work and this\n  contract are the same programme seen from two sides.\n- **`TurnLane` must be open from its first commit** (#3274 slice 1). A wrapper\n  plugin contributes a lane row. Retrofitting an open enum after the parity\n  matrix is written against a closed one is a matrix rewrite, and it is the one\n  decision here that is expensive to defer.\n\n## Definition of done\n\n- The four points exist as one declared contract, in one place, with a manifest\n  vocabulary for declaring which ones a plugin wants.\n- `stella-pipeline` reaches the engine only through that contract: no private\n  channels, no private builder fields, no engine construction of its own.\n- Goal mode's round loop and its verifier view are the same contract, not a\n  second implementation. #2819 closes as moot or is fixed inside the shared\n  judge, whichever comes first.\n- A witness test: a wrapper declaring only `judge` gets a turn it did not plan\n  and a verdict it did decide — proving the points are independent and that an\n  undeclared point is never invoked.\n- No built-in wrapper remains reachable from `stella-core`.\n\nDesign: `doc:turn-loop-wrappers` §4. Blocked by #3379. Refs #3274, #3246, #3243,\n#2819, #2584.\n\n\n---\n\n## Appendix — resolutions from `doc:turn-loop-wrappers` §9 (2026-08-16)\n\nThree amendments from the design-resolution pass. Nothing above is withdrawn.\n\n**1. The socket cannot be defined in `stella-core` (§9.1).** `before_turn` does\nrecall and research; `after_turn` runs a test command or an oracle process.\nThat is I/O, and invariant 2 forbids it in the engine. The contract is defined\nin `stella-runtime` (already assembly-owning, reads no ambient environment by\ncontract). The hook surface stays what this issue says it is — the dispatch\nmechanism with its existing vocabulary — but the contract and the code that\nsequences it live above core.\n\nCore's share of this issue is therefore **subtractive**, and the \"Files\" list\nshould read that way:\n\n- `crates/stella-core/src/goal.rs` — **delete the round loop.** It is a\n  route-specific supervisor living inside the engine crate today, and deleting\n  it is what actually delivers \"zero built-in wrappers\". This issue's Files list\n  names `crates/stella-cli/src/agent/goal.rs` but not this one.\n\nNet effect: this issue makes `stella-core` smaller, which is a stronger claim\nthan \"core keeps one loop, one wrapper socket\".\n\n**2. \"Goal mode's independent verifier is `judge`\" contradicts \"`judge` may not\ncall a model\" (§9.2).** Goal mode's verifier *is* a model call, once per round —\nfor an open-ended goal with no test surface there is no measurement to take.\n\nResolution: **the model call belongs to `after_turn`, never to `judge`.** A\ngoal-mode wrapper spends its verifier call in `after_turn` and returns the\nparsed assessment as *evidence*; `judge` maps evidence to verdict\ndeterministically and is a synchronous, I/O-free function over owned data — so\n\"judge calls no model\" becomes a property of the signature rather than a rule to\nremember. \"Verification buys no model call\" still holds exactly where #2584 won\nit. What stops being pretended is that goal mode ever satisfied it; its spend is\nnow visible on the receipt against a declared role.\n\n**3. The blessed constructor is blocked, not merely related (§9.3).** This\nissue's central claim — that one blessed constructor deletes the\n`with_sleeper`-drops-three bug class — cannot be delivered until\n`TurnCapabilities` exists, because that constructor cannot attach the three\nseams today. Filed as **#3387**; add it to this issue's blockers alongside\n#3379. The wrapper is handed a `ChildTurn` port that takes a *role intent*,\nnever a provider, an engine, or a credential.\n\nBlocked by #3379 and #3387. Refs #3386.\n"[m[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"number"[m[1;37m:[m 3380[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"title"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"One wrapper contract: before_turn / after_turn / judge / again? — and the pipeline becomes its first plugin"[m
  [1;37m}[m[1;37m,[m
