    [1;34m"body"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"## The problem\n\nThe third of #3243 D5's `NoContextRecall` surfaces is `crates/stella-serve/src/pipeline_run.rs` (~line 326): the serve pipeline runs with no recall port. Unlike the fleet and resume surfaces (wired in the Phase 3 PR), this one **cannot** be wired locally: the module's design (its docs, ~lines 50–63) is reverse-RPC — the engine holds no ambient authority, there is no workspace root, no `Store`, and no `SessionMemory` in scope. Model calls and verification already remote back to the host (`RemoteProvider`, `RemoteVerificationRunner`).\n\n## What done looks like\n\nA `RemoteContextRecall` mirroring `RemoteVerificationRunner`'s shape: a reverse-RPC request the host answers by running recall in ITS workspace and returning structured `RecalledFrame`s over the wire. Sequencing:\n\n- New wire messages in the serve protocol (`docs/spec/serve-surface.md` governs; the wire-schema gate will require regenerating `docs/wire/`).\n- The host side answers from its own `SessionMemory` / `ContextRecallPort`.\n- `PipelinePorts.recall` switches from `NoContextRecall` to the remote port; a host that declines the capability degrades to no frames (the current behavior), never an error.\n\n## Constraints already discovered\n\n- Invariant 3 (zero telemetry egress) is not implicated — frames flow host→engine on a channel the host opened — but frame *content* crossing the wire is prompt material, so it rides the same session channel as everything else, never a side channel.\n- `ContextRecallPort::recall(&self, goal: &str)` is goal-only; if #3243 Phase 3's `TurnSignal`-shaped queries reach the pipeline later, design the wire request with room for the signal rather than a second message.\n\n## Verify\n\nA serve integration test where a scripted host answers one recall request and the pipeline's planner prompt carries the returned frame.\n\nRefs #3243, #3357\n\n\n\n---\n\n## Redirected by the turn-loop wrapper architecture (2026-08-16)\n\nDesign: `doc:turn-loop-wrappers` §4. Umbrella: #3380.\n\n**Hold this one until #3380 settles the shape.** The problem is real; the\nproposed solution builds in the direction we are moving away from.\n\n**Why.** This issue would add a fourth pipeline-specific reverse-RPC port\n(`RemoteContextRecall`, mirroring `RemoteVerificationRunner`) plus new wire\nmessages and a `docs/wire/` regeneration. That deepens a private connection\nbetween one wrapper and one surface, at the moment we are deleting the private\nconnections between that wrapper and the engine (#3379) and collapsing wrappers\nonto one four-point contract (#3380).\n\nUnder #3380, recall is not a pipeline concern. It is what a wrapper does at\n`before_turn`, using whatever context the host is willing to give it. A wrapper\nthat gets no context degrades to no frames — which is exactly the fallback this\nissue already specifies, and it is the current behaviour on serve today.\n\n**What changes about the plan**\n\n- The wire question is the same either way: **a host answering a\n  \"what could help here?\" request over reverse-RPC.** Design that request against\n  the wrapper's `before_turn` input, not against `ContextRecallPort::recall`.\n- This issue's own constraint already saw this coming: *\"if #3243 Phase 3's\n  `TurnSignal`-shaped queries reach the pipeline later, design the wire request\n  with room for the signal rather than a second message.\"* Take that as binding,\n  not as a hedge — the signal-shaped request is the one to build.\n- Serve's existing posture is the model to copy and is an existence proof for\n  #3380: the engine holds no ambient authority and remotes every model and tool\n  call to the host. A wrapper plugin is that same seam pointed the other way.\n- One wire message, answering one question, serving both the steering plane\n  (#3243) and every wrapper. Not one per wrapper and not one per corpus.\n\n**Definition of done, restated**\n\nUnchanged in outcome — a serve integration test where a scripted host answers one\nrequest and the wrapper's planner prompt carries the returned frame — but the\nrequest is the wrapper's context request, not a pipeline-only recall port.\n\n**If serve's recall gap starts costing something measurable before #3380 lands,**\nsay so on this issue and we ship the narrow port with a note that it is\ntemporary. Do not ship it quietly as the design.\n\nRefs #3243, #3357, #3379, #3380.\n"[m[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"number"[m[1;37m:[m 3365[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"title"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"serve: the pipeline's recall port needs a reverse-RPC design — the one D5 surface a local wire cannot fix"[m
  [1;37m}[m[1;37m,[m
  [1;37m{[m
    [1;34m"body"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"## Problem\n\nStella's multimodal input plane (`crates/stella-protocol/src/attachment.rs` → `crates/stella-model/src/attachment.rs`) ingests video natively only where the dialect declares `video: true` in its `DialectCaps`:\n\n- `gemini.rs:152` (`GEMINI_CAPS`) — video `inlineData`, native\n- `bedrock.rs:257` (`BEDROCK_CAPS`) — Converse video blocks, native\n- `anthropic.rs:491`, `openai.rs:254`, `zai.rs:575` — `video: false`; a `@clip.mp4` mention or pasted path degrades to a descriptive `WirePart::Text` note (`degrade_note`, `crates/stella-model/src/attachment.rs:406`) naming the file.\n\nThe degrade contract is correct and total (the note tells the model what it cannot see, so the model can relay that to the user), but it means \"watch a video and understand its contents\" only works on 2 of 6 dialects. Every image-capable model (Anthropic, OpenAI, vision-capable z.ai/OpenRouter models) could understand a video **via sampled keyframes** — extract N frames, attach them as `AttachmentKind::Image` parts plus a text note stating the sampling (timestamps, frame count, duration, that audio was not transcribed), so the model's answer is honest about what it saw.\n\n## Proposed shape\n\n- A pure decision half in the attachment layer: given a video attachment and a `DialectCaps` with `video: false, images: true`, plan a frame-sampling degrade instead of a text-only degrade. Keep the plan (frame count, timestamps) deterministic and unit-testable; no I/O in the planner.\n- The extraction half (I/O — invoking `ffmpeg` or a Rust decoder) belongs outside `stella-core`/the pure layer, likely beside the hydration path in `stella-model` or as a preprocessing step in `stella-cli` before the message is built. `ffmpeg` as a runtime probe-and-degrade dependency (absent → today's text note, with the note saying why), never a hard build dependency — check `deny.toml` implications before choosing a decoder crate.\n- The honesty constraint is the point: the accompanying text note must state that the model saw sampled frames, not the video — the model's relay to the user then reflects reality. This mirrors the asymmetric-caps reasoning documented at `crates/stella-model/src/zai.rs::caps_for`.\n- Cost guard: frames are image attachments; N should be bounded and probably configurable (default small, e.g. 8–16), and `MAX_ATTACHMENTS` (`crates/stella-cli/src/attachments.rs:25`) interaction decided explicitly.\n\n## Constraints discovered\n\n- Degradation must stay total — an unextractable/corrupt video degrades to the existing text note, never an error that kills the turn (module docs at the top of `crates/stella-model/src/attachment.rs`).\n- Sending media to a model that cannot take it is a terminal 400 on some dialects (z.ai comment at `zai.rs:573`) — frame images may only ride where `images: true` for the resolved model.\n- Provider parity discipline (AGENTS.md invariant #8): if this becomes per-provider divergent behavior, it needs declaration + witness tests in `crates/stella-model/src/provider_parity.rs`, same PR.\n- Witness test required: a video attachment on a `video: false, images: true` dialect produces image wire parts + a sampling note (fails today — today it produces one text note).\n\n## Definition of done\n\n`stella` given `@clip.mp4` on an Anthropic/OpenAI vision model answers questions about the video's visual content from sampled frames, states the sampling honestly, and degrades to today's descriptive note when extraction is unavailable. Native-video dialects (Gemini, Bedrock) are unchanged.\n"[m[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"number"[m[1;37m:[m 3340[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"title"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"multimodal: video understanding on non-video dialects — sample keyframes into image parts instead of degrading straight to a text note"[m
  [1;37m}[m[1;37m,[m
