    [1;34m"body"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"## Problem\n\nPR #TBD (fix-402-resilience) taught the shared chat-completions adapter to retry an OpenRouter HTTP 402 that names the affordable output ceiling (\"You requested up to 128000 tokens, but can only afford 47365\") once, with `max_tokens` reduced to 90% of the hint. The recovery is deliberately per-request: nothing is remembered between turns.\n\nConsequence: while the balance stays low, **every subsequent turn re-sends the original ceiling, eats one 402 round-trip, and only then retries reduced**. On a bench trial with dozens of turns that is dozens of wasted request/reject cycles (latency, not spend — the 402 bills nothing), and each one risks the gateway's rate limiting.\n\n## Where\n\n- `crates/stella-model/src/zai.rs` — `complete_inner` (the one-shot resend), `afford_capped_max_tokens`, `AFFORD_RETRY_FLOOR_TOKENS`.\n- The precedent for a bounded per-session latch is `crates/stella-model/src/stream_recovery.rs` (`StreamRecovery`, #2686): armed on a fault, consulted per attempt, bounded so it cannot wedge a session permanently.\n\n## Sketch\n\nA session-scoped cell (interior-mutable, like `StreamRecovery`) remembering the last afford-derived ceiling; `build_body` clamps `max_tokens` to it when set. It must decay or re-raise: a top-up mid-session should not leave the session capped forever (e.g. clear the latch after N successful turns, or re-try the caller's full ceiling once every M turns).\n\n## Constraints discovered\n\n- Parity: the behavior is declared as `BillingPosture::AffordHintRetry` in `crates/stella-model/src/provider_parity.rs`; a latch changes the mechanism wording and needs its witness extended (two turns: second turn must go out already-reduced without an intervening 402).\n- The floor (8192) and the 90% margin live beside `afford_capped_max_tokens`; keep one copy.\n- Byte-stability: the latch must not perturb requests when it never armed (prompt-cache contract, AGENTS.md invariant 7).\n\n## Done\n\nA wiremock witness showing: turn 1 → 402(hint) → reduced retry succeeds; turn 2 on the same provider instance goes out with the reduced ceiling on its **first** attempt. Fails on current main."[m[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"number"[m[1;37m:[m 3307[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"title"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"stella-model: the afford-hinted 402 retry is per-request — a persistently low balance pays one wasted 402 round-trip every turn"[m
  [1;37m}[m[1;37m,[m
  [1;37m{[m
    [1;34m"body"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"## The gap\n\nStella's self-improvement machinery is real and proven deterministic offline:\n\n- **Tool Foundry** (`crates/stella-core/src/tool_foundry.rs`, #830) mines repeated shell-invocation shapes from receipts into a proposed tool, gated through `foundry_author`/`foundry_witness`/`foundry_gate` in `stella-tools`. Pure by design — no model call.\n- **Skill mining** (`SessionMemory::auto_create_skills`, `crates/stella-cli/src/memory/learning.rs`) mines `reflections.jsonl` via `stella_core::skills::mine_skill_candidates` into `.stella/skills/<slug>/SKILL.md`.\n- **Rules mining** (`crates/stella-cli/src/memory/rules_mining.rs`) does the same for `.stella/rules/*.toml`.\n- All three are proven deterministic and replayable against recorded traces with zero model calls (#2304, closed).\n\nEvery one of these is designed around a single continuous engineer's `.stella/` directory persisting on disk across sessions. **A Terminal-Bench 2.1 trial run under arenabench/Harbor does not have that.** Each trial gets a fresh, isolated container/checkout; the container (and everything the agent wrote into `.stella/tools/`, `.stella/skills/`, `.stella/private/context.db`, `reflections.jsonl`, the tool-call receipt log) is destroyed after scoring. So:\n\n- Tool Foundry's ≥2-distinct-argument-set gate can only ever see evidence from *inside one trial*, because the receipt log doesn't survive to the next one.\n- A skill mined in trial N is invisible to trial N+1 — every trial starts from the same amnesiac baseline.\n- Running the same 89-task suite dozens of times with a cheap model (GLM 5.2) produces zero compounding today: there is no mechanism for what trial 40 knows that trial 1 didn't.\n\nThis is why nobody has observed the benchmark score trend upward across repeated runs — the infrastructure to let it isn't built. #2781 (\"promotion measures an artifact only on the panel that produced it — no transfer or decay panel\") is the closest existing issue, but it's framed generically; it doesn't name the bench-harness-wipe as the specific failure mode, and nothing today implements even the generic version.\n\n## The ask\n\nBuild the harness-side infrastructure so that a long, repeated TB2.1 loop (dozens of runs, cheap model, unattended) lets Stella's *existing* tool-foundry and skill-mining machinery actually compound across runs instead of restarting from zero every trial.\n\n## Design\n\n### 1. A durable corpus store, outside any trial's ephemeral workspace\n\nA directory on the host running the bench loop (e.g. `~/.arenabench/self-improve-corpus/<run-id>/`), versioned, holding:\n- authored custom tools (`.stella/tools/*.toml` + scripts) with their witness results,\n- mined skills (`.stella/skills/<slug>/SKILL.md`),\n- promoted rules/memories,\n- the tool-foundry evidence ledger (shell-invocation receipts), so ≥2-distinct-argument-set evidence can accumulate *across* trials, not just within one.\n\n### 2. Harvest — after every trial, before its container dies\n\nCopy out whatever `.stella/{tools,skills,memories,rules}`, `reflections.jsonl`, and tool-call receipts the trial produced. Tag every artifact with full provenance: which trial/task produced it, which model, timestamp, pass/fail outcome. `bench/harbor_adapter/stella_harbor/telemetry_export.py` and `lineage.py` already move data out of a trial and track cross-run lineage — extend rather than reinvent. This closes #2782's provenance gap specifically for the bench context.\n\n### 3. Promotion gate — between harvest and seed\n\nAn artifact from trial N must not seed forward just because it existed. Gate on:\n- **Witness passes** (tool foundry already requires this per #830).\n- **Transfer, not just local success** — run the candidate artifact against a small held-out subset of *other* TB2.1 tasks (never the task that produced it) before promoting; this is #2781's \"transfer panel,\" scoped concretely to this harness for the first time.\n- **No regression** — a promoted artifact that measurably correlates with *more* failures on the held-out set gets demoted, not just left alone (the decay half of #2781, and a direct answer to #2783's \"corpus information gain\" — a corpus that only accumulates duplicate/overfit artifacts must not be allowed to grow unchecked).\n\n### 4. Seed — before every trial's container starts\n\nMaterialize the current corpus's *promoted* subset into the fresh container's `.stella/` before the agent's turn begins. Never seed the raw harvest — only what passed step 3.\n\n### 5. Authority boundary — this is the actual evolution surface #2780 is missing\n\nInside one disposable, sandboxed trial container, autonomous tool authorship + auto-execution is safe to allow without the per-tool human sign-off #830 otherwise requires — blast radius is the container, which dies regardless. The real trust boundary is the **promotion step (3)**, not per-tool inside a trial. This gives #2780's \"six ways Stella changes itself, none declaring evidence/authority/rollback\" ledger a concrete new entry: evidence = witness + transfer-panel result, authority = automatic inside a bench container / promotion gate is the sign-off, rollback = decay (step 3) demotes or removes.\n\n### 6. Orchestration and the measurement that proves it worked\n\nRun N repeated passes over the 89 TB2.1 tasks (or a fixed subset) with GLM 5.2 as the worker model, harvest→promote→seed between passes. Track **score vs. pass number** as the headline metric, **with an ablation control**: an identical run count and task set with corpus seeding disabled, so any lift is attributable to the accumulated artifacts and not model variance or task memorization — per this repo's \"measure honestly\" rule and the established bench protocol (control before bisect, same-tool-basis comparisons).\n\n### 7. Prove the mechanics offline first, cheaply\n\nBefore spending GLM-5.2 budget on real TB2.1 passes, the harvest/promote/seed/decay logic should be provable deterministically against #2304's trace-replay harness (zero model calls) — debug the machinery there, then graduate to real bench passes. This also gives CI something to hold the harness logic to.\n\n## What this is *not*\n\nNot a request to build new miners — tool foundry, skill mining, and rules mining already exist and are already proven deterministic (#830, #2304). The net-new work is entirely the harvest/promote/seed/decay orchestration around the ephemeral bench harness, plus the ablation-controlled measurement loop that proves compounding is real.\n\n## Success criteria\n\nThe agent actively builds and tests tools and skills in a self-driving loop (dozens of TB2.1 passes, GLM 5.2), and the benchmark score trends measurably higher with each pass, with an ablation control proving the lift is attributable to the accumulated tool/skill corpus rather than model variance.\n\n## Refs\n\nBuilds on #830 (Tool Foundry, shipped), #2304 (trace-replay harness, shipped). Closest existing gap: #2781 (promotion/transfer/decay), #2780 (evolution-surface ledger), #2782 (provenance), #2783 (corpus information gain), #3243 (steering plane epic). Bench substrate: `bench/harbor_adapter/stella_harbor/telemetry_export.py`, `lineage.py`. Companion to `fullauto:bench` (Stella vs Claude Code on TB2.1) and the `fullauto` perpetual delivery loop skill, which already runs a fix→audit→bench→ship cycle but does not currently harvest or seed self-authored artifacts across passes.\n"[m[1;37m,[m
