    [1;34m"body"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"PR #3304 landed the oracle logic (`arenabench/hooks/oracle_stop_hook.py`); nothing invokes it yet. This is the wiring + measurement slice.\n\n## Problem\n\nThe oracle is a Stop hook: the engine consults it only if the SUT container has (a) the hook file, (b) a `.stella/settings.json` declaring `{\"hooks\": {\"Stop\": [{\"hooks\": [{\"command\": \"python3 /path/oracle_stop_hook.py\"}]}]}}`, and (c) the env the hook reads. None of that is provisioned by any match TOML today, so the code is unwired.\n\n## Definition of done\n\n1. A match TOML (sibling of `arenabench/matches/stella-five-tools*.toml`) that provisions the hook into the task container and sets:\n   - `STELLA_ORACLE_TASK` — the task statement text the judge reads. **This is the hard part**: the task statement must reach the container without also handing the worker a channel it did not have before. Confirm the harness already exposes it (Terminal-Bench task.yaml is present in the image) and read it from disk rather than plumbing a new env var if so.\n   - `STELLA_ORACLE_JUDGE_MODEL` + `OPENROUTER_API_KEY` (credential by SSM name, never a value — the standing arenabench rule).\n   - Optionally `STELLA_ORACLE_TEST_COMMAND` for tasks that ship one.\n2. Requires PR #3302 (bounded `MAX_STOP_CONSULTS`) merged and the SUT rebuilt from a commit containing it — with the once-per-turn latch the deny is issued but never re-checked, so the flip cannot be observed. Verify the SUT binary contains it before drawing any conclusion (`strings | rg` on the binary, the established practice).\n3. A paired panel: the same tasks, same model, same effort, with and without the hook. Report solve rate, added wall clock, and added judge cost per trial. The gate89high1 baseline is 39/54 = 72.2% valid at $1.67/trial (high effort), and its failure breakdown was 11 `failed_wrong_answer` / 3 `timeout_before_solve` / 1 step-cap `agent_error` — `failed_wrong_answer` is the population this oracle targets, so that is the number that must move.\n4. Read `stella-events.jsonl` for the arm, not `result.json`: confirm the Stop hook actually fired (count the consultations) before attributing any delta to it. A hook that never ran and a hook that always allowed look identical in the solve rate.\n\n## Constraints already discovered\n\n- Fail-open is deliberate: no key / no judge / unparseable → allow. So a misconfigured arm scores exactly like the control and **proves nothing** — hence requirement 4.\n- The hook writes `.stella/private/oracle-state.json`; `.stella/` is excluded from the verifier-facing diff by `is_stella_state_path`, but confirm the bench harness's own diff collection agrees.\n- Judge cost is per-turn, not per-trial: a task that holds open 3 times buys 3 judge calls. Budget accordingly.\n\nRefs #3234, #3246. Depends on #3302, #3304."[m[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"number"[m[1;37m:[m 3305[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"title"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"Wire the Stop-hook oracle into a bench arm and measure it (PR #3304's next slice)"[m
  [1;37m}[m[1;37m,[m
