    [1;34m"body"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"## The problem\n\n`crates/stella-core/src/mining.rs::terms` treats only **ASCII** alphanumerics (plus `_`) as word characters:\n\n```rust\nif ch.is_ascii_alphanumeric() || ch == '_' {\n```\n\nSo any text with no ASCII words tokenizes to the empty set. Every consumer of that function then silently does nothing useful:\n\n- **Skill selection** (`skills::select_skills`) scores the prompt against each skill's name+description in `terms` space. An empty prompt-term set means a lexical score of 0 for every skill, so a CJK, Cyrillic, Greek, or heavily-accented prompt can only ever select a skill via the domain-tag path.\n- That path was just correctly narrowed by #3296 (#3243 D1) — it now requires the prompt to name an existing workspace file. Both effects are individually right, and together they mean **a non-Latin-script prompt that names no path selects no skills at all**.\n- **Rule and skill mining** (`already_captured`, `cluster_observations`) compares term sets, so non-ASCII observations never cluster and never dedupe: each recurrence looks novel, and the \"observed enough\" promotion threshold can never be reached for them.\n\n## Why this was not fixed in passing\n\nThe function's own doc comment states the constraint, and it is real:\n\n> Widening this would change every mined `<slug>-<hash8>` id, so it is a deliberate migration, not a drive-by tweak.\n\n`crates/stella-core/src/skills/migration_contract.rs` guards that id space explicitly (it already reasons about what falls \"permanently out of clustering reach\" in `mining::terms` space). Changing the tokenizer re-tokenizes every stored artifact's provenance, so existing `.stella/skills/<slug>-<hash8>/` files and mined rule ids stop matching what a re-mine would produce — which means duplicate skills appearing beside the originals, not a clean upgrade.\n\n## Two sub-problems, and they are not the same difficulty\n\n1. **Accented Latin, Cyrillic, Greek** — `is_alphanumeric()` instead of `is_ascii_alphanumeric()` handles these correctly, because those scripts are space-separated. Also switch the `current.len() > 2` byte checks to `current.chars().count() > 2`, which is a no-op for ASCII and correct for multi-byte text (today a 1-character CJK \"word\" is 3 bytes and passes a length gate meant to drop 1-2 character tokens).\n2. **CJK** — has no spaces, so `is_alphanumeric()` turns an entire sentence into ONE term, which is not obviously better than zero terms. Doing this properly needs segmentation (character bigrams are the standard cheap approximation for CJK retrieval; a dictionary segmenter is the real answer and is a dependency decision).\n\nWorth solving (1) and (2) separately — (1) is a clear win, (2) needs a call on whether Stella wants a segmentation dependency.\n\n## Verify\n\nThe gap, before any change:\n\n```rust\n// crates/stella-core/src/mining.rs tests\nassert!(terms(\"café serveur\").is_empty());        // accented Latin: nothing\nassert!(terms(\"数据库查询格式化\").is_empty());       // CJK: nothing\n```\n\nand end to end, that a skill whose description shares terms with a non-ASCII prompt is not selected (`select_skills` returns empty).\n\n## Constraints already discovered\n\n- **Do not change `terms` without the id migration.** The blast radius is `already_captured`, `cluster_observations`, and every `<slug>-<hash8>` on disk. `slugify` is a separate function with the same ASCII assumption and the same id consequence — decide both together.\n- `jaccard` must keep its current semantics: the miners' `min_similarity` thresholds are tuned against it. (#3296 added `coverage` alongside it rather than changing it, for exactly this reason.)\n- A cheaper alternative worth considering first: leave `terms` alone for **ids and clustering**, and give *selection* its own tokenizer. Selection scores are ephemeral and mint no ids, so a Unicode-aware tokenizer there carries none of the migration cost. The tradeoff is two tokenizers in the tree, which the module docs note was itself a past bug (two byte-identical copies that drifted) — so it needs a clear comment naming which one is the id space and which is the scoring space.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nA non-Latin prompt whose wording matches a skill's description selects that skill, **and** no existing mined artifact's id changes without a migration that reconciles the on-disk files.\n\nRefs #3243, #3296\n"[m[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"number"[m[1;37m:[m 3298[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"title"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"steering: the ASCII-only tokenizer makes a non-Latin prompt select no skills and mine no lessons"[m
  [1;37m}[m[1;37m,[m
