    [1;34m"body"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"## Problem\n\nStella has exactly **one** turn loop — `crates/stella-core/src/driver/drive.rs:111` is the only `loop {` over `Engine::run_step` in the workspace, and a sweep for `run_step(` outside `driver/` returns one hit that is a doc comment. That part of the architecture is correct and does not need touching.\n\nWhat is duplicated is **lane assembly**: the act of deciding which of the engine's twelve optional capability slots a given run binds. That decision is made by hand at seven production sites, in three unrelated vocabularies, with nothing checking the result.\n\n**Vocabulary A** — `EngineConfig` fields: `checkpoint_sink`, `turn_halt`, `completion_gate`, `turn_budget`.\n**Vocabulary B** — `Engine::with_*` builders: `hooks`, `hook_approvals`, `calibration`, `gate`, `steering`, `bus`, `outcomes`, `fallback`.\n**Vocabulary C** — `TurnControls` published to the `ToolRegistry`: the pause gate *again*, so dispatched sub-agents inherit it.\n\nThe gate must therefore be bound **twice**, through two mechanisms, at every site that wants it (`fleet_cmd.rs:882-887`, `subsession.rs:749-758`, `command_deck.rs:4244-4252`). A site that does one and forgets the other is silently half-wired.\n\n`crates/stella-core/src/subagent.rs:61` documents the structural consequence in its own module doc: `Engine::with_sleeper` cannot carry `gate`/`steering`/`hooks`, so a fork drops three capabilities by constructor signature rather than by decision.\n\n## The seven lanes and their sites\n\n| Lane | Assembly site |\n|---|---|\n| Lead (deck) | `stella-cli/src/command_deck.rs:4244` |\n| Resume | `stella-cli/src/agent/resume.rs:311` |\n| Sub-session | `stella-cli/src/subsession.rs:729` |\n| Subagent fork | `stella-core/src/subagent.rs:705` |\n| Fleet worker (raw) | `stella-cli/src/fleet_cmd.rs:914` |\n| Pipeline stage | `stella-pipeline/src/pipeline/execute_stage.rs:363`, `witness_stage.rs:361` |\n| Serve session | `stella-serve/src/session.rs:675` |\n\nSeven is the count of *builtin* lanes. It is deliberately not the count of lanes — see \"Plugin lanes are not a later phase\" below.\n\n## Why this is the right framing\n\n`#3232`, `#3233` and `#3242` are three cells of one table, discovered months apart, each by someone tripping over it. `#3242` was found as *damage* — a sub-session inheriting the lead's sink and destroying its resume point — and fixed by stripping the cell rather than deciding it.\n\nThe deciding problem: **a correct decision and a forgotten one render identically.** Nothing distinguishes `subsession_engine_config_for`'s deliberate `checkpoint_sink: None` (defended by a named test) from `subagent.rs:705`'s hard-coded `None`, which is a default nobody revisited.\n\nThe fleet case shows how invisible this is: `SessionDurability::sink()` returns `None` while unbound, and `durability::bind_session` is called from `command_deck.rs`, `agent/presence.rs` and `agent/resume.rs` — never from `fleet_cmd.rs`. The worker's raw path calls `agent::engine_config_for(&cfg)`, which *does* read `cfg.durability.sink()`, so the site looks wired. It is unwired three files away, and nothing declares that.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nAdding a capability to the turn loop is a **compile error at every builtin lane that has not declared a posture for it**, and a **load-time rejection or a reported defaulted slot at every plugin lane**. Both halves are required; see the two-regime table below.\n\n---\n\n## Plugin lanes are not a later phase\n\n**This is the constraint that governs slice 1, and the reason this epic cannot be implemented from its slice list alone.** Full design: `doc:turn-lane-assembly` §9 and §10; plugin platform epic is #3246 (#3245 is CLOSED and superseded by it).\n\n> \"Plugins should be able to create their own lane. Ideally pipeline is moved out of core stella and core stella remains the bare loop with a plugin surface that can change the behaviors of the loop — in other words we need some way to drive this from manifests/registries.\" — Mac, reviewing #3273\n\nA plugin that participates in the turn loop is, structurally, **a plugin that contributes a lane row**. Four consequences, recorded so nobody re-derives them:\n\n1. **`TurnLane` is open from the first commit.** `Builtin(BuiltinLane)` stays closed — that is what preserves slice 2's compile-error property for everything in this workspace — while `TurnLane` itself is not, which is what lets a manifest contribute a row. Both halves are load-bearing. Making it open costs one enum variant today; retrofitting it after seven lanes and a parity matrix are written against a closed enum is a matrix rewrite. **This is the single decision in this epic that is expensive to defer, and it is paid in slice 1.**\n\n2. **Totality has two regimes, and the weaker one is named rather than pretended equal.**\n\n   | Lane origin | Enforced | By what | When a capability is added |\n   |---|---|---|---|\n   | `TurnLane::Builtin(_)` | compile time | no `Default` on `TurnCapabilities`, exhaustive destructuring in the parity test | the workspace does not build until every builtin lane declares |\n   | `TurnLane::Plugin(_)` | **load** time | manifest validated against a capability registry generated from the same table | the plugin loads with the new slot forced to `Declined`, and `stella plugins doctor` reports every lane sitting on a defaulted slot |\n\n   You cannot give an out-of-tree file a build failure. Two rules keep the load-time half from rotting: an unknown capability name in a manifest is a **load error, never ignored** (#1400's manifest rule applied one level down), and a defaulted slot is **reported, not assumed**.\n\n3. **`participation` is derived, never separately authored.** #3245's manifest ladder (`none | observer | steering | arbiter`) and the capability vector are the same statement at two resolutions — `observer` implies `bus`; `steering` adds `steering`, `hooks`; `arbiter` adds `gate`, `halt`, `completion_gate`. The manifest declares capabilities; `participation` is derived from them and displayed in `stella plugins doctor`, the Command Deck chip, and the lane matrix. A repository that has already lost one limit to having a number in two places does not get to invent a second vocabulary for lane capability on purpose.\n\n4. **`granted = requested ∩ authorized(principal, capability)`.** The matrix as specified in slice 4 records what a lane *has*; for a builtin lane those are the same thing, for a plugin lane they are not, and conflating them turns a design document into a security claim it has not earned. `authorized` is the #2716 authority vocabulary (`ToolContract` / `AuthzGate` / `Principal` / `RiskLevel`), verified to have **zero code in the tree** and **reopened 2026-08-14** as the plugin-lane blocker. The matrix carries `requested` and `granted` as **separate columns from day one**, so the day #2716 lands the enforcement point has somewhere to write its answer instead of needing a schema change.\n\nTwo mechanics #2456 already settled — do not re-derive them:\n\n- **Resolution is builtin-first, never registry-first.** A manifest naming `lead` is a **load-time rejection** — *that lane exists and is not yours* — not a silent override. Consulting the plugin registry ahead of the builtins creates two paths for one name, which is the failure this epic is about, re-introduced at the seam meant to fix it.\n- **The custom lane set nests: `[lanes.custom.<id>]`, never `#[serde(flatten)]`.** A flattened map is silently skipped by the settings scope-merge (the overlay iterates a closed list of known keys), so `flatten` reads correctly and quietly drops project-scope overrides — a silent capability drop arriving through the config plane instead of the assembly plane.\n\nFeasibility is an existence proof, not an analogy: `stella-serve` already ships an engine holding no ambient authority that remotes every model and tool call to a host process. **A plugin-driven lane is that seam pointed the other way.**\n\n---\n\n## Plan\n\nFull design: `docs/spec/turn-lane-assembly.md` (id `turn-lane-assembly`), landed in #3273. Eight slices, each independently shippable.\n\n0. **Delete `stella-runtime`'s dead `stella-pipeline` dependency** (#3280) — declared in `Cargo.toml`, referenced nowhere in `src/`. Free, unblocks nothing, blocks nothing; can go first. Size XS.\n\n1. **`TurnLane` in `stella-protocol`** — name the lanes so a matrix can have rows; stamp it on `agent.turn.started` (no surface can answer \"which lane\" today, Observatory included). **Open from this commit**: `enum TurnLane { Builtin(BuiltinLane), Plugin(LaneId) }` with `BuiltinLane` closed over the seven. Shipping a closed `TurnLane` here is the one mistake in this epic that cannot be cheaply undone. Witness: a lane-tagged `TurnStarted` round-trips (invariant #4). Size S.\n\n2. **`TurnCapabilities` in `stella-core`** — one `#[non_exhaustive]` struct with **no `Default`**, collapsing vocabularies A/B/C; `Engine::assemble` replaces `with_sleeper` + eight builders, with the builders kept as deprecated shims. **Must admit owned slots, not borrows only** — `Engine<'a>` holds `&'a dyn Provider/ToolExecutor/Sleeper` (`driver.rs:480-484`) and an out-of-process plugin cannot hand the engine a borrow; a `&'a dyn`-only design forces plugin lanes into a second parallel vocabulary, which is the exact disease this epic diagnoses. Either the struct is generic over ownership or the owned form is defined in the same PR. Witness: a subagent fork carries `gate`/`steering`/`hooks` — the three capabilities `subagent.rs:61` documents as undeliverable; fails on main for a structural reason. Lands in `driver/capabilities.rs`, not `driver.rs` (grandfathered god file, 1917 lines). Size L.\n\n3. **Migrate all seven builtin sites**, delete the shims, no `Default`. Must not be split across sessions — half a mechanical sweep is worse than none. Size M.\n\n4. **Lane matrix in `stella-parity`** — `LANES: &[LaneCapability]` with `Bound { how, witness } / Declined { reason } / Deferred { issue, waiting_on }`, enforced from both sides exactly as `CAPABILITIES` is: every `TurnCapabilities` field claimed by a row (exhaustive destructuring), every `Bound` row's witness test still existing. Rows carry **`requested` and `granted` separately** and a plugin row carries its **origin**, so a reader knows what a `Plugin` row is worth. #3233 becomes a `Deferred` row. Size M.\n\n5. **`ResumeAuthority`** — `Own` / `Parent` / `Redispatch`, plus the terminal-frame write side. Closes #3233. Size M.\n\n6. **Manifest-declared lanes** — `LaneId`, load-time totality against the generated capability registry, builtin-first resolution, `[lanes.custom.<id>]`, `stella plugins doctor`. **Gated on #2716** for the `granted` half. Refs #3246. Size L.\n\n7. **Pipeline registers as a lane**; cut `stella-cli`'s `stella-pipeline` dependency (169 refs across 41 files). **Gated on a side-by-side bench, not on a review.** Done when `rg 'stella-pipeline' crates/stella-cli/Cargo.toml` returns nothing, `stella run` still delivers today's pipeline mode through a manifest-registered lane, and the bench holds on the same panel. Refs #3246. Size XL.\n\nSlices 1 and 2 are independent and can run in parallel. Slice 0 can go first.\n\n## Resolved: `ResumeAuthority` (was \"open design question\")\n\nSlices 1-4 do not close #3233 by themselves, because it was blocked on a real question: nothing reads a fleet worker's or a sub-session's checkpoint, so binding the write side alone buys a whole-transcript serialization per step with no reader.\n\nModelled as a property of the lane:\n\n- `Own` — the lane resumes itself (Lead, Resume, ServeSession)\n- `Parent` — the checkpoint is read by the parent for *reporting*, not resumption (SubSession, SubagentFork)\n- `Redispatch` — the supervisor re-runs the unit; the checkpoint is evidence (FleetWorker, PipelineStage)\n\n`Parent` and `Redispatch` lanes then need only a **terminal frame** (last committed step + transcript, written once when the lane dies), not a per-step resume point — far cheaper than the write side the earlier fix declined, and with a reader by construction.\n\n**Decided (Mac, 2026-08-14): approved as written** — recorded in `doc:turn-lane-assembly` §6, which this section previously contradicted by still calling it undecided. Slice 5 is unblocked. A **plugin lane declares its own authority in its manifest**; an undeclared authority is a load-time rejection, never a silent `Redispatch`.\n\n## Non-goals\n\n- Does **not** merge the pipeline into the loop. The pipeline is a supervisor running several turns through the one loop; `stage_rank` in `stella-pipeline/src/replay.rs` is already its canonical ordering. The matrix makes that boundary explicit rather than moving it. Extraction (slice 7) is the opposite direction and is scheduled; merging is **never**.\n- Does **not** reduce the number of builtin lanes. Seven is arguably right — they genuinely differ. The goal is that every difference is a written, tested decision rather than the residue of whichever site was edited last.\n- Does **not** claim the extraction delivers the one-place property. \"When I add a feature to the turn loop I should only have to go to one place\" is delivered by slices 2 and 3, whether or not the pipeline ever moves. Keeping those claims apart is what stops slice 7 being sold on a benefit it does not deliver.\n\n## Constraints discovered\n\n- `stella-core` must stay I/O-free (invariant #2), so lane→capabilities resolution belongs in `stella-runtime`, which already owns the assembly bottom half and reads no ambient environment by contract.\n- **`stella-core` is already the bare loop** — its dependencies are `stella-protocol`, serde, toml, thiserror, tokio, async-trait, futures-util, rand, sha2; no `stella-pipeline`, no witness or flip-oracle module. The work is not carving policy *out of* the loop but giving the loop a registry so policy plugs *into* it from outside, which is a smaller and much less dangerous job.\n- **A blocking plugin cannot live on the `HookBus`** — its handlers are sync closures (`Fn(&HookEvent) -> HookDecision`, `bus.rs:190`) and cannot await. An arbiter lane whose definition of done involves running a test suite belongs on the async out-of-process hook plane (60s default, 10min ceiling), which is the *better* substrate for that work, not the fallback.\n- `driver.rs` is a grandfathered god file — plan new code into a sibling module (`driver/settlement.rs` is the precedent).\n- Slices 2-3 are refactors with no behavioural witness beyond the fork case; per CONTRIBUTING that must be declared in the PR, not dressed up.\n- #2793 (MCP + custom tools bypass the blocking policy chains) is a hole a plugin lane inherits on day one; slice 6 should not assume the chains are total.\n\nRefs #3232\nRefs #3233\nRefs #3242\nRefs #3246\nRefs #3273\nRefs #3280\nRefs #2716\nRefs #2793\n\n\n\n---\n\n## Extended by the turn-loop wrapper architecture (2026-08-16)\n\nMac's review of the turn-loop deck (#3377) adds three moves this epic did not\ncover. They do not change anything already written here — the seven lanes, the\ntwo-regime totality table, the open `TurnLane`, and slice 0 (#3280) all stand.\nThey add the thing a lane is *for*. Design: `doc:turn-loop-wrappers`.\n\n**The three moves, and where they sit against this epic**\n\n| Move | Issue | Relationship to this epic |\n|---|---|---|\n| The engine owns its own ending — delete the pipeline's private event channels | #3379 | **New precondition.** Independent of lane assembly, and it should land first. |\n| One wrapper contract (`before_turn` / `after_turn` / `judge` / `again?`), pipeline becomes its first plugin | #3380 | **This is what a plugin lane carries.** The epic's §9/§10 said a plugin contributes a lane row but never said what the row *does*; #3380 is the answer. |\n| Stages as a manifest, variant id as a store column, `--pipeline` replaces `--no-pipeline` | #3381 | Downstream of both. |\n\n**What this sharpens in the epic's existing text**\n\n1. **Slice 1's open `TurnLane` gets a second reason to be paid now.** The epic\n   already argues it is the one expensive-to-defer decision. #3380 makes it\n   concrete: the pipeline itself becomes a plugin lane, so the first out-of-tree\n   consumer of the open enum is our own code, not a hypothetical.\n2. **`participation` is derived, and the wrapper points are what it derives\n   from.** The ladder (`none | observer | steering | arbiter`) and the four\n   wrapper points are the same statement at two resolutions — a lane declaring\n   `judge` and `again?` is an `arbiter`. Do not author a third vocabulary.\n3. **\"Zero built-in wrappers\" is the end state.** Today's seven lanes count\n   builtins. When #3380 finishes, the pipeline stage lane and the goal-round\n   loop are plugin lanes, so the builtin count goes **down**, not up. Any slice\n   that assumes seven is a floor should be re-read.\n4. **#3379 removes one thing this epic would otherwise have to model.** A lane\n   that owns the engine's terminal event is a lane with a capability nothing\n   else has. After #3379 there is no such capability, so the matrix has one\n   fewer column to argue about.\n\n**One thing to decide inside this epic, not outside it**\n\nWhether the wrapper socket is a **capability slot** in the lane matrix (a lane\neither has a wrapper bound or does not) or a **separate registry** consulted\nafter lane assembly. The epic's builtin-first resolution rule points at the\nfirst, and the second would create the two-paths-for-one-name failure this epic\nexists to fix — but it should be written down as a decision rather than\ninherited from a tone of voice.\n\nRefs #3379, #3380, #3381, #3246.\n"[m[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"number"[m[1;37m:[m 3274[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"title"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"Epic: one loop, seven lanes — make turn-loop capability assembly a single place"[m
  [1;37m}[m[1;37m,[m
