    [1;34m"body"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"## Finding\n\nOn Terminal-Bench 2.1 at **stock** timeouts, Stella's losses on flaky tasks are\npredominantly **clock-bound, not reasoning-bound**. The agent does not think its\nway to a wrong answer; it runs slower per step and gets cut off.\n\nMeasured on run `preregA2` (SUT `080faea91`, five-tool bare loop,\n`claude-sonnet-5` medium, 3 attempts x 10 tasks), restricted to the five tasks\nthat solved on some attempts and not others:\n\n| | n | mean steps | mean agent wall |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| wins | 6 | 59.3 | 845s |\n| losses | 9 | 48.6 | **1129s** |\n\nLosses spend **284s more wall clock while completing 11 fewer steps**, and\n**5 of the 9 losses terminate `interrupted`** (the timeout) rather than\n`completed`:\n\n```\nrstan-to-pystan               WIN   52 steps  1240s  completed\nrstan-to-pystan               loss  59 steps  1800s  interrupted\nrstan-to-pystan               loss  53 steps  1595s  interrupted\nschemelike-metacircular-eval  WIN   88 steps  1723s  completed\nschemelike-metacircular-eval  loss  62 steps  2400s  interrupted\nschemelike-metacircular-eval  loss  75 steps  2400s  interrupted\n```\n\n## Why it matters\n\n1. **It explains why raising effort does not help.** Runs `preregHIGH` /\n   `preregHIGH2` set effort `high` on the same frozen panel. High solved **zero**\n   tasks medium could not, and the three tasks medium solved but high never did\n   (`rstan-to-pystan`, `schemelike-metacircular-eval`,\n   `torch-pipeline-parallelism`) are where the clock is tightest. More effort\n   buys depth per step and spends wall clock — and wall clock is the binding\n   constraint. See [#3256](https://github.com/macanderson/stella/issues/3256)\n   for why every earlier run (at 2x timeouts) could not see this.\n2. **It relocates the lever for the headline number.** pass@1 is 60.0% while\n   best-of-3 is 90.0% on the same data — 30 points of pure variance on tasks\n   Stella already solves at least once. If most of that variance is a clock\n   race, the work is in **steps-per-second**, not in prompt or model tuning.\n\n## Not yet established\n\n- `torch-pipeline-parallelism` finishes in 360-513s and is **not** clock-bound,\n  yet is still flaky. So the clock explains the long tasks, not all flakiness.\n- n = 15 trials across 5 tasks. This is a well-supported hypothesis, not a\n  settled law.\n- **Where the wall time actually goes is unmeasured.** Steps are slower in the\n  losing trials, but nothing here separates model latency from tool wall clock.\n  That is the next measurement, and it is cheap: join `tool_start` ->\n  `tool_result` in `stella-events.jsonl` for real per-tool wall clock, and\n  compare the distribution between winning and losing trials of the same task.\n\n## Definition of done\n\n1. A per-trial breakdown of agent wall clock into model time vs tool time vs\n   engine overhead, for the winning and losing trials of the same task, from\n   `stella-events.jsonl` rather than from `result.json` aggregates.\n2. If tool wall clock dominates: identify the slow tools on those tasks and\n   whether the step count is inflated by retries or repeated reads.\n3. If model latency dominates: that is a routing/provider question\n   (`openrouter-auto` does not pin an upstream, and upstream choice changes\n   latency) — see the gateway note in\n   `bench/prereg/tb21-preregA-2026-08-14.md`.\n\n## Verify\n\n    python3 bench/prereg/effort_compare.py\n\nand the flaky-task table above is reproducible from\n`s3://arenabench-artifacts-578673726240/runs/preregA2/`.\n\n## Context\n\nFound while running the pre-registered stock-timeout series (#3256). Effort\ncomparison committed on branch `worktree-tb21-prereg`.\n"[m[1;37m,[m
