    [1;34m"body"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"## Problem\n\nEvery Stella Terminal-Bench 2.1 number recorded to date was measured with\n`agent_timeout_multiplier = 2.0` — double the agent's execution clock.\n\nThe tbench.ai methodology for the Terminal-Bench 2.1 leaderboard states:\n\n> submissions may not modify timeouts or resources\n\nSo none of those numbers is leaderboard-eligible, and none may be placed beside\na published leaderboard row. This is not currently written down anywhere, and\nthe numbers are quoted in memory, issues and PR descriptions without the\nqualifier — which is how a non-comparable number becomes a claim.\n\n## The distortion is measurable, not hypothetical\n\nOur own recorded Claude Code arm gives the control, because Claude Code + Sonnet\n5 also has a published stock-timeout row:\n\n| Measurement | Value |\n| --- | --- |\n| Claude Code, our harness, 85 tasks, `agent_timeout_multiplier = 2.0` | 82.4% |\n| Claude Code + Sonnet 5, published tbench.ai row (stock) | 74.6% ± 1.6% |\n\nRoughly **eight points** of apparent standing come from the extra wall clock.\n(Confounded by CC version and the leaderboard's 3-repeat averaging, so treat the\nmagnitude as indicative and the direction as established.)\n\nThe same 2.0 multiplier is set in `matches/stella-five-tools.toml` and in the\n`gate89high1` run's `match.toml`, and appears in every fetched\n`result.json` under `config.agent_timeout_multiplier`.\n\n## Why it matters beyond bookkeeping\n\nStella's losses concentrate in the long-horizon tail — on the 13 tasks that\nwere only measured in the solo run, Claude Code went 9/13 and Stella 5/13. Those\nare exactly the tasks where a doubled clock helps most, so the 2.0 multiplier\nprobably flatters Stella *more* than it flatters Claude Code. The honest\nexpectation is that our stock-timeout number lands materially below the 76.5%\nrecorded at 2×.\n\n## Definition of done\n\n1. A short note in `bench/README.md` (or `arenabench/README.md`) stating that\n   `agent_timeout_multiplier` must be `1.0` for any number that will be compared\n   to, or submitted to, the public leaderboard — and that a run above 1.0 must\n   carry the qualifier wherever it is quoted.\n2. `matches/*.toml` intended as leaderboard-comparable set `1.0`; the ones that\n   are deliberately diagnostic keep their multiplier and say so in a comment.\n3. Ideally: `arenabench` prints a one-line banner at submit when\n   `agent_timeout_multiplier != 1.0`, naming the run as non-submittable. That is\n   the cheapest place to catch it, because the operator is right there.\n\n## Verify\n\n    rg -n 'agent_timeout_multiplier' arenabench/matches/ bench/\n\nand confirm each non-1.0 occurrence carries a comment explaining why the run is\ndiagnostic rather than submittable.\n\n## Context\n\nFound while pre-registering the stock-timeout series\n(`bench/prereg/tb21-preregA-2026-08-14.md`, branch `worktree-tb21-prereg`),\nwhich is the first Stella run configured at 1.0.\n"[m[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"number"[m[1;37m:[m 3256[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"title"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"bench: every recorded TB2.1 number used a 2x agent timeout and is not leaderboard-eligible"[m
  [1;37m}[m[1;37m,[m
