    [1;34m"body"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"Companion to #3243. That issue covers the *retrieval* plane (what context to\ninject); this one covers the *authority* plane (what may change the loop). They\nmeet at one place — §5's sequencing — and are otherwise independent.\n\n**Evidence basis.** `main` at `a4b87649a` (2026-08-13T14:44:24-07:00). Every\n\"shipped/not shipped\" claim below was checked against the tree, not against the\nissue that proposed it.\n\n---\n\n## 1. The requirement (Mac, 2026-08-13)\n\nA plugin is more than a skill or a custom tool. It can:\n\n- **change the definition of done** — the exit condition of the turn loop\n- **block turn completion** to do synchronous work (author a witness, run it,\n  read the oracle flip) before the turn may finish\n- **append to the system prompt** when installed, and to the volatile part per turn\n- **subscribe to lifecycle events** — before/after enter loop, before/after exit\n  loop, on error/abort — where the event *cannot complete* until the plugin\n  answers OK\n- **fire its own events**\n- **write to traces** in a form that supports training custom models from\n  trace + oracle flip\n- be **written in Python against an SDK**, with Rust running parts of the loop\n  through it\n\nThe first customer is Oxagen's paid verification plugin.\n\n---\n\n## 2. The search: five issues, and the two that matter were closed five days ago\n\n| Issue | State | Actually in the tree? |\n|---|---|---|\n| #1400 Stella Apps — manifest, OAuth lifecycle, host API, marketplace | closed, **not planned** (frozen behind #2374) | no |\n| #2716 tool-first governance — `ToolContract`, `AuthzGate`, `Principal`, `RiskLevel` | closed **NOT_PLANNED** 2026-08-12 | **no — verified zero code** |\n| #2836 hook events `UserPromptSubmit`, `SubagentStart/Stop` | closed **NOT_PLANNED** 2026-08-12 | **no — verified zero code** |\n| #2684 hook surface: `Stop`, `PreCompact`, `Modify`, decisions | closed, completed | **yes** |\n| #1133 HookBus lifecycle emitters | closed, completed | **yes** |\n| #459 HookBus resilience — observer budget, bounded forward | closed, completed | **yes** |\n| #1042 TraceSink | closed, completed | **yes** (`crates/stella-cli/src/trace.rs`) |\n| #2793 MCP + custom tools bypass the blocking policy chains | **open** | the hole is live |\n\nVerification of the two NOT_PLANNED closes:\n\n```\nrg -n \"struct ToolContract|trait AuthzGate|enum RiskLevel|struct Principal\" crates/   → 0 hits\nrg -n \"UserPromptSubmit|SubagentStart\" crates/stella-core/src/hooks.rs                → 0 hits\n```\n\nBoth were closed in the 2026-08-12 mission-scope sweep with the same reason\n(\"doesn't serve Stella's 4 mission goals… reopen with a mission-tie if that's\nwrong\"). **A paid plugin platform is that mission tie.** #2716 in particular is\nnot a nice-to-have here — see §4, O4.\n\n---\n\n## 3. The headline finding\n\n**Stella already has two plugin planes. They have complementary strengths, they\ndo not know about each other, and the plugin system is largely the work of\nmerging them.**\n\n### Plane A — `HookBus` (in-process, compiled)\n\n`crates/stella-core/src/bus.rs` + `crates/stella-core/src/bus/names.rs`\n\n- **97 declared event names**, dotted and namespaced, matched exactly, by\n  wildcard (`tool.*`), or globally (`*`).\n- **9 of them can block** (`names.rs:243-253`): `tool.call.requested`,\n  `file.created/updated/deleted`, `command.started`,\n  `git.commit/push.requested`, `pull_request.requested`,\n  `deployment.requested`.\n- Decision vocabulary: `Allow | Modify{payload} | Deny{reason} | RequireApproval`\n  (`bus.rs:142`). The chain folds `Modify` into the payload and stops on `Deny`\n  (`bus.rs:489-540`).\n- **Explicitly open to extensions**: \"Extensions may emit custom names — the\n  catalog is the contract for what the *host* emits, not a closed set\"\n  (`names.rs:3-4`).\n- #459's resilience shipped: a 500 ms per-dispatch observer budget with\n  quarantine after 3 consecutive overruns (`bus.rs:277-292`), and `forward_to`\n  is now a bounded `mpsc::Sender` with a `ForwardDropped` counter (`bus.rs:809`).\n- **Two hard limits.** Handlers are synchronous closures\n  (`Fn(&HookEvent) -> HookDecision`, `bus.rs:190`) — a blocking handler\n  **cannot await**. And registration requires compiled Rust.\n\n### Plane B — settings-declared shell hooks (out-of-process, any language)\n\n`crates/stella-core/src/hooks.rs` + `crates/stella-core/src/driver/user_hooks.rs`\n\n- **5 events**: `SessionStart`, `PreToolUse`, `PostToolUse`, `Stop`, `PreCompact`.\n- **JSON payload on stdin, JSON decision on stdout** — same `HookDecision`\n  vocabulary as Plane A, folded by `hooks/decision.rs::run_decision_hooks`.\n- **Async with a real timeout**: 60 s default, 10 min hard ceiling\n  (`hooks.rs:86-89`).\n- Language-agnostic by construction. **Python works today, with no SDK and no\n  new protocol.**\n\n### Why this matters more than it looks\n\nThree of the seven requirements in §1 are **already shipped on Plane B**:\n\n1. *\"the system prompt needs to be updated/appended when the plugin is installed\"* —\n   `SessionStart` stdout is appended to the system prompt, budgeted at 4,000 chars\n   with a visible truncation marker, fired exactly once per session\n   (`crates/stella-cli/src/agent/engine.rs:459-524`). The byte-stability\n   contract is stated there and is the plugin author's side of the bargain.\n\n2. *\"the plugin needs to be called prior to a turn being called done\"* and\n   *\"a way of blocking turn completion\"* — `stop_hook_feedback`\n   (`crates/stella-core/src/driver/user_hooks.rs:254-308`) runs at completion,\n   and a `Deny{reason}` **holds the turn open and keeps stepping**, injecting the\n   reason as a marked tail message. This is the verification plugin's core\n   mechanic, already built.\n\n3. *\"blocking work\"* — `Deny` from a shell hook may take up to 10 minutes, in a\n   subprocess, without holding an engine thread.\n\nAnd one property is deliberately, correctly in place for exactly this use case:\n\n> \"Blocking policy hooks are **never** timed or skipped — a slow `Deny` must\n> still run.\" — `bus.rs:283-285`\n\n---\n\n## 4. Five opinions on the design as it exists\n\n### O1 — The \"future object\" is the wrong primitive. The deny-loop you already have is the right one.\n\nThe requirement says a plugin \"has authority to return a future object that\npromises something that isn't there yet.\" I'd argue against building that.\n\nA future is a poor fit for three reasons specific to this codebase:\n\n- **It is unreplayable.** Determinism and replayability are non-negotiable for\n  the agent loop (CLAUDE.md). A promise resolved out-of-band has no deterministic\n  position in the journal; a `Deny{reason}` followed by more steps has exactly\n  one.\n- **It has no natural failure mode.** A future that never resolves wedges the\n  turn with no diagnostic. `Deny` already has the documented posture: a broken\n  Stop hook is a diagnostic and never a block (`user_hooks.rs:300-306`), because\n  \"a broken Stop hook must not wedge the turn into never completing.\"\n- **You already have the loop.** Verification is inherently iterative: author\n  witness → run → no flip → tell the worker why → it works more → re-check. That\n  *is* deny-and-continue. A promise models it as one long await; the deny loop\n  models it as what it is.\n\n**What actually needs to change**, and it is small:\n\n1. **The once-per-turn latch.** `stop_fired` (`user_hooks.rs:262-265`) fires the\n   Stop hooks at most once per turn, whatever they decide. Correct as a\n   footgun-guard for shell hooks; wrong for verification, which must be\n   consulted again after the worker responds to its feedback. Replace the\n   boolean with a bounded counter (the loop-steer precedent — `MAX_LOOP_STEERS`\n   — is the shape, and #2810 is the live lesson that the *last* iteration must\n   say it is the last one).\n2. **`Deny{reason}` carries only a `String`.** For verification the plugin must\n   hand back structure: which witness, which test, flip achieved or not, the\n   digest. Widen the payload — this is invariant #5's \"a caller has to branch on\n   it\" test, and here the caller (the driver, and the trace fold) genuinely does.\n3. **`RequireApproval` at a turn boundary is currently surfaced as inapplicable**\n   (`user_hooks.rs:284-296`). For a paid plugin that may need a human (\"your\n   verification budget is exhausted, continue?\") that will want a real answer.\n\n### O2 — Do not let plugins write traces. Make them emit journal events and leave the trace a fold.\n\nThe requirement is *\"plugins write to traces in a manner that supports using the\ntrace files + oracle flip to train custom models\"* — with the honest caveat *\"i\ndon't know the right architecture pattern on how to do this yet.\"*\n\nThe pattern is already written down in this repo, in the module that would\nreceive the writes. `crates/stella-cli/src/trace.rs:8-18`:\n\n> **Assembly, not a second capture path.** …this module deliberately captures\n> nothing at runtime — it is a fold over what the closeout just settled… A\n> second live capture path would duplicate the journal and could drift from it;\n> the journal is already the append-only trace store.\n\nSo: **a plugin emits events into the journal; the trace fold picks them up.**\nNothing writes `traces.jsonl` but the fold. Consequences, all of them good:\n\n- Plugin-contributed facts inherit replayability for free.\n- They inherit `TRACE_SCHEMA_VERSION` (currently 3), whose own doc explains why\n  a reader must *skip* an unknown version rather than guess (`trace.rs:50-66`) —\n  precisely the discipline a training-data consumer needs.\n- They inherit the privacy posture: `.stella/private/` 0700, file 0600, every\n  string leaf through `redact_secrets`, and **nothing enters `store.db`**, so\n  invariant #3's content-free gate is not even in the path (`trace.rs:30-37`).\n  A plugin writing traces directly would route around all of that.\n- The oracle-flip half is not new work: `RewardLabel`/`RewardPolicy` are already\n  on the record (`stella_pipeline::reward`), and the flip oracle already exists\n  in `crates/stella-pipeline/src/verify.rs`.\n\nThe one thing to add is a **plugin event namespace** in the bus catalog\n(`plugin.<id>.*`), which `names.rs:3-4` already contemplates, plus the fold arm\nthat reads it.\n\n### O3 — Out-of-process is the *better* substrate here, not the weaker one.\n\nThe instinct is usually that in-process = powerful, subprocess = fallback.\nFor this requirement it is inverted, and the code says so:\n\n| | Plane A (in-process) | Plane B (subprocess) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Events | 97 | 5 |\n| Can block | 9 events | 3 events |\n| **Async / long work** | **no — sync closure, cannot await** | **yes — 10 min ceiling, own process** |\n| Language | Rust only | any |\n| Isolation | none — a panic is caught, a hang is not | process boundary |\n| Packaging | recompile | a file path |\n\nA verification pass that runs a test suite is *exactly* the workload Plane A\ncannot host and Plane B can. So the plugin system should be **Plane B extended\nwith Plane A's event catalog**, not Plane A opened up to dynamic loading. That is\nalso what #2684 already did for two events — the pattern is proved, it just needs\ngeneralizing.\n\n### O4 — Authority is the real blocker, and it is the thing that was closed.\n\nThis is my strongest recommendation. **A plugin that changes the definition of\ndone is the highest-authority object in the system** — higher than a tool, higher\nthan a rule, because it decides when work stops. Today there is no vocabulary to\nsay so:\n\n- No `Principal` — the pre-dispatch seams see `{tool, input}` and nothing about\n  *who* is asking (#2716's own survey).\n- No `RiskLevel`, no `requires_approval`, no contract version.\n- `ToolPolicy` is session-global config, not per-caller — so \"this plugin may\n  veto completion, that one may only observe\" is inexpressible.\n- Hooks **concatenate across scopes** (`crates/stella-cli/src/settings/merge.rs:39`,\n  `:157-159`) — no scope can remove another's. Fine for operator hooks; wrong for\n  plugins, where uninstall must actually uninstall.\n- **#2793 is open**: MCP and custom tools already bypass the blocking policy\n  chains. A plugin system built on today's seams inherits that hole on day one.\n\nWithout this, a paid verification plugin and a hostile plugin have identical\nauthority, and the only control is \"did the user put it in settings.json.\" That\nis not a platform an enterprise buys.\n\n**Recommendation: reopen #2716 with the mission tie its close invited** —\ndataset generation for open-weight models (mission goal 4) runs on\nplugin-authored trace records, and those are only trustworthy if the authoring\nprincipal is named. It is a prerequisite, not a parallel track.\n\nNote also #2780 (open): the evolution-surface ledger. A plugin is a sixth way\nStella changes itself, and that issue is the reviewed table where its\n`evidence`/`authority`/`rollback` row belongs.\n\n### O5 — Python: do not embed. And MCP is the candidate to beat.\n\nThree existing out-of-process patterns, zero embedded interpreters\n(`rg pyo3` finds only `tree-sitter-python`, a grammar):\n\n1. shell hooks — JSON on stdin, JSON decision on stdout\n2. custom tools — argv spawn, JSON on stdin + `STELLA_INPUT_*` env\n   (`crates/stella-tools/src/custom.rs:32-67`)\n3. MCP — JSON-RPC over stdio, with a full client in `stella-mcp`\n\nEmbedding CPython would mean the GIL on an async runtime, a packaging story for\nevery platform Stella ships to, and a concretion inside crates that invariant #1\nkeeps free of them. The SDK should be a thin Python client over a protocol the\nhost already speaks.\n\n**Which protocol is the real decision, and MCP deserves a serious look.** As a\nplugin transport it brings, already built and already tested in-tree: stdio\nJSON-RPC, a registry + `stella mcp search/install`, OAuth with auto-refreshing\ntokens (`.stella/private/mcp_oauth.json`), a 401 suppression cache, per-server\nenable/disable, and — decisively — **an official, mature Python SDK Oxagen would\nnot have to write or maintain.**\n\nWhat MCP does *not* give you is the lifecycle-event half: it has no notion of\n\"the host is about to finish a turn; may it?\" That is the delta, and it is a\ndocumented protocol extension rather than a new protocol.\n\nThe honest counterweight: MCP tools are currently hardcoded\n`read_only: false, speculation_safe: false` (`crates/stella-mcp/src/toolset.rs:776`)\nand are therefore invisible to every `ReadOnlyTools` view, and #2793's bypass is\nan MCP-shaped hole. Both are fixable and both are worth fixing anyway.\n\n**I would not decide this from the armchair.** The question is narrow enough to\nanswer with a spike: implement the verification plugin's Stop-hook path twice —\nonce as a settings-declared shell hook with a Python script, once as an MCP\nserver with a lifecycle extension — and compare on packaging, auth, and how much\nprotocol we end up owning.\n\n---\n\n## 5. What I would sequence\n\nDeliberately ordered so the first two are useful even if the platform never\nships, and nothing irreversible happens before authority exists.\n\n**P0 — Reopen and land the authority vocabulary (#2716).** `Principal`,\n`RiskLevel`, `ToolContract`, `AuthzGate`. Close #2793's bypass in the same\nbreath, since a plugin platform inherits it. *Nothing else here should start\nfirst.*\n\n**P1 — Make the existing Stop-hook path production-grade for verification.**\nBounded re-consultation instead of the once-per-turn latch; structured `Deny`\npayload; `RequireApproval` answerable at a turn boundary. This alone makes the\nverification plugin buildable as a shell hook, in Python, today.\n\n**P2 — Plugin event namespace + trace fold arm.** `plugin.<id>.*` in the bus\ncatalog; the trace fold reads them. Delivers the training-data requirement\nwithout a second capture path.\n\n**P3 — Loop-lifecycle events, and only then make them blocking.** The names you\nwant (`agent.turn.*`, `step.*`) exist and emit, but **none is in `BLOCKING`** —\n`agent.turn.parked` is documented as \"deliberately NOT in `BLOCKING`\"\n(`names.rs:36-39`). Adding turn/loop events to the blocking set is a real\nchange: blocking handlers are never timed or skipped by design, so a wedged\nplugin on `before_enter_loop` stalls every turn forever. Blocking loop events\nneed Plane B's subprocess+timeout shape, not Plane A's sync closure.\n\n**P4 — Manifest, identity, install, uninstall (#1400's core, minus the\nmarketplace).** Note #1400's non-negotiable rule still applies and is worth\nre-reading before anyone writes a line: *\"the control plane is a guest, never a\ncomponent\"*, enforced by `rg -i 'oxagen' crates/ → zero matches`. **The Oxagen\nverification plugin must be indistinguishable from any third party's**, which is\na constraint on P0–P3 too, not just on P4.\n\n**P5 — The Python SDK**, over whichever protocol the P1 spike settles.\n\n**P6 — Plugins as a steering source.** #3243's `SteeringSet` gains a fifth\nvariant. Designing the `source` enum in that epic's Phase 2 with this in mind\ncosts nothing now.\n\n---\n\n## 6. What I did not verify\n\n- **I did not run anything.** No plugin, no hook, no trace was executed; every\n  claim is from reading `main@a4b87649a`.\n- **The one load-bearing claim was checked, and holds.** `Stop`-hook `Deny`\n  really does produce more steps: `dispatch_completion`\n  (`crates/stella-core/src/driver/completion.rs:183-189`) pushes the reason as a\n  `STOP_HOOK_MARKER_PREFIX`-marked user message and returns `None`, so no\n  `TurnOutcome` is produced and the step loop continues. The assistant message\n  is pushed to history *first* (`:170-178`), deliberately, \"so the hook's\n  feedback answers a recorded turn.\" What I have **not** run is a live hook\n  end-to-end; this is a read of both halves of the call, not an execution.\n- **The MCP recommendation in O5 is an argument, not a finding.** I did not\n  check whether MCP's notification model can carry a blocking host→server\n  request, which is the question the spike exists to answer.\n- **The 97 event names is a count of `pub const` declarations** in\n  `bus/names.rs`, not a hand-audit that each is emitted. #1133 wired the\n  emitters; I did not re-verify each one.\n- I did not assess cost, pricing, or licensing implications of a paid plugin\n  against the AGPL/commercial dual track. That interacts with #1400's \"an app is\n  data, never Rust in this tree\" rule and deserves its own read.\n\nRefs #1400, #2716, #2836, #2684, #1133, #459, #1042, #2793, #2780, #3243.\n\n\n\n---\n\n## Sharpened by the turn-loop wrapper architecture (2026-08-16)\n\nMac's review of the turn-loop deck (#3377) names the specific shape this\nplatform has to carry, which §1's requirement list left open. Design:\n`doc:turn-loop-wrappers`. Filed as #3379, #3380, #3381.\n\n**The finding this adds to §3.** The two planes this issue found are both\n*event* planes — they answer \"who gets told, and who may say no\". Neither of\nthem answers the question the pipeline actually needs, which is **\"who runs work\naround a turn, and who decides whether to go again\"**. That is a third shape,\nand it is the one the paid verification plugin is:\n\n| Point | When | May do | May not do |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `before_turn` | before the loop is asked for a turn | plan, triage, add context | run the loop |\n| `after_turn` | once the turn's `Complete` lands | author a witness, run the oracle, read the flip | change the turn that ran |\n| `judge` | after `after_turn` | verdict from evidence | call a model |\n| `again?` | after `judge` | ask for another turn, or stop | fake an ending the engine did not emit |\n\nMap that onto §1's requirement list and every bullet lands somewhere:\n\n- *change the definition of done* → `judge`\n- *block turn completion to do synchronous work* → `after_turn`, then `again?`\n- *append to the system prompt per turn* → `before_turn`\n- *subscribe to lifecycle events where the event cannot complete until the\n  plugin answers* → the existing blocking HookBus (Plane A), unchanged\n- *fire its own events / write to traces* → the existing bus, unchanged\n\nSo the merge of the two planes is still the bulk of the work, and this table is\nthe fourth piece that has to exist alongside it.\n\n**The precondition this issue should adopt as a blocker: #3379.**\n\n§1's requirement \"the pipeline becomes a plugin so Stella does not depend on it\"\nis **structurally impossible today**, for a reason worth stating in this issue\nrather than discovering during implementation: the pipeline holds a private\nevent channel into each `run_turn` and swallows the engine's terminal events\n(`crates/stella-pipeline/src/pipeline.rs`, \"Event ownership\"). A plugin cannot\nhold a private channel into the engine. That coupling has to die before anything\nhere can ship, and #3379 is only that deletion.\n\n**Sequencing, restated against §5**\n\n1. #3379 — the engine owns its ending. No plugin work depends on the platform\n   being designed; this is pure subtraction.\n2. #3274 slice 1 — `TurnLane` open from its first commit.\n3. #3380 — the four points, then the pipeline ported onto them, then goal mode.\n4. #3381 — stages as a manifest, and the variant column that makes plugin\n   variants measurable.\n\n**#2716 stays the blocker it already is.** The four points are capabilities a\nprincipal is granted, and `granted = requested ∩ authorized`. A wrapper plugin\nholding `judge` and `again?` can decide when work is done, which is the\nstrongest grant on the platform. The authority vocabulary has to exist before\nthat grant is handed to an out-of-tree file.\n\nRefs #3379, #3380, #3381, #3274, #2716.\n"[m[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"number"[m[1;37m:[m 3246[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"title"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"Plugin platform: the substrate mostly exists across two planes that do not know about each other — design review and sequencing"[m
  [1;37m}[m[1;37m,[m
  [1;37m{[m
    [1;34m"body"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"## The directive (Mac, 2026-08-13)\n\n> Ensure that context is being proactively and speculatively queried and injected\n> when it truly will help or needs to be used to steer (like for a rule or fact).\n> What we need is one part of the loop that considers the changes happening to the\n> loop and the work and queries for \"things\" that can help an agent. Those things\n> are: context records/memories (which are context record type now — same for\n> rules, facts, etc.), MCP servers, skills, and custom tools. Eventually plugins\n> join that list. It needs to be managed in one spot, behind a flag that defaults\n> on but can be turned off by environment variable or the TOML.\n\nThis issue is the survey plus the design. It files no code. **Read §2 before\nestimating anything** — four selection engines already exist, and knowing that\nchanges what the work is.\n\n**Evidence basis.** Everything below was read on `main` at `a4b87649a`\n(2026-08-13T14:44:24-07:00). Where a claim depends on a branch, the branch is\nnamed. Line numbers are from that commit.\n\n---\n\n## 1. The one-paragraph finding\n\nStella already queries four different \"what could help here?\" planes on every\nturn. They run at the same moment, from the same trigger, over corpora that\noverlap — and **not one of them knows the other three exist.** They use four\nunrelated relevance engines, four unrelated budgets, four unrelated failure\npostures, and three unrelated on/off switches. Two of the four are effectively\nalways-inject (relevance is computed and then ignored), one is reactive-only and\nwired on 1 of 6 surfaces, and the fourth advertises its entire corpus on every\nrequest at a measured ~6–9k tokens.\n\nThe work is not to build a retrieval system. It is to **collapse four into one\nseam, give it the one signal none of them has today (what is actually happening\nin this turn), and put it behind one flag.**\n\n---\n\n## 2. What exists today — four planes, four engines\n\n| Plane | Selector | Signal it matches on | When | Budget | Off switch |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| **Recall frames** (memories, episodes, facts) | `stella-context` RRF fuse + MMR + evidence gate — `crates/stella-context/src/retrieval.rs:1028-1080` | raw prompt string + ≤4 existing-file path anchors | turn start | 5 frames / 1200 tok | `context.retrieval.ab_recall_rate` (1-in-N), `STELLA_NO_SETTINGS` |\n| **Context records** (rules/facts/directives) | `applies_to` glob/keyword match — `crates/stella-core/src/records/select.rs:66-92` | prompt text + ≤32 path tokens | turn start | 16k chars cached / 2k volatile | none |\n| **Skills** | Jaccard + domain boost — `crates/stella-core/src/skills.rs:421-476` | prompt terms vs `name + description` | turn start | **none** | per-skill sidecar, A/B arm |\n| **Tools** (native + MCP + custom) | none by default; `tool_search` rank when lean — `crates/stella-cli/src/discovery.rs:390-420` | model must ask | every model call | 32 KiB/MCP server, no aggregate | `STELLA_LEAN_TOOLS`, `tools` policy map |\n\nFour selection engines: RRF/MMR, glob matching, Jaccard, and `discovery::rank`.\nA skill the host declines to inject can be the #1 `skill_search` hit, because\nthey are different rankers with different thresholds and different tokenizers\n(one is Unicode-aware, one is ASCII-only).\n\n---\n\n## 3. The defects that matter, ranked\n\nEach is independently filable; several are one-line fixes with outsized effect.\n\n### D1 — Skill selection passes the entire workspace taxonomy as \"active domains\"\n\nAll three call sites pass `&self.domains.names()`:\n\n- `crates/stella-cli/src/memory/recall.rs:166`\n- `crates/stella-cli/src/memory/recall.rs:239`\n- `crates/stella-cli/src/memory.rs:660`\n\n`domains` is loaded once at session open from `.stella/domains.toml`\n(`memory.rs:542-545`) and is *every* domain the repo declares. So\n`matched_domains` in `select_skills` answers \"is this skill tagged with a domain\nthis repo has?\" — never \"is this domain active this turn?\". Consequences,\nreading `skills.rs:427-476`:\n\n- domain boost is `0.5` per matched tag, `min_score` is `0.08`, and\n  `corroborated` is satisfied by any domain match — so **any domain-tagged skill\n  is injected on every non-control turn regardless of prompt.**\n- ranking among tagged skills is `tag_count × 0.5`, so a 3-tag skill permanently\n  outranks a perfectly-matching 1-tag skill.\n- the top-5 cap is consumed by the most-tagged skills, not the most-relevant.\n\nThe fix already exists next door: memory recall got per-query domain scoping in\n#2333 via `query_domain_scope(&domains, &anchors)`\n(`crates/stella-cli/src/contextgraph.rs:106-129`). Skill selection was not\nmigrated. This is a one-line swap at three call sites.\n\n### D2 — Jaccard is symmetric, so lexical skill selection cannot fire on real prompts\n\n`score = |A∩B| / (|A|+|B|-|A∩B|)` over prompt terms vs `name + description`\n(`crates/stella-core/src/mining.rs:52-63`). With a 15-term description and\n`min_score = 0.08`, a 50-term prompt needs 5 shared terms and a 200-term prompt\nneeds ~16. Combined with D1 the effective behavior is binary: **domain-tagged\nskills always inject, untagged skills never do.** An asymmetric coverage score\n(`|A∩B| / |B|`) is the standard fix.\n\nRelated: the skill **body is never matched** (`skills.rs:431` builds the\nhaystack from name + description only), and non-ASCII prompts tokenize to the\nempty set (`mining.rs:30-48`), so a CJK or accented prompt can only ever select\nvia the broken domain path.\n\n### D3 — The cached channel is always-inject, and `applies_to` on it is parsed but never matched\n\n`must`/`should` records and every `.stella/memories/*.md` enter the byte-stable\nprefix on every turn regardless of topic, up to 32k chars combined\n(`crates/stella-core/src/records/render.rs:87-88`,\n`crates/stella-cli/src/agent/prompt.rs:623-701`).\n`crates/stella-core/src/records/select.rs:13-19` states plainly that\n`applies_to` on a pinned record is validated, displayed, and never matched. The\n`Tier::Scoped` escape hatch exists but nothing auto-tiers — an author must\nhand-write it.\n\nThis is defensible for a `must` rule and indefensible for 16k chars of memories.\nIt is also the reason the volatile channel's 2k budget is the wrong place to\neconomize.\n\n### D4 — On pipeline turns (i.e. every `stella run`) the recall→citation loop is dead\n\n`assemble_user_message` (`crates/stella-pipeline/src/pipeline/user_message.rs:76-140`)\nemits `## Recalled context` with **no `RECALL_MARKER`**. `user_block_kind`\n(`crates/stella-core/src/receipts.rs:351-366`) prefix-matches that marker, so\nthe whole message classifies as one `BlockKind::UserGoal` with `memory_id: None`.\n`crates/stella-cli/src/memory/uses.rs:166-175` then finds no `record_ids` and\nreturns zero.\n\nCompounding it, the pipeline line format is `- [label] (source) [nod_id]` while\n`parse_recall_item` (`receipts.rs:428-446`) expects `- [nod_…] label — body`, so\neven with the marker the id would not parse.\n\nNet: `stella run`'s only mode produces **no context-use telemetry at all.** #2195\nwas closed on the strength of the pipeline learning to *ask* for a citation\n(`user_message.rs:116`); the receipt half that would record the answer was not\nfixed. Selection health and efficacy-based retirement remain structurally\nunreachable there — and separately, the cached channel discards\n`RenderedChannel.rendered` at every production caller\n(`recall.rs:101-110`, `prompt.rs:396-416`), so no baked memory or pinned rule\never produces a `ContextUse` row on any surface.\n\n### D5 — Three shipping surfaces recall nothing\n\n`NoContextRecall` is passed at:\n\n- `crates/stella-cli/src/fleet_cmd.rs:841` (fleet workers — which additionally\n  open **no `SessionMemory` at all**, so no skills, no records, no draft claims)\n- `crates/stella-cli/src/agent/resume.rs:268` (a resumed run silently loses all\n  context the original had — the frame restores `task_class`, `goal`, `roster`,\n  `max_revisions`, but not the recall port)\n- `crates/stella-serve/src/pipeline_run.rs:327`\n\nAnd the reactive `recall_context` tool is wired at exactly one production site,\n`crates/stella-cli/src/command_deck/pipeline_turn.rs:98`. That was #2900, closed\n2026-08-12 in the mission-scope sweep as dev-ergonomics — **closed, not fixed**;\nthe code is unchanged. Phase 3 gives it a mission tie (a plane that re-queries\nmid-turn is the explainability and cost story), which is the reopen condition\nthat close invited.\n\n### D6 — Nothing re-queries when the work changes\n\nEvery plane fires once, at turn start, against the raw prompt string\n(`crates/stella-cli/src/memory/recall.rs:362-381`: `query_text` is the prompt\nverbatim; no history, no coreference resolution, no expansion). A 40-step turn\nthat starts on \"fix the flaky test\" and ends up in the provider adapter recalls\nagainst the first sentence and nothing else. Anchors additionally require the\nfile to *exist* (`recall.rs:522`), so a goal naming a file to be **created**\ncontributes no anchor — and with `require_evidence = true` (the default) the\nanchor channel is one of only five admission paths.\n\n**This is the defect the directive is actually about**, and it is the only one on\nthis list with no existing mechanism to fix.\n\n### D7 — Unbudgeted volatile sections\n\nSkills (`recall.rs:162-171`) and draft claims (`recall.rs:177-180`) enter the\nvolatile block with no character cap, while frames (1200 tok), records (2k\nchars) and the date line are all budgeted. `RECORD_CHANNEL_BUDGET`'s own doc\ncomment (`recall.rs:40-48`) argues the volatile channel \"competes with recalled\nmemories and skills for one turn's attention\" — and then budgets only itself.\n\n### D8 — Tool advertisement has no relevance path that fires by default\n\n`crates/stella-core/src/driver.rs:1403` calls `self.tools.schemas()` per model\ncall, unfiltered. The measured cost is stated in-tree at\n`crates/stella-core/src/estimator.rs:918` (~6–9k tokens/request; the calibration\ntest models it as a flat 8,000). Lean mode + activation is fully built\n(`discovery.rs:797-822`, session-durable via `ActivatedTools` at `:150-156`) and\nis **env-only, off by default, with no settings key** — `with_lean` is\n`#[cfg(test)]` (`discovery.rs:339-343`). Its only in-repo consumer is a bench\nmatch file. Already filed as #1856 and #3033.\n\nAlso: MCP is capped per-server at 32 KiB (`crates/stella-mcp/src/toolset.rs:88-105`)\nwith **no aggregate ceiling** across native + custom + N servers.\n\n### D9 — The promote/retire loop is a closed circuit with both ends unwired\n\nSkill promotion runs on frequency alone: `AutoCreateConfig::require_measured_lift\n= false` (`crates/stella-core/src/skills.rs:797-806`). Retirement can never fire:\nnothing writes `skill_trials.jsonl`, so `sweep` always sees an empty ledger and\n`decide_demotion` always returns `Keep` (`crates/stella-cli/src/memory/appraisals.rs:56-198`,\nall `#[allow(dead_code)]`). Turning the eval gate on today would stop all\npromotion, exactly as its own doc comment predicts.\n\n### D10 — Frame verification and anchor staleness are dead in the binary\n\nCGP `verify` is implemented at two layers\n(`crates/stella-cli/src/contextgraph.rs:161-163`, `:240-248`) with a documented\ndigest-comparison contract, and **no CLI caller invokes it**. There is no\ncross-turn frame reuse cache, so a memory recalled at turn 1 and still in history\nat turn 30 is never revalidated. `scan_stale_anchors`\n(`crates/stella-cli/src/memory/anchor_scan.rs:34`) runs only from\n`stella memory validate` and is read-only by default, so a memory anchored to a\ndeleted file is recalled indefinitely.\n\n---\n\n## 4. The design — one `SteeringPlane`\n\n### The shape\n\nOne port in `stella-core`, one adapter per source, one call site per boundary.\n\n```\n                    ┌─────────────────────────────────────┐\n   turn boundary ──▶│  SteeringPlane::query(&TurnSignal)   │──▶ SteeringSet\n   step boundary ──▶│                                     │\n                    └─────────────────────────────────────┘\n                       │        │         │        │\n                   records   skills    tools    plugins\n                   adapter   adapter  adapter   adapter\n```\n\n**`TurnSignal` is the thing that does not exist today.** Every current selector\ntakes a `&str` prompt. The signal must carry what the turn has *become*:\n\n| Field | Source | Why |\n|---|---|---|\n| `prompt` | the turn's goal | what we have today |\n| `recent_tool_calls: &[ToolName]` | driver step history | D6 — the turn drifted |\n| `touched_paths: &[PathBuf]` | file tool receipts | real anchors, incl. **created** files (fixes `recall.rs:522`) |\n| `active_domains` | `query_domain_scope(anchors)` | D1 |\n| `step: u32`, `since_last_query: u32` | driver | re-query cadence + hysteresis |\n| `errors_seen: &[DiagnosticCode]` | diag plane | \"we are stuck\" is the strongest possible retrieval cue |\n\n`SteeringSet` returns typed candidates, each carrying `source`, `handle`,\n`score`, `why`, and `est_tokens` — so one budgeter can rank a rule against a\nskill against a tool schema instead of four budgets that cannot see each other.\n\n### Non-negotiables\n\n1. **Pure decision logic in `stella-core`** (invariant #2). The plane is a port;\n   adapters do the I/O. This is what makes it property-testable, and skill\n   selection is already the right shape — it just needs the other three joined\n   to it.\n2. **Prefix stability is not negotiable** (invariant #7). The plane may write to\n   the **volatile tail only**. Nothing it returns may enter the byte-stable\n   prefix mid-session. `inject_recall_block`'s append-never-rewrite discipline\n   (`recall.rs:652-700`) is the pattern; keep it.\n3. **Hysteresis, or the cache dies.** A re-query that changes the volatile block\n   every step invalidates nothing (tail append), but it *does* pay for the block\n   each time. Re-query on signal change, not on a step counter.\n4. **Every injected candidate is attributable.** D4 exists because the render\n   and the receipt disagree about a format. One renderer, one parser, a\n   round-trip property test — this is the `serde-first` invariant applied to the\n   prompt.\n5. **One budget.** `est_tokens` across all sources, one `pack_to_budget`, one\n   drop report. Today there are three packing passes over recall alone using two\n   different units (tokens, then chars).\n\n---\n\n## 5. The flag and its precedence\n\n### The check Mac asked for\n\n**Your instinct matches the house convention, with one exception you should keep.**\n\nThe dominant pattern in this tree is *env wins over settings*:\n\n- every clap `env =` slot — `crates/stella-cli/src/cli.rs:116,136,156,168,183,201`\n- `STELLA_ENGINE_CONFIG_JSON` replaces `agent_engine_config` wholesale —\n  `crates/stella-cli/src/config.rs:610-614`\n- `STELLA_NO_SETTINGS` short-circuits the entire chain — `merge.rs:363`\n\nThe one deliberate inversion is `--tools`, folded in **after** settings so it can\nonly narrow (`crates/stella-cli/src/main.rs:1016-1025`, \"the lowest-authority\nscope (#1263)\"). The principle behind that inversion is not \"CLI loses\" — it is\n**withholding is always allowed, granting is bounded by the ceiling.**\n\nSo the consistent rule, which I recommend adopting verbatim:\n\n```\nbuilt-in default (ON)\n  < user settings\n  < org-managed settings\n  < project settings\n  < STELLA_CONTEXT_STEERING            ← env wins the ordinary chain\n  ⟂ managed ceiling applied LAST       ← if the org turned it off, nothing turns it on\n```\n\nRead as two sentences: **anyone may turn steering off; only the org may prevent\nit being turned back on.** That is exactly `apply_tool_ceiling`'s existing\ntwo-part shape (`crates/stella-cli/src/settings/authority.rs:210-239`) and needs\nno new concept.\n\n### Truthiness\n\nUse `settings::env_flag` (`crates/stella-cli/src/settings.rs:1443-1460`), the\nstrict `1|true|yes|on` allowlist. Its doc records why: the looser\n\"non-empty and not `0`\" convention meant `STELLA_TRUST_PROJECT=false` **opened**\nthe trust boundary. Do not use the looser form, even though\n`term_policy.rs`/`env_files.rs` do — and note `STELLA_NO_ANIM` is currently read\nthree incompatible ways in one binary (`term_policy.rs:70` vs `init_fx.rs:245`\nvs `stella-tui/src/deck_shell.rs:519`), which is the cost of having two\nconventions.\n\n### The trap that will eat this flag if it is not read first\n\n`overlay_scope` (`crates/stella-cli/src/settings/merge.rs:153-247`) is a\n**hand-maintained allowlist with no completeness check**. A `Settings` field not\nnamed there parses in every scope and merges to `None`. Six comments in that file\nbeg future authors to remember, because it already shipped once for\n`enable_recap` (`merge.rs:184-195`).\n\n**It is currently shipping again, undetected:** `ProviderSettings::overlay`\n(`settings.rs:121-137`) copies eight fields and omits `upstream_pin`, so\n`providers.<id>.upstream_pin` is dropped from all three scopes and\n`config.rs:282` always reads `None`. The only test exercises the pure\n`pin_source` and never goes through the merge. That is a separate bug worth its\nown issue; here it is the proof that the trap is live.\n\nFour sites must change in lockstep for one key: the struct (`settings.rs`),\n`overlay_scope` (`merge.rs:153`), `unknown::ROOT_FIELDS` (`unknown.rs:43-59`, or\nthe key warns as a typo), and the TOML document + lowering (`toml_config.rs`).\nPhase 0 below adds the missing completeness test so the next flag cannot repeat\nthis.\n\n### Scope note\n\n`context` is currently a **whole-block last-wins** merge honored from an\nuntrusted project scope (`merge.rs:229-231`), unlike `context_providers` which\nwas moved behind the code-execution trust boundary after an RCE\n(`merge.rs:286-294`). A steering flag that can reach plugins or MCP **must** sit\non the trusted side. Decide this in Phase 0, not later.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Phases\n\nSized so each lands independently and each is provable. Phases 1–2 are worth\nshipping even if the epic stops there.\n\n### Phase 0 — make the flag safe to add (no behavior change)\n\n- Add the `overlay_scope` completeness test: every `Settings` field is named in\n  the overlay or explicitly listed as intentionally-unmerged. Witness: it fails\n  today on `upstream_pin`.\n- Fix `ProviderSettings::overlay`'s dropped `upstream_pin` (or file it and\n  reference it).\n- Decide the trust side for `context` (§5 scope note) and record it in\n  `merge.rs`'s comment block.\n- **Done when:** a new settings key cannot silently merge to `None`.\n\n### Phase 1 — the cheap correctness fixes (no new architecture)\n\nEach is independently witnessable and none needs the plane.\n\n- **D1**: swap `&self.domains.names()` → `query_domain_scope(...)` at the three\n  `select_skills` call sites. Witness: a skill tagged with an inactive domain is\n  no longer selected for an unrelated prompt (fails on `main`).\n- **D2**: asymmetric coverage score in `select_skills`, and match the body.\n  Witness: a long prompt selects a lexically-matching untagged skill.\n- **D4**: one renderer + one parser for recall lines, marker included on the\n  pipeline path. Witness: a `stella run` turn produces a non-empty\n  `memory_citations` row. **This is the one that unblocks every measurement\n  downstream** — D9's retirement, selection health, and any A/B of the plane\n  itself are all dark until it lands.\n- **D7**: budget the skills and draft-claims sections.\n- **Done when:** the existing planes are honest about relevance and the loop can\n  measure itself.\n\n### Phase 2 — the flag and the seam (still one query per turn)\n\n- `SteeringPlane` port in `stella-core` + `TurnSignal`/`SteeringSet` types.\n- Adapters wrap the four existing selectors unchanged — this phase is pure\n  refactor, same output.\n- One budgeter, one drop report, one `why` field per candidate.\n- `context.steering.enabled` (default on) + `STELLA_CONTEXT_STEERING`, per §5.\n- **Done when:** turning the flag off removes every non-prefix injection, and a\n  golden test shows the on-path output is byte-identical to today's.\n\n### Phase 3 — proactive re-query (the directive's actual ask)\n\n- Populate `TurnSignal` from live turn state (tool calls, touched paths incl.\n  created, diagnostic codes).\n- Re-query at step boundaries on signal change, with hysteresis.\n- Fix D5's three `NoContextRecall` surfaces in the same PR — the plane makes it\n  one wiring change instead of three.\n- **Done when:** a turn that drifts from its opening prompt injects context\n  relevant to where it *is*. Witness: a two-phase task where the phase-2 skill\n  is provably absent on `main`.\n\n### Phase 4 — tools join the plane\n\n- Tool advertisement becomes a plane consumer: the ordered-menu design of #3033\n  ranked by `SteeringSet`, not by `sort_by_name`.\n- Settings key for lean (D8), aggregate MCP ceiling.\n- **Done when:** always-visible schema tokens are measured flat or lower against\n  today, with the bench arm #1856 asks for — not token counts alone.\n\n### Phase 5 — plugins join as a fifth source\n\nDeliberately last, and specified separately (see the plugin analysis filed\nalongside this). The plane's `source` enum and `SteeringSet` contract are what a\nplugin contributes through; designing them in Phase 2 with a fifth variant in\nmind costs nothing now and avoids a second seam later.\n\n---\n\n## 7. What I did not verify\n\nStated so nobody inherits an unfalsifiable claim:\n\n- **The 12-tool surface is not on `main`.** `main@a4b87649a`'s catalog declares\n  79 entries; `worktree-tool-purge` declares 12. Everything in §2/§3 about tools\n  describes the 79-entry tree. If the purge lands first, D8 shrinks\n  dramatically — but the plane matters *more*, because with 12 built-ins every\n  other capability arrives via MCP, custom tools, skills and plugins, which is\n  precisely what the plane ranks.\n- **I did not measure any of this.** The ~6–9k token figure is quoted from\n  `estimator.rs:918`, not re-measured. No claim here about *solve rate* impact\n  is made or implied; Phase 4's done-condition deliberately demands a bench arm\n  rather than a token count, per #1856's own instruction.\n- **D9's \"retirement can never fire\" is read from `#[allow(dead_code)]` plus the\n  absence of a writer**, not from a runtime observation of an empty ledger.\n- The four surveys behind this issue were produced by parallel readers; every\n  citation in §3 that carries a behavioral claim was spot-checked against the\n  tree, but **not every line number in §2's table was independently re-read.**\n\n## 8. Related issues\n\nExisting, not duplicated: #3033 (tool menu), #1856 (lean default + MCP schema\ncap), #2476 (recalled memory text doubled), #2431 (no witness that a cited\nmemory reaches `memory_citations` — D4 is the mechanism behind it), #2302\n(untrusted workspace silently withholds steering), #3162 (context records\nunsearchable), #2780 (evolution-surface ledger), #2694 (tool-first epic).\n\nClosed but not fixed, and load-bearing here: #2900 (`recall_context` on one\nsurface — see D5).\n\n\n\n---\n\n## Alignment note — where the steering plane sits for a wrapped run (2026-08-16)\n\nDesign: `doc:turn-loop-wrappers` §4. Refs #3380.\n\nThe turn-loop wrapper architecture adds a four-point contract for anything that\nwraps a turn (`before_turn` / `after_turn` / `judge` / `again?`), and the staged\npipeline becomes the first plugin using it.\n\n**This does not add a fifth plane.** `before_turn` is *where the steering plane\nis asked its one question* on a wrapped run — it is a call site, not a selector.\nThe four engines this issue collapses stay collapsed into one.\n\nTwo consequences worth recording here so nobody re-derives them:\n\n- **The pipeline's recall stage is a `before_turn` caller, not a separate\n  corpus.** Today the pipeline recalls through its own port\n  (`crates/stella-pipeline/src/ports.rs`), which is a fifth path to the same\n  question by another name. Once #3380 lands it should be asking the steering\n  plane, and D5's per-surface wiring gaps stop being per-surface.\n- **#3365 (serve's missing recall port) should be designed as the steering\n  plane's reverse-RPC request, not as a pipeline-only one.** That issue's own\n  constraint already anticipated `TurnSignal`-shaped queries reaching the\n  pipeline; treat that as binding. One wire message, answering one question,\n  serving every wrapper and every surface.\n\nNothing in this epic's phases or defect list changes. This is a note about who\ncalls the seam, not about what the seam does.\n"[m[1;37m,[m
