    [1;34m"body"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"## Problem\n\nThe tool catalog (`crates/stella-tools/src/catalog.rs`) is the reservation source for `custom::RESERVED_NAMES`. With the catalog reduced to 12 rows, names like `bash`, `read_file`, `write_file`, `web_fetch` are **no longer reserved against custom-tool shadowing** — an operator manifest may now define a custom tool with any of those names.\n\nThis is the honest consequence of the catalog being the single source of truth, but it has two sharp edges:\n\n1. A custom tool named like a famous retired built-in inherits the model's strong priors about that name's semantics — a `bash` custom tool that is not a shell will be called as if it were one.\n2. Session steering, hook matchers, and operator policy written against historical names (`tools.bash: \"off\"` in an org-managed file) silently target the custom tool instead of nothing.\n\n## Decision needed\n\nEither (a) accept open naming and document it in the custom-tools docs, or (b) maintain a small deny-list of retired names in `custom.rs` validation with a pointer to why. Note `ManagedAuthoritySettings` dropped its bash/web fields in the purge (`crates/stella-cli/src/settings/authority.rs`) — org-managed files carrying those keys now fail closed under `deny_unknown_fields`, which is related compatibility fallout to decide in the same pass.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nA reviewed decision shipped as either docs or validation, with a test witnessing whichever contract is chosen.\n"[m[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"number"[m[1;37m:[m 3237[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"title"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"tools: retired built-in names are no longer reserved against custom-tool shadowing"[m
  [1;37m}[m[1;37m,[m
