    [1;34m"body"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"## Context\n\nA deck sub-session (`crates/stella-cli/src/subsession.rs`) is a real engine session — its own provider, tool registry, budget guard, execution row and event lane — dispatched onto its own OS thread *because* the lead conversation is mid-turn. It runs the full engine step loop.\n\nIt used to build its engine through `agent::engine_config_for`, which attaches the session's `checkpoint_sink`. Because `Config` is `Clone` over one `Arc` cell (`crate::durability::SessionDurability`), the worker and the lead were two writers of one `CHECKPOINT_BLOB`: every worker step overwrote the lead's in-flight transcript, and the worker's terminal `discard` deleted the lead's resume point while the lead still needed it. That is now fixed — `agent::subsession_engine_config_for` strips the sink, mirroring what `stella_core::subagent` already does for dispatched children.\n\n**Stripping was the correct fix for the damage, but it leaves a sub-session with no step-level durability at all.** This issue is that follow-up.\n\n## Problem\n\nA sub-session's turn is not recoverable. Kill the deck while a `req:<n>` or `sub:<task-id>` worker is 30 steps into a delegated task and the whole transcript is gone — the worker's file edits remain in the shared tree, but nothing records the conversation that produced them, and nothing can resume it.\n\nThe strip is deliberate and is documented as such at `crates/stella-cli/src/agent/engine.rs::subsession_engine_config_for`: a worker lane has no durable identity of its own, so a second sink would be a write with no reader, paid for with a whole-transcript serialization per step (`Engine::persist_checkpoint` encodes before the sink ever sees the bytes). Giving a lane a real identity is what would make that cost worth paying.\n\n## What \"done\" looks like\n\n1. Each live sub-session lane binds its own `SessionDurability` under its own session id, so its turns checkpoint into their own `refs/stella/<session>/head` namespace. `crates/stella-store/src/work_journal.rs`'s module docs establish that concurrent sessions over one workspace are safe by design — separate ref namespaces, separate `GIT_INDEX_FILE`, sharing only the object store — so the lead and N workers can each hold a binding without contending.\n2. A read path: something must be able to find and re-enter an interrupted lane's turn. Today `stella resume` (`crates/stella-cli/src/agent/resume.rs`) restores the session record's own turn and knows nothing about lanes.\n3. `a_sub_session_carries_no_checkpoint_sink` in `crates/stella-cli/src/agent/tests/durability_isolation.rs` is the assertion that will fail when this lands — that is intentional, and it is the right place for the review conversation to happen. Replace it with an assertion that the lane's sink is *different from the lead's*, not that it is absent.\n4. `a_sub_sessions_turn_cannot_destroy_the_leads_resume_point` (same file) must still pass: whatever identity a lane gets, it must not be the lead's.\n\n## Constraints\n\n- A lane already has an identity for other purposes: `SubSessionSpec::lane` (`req:<n>` / `sub:<task-id>`) plus the spawn `generation` counter that `SubSessions::started` mints. The generation matters — a lane can be restarted, and two spawns of one lane must not share a resume point.\n- The deck re-binds `cfg.durability` on every in-deck session switch (`crates/stella-cli/src/command_deck.rs:1225`). Lane bindings must not be stranded or silently re-pointed by a switch.\n- `bind_session` also calls `registry.restore_observed(...)` and `registry.attach_work_journal(...)`. A sub-session builds its own `ToolRegistry`, so this is cleaner here than in the fleet case (#3232), but the staleness-map semantics across lead and lanes in one shared tree need deciding: a lane and the lead reading the same file is normal, and the no-clobber guard must not start refusing legitimate writes.\n\n## Related\n\n- #3232 — the same missing-durability question for fleet workers, which has the same read-side blocker.\n- `crates/stella-cli/src/durability.rs` — `SessionDurability`'s \"What must NOT inherit a binding\" section names all three doors (sub-agent, isolated candidate, sub-session).\n"[m[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"number"[m[1;37m:[m 3233[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"title"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"deck sub-sessions have no step-level durability — a killed lane loses its whole transcript"[m
  [1;37m}[m[1;37m,[m
