    [1;34m"body"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"## Problem\n\n`resolve_ts_relative` in `crates/stella-graph/src/import.rs` builds its candidate list from the TS/JS extension ladder, and only pushes the *literal* joined path when the specifier already carries a **recognized** extension (`ts`/`tsx`/`js`/`jsx`/`mjs`/`cjs`/`json`). `ImportSpec::PathRelative` — C's quoted `#include \\\"kvstore.h\\\"` and PHP's `require 'bootstrap.php'` — routes through the same function, and `.h`/`.php` are not on that list, so the candidates become `kvstore.h.ts`, `kvstore.h.tsx`, …, `kvstore.h/index.ts` — never `kvstore.h` itself. A quoted include or a PHP require therefore **never resolves to a file that exists**, and every such edge is stored unresolved.\n\nThe existing tests never caught this because they assert only the `ImportSpec` classification (`crates/stella-graph/src/parse.rs`, `added_language_tests::c_symbols_and_both_include_forms` / `php_symbols_and_the_two_import_mechanisms`), not the resolution.\n\n## Where\n\n- `crates/stella-graph/src/import.rs` — `resolve_ts_relative` (the candidate ladder) and the `ImportSpec::TsRelative | ImportSpec::PathRelative` arm of `resolve`.\n- The `ImportSpec::MarkdownLink` arm added by #3103's PR is the shape the fix wants: resolve the literal path against the importing file's directory via `existing_within_root`. `PathRelative` should take literal-first resolution (then, if desired, no ladder at all — C and PHP name their files exactly). Do **not** change `TsRelative`'s behavior: `import './x'` must keep resolving to `x.ts` first, not to an extensionless file `x`.\n\n## Repro / verify\n\nEnd-to-end, in `crates/stella-graph/tests/languages.rs` (the `Fixture` harness):\n\n- files `src/main.c` = `#include \\\"kvstore.h\\\"\\n` and `src/kvstore.h` = `int put(void);\\n`\n- `fx.import_targets(\\\"src/main.c\\\")` should contain `src/kvstore.h`; today it contains only the unresolved specifier.\n- Same shape for PHP: `require 'bootstrap.php'` beside a real `bootstrap.php`.\n\n## Definition of done\n\n- `PathRelative` resolution tries the literal path (first), so both repro cases resolve; `importers_of` returns the reverse edges.\n- A witness test in `tests/languages.rs` that fails on today's `main` and passes with the fix, for both C and PHP.\n- `TsRelative` behavior unchanged (existing tests `typescript_index_and_relative_imports_resolve` / `javascript_require_resolves_relative` keep passing — note `require('./math')` is classified `TsRelative`, not `PathRelative`, so it keeps the ladder).\n\nFound while implementing #3103 (markdown link edges), which needed literal-path resolution and added its own arm rather than silently widening this one."[m[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"number"[m[1;37m:[m 3197[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"title"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"stella-graph: C #include \"x.h\" and PHP require 'x.php' never resolve — the candidate ladder omits the literal path"[m
  [1;37m}[m[1;37m,[m
  [1;37m{[m
    [1;34m"body"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"## Problem\n\nPR for #3163 gives `search` a session-lifetime per-file gathered-context cache: `crates/stella-tools/src/search/cache.rs`, consumed by `gathered_neighborhood` in `crates/stella-tools/src/search/enrich.rs`. Entries are keyed by `(path, sha256(file bytes))`, which invalidates **exactly** for every fact that is a function of the file's own bytes (symbols, kinds, import specifiers, and all source-derived facets, which are re-read fresh anyway).\n\nTwo facts in the cached `FileNeighborhood` are drawn from the *rest of the tree*:\n\n- `importers` — files whose imports resolve to this file;\n- the resolution of this file's own import specifiers to paths (`stella_graph::CodeGraph::file_neighborhood` returns resolved paths where resolution succeeds).\n\nFile B gaining an import of A does not change A's bytes, so A's cached entry keeps its old `importers` list until A itself changes or the entry is evicted (256-entry LRU). The per-symbol facets (callers, callees, body spans) are deliberately **not** cached and stay live for exactly this reason — the gap is confined to the two neighborhood facets above.\n\n## Repro / verify\n\n1. Search for a symbol defined in `a.rs`; note the `imported by:` line.\n2. Add `b.rs` importing `a.rs`; search again **in the same session** without touching `a.rs`.\n3. The cached entry serves the old `imported by:` list (a fresh session, or any edit to `a.rs`, shows the new one).\n\nA regression test would drive `enrich::render_hit` twice against one `GatherCache` with an importer added and the graph re-indexed between renders, asserting the second render names the new importer.\n\n## Constraints discovered\n\n- A per-call flush keyed on this call's own `index_all` stats (`IndexStats::files_parsed`/`files_pruned`) is NOT sufficient: the session's live watcher (`CodeGraph::mount` in the CLI session graph) can index B's change first, so the search call's own catch-up pass sees nothing to do.\n- A correct generation stamp is a digest over the index's `(path, sha)` rows (one table scan per search call) — equal digest proves every graph-derived fact is byte-identical to gather time; unequal flushes the whole cache. `stella-graph` would need a small read-only accessor for it.\n- The decision logic should stay pure/property-testable (invariant 2); `cache.rs` already has no I/O.\n\n## Done\n\n- Either the generation stamp above (or an equivalent) keys the cross-file half, or `importers`/import-resolution are excluded from the cached value and queried live, with the module docs' \"precision boundary\" section updated to match.\n- The regression test in step 3 exists and passes.\n\nRefs #3163 (the cache itself and where the boundary is documented: `crates/stella-tools/src/search/cache.rs` module docs)."[m[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"number"[m[1;37m:[m 3196[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"title"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"search gather cache: cross-file facets (importers, import resolution) can serve one generation stale under per-file identity keys"[m
  [1;37m}[m[1;37m,[m
  [1;37m{[m
    [1;34m"body"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"## The defect\n\n`crates/stella-tools/src/catalog.rs` declares a tool named `task` (sub-agent\ndelegation, #922) whose `group` is also named `task` — the group shared with the\nsix task-board tools (`task_create`, `task_list`, `task_start`, `task_complete`,\n`task_cancel`, `task_assign`).\n\n`ToolPolicy::allows` (`crates/stella-tools/src/policy.rs:85`) resolves a switch\nkey exact-name-first, then by group, so the single key `task` is irreducibly\nambiguous:\n\n- `--tools \"*:off,task:on\"` (or `\"tools\": {\"task\": \"on\"}` under a wildcard\n  deny) re-enables **seven** tools when the operator may have meant one — the\n  exact mechanism measured on the wire in #3120, where `search:on` leaked\n  `grep`/`glob`/`graph_query`/`semantic_code_search`.\n- `\"tools\": {\"task\": \"off\"}` disables the delegation tool **and** the whole\n  board; an operator who only wanted to stop sub-agent spawning cannot say so.\n\n## Why it was not fixed alongside #3120\n\n#3120's fix renamed the `search` group to `retrieval` (the measured leak) and\nadded the catalog invariant test\n`a_group_key_never_doubles_as_another_tools_switch`\n(`crates/stella-tools/src/catalog.rs`), which fails on any group that shares a\nname with a tool while holding other tools. `task` is the one declared\nexemption in that test, citing this issue: resolving it needs a vocabulary\ndecision a maintainer should make, because either direction silently changes\nwhat existing `\"task\": …` settings entries mean:\n\n1. **Move the delegation tool to its own group** (e.g. `subagent`): `task`\n   then addresses only the board... except the *group* `task` still collides\n   with the tool name `task` unless the board group is also renamed — the key\n   would still resolve exact-first to the tool and group-level for the board.\n   So this option requires renaming the board group too (e.g. `tasks` or\n   `board`), after which `\"task\": \"off\"` stops disabling the board (a silent\n   widening for configs that used it that way).\n2. **Rename the delegation tool** (e.g. `delegate`): keeps the group\n   vocabulary, but the tool name is load-bearing in prompts/steering and its\n   schema description; chase every mention in the same PR.\n\n## Definition of done\n\n- No `catalog!` row's `group` equals another row's `name`.\n- The `task` exemption in `a_group_key_never_doubles_as_another_tools_switch`\n  is deleted (the test then enforces the invariant with no carve-outs).\n- Consumers chased in the same PR: `crates/stella-tui/src/tool_class.rs`\n  (`classify` matches group strings; its `every_catalog_group_has_an_explicit_class`\n  test enforces the explicit list), `docs/tools/` regenerated\n  (`make tool-docs-update` — `category` field), the group list in\n  `website/content/docs/configuration/settings.mdx` (§ tool switch keys), and\n  any skill-grant docs naming the group.\n- The chosen direction and its settings-migration consequence stated in the PR\n  body (see #3120's PR for the `search`→`retrieval` precedent).\n\nRefs #3120."[m[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"number"[m[1;37m:[m 3192[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"title"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"Policy key `task` addresses two surfaces: the delegation tool AND the six task-board tools — the same group/tool name collision #3120 fixed for `search`"[m
  [1;37m}[m[1;37m,[m
  [1;37m{[m
    [1;34m"body"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"## Context\n\n#3183 routes C++ source (`.cpp`/`.cc`/`.cxx`/`.hpp`/…) through the existing C tree-sitter grammar so C++ files get *some* symbols instead of zero (search had nothing to rank over C++ codebases like `build-pov-ray` and fell to lexical scan). That's a dependency-free stopgap.\n\n## The sharper fix\n\nThe C grammar cannot parse C++-specific syntax — classes, templates, namespaces, member functions, `::` qualification — so those degrade to tree-sitter error nodes and their symbols are lost. A C++-heavy file (STL-heavy, template metaprogramming, class hierarchies) still under-indexes.\n\nAdd `tree-sitter-cpp` as an optional `lang-cpp` feature and a `Language::Cpp` variant:\n- `crates/stella-graph/Cargo.toml`: `tree-sitter-cpp` dep (workspace), `lang-cpp` feature (needs a deny.toml/license check — the workspace is AGPL dual-licensed, so verify the grammar's license before adding).\n- `crates/stella-graph/src/lang.rs`: add `Cpp` to the enum/`ALL`/`tag`/`ts_language`/`symbol_query`/`import_query`/`call_query`, and route the C++ extensions to it in `from_path`.\n- `crates/stella-graph/src/queries.rs`: `CPP_SYMBOLS`/`CPP_IMPORTS`/`CPP_CALLS` capturing classes, methods, namespaces, templates.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nA C++ file with a class method and a namespaced free function yields both as distinct symbols (the C grammar yields neither cleanly today). Witness: index a small C++ fixture, assert the class/method symbols appear. Re-point the C++ extensions from `Language::C` to `Language::Cpp` and update `cpp_source_and_headers_index_under_the_c_grammar`.\n\n## Related\n- #3183 (the stopgap this supersedes), #3172 (the bench investigation that surfaced it)."[m[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"number"[m[1;37m:[m 3184[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"title"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"search: index C++ with a dedicated tree-sitter-cpp grammar instead of routing .cpp through the C grammar"[m
  [1;37m}[m[1;37m,[m
  [1;37m{[m
    [1;34m"body"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"## Problem\n\n#3145 (PR: `fix/3145-tool-error-class`) gave `ToolOutput::Error` an optional\n`class: Option<ErrorClass>` and classified only the **mechanical tranche**:\n\n- the registry's unknown-tool refusal → `NotFound`\n  (`crates/stella-tools/src/registry.rs`),\n- every `crate::input::InputError` → `InvalidInput`, via one\n  `impl From<InputError> for ToolOutput` in `crates/stella-tools/src/input.rs`\n  (21 call sites converted),\n- `bash`'s wall-clock timeout → `Timeout` (`crates/stella-tools/src/bash.rs`),\n- the four approval / extension-policy denials → `RefusedByPolicy`\n  (`crates/stella-tools/src/registry/approval.rs`).\n\nThat is 32 classified constructions against **373 remaining\n`ToolOutput::error(...)` sites that are still unclassified** (`class: None`).\n`None` is a declared default meaning \"not audited yet\" — deliberately not a\nclass — so today an error-rate query can *exclude* the classified kinds but\ncannot yet attribute the rest.\n\nTwo things are therefore still missing before the ceiling #3145 exists to\nenable can actually be set:\n\n1. **The remaining sites are unaudited**, and nothing stops a new one being\n   added unclassified.\n2. **No ratchet**, so the unclassified count can drift upward silently.\n\n## What to do\n\n### 1. Classify the remaining sites\n\nEnumerate with:\n\n```sh\nrg -n 'ToolOutput::error\\(' crates/ | wc -l     # 373 at the time of filing\nrg -n 'ToolOutput::error\\(' crates/stella-tools/src\n```\n\nThe house rule from #3145's `ErrorClass` doc comment is *whose problem is\nthis*: the model's (`InvalidInput`, `NotFound`), the policy plane's\n(`PermissionDenied`, `RefusedByPolicy`), the world's (`Timeout`,\n`Environment`), or ours (`Internal`). `Internal` is the class an error-rate\nceiling is actually about, so it is the one that must not be applied loosely.\n\nHard constraint carried over from #3145: **the refusal message bytes must not\nchange.** The loop detector and the prompt cache both compare those strings; a\nclassification PR that rewords a message is a behavior change wearing a\nrefactor's clothes. Add the class beside the prose, never in it.\n\nSuggested slicing (one PR each, so review stays possible):\n\n- `stella-tools` file tools (`read.rs`, `write.rs`, `edit.rs`,\n  `apply_edits.rs`, `delete.rs`, `glob.rs`, `grep.rs`) — mostly\n  `NotFound` / `PermissionDenied` / `Environment`.\n- `stella-tools` process + exec (`bash.rs`, `exec.rs`, `process.rs`) —\n  `Environment` vs `Timeout` vs `Internal`.\n- `stella-tools` network-facing (`web.rs`, `ci.rs`, `issues.rs`, `repo/`) —\n  mostly `Environment`.\n- `stella-mcp` (`toolset.rs`, `client.rs`, `error.rs`) — an MCP server's\n  failure is `Environment` unless it is an auth refusal.\n- `stella-cli` / `stella-pipeline` synthetic errors — these are engine-side\n  and several are genuinely `Internal`.\n\n### 2. Add a down-only unclassified ratchet\n\nModel it on `scripts/check-typed-errors.py` +\n`scripts/typed-errors-baseline.txt` (AGENTS.md invariant #5 describes the\npattern and why a ratchet is legitimate only when the rule predates the\nguard — which is exactly the case here):\n\n- count `ToolOutput::error(` constructions per crate,\n- fail the gate if any crate's count **rises** above its baseline,\n- `make …-update` refuses to write a bigger number, so the only way past it is\n  to classify,\n- wire it into `GATE_STEPS` in the `Makefile` and update **both** AGENTS.md's\n  and CONTRIBUTING.md's gate blocks (`check-gate-parity.sh` fails otherwise).\n\nThe baseline starts at today's counts and is meant to reach zero.\n\n### 3. Per-class rollup (explicitly out of scope in #3145)\n\n`tool_usage_rollup` and the `~/.stella/usage.db` hub were deliberately **not\ntouched** by #3145: the hub is guarded by the content-free allowlist\n(`crates/stella-store/src/content_free.rs`, invariant #3), so adding a\nper-class column there needs a human to answer \"is this content?\" in the same\nPR. An `ErrorClass` token is a closed vocabulary with no user content in it, so\nthe answer is probably yes-it-is-safe — but it must be *decided*, with the\nallowlist edited in the same PR, not assumed.\n\nAlso worth doing here: the Observatory's per-tool error rate\n(`crates/stella-observatory/src/db.rs`, which selects `tool_calls` columns\nexplicitly and so was unaffected) should read `error_class` and split the\nnumber, since an unsplit error rate is the exact metric #3145 says cannot mean\nanything.\n\n## Definition of done\n\n- Every `ToolOutput::error(` site in `crates/` is either classified or listed\n  in the ratchet baseline with a reason.\n- The ratchet is a `make gate` step and refuses to rise.\n- A query answering \"error rate for `bash`, excluding `invalid_input` and\n  abandoned calls\" runs over `tool_calls` with **no string matching** (this is\n  #3145's own definition-of-done clause, and it is only half-met until the\n  sites are classified).\n- `bench/trace_triage`'s `tool-error-envelope` detector reads the class\n  (unlocks the #3053 verification path).\n\nRefs #3145, #3053, #3157 (the `abandoned` store state this class set\ndeliberately excludes), AGENTS.md invariants #3, #5, #10.\n"[m[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"number"[m[1;37m:[m 3167[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"title"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"classify the remaining ToolOutput::Error sites, with a down-only unclassified ratchet"[m
  [1;37m}[m[1;37m,[m
