    [1;34m"body"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"## Problem\n\nThe fused `search` tool (#3076, hardened in #3154) is positioned as the one place an agent goes to retrieve things — but whole categories of workspace truth are invisible to every rung of its ladder:\n\n- **The graph rungs cannot see TOML.** `Language::from_path` (`crates/stella-graph/src/lang.rs`, the `match ext` around line 51) has no `toml` arm, so `.toml` files are never parsed, never chunked, never embedded — `Cargo.toml`, `deny.toml`, `.stella/tools/*.toml`, and every context record among them. The semantic, exact-symbol, and name rungs all read the graph, so all three skip them.\n- **The scan rung skips `.stella/` wholesale.** `SKIPPED_DIRS` in `crates/stella-tools/src/search/scan.rs` (around line 45) contains `\".stella\"`, and the walk also skips every dotted entry. Right for `.stella/private/` (SQLite state must not be grepped), wrong for `.stella/rules/*.toml` — the published context records are the one part of `.stella/` deliberately tracked in Git *because they steer teammates' sessions* (AGENTS.md § \"The `.stella/` directory\"), and the tool built to surface the most relevant information first cannot see the repository's own steering policy.\n\nThe principle (from the maintainer, on #3154): the answer must show the most relevant information first regardless of category — embeddings, name match, scan, whatever rung — and nothing in the workspace should be unsearchable.\n\n## What done looks like\n\n- A query whose best answer is a context record (e.g. words from a record's rationale) surfaces that record's file, on a workspace with and without a built graph index.\n- TOML files rank alongside code rather than being silently absent: indexed (and embedded, where an embedder is configured) by `stella-graph`, or at minimum visible to the scan rung — the design choice between \"teach the graph a TOML adapter\" vs. \"un-skip specific `.stella` subpaths in the scan\" needs making, and the answer may be both.\n- `.stella/private/` stays unsearchable (secrets/SQLite; invariant 3's spirit), and the exclusion is explicit rather than a side effect of skipping all of `.stella`.\n- Witness tests for whichever rungs gain coverage (a record-shaped fixture that fails to surface on `main`, passes after).\n\n## Constraints already discovered\n\n- `SKIPPED_DIRS` skips dotted entries generally, so surfacing `.stella/rules/` needs a carve-out, not removal of the dot rule.\n- The graph's parser registry maps extension → tree-sitter grammar (`crates/stella-graph/src/lang.rs`, `parse.rs`, `storage.rs::AdapterFamily`); Markdown already shows the shape for a non-code format.\n- Context records have a spec (`docs/spec/adaptive-context/context-pr.md`) — a TOML record's searchable prose lives in specific fields, which may argue for field-aware chunking over raw-file embedding.\n- The scan rung's `MAX_FILES_SCANNED` cap and shallow-first walk (#3154) interact with any new visibility: more visible files must not silently push relevant ones past the cap.\n\nRefs #3154 (the maintainer's request, in the PR conversation), #886 (introduced the records), #2314 (records' TOML/markdown parsing hazard)."[m[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"number"[m[1;37m:[m 3162[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"title"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"search: context records and other TOML are unsearchable on every rung — nothing in the workspace should be beyond the one retrieval tool"[m
  [1;37m}[m[1;37m,[m
