    [1;34m"body"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"## Problem\n\n`search`'s enrichment (`crates/stella-tools/src/search/enrich.rs`, `Facet::Callers`) lists `graph.callers(&symbol.name)` for a hit's leading symbols. The lookup is name-based by design (#335: reverse name lookup, no resolution), so a hit whose leading symbol wears a common name shows callers of *unrelated* symbols — observed live on this repo: searching `SearchConfig` renders `fn test_the_label_is_a_set_not_a_sequence (arenabench/tests/test_tool_set.py:126)` as a \"caller\", because the Rust file's leading symbol `label` name-matches a Python call site.\n\nThe callees side was scoped to the hit's own file in the parent PR (`frame_is_about`), because a callee list is *about* this file's definition. Callers legitimately live in other files, so the same filter is wrong there — but cross-**language** hits are almost never the same symbol, and the line does not disclose that it is a name match at all (the `why:` line's \"not by meaning\" disclosure covers the ranking, not the facets).\n\n## Direction\n\n- Filter caller frames to call sites in the same language as the hit file (the graph stores `language` per file), which removes the worst conflations without claiming resolution.\n- And/or label the line `callers (by name):` so the model weighs it as the best-effort signal it is — the same honesty `graph_query callers` already has in its own docs.\n\n## Definition of done\n\n- The `SearchConfig` repro above no longer lists Python call sites for a Rust hit, or the line labels itself name-based; a test pins whichever is chosen.\n\n## Files\n\n- `crates/stella-tools/src/search/enrich.rs` — `Facet::Callers` branch, `frame_labels`\n- `crates/stella-graph/src/frames.rs` — `callers` (if per-language filtering lands there instead)\n\nRelated: #335 (name-based call edges, non-goals), #3138 (name-rung ranking)."[m[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"number"[m[1;37m:[m 3142[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"title"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"search: the callers facet conflates same-named symbols across files and languages, and does not say it is name-based"[m
  [1;37m}[m[1;37m,[m
  [1;37m{[m
    [1;34m"body"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"## Problem\n\n`stella_core::search::allocate` spends `Facet::estimated_cost` values (`Body` = 1,600 chars), but the renderer (`crates/stella-tools/src/search/enrich.rs`) then renders the real thing: a `Body` facet quotes up to `MAX_BODY_LINES = 40` source lines, which at ordinary line lengths is 3–4,000 characters, and caller/importer lists vary similarly. So an answer can exceed `STELLA_SEARCH_BUDGET` by a small multiple while reporting nothing — the budget is honest about what it *dropped* but not about what it *spent*.\n\n`Facet::estimated_cost`'s doc states the trade deliberately (\"an estimate that is uniformly a little wrong still orders the ladder correctly\"), and ordering is indeed unaffected. What is not covered is the absolute overshoot: an operator setting `STELLA_SEARCH_BUDGET=9000` to bound context spend can receive materially more. Since the promotion pass (this issue's parent PR) now grants deep facets much more often, the overshoot is more common than when depth 4 kept everything shallow.\n\n## Direction\n\n- Cheapest: measure the rendered block per hit and stop appending whole hit blocks once the real total passes the budget, reporting the stop in the existing TRUNCATED line. Whole-block granularity keeps the monotone-facet property intact (never truncate inside a block — a partial block would break the depth-superset test).\n- Alternatively: recalibrate `estimated_cost` against measured renders on this repo and accept a bounded error.\n\n## Definition of done\n\n- A test renders an answer whose estimated cost fits the budget but whose real render exceeds it, and asserts the rendered length is within one hit-block of the budget with the truncation line present.\n\n## Files\n\n- `crates/stella-core/src/search.rs` — `Facet::estimated_cost`, `allocate`\n- `crates/stella-tools/src/search.rs` — `render`\n- `crates/stella-tools/src/search/enrich.rs` — `render_hit`, `MAX_BODY_LINES`"[m[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"number"[m[1;37m:[m 3140[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"title"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"search: the character budget bounds an estimate, not the rendered answer — a real answer can exceed STELLA_SEARCH_BUDGET"[m
  [1;37m}[m[1;37m,[m
  [1;37m{[m
    [1;34m"body"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"## Problem\n\n`Search::schema()` (`crates/stella-tools/src/search.rs`) tells the model, in caps, that the tool \"matches by MEANING, not just by text\" and that the words used \"do not have to appear anywhere in the code\". That is true only when an embedder resolves (`STELLA_EMBED_URL`/`STELLA_EMBED_MODEL`, or the provider-key shortcuts). On a host with none configured — which is the *normal* case on Terminal-Bench per the module's own docs — the semantic rung never runs and every prose query degrades to term matching (see #3138 for how that ranks today).\n\nThe **answer** is honest (`via: names … (not by meaning)`), but the **description** is what the model decides on, and a model that believes the meaning promise will trust a term-matcher's miss as semantic absence — the exact \"miss reads as evidence of absence\" failure the coverage note exists to prevent, one level up.\n\n## Direction\n\nTwo options, either acceptable:\n\n1. **Configure an embedder for the benchmark** (and document that the description assumes one). #2994's in-process embedder would make this moot.\n2. **Vary the description at registration time.** `Search::from_env()` already runs once at registration; `stella_embed::from_env()` resolution is known at the same moment. A description that says \"matches by meaning\" only when an embedder resolved stays byte-stable for the whole session (invariant 7 holds — the schema is fixed per session, which is the actual cache boundary), and tells the truth on hosts where it can't.\n\n## Definition of done\n\n- On a host with no embedder, the advertised description no longer promises meaning-matching, OR the benchmark environment pins an embedder and the decision is recorded.\n- A test pins whichever behaviour is chosen (the existing `the_schema_offers_one_query_and_no_mode_selector` asserts on the description text and will need to know about the variant).\n\n## Files\n\n- `crates/stella-tools/src/search.rs` — `Search::schema`, `Search::from_env`\n- `crates/stella-embed/src/http.rs` — `Resolution`\n\nRelated: #2994, #3138, #3032 (the fused-tool experiment this tool exists for)."[m[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"number"[m[1;37m:[m 3139[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"title"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"search: the schema description promises meaning-matching that an embedder-less host cannot deliver"[m
  [1;37m}[m[1;37m,[m
  [1;37m{[m
    [1;34m"body"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"## Problem\n\nWithout an embedder, `search` answers prose queries through `Strategy::GraphNames` (`crates/stella-tools/src/search/enrich.rs::name_hits`), which scores a file by how many distinct query terms appear as **substrings** of its path + concatenated symbol names. Two compounding defects make this mis-rank behaviour-shaped queries:\n\n1. **No stemming.** `streaming` does not substring-match `StreamFallbackPosture` (`stream` would); `recovers` does not match `Recovery`. The spelling the query uses is exactly the one the haystack misses.\n2. **Symbol-count bias.** A file with 50 long snake_case symbol names has 50 chances to contain any term as an incidental substring; score is distinct-terms-matched, so large test files hoover up terms.\n\n## Repro (measured on this repo, 2026-08-13)\n\n```\nstella search \"how a provider recovers when its streaming path is broken\"\n```\n\nTop 10 are all bench/arenabench Python files. Rank 1, `bench/terminal_bench_analysis/tests/test_tb21_analysis.py`, matches `provider`, `recovers`, `path` as incidental substrings of its symbol list. The real answer, `crates/stella-model/src/provider_parity.rs` (`StreamFallbackPosture`), matches only `provider` — verified directly against `code_graph_symbols` — and is absent from the answer entirely.\n\nThe answer *is* honest (\"not by meaning\"), so this is a ranking-quality defect, not a lying-output defect. It matters most on the benchmark, where no embedder is configured and the name rung is what prose queries get.\n\n## Direction (not prescriptive)\n\n- Light suffix stemming of query terms (`-ing`, `-ed`, `-s` → ≥4-char stem) before substring matching.\n- Match against symbol *word units* (split on `_` and case boundaries) rather than the raw concatenation, to kill incidental-substring hits.\n- Weight terms by corpus rarity (a term matching half the tree carries no signal — the same argument `STOPWORDS` already makes, generalized).\n\nAny change here needs an eval, not vibes: extend `crates/stella-tools/tests/search_recall.rs` with no-embedder probes first, then tune against them.\n\n## Definition of done\n\n- The repro query ranks `crates/stella-model/src/provider_parity.rs` in the top 5 with no embedder configured.\n- A no-embedder probe set in `search_recall.rs` (it currently requires a live embedder) pins the behaviour so regressions are measured.\n\n## Constraints\n\n- Determinism (invariant 7): ranking must stay byte-stable across runs.\n- Keep `name_hits`'s term semantics in lockstep with `crate::graph::semantic`'s (the `STOPWORDS` comment states this contract).\n\nRelated: #3125 (exact-name rung, fixed), #2994 (in-process embedder would make the semantic rung the common case and shrink this rung's blast radius)."[m[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"number"[m[1;37m:[m 3138[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"title"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"search: the name rung mis-ranks prose queries — substring matching with no stemming and a per-file symbol-count bias"[m
  [1;37m}[m[1;37m,[m
  [1;37m{[m
    [1;34m"body"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"## Problem\n\n`crates/stella-context/src/store/node.rs:231` declares `pub valid_from:\nOption<String>` on the public `Node` struct, and its own doc comment says the\nfield is never populated:\n\n> **Always `None` today.** Only EDGES are versioned (see `MIGRATION_V1`);\n> `upsert_node` never writes `node.valid_from`, so this reads back the column's\n> NULL for every row. It is kept because the column exists and a point-in-time\n> node reader would populate it — not because anything currently sets it. Read\n> it as \"not yet wired\", never as \"unknown\".\n\nThe documentation is honest and the distinction it draws (*not yet wired* vs\n*unknown*) is the right one — a consumer that read `None` as \"no valid-time\ninformation exists for this fact\" would be drawing a false conclusion. But per\nAGENTS.md § \"Nothing left behind\", unwired surface needs a tracking issue, and\nthis field names none. This is that issue.\n\nSeverity is low: nothing is broken today, and the field is read-only in\npractice. The risk is a future consumer treating `None` as data.\n\n## Files\n\n- `crates/stella-context/src/store/node.rs:231` — the field and its doc.\n- `crates/stella-context/src/store/node.rs` — `upsert_node`, which does not\n  write the column.\n- `MIGRATION_V1` in the same crate — where edge versioning is defined, and the\n  reason nodes are currently unversioned.\n- `crates/stella-context/src/writeback.rs` — bitemporal fact writes\n  (`facts_as_of`, supersession), the closest existing model for what\n  point-in-time node reads would need.\n\n## Verify the gap\n\n```bash\nrg -n \"valid_from\" crates/stella-context/src\n# `upsert_node` should show no write to the column\n```\n\nA sharper check — insert a node and read it back; `valid_from` is `None`\nregardless of what was supplied.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nOne of:\n\n1. **Wire it.** `upsert_node` populates `valid_from`, and a point-in-time node\n   reader uses it. Witness: a test that writes a node with a valid-time\n   preceding its `recorded_at` and reads it back at an as-of timestamp, failing\n   before the change (the field comes back `None`) and passing after.\n2. **Or remove it** from the public `Node` projection, leaving the column in\n   place for a future reader. This is the smaller change and may be the right\n   one: a field that cannot carry information is a field a consumer can\n   misread, and the column's existence does not require the struct to expose\n   it.\n\nEither way, the doc comment's \"not yet wired\" phrasing should stop being the\nonly record of the gap.\n\n## Constraints already discovered\n\n- Valid time may precede `recorded_at` (observation) and never follows it —\n  the ordering is part of the bitemporal contract stated on the field.\n- `recorded_at` is a **creation** time, not a modification time: `upsert_node`\n  updates content in place without touching it, and recall's recency ranking\n  sorts on that. Any wiring must not change that meaning.\n- `stella-context` and `stella-graph` use separate databases\n  (`.stella/private/context.db` and `.stella/private/codegraph.db`). They used\n  to share one; do not revert that (AGENTS.md § Gotchas).\n- Nodes are currently unversioned by design — versioning them is a schema\n  decision with a migration, not a local edit.\n\n## Context\n\nFound during a first-pass adversarial audit of the workspace, alongside #3135\n(the same shape at protocol level, larger). Filed for tracking; not fixed in\nthe audit's PR because both options above are design calls."[m[1;37m,[m
