    [1;34m"body"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"## Why file-level embedding is not good enough\n\nSemantic search now works end to end (#2995 — the key in SSM was a MongoDB\nAtlas key returning 403; a real `pa-` Voyage key is stored as version 2). The\nindex on this workspace:\n\n```\n$ sqlite3 .stella/private/codegraph.db \"select 'files',count(*) from code_graph_files\n    union all select 'symbols',count(*) from code_graph_symbols\n    union all select 'vectors',count(*) from code_graph_vectors;\"\nfiles|1431\nsymbols|27150\nvectors|1431\n```\n\nOne vector per **file**. Here is what that buys, from a real query — *\"where is\nthe prompt cache engaged on outbound requests\"*:\n\n```\nvia: semantic (embedding rank over the code-graph index)\n 1 crates/stella-protocol/src/cache.rs                       0.656\n 2 crates/stella-pipeline/src/management_prompt.rs           0.641\n 3 crates/stella-tui/src/cache_panel.rs                      0.636\n 4 crates/stella-model/src/provider_parity.rs                0.628\n 5 crates/stella-cli/src/agent/prompt/tests.rs               0.621\n 6 crates/stella-model/src/openai.rs                         0.615\n 7 crates/stella-cli/src/agent/tests.rs                      0.612\n 8 crates/stella-mcp/src/suppress.rs                         0.607\n 9 crates/stella-cli/src/memory/reflection/tests.rs          0.606\n10 crates/stella-model/src/anthropic/tests/cache_breakpoints.rs 0.604\n```\n\nTwo defects in one result:\n\n- **The answer is absent.** The engagement site is Anthropic's `cache_control`\n  breakpoint insertion in `crates/stella-model/src/anthropic.rs`. It does not\n  appear. Only its *test file* does, at rank 10.\n- **The ranking barely ranks.** 0.656 to 0.604 — a **0.05 spread across ten\n  results**. That is a tie dressed as an ordering, and it is the expected\n  consequence of averaging a 900-line multi-topic file into one 1024-dim\n  vector. `render_file_text` already concedes the point: it truncates at\n  `MAX_CONTENT_CHARS = 6_000` and names at most `MAX_SYMBOL_NAMES = 60`,\n  because \"a whole large file dilutes its own vector besides\".\n\nThis matters beyond retrieval quality. #3063's whole argument for a fused\n`search` tool is that meaning-ranking gives the model something a shell\npipeline cannot assemble. At file granularity with a 0.05 spread, `rg -n\n'cache_control'` wins — so the tool loses the composability argument it was\ndesigned to win, and the tool-effectiveness arm would measure that loss.\n\n## The fix: embed symbols, not just files\n\nThere are **27,150 symbols against 1,431 files** — a 19x sharper index, and\n`code_graph_symbols` already carries `start_line`/`end_line`, so each symbol's\nbody is a slice of a file already being read.\n\nCost is not the obstacle. `voyage-code-3` bills around $0.06/1M tokens; at a\ngenerous ~400 tokens per symbol that is ~11M tokens, well under $1 for a full\ncold pass on this workspace, and incremental afterwards.\n\n### Schema\n\nA sibling table mirroring `code_graph_vectors` exactly, including its\ndeliberate rowid choice (`store.rs:165` — `WITHOUT ROWID` spills a ~6 KiB\nvector onto overflow chains against a 4 KiB page, and every rank pass reads\nevery row):\n\n```sql\nCREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS code_graph_symbol_vectors (\n    symbol_id      INTEGER NOT NULL REFERENCES code_graph_symbols(id) ON DELETE CASCADE,\n    fingerprint    TEXT NOT NULL,\n    content_sha256 TEXT NOT NULL,\n    dims           INTEGER NOT NULL,\n    vector         BLOB NOT NULL,\n    embedded_at    INTEGER NOT NULL,\n    PRIMARY KEY (symbol_id, fingerprint)\n);\nCREATE INDEX IF NOT EXISTS code_graph_symbol_vectors_fp\n    ON code_graph_symbol_vectors(fingerprint);\n```\n\nAdd `\"code_graph_symbol_vectors\"` to the table list at `store.rs:82`.\n\n**`content_sha256` must hash the symbol's own rendered text, not the file's.**\nHashing the file would re-embed every symbol in a file when one function\nchanges — the incremental pass exists precisely to avoid that.\n\n### `crates/stella-graph/src/vectors.rs`\n\nMirror the five existing functions. Signatures, following `pending`'s\npath-cursor discipline (#3016 — it fills *past* skips rather than spending the\nwindow on them, and the cursor is what makes resume work):\n\n```rust\npub fn render_symbol_text(rel_path: &str, name: &str, kind: &str, body: &str) -> String;\npub fn pending_symbols(conn: &Connection, root: &Path, fingerprint: &str, limit: usize)\n    -> Result<PendingSymbolScan, GraphError>;\npub fn store_symbol_vectors(conn: &mut Connection, fingerprint: &str, rows: &[SymbolVector])\n    -> Result<usize, GraphError>;\npub fn stored_symbols(conn: &Connection, fingerprint: &str)\n    -> Result<Vec<(SymbolRef, Vec<f32>)>, GraphError>;\npub fn symbol_count(conn: &Connection, fingerprint: &str) -> Result<usize, GraphError>;\n```\n\n`render_symbol_text` should lead with `path::name (kind)` then the body, for\nthe same reason `render_file_text` leads with the path: the model reading\n`stella-model/src/anthropic.rs::apply_cache_control (function)` has most of\nwhat it needs before a line of body. Keep it plain — no headings a tokenizer\npays for.\n\nThe cursor for `pending_symbols` is `(path, start_line)`, not `path` alone, so\nresume is stable within a file.\n\n### Ranking\n\n`search`'s semantic rung should rank **symbols**, then group hits by file so\nthe result still reads as \"these files, and here is the symbol in each that\nmatched\". That preserves the existing result shape while making the score mean\nsomething specific.\n\nKeep file vectors. They answer \"what is this module about\", which a symbol\ncannot, and the fallback ladder wants both rungs.\n\n## Definition of done\n\n- `code_graph_symbol_vectors` has one row per indexable symbol after\n  `stella init` on this workspace (expect ~27k, not 1,431).\n- **The witness is the query above.** `search(\"where is the prompt cache\n  engaged on outbound requests\")` must return\n  `crates/stella-model/src/anthropic.rs` in the top 3, and the spread across\n  the top 10 must exceed the current 0.05. Assert both — a test that only\n  checks the table is non-empty proves the pass ran, not that it helped.\n- A second query whose answer is a single function, to catch the case where\n  symbol embedding merely reshuffles rather than sharpens.\n- Incremental: editing one function re-embeds one symbol, not a file's worth.\n- `make gate` green.\n\n## Related\n\n#2995 (the key — now resolved and stored), #3063 (the `search` design this\nserves), #3087 (the empty index in bench containers, still open and separate),\n#3078 (the displacement metric this should move).\n"[m[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"number"[m[1;37m:[m 3089[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"title"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"Embed symbols, not just files — the file-level index ranks with a 0.05 spread and misses the answer"[m
  [1;37m}[m[1;37m,[m
