    [1;34m"body"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"## The problem\n\n`arenabench` match templates can now declare which tools a Stella seat is\noffered (`[contestant.engine] tool_set = [...]`, shipped for #3032). The value\nis validated for **shape** and never for **membership**:\n\n- `arenabench/arenabench/model.py::is_tool_name` accepts any\n  `[a-z][a-z0-9_]*`.\n- `bench/harbor_adapter/stella_harbor/tool_set.py::validated_tool_set` applies\n  the same shape rule on receipt.\n- `crates/stella-cli/src/discovery.rs::lean_from_env` builds a\n  `LeanConfig { core }` from the names and then *filters* the live schema list\n  by set membership — a name matching no registered tool simply matches\n  nothing.\n\nSo `tool_set = [\"bash\", \"read_fil\", \"write_file\"]` runs an arm advertising two\ntools, not three, and nothing anywhere says so. The provenance record\n(`tool_set_seats` in `provenance.json`) faithfully records the typo, so the arm\nis legible but **wrong**: its comparability key differs from the intended arm's,\nand the measured difference is attributed to the tool that was supposedly added.\n\nThis matters more than a normal typo because the tool set is the *independent\nvariable* of the tool-effectiveness experiment #3032 describes — the whole\nprotocol is \"add one tool per arm and measure the difference\".\n\n## Why it was not fixed in the shipping PR\n\nArenaBench deliberately does not link the tool catalog\n(`crates/stella-tools/src/catalog.rs`), and a hand-copied roster of names in\nPython would be wrong the first time a tool is added — the exact\nsecond-copy-drift failure `catalog.rs` exists to prevent. The correct fix is\ntherefore on the Stella side, where the catalog is already in scope.\n\n## Suggested fix\n\nIn `crates/stella-cli/src/discovery.rs`, after resolving `LeanConfig`, compare\n`core` against the session's resolved tool names and surface every unmatched\nname as a one-line boot notice — the same \"never a silent drop\" discipline\nAGENTS.md invariant #8 applies to a pinned reasoning effort against a provider\nthat cannot honour it. A notice rather than a refusal: lean mode is a\nprompt-budget measure and never a capability gate, and a hard failure would\nmake a stale template unrunnable rather than merely noisy.\n\nConsider also surfacing it in the adapter's trial manifest beside\n`stella_tool_set` (`bench/harbor_adapter/stella_harbor/__init__.py`), so an\nanalysis reads the unmatched names from metadata rather than from a log line.\n\n## Files\n\n- `crates/stella-cli/src/discovery.rs` (`lean_from_env`, `LeanConfig`, `DEFAULT_CORE_TOOLS`)\n- `crates/stella-tools/src/catalog.rs` (the name authority)\n- `bench/harbor_adapter/stella_harbor/tool_set.py`\n- `arenabench/arenabench/model.py` (`is_tool_name`)\n\n## Verify\n\nWitness: a session configured with `STELLA_LEAN_TOOLS=bash,read_fil` emits a\nnotice naming `read_fil`, and one configured with only real names emits none.\nFails on `main` today (no notice exists at all).\n\n## Constraints\n\n- `crates/stella-cli/src/agent.rs` and `src/command_deck.rs` are grandfathered\n  god files at exactly their recorded ceilings\n  (`scripts/file-size-baseline.txt`) — plan the notice so it does not add lines\n  there. `discovery.rs` has headroom.\n- Lean-mode execution is deliberately permissive (a hidden tool called by name\n  still runs), and that property must survive.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nAn operator who misspells a tool name in a match template learns it from the\nrun rather than from an unexplained result, and the unmatched names are\nrecoverable after the fact from the trial's recorded metadata.\n\n## Related\n\n- #3032 (the measurement this knob serves), #3033 (the design that would\n  replace `DEFAULT_CORE_TOOLS` with a `catalog.rs` column — this check becomes\n  cheaper once the resident set is derived there)."[m[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"number"[m[1;37m:[m 3082[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"title"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"bench: a declared tool_set is never checked against the tool catalog, so a typo silently runs a different arm"[m
  [1;37m}[m[1;37m,[m
