    [1;34m"body"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"# Spec: `search` — one way to find things\n\n## Status\n\nDesign. The tool itself is being built now; the prompt-time injection half and\nthe depth sweep are not started. Pipeline is **off** for all of this work —\nevery arm is the bare loop. The basics have to work before the staged pipeline\nis worth measuring again.\n\n## The problem, measured\n\nStella sends **72 tool schemas per request — 11,797 tokens, 71% of the opening\ncontext**, re-read on every turn. Claude Code sends 25, of which five are\ncoding tools. Across 408 bench trials and 15,304 completed model calls:\n\n| tool | calls | first reached at call |\n|---|---|---|\n| `bash` | 10,835 | 2.2 |\n| `grep` | 120 | 2.3 |\n| `glob` | 83 | 33.1 |\n| `project_overview` | 33 | 8.1 |\n| `read_symbol` | 23 | 8.0 |\n| `graph_query` | 3 | 9.7 |\n| `gather_context` | 0 | — |\n| `semantic_code_search` | 0 | — |\n\nSix ways to find code. The agent collapses to the shell.\n\n### The decisive evidence\n\n`gather_context` already implements the \"one fused sweep\" idea. Its description\nreads: *\"Run one deterministic context sweep (grep patterns + file globs +\ncode-graph symbol lookups + bounded excerpts) in a single call… Prefer this\nover issuing several separate grep/glob/graph_query/read_file calls.\"*\n\n**Zero calls in 408 trials.** Meanwhile `grep`, which does strictly less, is\nthe second thing Stella touches.\n\nSo the concept did not fail. The name did — `gather_context` names an internal\nconcept rather than the caller's intent. An agent mid-task thinks *\"where is\nthe header sanitised\"*; it does not think *\"I should gather context.\"*\n\nThe same lesson appears twice more. `glob` is reached at call 33.1 against\n`grep`'s 2.3 for overlapping work — familiarity beats capability. And\n`semantic_code_search` shipped with an eager index and an explicit prompt rule\npointing at it (#3014, #3020) and scored **zero calls in 19 trials**. Adding a\nbetter option to a crowded menu does not change what gets chosen.\n\nThree months of remedies — better descriptions, prompt steering, a dedicated\ntool — were all **additive**. Each made the menu longer while trying to fix\nwhat the menu was causing.\n\n## Design\n\n### One operation, no selector\n\n`search(query)`. We choose the strategy; the model does not.\n\nThis is not a stylistic preference. Invariant #9 states that a parameter may\n*scope* an operation and never *select* one. `search(query, mode=\"regex\" |\n\"semantic\" | \"symbol\")` would be three tools wearing one schema and would\nreproduce the present problem inside a single tool.\n\nThe payoff is control and durability. Dispatch is ours to tune, and the\ninterface outlives its implementations — embeddings can be swapped, an index\nadded, grep dropped entirely, and the agent's habits need not change because it\nnever knew.\n\n### Always rank semantically\n\nEvery `search` embeds the query and ranks indexed files, then enriches from the\ncode graph.\n\nBeyond retrieval quality, this fixes a measurement problem. Today `graph_query`\nhas 3 calls in 408 trials, so we cannot tell whether the code graph is bad or\nmerely unchosen — usage confounds quality. Behind a dispatcher that always runs\nit, every search produces evidence about whether the graph helped. An\nunanswerable question becomes a logged one.\n\n### Enrichment is budget-driven, not intent-driven\n\nAfter ranking, spend a fixed context allowance adding structure. Decide from\n**what was found**, not from what was asked.\n\nIntent inference is a heuristic, and a wrong inference is *unrecoverable* here\nprecisely because we removed the mode flag — the model has no way to ask\ndifferently. A budget degrades instead of misfiring: a small function affords\ncallers and execution paths; a 3,000-line file does not.\n\n### The depth ladder\n\nHow much to say about a thing, on a scale the maintainer described as *\"10 =\nevery bloody detail, 1 = just the name.\"*\n\n| depth | adds |\n|---|---|\n| 1 | name |\n| 2 | signature |\n| 3 | doc comment |\n| 4 | body |\n| 5 | caller names |\n| 6 | callee names |\n| 7 | caller excerpts |\n| 8 | callee excerpts |\n| 9 | import / execution paths |\n| 10 | file neighbourhood |\n\nThree properties, each load-bearing:\n\n- **Monotone.** Level *n+1* is a strict superset of *n*, so a sweep between\n  settings is interpretable. Without this, comparing depth 4 to depth 6 compares\n  two different things.\n- **Configuration, not an argument.** The model does not set it. Everything\n  measured says a knob the model must learn to use well will not be used well —\n  and as config it becomes a bench variable we can sweep while holding the rest\n  fixed.\n- **Per-hit within one allowance.** The top result can afford depth 8 while\n  results 2–5 get depth 2. Depth is intent; the token budget is the hard stop,\n  and a truncated response must **say** it truncated so the agent knows more\n  exists rather than silently receiving a partial view.\n\n### Fallback must be honest\n\nNo index, no embedder, or an unindexable workspace still returns useful results\nand **names which strategies ran**. `stella init` is best-effort by design and a\nworkspace with no tree-sitter grammar is the normal case on Terminal-Bench, so\nthis is not an edge case. A silent degradation here would be indistinguishable\nfrom the tool working.\n\n### Nothing is deleted\n\n`grep`, `glob`, `graph_query`, `read_symbol`, `project_overview` and\n`gather_context` keep existing and working. The experiment is about what is\n**sent**, not what exists.\n\n### Prompt-time injection (separable, not yet built)\n\nEmbed the task description at startup and inject top-k as context, before the\nfirst turn.\n\nThis is the only part that does not depend on the agent choosing anything,\nwhich is why it sidesteps the failure this whole spec is about. `stella init`\nalready runs before the first turn on its own budget and already builds the\nindex eagerly (#3014), so this is a small addition to work that happens anyway.\nIt lands context *before* the first grep rather than after the twelfth.\n\n## The first arm\n\nFive tools: **`search`, `read_file`, `edit_file`, `write_file`, `bash`.**\nBare loop. Pipeline off.\n\nNote this set has **no `verify_done`**, so the witness contract does not run and\nthe arm measures *raw* completion rather than *verified* completion. That is\nintended and must be stated wherever the number is reported. `verify_done`\ncurrently fails 92.5% of the time (419 of 453 calls) and is reached at call\n27.9, so its absence may help — which is itself worth knowing.\n\n## Measurement\n\nBaseline: run `post1` — 10 tasks × 2 attempts, claude-sonnet-5 via OpenRouter,\nbare loop. **30.0 tool calls per trial, 1.01 tools per turn, 16/20 solved,\n11,797 menu tokens.**\n\nReport, in this order:\n\n1. **Menu tokens** — mechanical, will fall, proves the cheap half.\n2. **`search` call count and its position** — does the agent reach for it, and\n   how early? `grep` sits at 2.2; anything above ~10 means it is a fallback.\n3. **`bash` share of calls** — the falsification. A cheaper menu that produces\n   the same collapse to the shell has saved tokens and fixed nothing. Worth\n   having, but not the win.\n4. Tool calls per trial, then solve rate.\n\n**Read no partial average as a result.** Trials complete in cost order, and\nthree separate conclusions dissolved this way in one day — one prompt\nexperiment read −52% at n=12 and finished at −2.7% with a worse solve rate.\n\n## What would falsify this\n\n- `search` is offered and unused, like `gather_context` before it. Then the\n  problem is neither naming nor menu size and the next hypothesis is elsewhere.\n- `search` is used and `bash`'s share is unchanged. Then search was never the\n  bottleneck.\n- Solve rate falls. Then six specialised tools were carrying weight the fused\n  tool does not.\n\n## Open questions\n\n- **Per-call embedding cost.** `search` would be called at roughly `grep`'s\n  rate — early and often. The eager whole-workspace index measured 1–3 seconds;\n  a single query embedding is smaller, but it sits on the hottest path and is\n  unmeasured.\n- **The default depth.** Unknown until swept. Start mid-ladder and move.\n- **Whether `execute` follows.** Merging shell and process was considered and\n  is *not* proposed here: `read_only` cannot be declared honestly for a tool\n  covering both `ls` and `stop_process`, which corrupts the engine's concurrency\n  contract, and per-tool policy could no longer withhold the destructive verb\n  without the benign one. `search` unifies things sharing a return type and a\n  safety class; `execute` would unify things sharing only a mechanism.\n\n## Related\n\n#3032 (the 72-tool menu, with the capture that proved schemas are sent),\n#3033 (the ordered-name-menu design), #3014 (`semantic_code_search` and the\neager index), #3020 (the prompt rule that did not move it), #639 (the same\ntoken-duplication argument, prompt side).\n"[m[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"number"[m[1;37m:[m 3063[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"title"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"spec: search — one way to find things, with budget-driven graph enrichment and a tunable depth"[m
  [1;37m}[m[1;37m,[m
