    [1;34m"body"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"## Problem\n\n`arenabench cloud run` now refuses to start unless a banned-behavior gate is\narmed or `--no-gate` waives it deliberately (PR #3044). The waiver is **printed**\nin the run header:\n\n```\ngate      : DISABLED (--no-gate)\n```\n\n…and that is all. It does not reach `s3://<bucket>/runs/<run-id>/jobs.json`, which\nis the durable record of what the run was, so a run that was submitted unguarded\nis indistinguishable from a guarded one to anything that reads the artifacts\nlater — `arenabench assemble`, `bench/trace_triage/postmortem.py`, or a human\nopening the mirror a week afterwards.\n\nThe same applies to `--gate-allow CODE`: a run that muted a specific banned\nbehavior is, on disk, a clean run.\n\nThat is the wrong direction for exactly the reason the gate exists. The\n`banned.py` half already refuses to lose this — its verdict JSON carries a\n`waived` list precisely so \"a run that muted a check cannot later be read as\nclean\" — but the *run-level* waiver has no such home.\n\n## Why it was not fixed in #3044\n\n`jobs.json` is written by `CloudExecutor.submit_run(...)`, whose returned\nstructure is the substrate contract every other cloud verb reads back\n(`load_jobs`, `fetch_results`, `cloud status`, `cloud_watch.CloudScoreboard`,\n`cancel_run`). Adding a field means touching that shape and its readers, which\nis a wider change than the gate itself and wants its own review. The current\nstate is recorded in a comment at the call site in\n`arenabench/arenabench/cloud.py` (`_cmd_cloud_run`, just above `print(gate_line)`).\n\n## Suggested shape\n\nAdd a `gate` object to the `jobs.json` record:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"run_id\": \"...\",\n  \"queue\": \"...\",\n  \"gate\": {\"armed\": false, \"command\": null, \"allowed\": [], \"waiver\": \"--no-gate\"},\n  \"jobs\": [...]\n}\n```\n\n`armed: true` carries the resolved command and any `allowed` codes. Readers that\ndo not know the key ignore it, so the change is additive.\n\nThen surface it: `postmortem.py` should say, in the report it writes onto the\nmatch, that the run was ungated or that behavior `X` was waived — a postmortem\nthat does not mention the run had no gate is missing the most load-bearing fact\nabout it.\n\n## Files\n\n- `arenabench/arenabench/cloud.py` — `submit_run`, `_cmd_cloud_run`\n- `arenabench/arenabench/gate.py` — `GateSpec`, `arm_gate` (the spec already\n  carries everything that needs recording; it just has nowhere to go)\n- `bench/trace_triage/postmortem.py` — the consumer that should surface it\n\n## Definition of done\n\nA run submitted with `--no-gate` or `--gate-allow CODE` leaves that fact in\n`jobs.json`; a test in `arenabench/tests/test_cloud.py` asserts it for both\nflags and for the armed case; and `postmortem.py` names it in the report.\n\n## Related\n\n- PR #3044 — the gate\n- #3024 — the arenabench↔trace_triage boundary the gate crosses by subprocess\n"[m[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"number"[m[1;37m:[m 3059[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"title"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"arenabench: a --no-gate or --gate-allow waiver is printed but never recorded, so an ungated run reads as clean on disk"[m
  [1;37m}[m[1;37m,[m
