    [1;34m"body"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"## Problem\n\n#2786 (`feat(stella-model): pin the gateway's upstream and record who served the\ncall`) added `upstream_provider: Option<String>` to `AgentEvent::StepUsage`\n(`crates/stella-protocol/src/event.rs`) and `CompletionResult`\n(`crates/stella-protocol/src/completion.rs`). Found while documenting\n`--upstream-pin` for #3042: the field is genuinely durable, but only as part of\nthe raw `events` table payload —\n\n```bash\nsqlite3 -json .stella/private/store.db \\\n  \"SELECT payload FROM events WHERE execution_id = 42 AND event_type = 'step_usage'\" \\\n  | jq -r '.[].payload | fromjson | select(.upstream_provider) | \"\\(.provider) → \\(.upstream_provider)\"'\n```\n\nNeither `step_receipt` nor the `telemetry` table (`crates/stella-store/src/ddl.rs`)\nhas a column for it — both carry only `provider`, which for a gateway call is\nalways the gateway's own id (`openrouter`), never the vendor it routed to. So:\n\n- **`stella inspect <EXEC>`** (`crates/stella-cli/src/inspect.rs`,\n  `RecordedCall`/`CallJson`) cannot show which vendor served a past call. It\n  reads `step_manifest`/`step_receipt`, not `events`.\n- **`stella stats`** and the Observatory read `telemetry`/`step_receipt`\n  aggregates, same gap.\n- **The TUI** constructs `upstream_provider` in test fixtures\n  (`crates/stella-tui/src/views/agents.rs`) but no non-test call site reads\n  the field to render it live either.\n\nA reader auditing a past run — the exact scenario #2786's own commit message\ndescribes (\"a gateway benchmark unauditable: a run that claims to hold the\nmodel provider fixed could not prove it did\") — cannot currently get the\nanswer from any of stella's normal query surfaces. They have to know to query\n`events` directly, which is documented as a workaround in\n`website/content/docs/telemetry/index.mdx` (added by #3042) but is not a\nsubstitute for the field reaching a real column.\n\n## What to build\n\nAdd an `upstream_provider` column to whichever table is the right home —\n`step_receipt` is the more natural fit (`crates/stella-store/src/ddl.rs`,\n`crates/stella-store/src/usage.rs` for the write path), since it's already the\nper-model-call table `provider` lives on. Wire it through:\n\n- The store write path that projects `AgentEvent::StepUsage` into\n  `step_receipt` (or wherever the equivalent projection lives today —\n  confirm whether one already exists per the pattern used for `tool_calls` in\n  `crates/stella-store/src/lib.rs::record_event`, or whether `step_receipt` is\n  written from a different call site entirely).\n- `stella inspect`'s `RecordedCall`/`CallJson` (`crates/stella-cli/src/inspect.rs`),\n  so a past call names its actual vendor when the field is present.\n- Optionally `stella stats` / the Observatory, if a per-vendor cost/count\n  breakdown is judged useful there — check before adding, this may be scope\n  creep beyond the audit fix.\n\n## Verify\n\nA witness test: pin an upstream on a call through the shared Chat Completions\nadapter test harness (`crates/stella-model/src/zai/tests/upstream_pin.rs` has\nthe existing fixtures), run it through the store's write path, and assert\n`stella inspect <EXEC> --format json` includes the upstream vendor.\n\n## Definition of done\n\n- `stella inspect` can answer \"which vendor served this call\" for a stored\n  execution without a raw SQL query.\n- `website/content/docs/telemetry/index.mdx`'s \"Who actually served a\n  gateway-routed call\" section (added by #3042) is updated to reflect the\n  real surface instead of the `events`-table workaround, once this ships.\n\n## Constraints\n\n- Related: #2786 (added the field), #3042 (the docs issue that surfaced this\n  gap while documenting `--upstream-pin`).\n- Store schema changes need a migration — check\n  `crates/stella-store/src/ddl.rs`'s migration chain convention before adding\n  a column to an existing table.\n"[m[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"number"[m[1;37m:[m 3054[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"title"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"stella-store: step_receipt/telemetry have no column for upstream_provider, so inspect/stats can't say who served a gateway-routed call"[m
  [1;37m}[m[1;37m,[m
