    [1;34m"body"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"## Outcome\n\nA tool tells the truth about what it will do. Its schema does not declare a\nfield the backend silently discards, its refusal messages do not name a relief\nthat cannot relieve, and a capability it has is reachable from the surfaces that\nclaim to expose it.\n\n## Why this is one epic and not three tool bugs\n\nAll three are the same defect wearing three hats: **the declaration and the\nbehaviour disagree, and the caller cannot tell.** That is the failure mode\nAGENTS.md invariant #9 exists to prevent one level up (a tool does exactly one\nthing, and its `description` is the only thing the model reads), and it is the\nsame discipline invariant #8 applies to providers — a divergence is *declared*,\nnever inferred.\n\n- **The schema declares a filter the backend drops** — #2987.\n  `crates/stella-tools/src/grep/fallback.rs` runs when `rg` is not on PATH,\n  which is the normal case in **benchmark and CI containers**. It cannot honour\n  `type` (there is no file-type database for POSIX `grep` to consult), so it\n  drops the filter and searches everything, returning hits the caller explicitly\n  scoped out. The divergence is declared only in schema prose and a code\n  comment; nothing at runtime tells the caller the filter it passed was\n  discarded on **this** call. Second of the two backend divergences named in\n  #2982 — that issue fixed the first (BRE vs ERE, which made the fallback\n  *under*-answer with a false `(no matches)`); this one makes it *over*-answer.\n- **The refusal names a relief that does not relieve** — #2999. After #2864,\n  `stop_process` refuses a live process, and `restart_process` reuses the slot\n  it already holds. So `MAX_LIVE_PROCESSES` (16) can no longer be relieved by\n  the agent at all — only a natural exit or session end frees a slot. The cap\n  error was updated to name `restart_process`, which is honest about the\n  replacement but does not say that the 16 slots are now effectively permanent\n  for the session.\n- **A capability with no door on a surface that claims to have one** — #2992.\n  The semantic code-search op is reachable from the agent tool surface and not\n  from `stella graph <op> <target>`, the human door to the same query surface.\n  The cause is mechanical, not conceptual: the CLI entry point is synchronous by\n  contract (`gather_context` batches sync queries against one open handle) while\n  the semantic path awaits an embedding model between graph reads.\n\n## Definition of done\n\n- **#2987** — a witness test **through the `Fallback` seam** (not\n  `Grep::execute`, which takes the `rg` arm on any machine with ripgrep\n  installed and would pass for the wrong reason) asserting the chosen behaviour:\n  refuse naming `type` and point at `glob`, or translate known types to\n  `--include`. The schema description and module docs say the same thing the\n  code does. Do **not** close it by dropping the fallback — hosts without `rg`\n  are where that backend is load-bearing.\n- **#2999** — either the cap error says plainly that a slot is freed only by a\n  process exiting, or the runtime (not the agent) gets a teardown path, with\n  #2666's deliberate survival contract distinguishing \"the turn ended\" from \"the\n  session ended\".\n- **#2992** — `stella graph semantic \"<query>\"` prints what the tool returns,\n  reached without making `gather_context`'s batch path async, and with the docs\n  row `make command-docs` requires.\n\nIn each case the fix is allowed to be \"declare it louder\" rather than \"close the\ngap\" — what is not allowed is a caller who cannot tell which happened.\n\n## Children\n\n#2987 #2992 #2999\n\n## Constraints every child shares\n\nAGENTS.md invariant #5 (typed errors, no panics) and #9 (single-purpose tools —\neach of these is a *scoping* parameter or a surface, never a mode flag, so none\nof them splits into two tools). `make gate` includes `command-docs`; a new CLI\nsurface without its doc row fails it.\n\nRelated: #2982 (the ERE half of #2987), #2864 and #2666 (the change and the\nsurvival contract behind #2999), #3014 (which moved the semantic op into its own\ntool after #2992 was filed — see the note on that issue).\n"[m[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"number"[m[1;37m:[m 3051[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"title"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"Epic: a tool schema, its refusals, and its surfaces agree with what it actually does"[m
  [1;37m}[m[1;37m,[m
