    [1;34m"body"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"## Outcome\n\nA fetched bench run reads itself. `arenabench cloud fetch` leaves an assembled\nmatch with a trace postmortem beside it, every band that postmortem reports\nreplays from the traces it was measured on, and none of it depends on an\noperator remembering a second command.\n\n## Why this is one epic and not three bench chores\n\nCLAUDE.md's hardest bench rule is that **a conclusion comes from the trace,\nnever from a surface signal** — and the machinery that makes the trace readable\ncurrently fails at three consecutive links of one chain:\n\n1. **Fetch** — a default `arenabench cloud fetch` downloads only each trial's\n   `results.json`. `assemble` needs the per-trial artifact trees, so a default\n   fetch assembles nothing, `bench/trace_triage` can read nothing at all, and\n   the operator learns this **after** paying for the download (#3025).\n2. **Assemble → probe** — nothing runs `bench/trace_triage/postmortem.py`\n   automatically. `cloud fetch` assembles the match and prints nothing about the\n   postmortem; a human has to remember `make postmortem RUN=<dir>`. The stated\n   purpose of the report is to have the probe done in automation *if we forget\n   to read it ourselves*, and a step a human must remember is exactly the thing\n   it was built to replace (#3024).\n3. **Report** — one of the four per-trial columns the survey records does not\n   replay from the traces. `bands.py`'s docstring table scores `s5b2` at 2.4\n   empty ok-results per trial; three candidate definitions replay at 0.0, 0.0\n   and 1.3. The other three columns reproduce exactly, so the disagreement is\n   this metric's definition, not the loader and not the fixture — and it\n   therefore ships as `EMPTY_OK_UNRESOLVED` and **no band at all** (#3023).\n\nEach link makes the next one moot: an unassemblable fetch has no traces to\nprobe, an unrun probe has no bands to report, and a band that cannot be\nreproduced fires or stays silent for reasons nobody can check.\n\n## Definition of done\n\n- A plain `arenabench cloud fetch` either produces an assemblable match or says\n  so **before** spending the download, and the size tradeoff is stated in\n  `cloud.py`'s module docstring rather than only in a flag's help text (#3025).\n- A single fetch of a run leaves `postmortem.md` and `postmortem.json` beside\n  the assembled match without a second command, and `arenabench` still imports\n  nothing under `bench/`. The chosen option is named in the PR description with\n  the boundary argument, because whichever one wins is a decision about the\n  planned ejection of `arenabench/` into its own repository and not only about\n  convenience (#3024).\n- Either a definition of \"empty ok-results\" replays all nine surveyed arms\n  within rounding — at which point it becomes a `Band(verdict_capable=false)`\n  row and `EMPTY_OK_UNRESOLVED` is deleted — or the survey column is re-measured\n  from the traces and `bands.py`'s docstring table is corrected to what\n  actually replays (#3023).\n\n## Children\n\n#3023 #3024 #3025\n\n## The boundary every child has to respect\n\n`arenabench/` is an installable, agent-agnostic package being prepared for\nejection into its own repository (`.github/workflows/bench.yml` says so).\n`bench/trace_triage/` is a set of flat stdlib modules whose every detector cites\na **Stella crate path** and is Stella-specific by construction. The fold and the\nprobe therefore meet through a **file**, not an import — do not close #3024 by\nmaking `arenabench` import `trace_triage`.\n\nRelated: #2775 (arenabench measurement fidelity — the capture side of the same\nchain; this epic is the read side).\n"[m[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"number"[m[1;37m:[m 3049[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"title"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"Epic: a fetched bench run reads itself — assembly, replay, and the postmortem without an operator remembering"[m
  [1;37m}[m[1;37m,[m
