    [1;34m"body"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"## Problem\n\n`scripts/check-command-docs.sh` asserts every subcommand has a reference page, and\n`stella-cli`'s `the_docs_index_summaries_are_the_clis_own` asserts each index one-liner\nbyte-equals the CLI's `about`. Nothing anywhere relates a **flag** to its documentation.\n\nA docs-freshness audit on 2026-08-12 (`main` at c9511b5e8) found the predictable result:\nthe \"Global flags\" grid in `website/content/docs/commands/index.mdx` listed **7 of the\nCLI's 17 session flags**. Missing were `--upstream-pin`, `--turn-budget`,\n`--max-output-tokens`, `--plan-mode`, `--tools`, `-v/--verbose`, `--log-level`,\n`--log-file`, `--foreground`, `--detach`. Seven of those appeared **nowhere** on the docs\nsite at all. `--upstream-pin` shipped 2026-08-10 (#2786) and was undocumented within two\ndays of a docs-touching commit.\n\nTwo per-command instances of the same shape, both landed *before* their page's most\nrecent edit and still missed:\n\n- `stella auth set --field` — shipped 2026-08-03 (#1301/#1325), absent from\n  `commands/auth.mdx` whose last edit was 2026-08-05.\n- `stella fleet --task-timeout` — shipped 2026-08-02 (#1214), absent from\n  `commands/fleet.mdx` whose last edit was 2026-08-05.\n\nPR `docs/freshness-audit-2026-08-12` fixes all three by hand. The mechanism that let them\ndrift is untouched, so it will recur — this is the same argument #993 made for\n`check-command-docs.sh` at the subcommand level, one level down.\n\n## What to build\n\nA gate step (`make flag-docs`, alongside `command-docs` in `GATE_STEPS`) asserting both\ndirections, mirroring `scripts/check-command-docs.sh`'s structure and its\nbuffered-report / `contains` / exemption-file conventions:\n\n1. **Every global session flag has a card in `website/content/docs/commands/index.mdx`.**\n   The flag list is the `GlobalArgs` struct in `crates/stella-cli/src/cli.rs` (the flags\n   marked `global = true`, which `main.rs` already has a test asserting completeness of).\n2. **Every non-global flag of a subcommand appears somewhere in that subcommand's page**,\n   `website/content/docs/commands/<slug>.mdx`.\n3. **No page names a `--flag` that its command does not have** — the orphan direction, the\n   one that reads as current and is wrong.\n\nExemptions go in `scripts/flag-docs-exempt.txt`, one `command:flag` per line with a\nreason, committed and visible in review like `scripts/command-docs-exempt.txt`. That file\nshould start close to empty: the audit's reverse sweep found only 3 undocumented\nsubcommand flags across all 38 commands, and 2 were real.\n\n### Implementation note\n\nThe audit did this with the built binary's `--help` output, which is the most faithful\nsource (clap strips a trailing period from a doc comment, so `cli.rs` is not ground truth\nfor text — see the note in `check-command-docs.sh`). But `make gate` must run on a bare\nrunner with no toolchain for the early guards. Two options, and the choice is the design\nquestion this issue is really asking:\n\n- **Parse `cli.rs`** with POSIX awk as `check-command-docs.sh` already does, keeping the\n  guard toolchain-free but blind to clap's own transformations.\n- **Make it a `stella-cli` test** rather than a shell guard, so it reads the built\n  `clap::Command` tree and cannot disagree with what ships — the way\n  `every_subcommand_has_a_reference_page` in `crates/stella-cli/src/main_tests.rs` already\n  does. This composes with the three existing docs locks and needs no new Makefile step.\n\nThe second is probably right for exactly the reason the memo in\n`the_docs_index_summaries_are_the_clis_own` gives, but it moves the check behind a\ncompile, which the first guard deliberately avoided.\n\n## Repro / verify\n\n```sh\n# The audit's own sweep, run against the built binary:\nstella --help                       # 17 session flags under \"Session flags:\"\nrg -c 'OptionCard name=\"--' website/content/docs/commands/index.mdx\n```\n\nBefore PR `docs/freshness-audit-2026-08-12`: 7 cards against 17 flags.\n\n## Definition of done\n\n- A gate step (or `stella-cli` test) fails when a flag is added to any command without a\n  corresponding docs edit, and when a page names a flag that no longer exists.\n- It is wired into `GATE_STEPS` **and** the `make gate` block in both `AGENTS.md` and\n  `CONTRIBUTING.md` (`gate-parity` enforces those two stay in sync), or, if implemented as\n  a test, needs no wiring and this bullet is dropped.\n- The exemption file exists, is documented in the script header, and rejects a stale\n  entry the way `command-docs-exempt.txt` does.\n- A witness: add a flag to some command in a scratch commit, confirm the gate goes red,\n  document it, confirm green.\n\n## Constraints\n\n- `website/content/docs/**` is in `ci.yml`'s ignored paths, so a docs-only PR does not\n  start the required job — `docs-guards.yml` is where a shell-guard version has to run\n  (it already re-runs `command-docs` for this reason). A `stella-cli` test version runs in\n  `ci.yml` on any Rust change but would **not** run on a docs-only PR that deletes a card,\n  so a test-based implementation still wants a `docs-guards.yml` entry.\n- Related: #993 (the subcommand-level guard this extends), #928 (the seven undocumented\n  surfaces that motivated it), #1493 (`--output-format` is declared per-command, so the\n  guard must not treat it as global).\n"[m[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"number"[m[1;37m:[m 3041[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"title"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"docs gate: nothing relates a CLI flag to its documentation, so 10 of 17 global flags drifted undocumented"[m
  [1;37m}[m[1;37m,[m
