    [1;34m"body"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"Design (no code in this issue): make cross-tool steering **data attached to tools, emitted from the resolved tool set**, and make **human presence** an explicit input to that resolution. Today both are hand-written prose copy-pasted into two persona constants, and the prose can — and does — recommend a tool the session does not have.\n\nThis is a design handoff. It argues the decisions, names the files, and flags the three places a maintainer has to choose. Nothing here is settled by fiat.\n\n---\n\n## 1. The problem, with the evidence\n\n### 1a. The prompt recommends a tool that is not registered\n\n`crates/stella-cli/src/agent/prompt.rs` holds two persona constants, `SYSTEM_PROMPT` (:233) and `PIPELINE_SYSTEM_PROMPT` (:286). Both name `ask_user`:\n\n- `action_care!()` (:196): *\"when the mandate is unclear, ask_user is the escalation surface: confirm first\"*\n- `SYSTEM_PROMPT` (:280) and `PIPELINE_SYSTEM_PROMPT` (:339), verbatim in both: *\"When a choice is ambiguous AND getting it wrong would be costly, use ask_user rather than guessing\"*\n\nIn a run with no human, `ask_user` is still advertised in the schema list and answers with an error string — `crates/stella-cli/src/interactive.rs:84-93`:\n\n> `\"interactive input is unavailable in this run (no TTY / machine output format) — proceed with your best judgment instead of asking\"`\n\nSo the agent spends a model turn discovering a capability it never had. The originating session reports `ask_user` called in 6 of 208 bench trials, all on `sanitize-git-repo` — **that measurement is reported, not verified in this issue**; the source-level defect below is verified and does not depend on it.\n\nThe codebase already states the principle it is breaking, two files away, about a different tool (`interactive.rs:271-273`):\n\n> *\"a tool that is offered and then always answers \"unavailable\" teaches the model to stop calling it\"*\n\n— which is why `with_recall` and `with_skill_registry` register conditionally rather than registering-and-failing.\n\n### 1b. Four surfaces already withhold `ask_user`; all four still ship the prose\n\n| Surface | Withholds the interactive layer | Still gets a persona naming `ask_user` |\n|---|---|---|\n| Pipeline candidates | `crates/stella-cli/src/candidate_ws.rs:103-107` — *\"withheld regardless of phase, unconditionally\"* | `PIPELINE_SYSTEM_PROMPT` |\n| Sub-agent lanes | `crates/stella-cli/src/subsession.rs:21` — *\"no interactive ask_user — an autonomous worker has nobody to ask\"* | `build_system_prompt` at `subsession.rs:691` |\n| Fleet workers | no `InteractiveToolSet` in the stack (`crates/stella-cli/src/fleet_cmd.rs:705`) | `fleet_cmd.rs:722-729` |\n| Headless one-shot / `stella run --output-format json` | registers it with `HeadlessAskUserIo` (`agent.rs:1983-1987`) | both personas |\n\nThe sub-agent path is the tell: `subsession.rs:483` patches the gap by appending *\"Work autonomously — nobody is watching live\"* to the **user message**, i.e. outside the cached prefix, at one surface, invisible to every guard. Somebody already needed this text and had nowhere correct to put it.\n\nTwo more layers carry the same stale assumption:\n\n- `crates/stella-cli/src/discovery.rs:59-87` — lean mode's `DEFAULT_CORE_TOOLS` includes `ask_user`, doc'd as *\"the definition of done, and asking the user\"*. Withholding the tool without fixing this list re-advertises it one layer below the prompt.\n- `tool_steering!()` (`prompt.rs:42-55`) asserts *\"The user watches your plan on screen the whole time you work\"* — false in every row of the table above, where the task board is a store row nobody is reading live.\n\n### 1c. Steering and the tools it steers are not coupled by anything but memory\n\n`semantic_code_search` shipped in #3014. The prompt rule teaching the model when to reach for it was a **separate PR**, #3020, landing hours later, and #3015 is the issue that existed in between — a registered tool with no steering, for as long as it took someone to remember. Nothing failed. Nothing could have: there is no place a tool's steering is required to exist.\n\n### 1d. Two copies drift, and the guard only covers what someone registered\n\n`crates/stella-cli/src/agent/prompt/parity.rs` exists because they already drifted (#450: *\"`verify_done` and `ask_user` took comma splices in the pipeline copy where the base copy took an em dash\"*), and #2231 because the per-contract tests covered the contracts that existed and said nothing about the next one. `parity.rs` derives the contract set from `prompt.rs`'s own source (`include_str!(\"../prompt.rs\")`, :71) — genuinely good, and the model to preserve. What it cannot check is whether a contract **should** exist, or whether the tool it names is registered.\n\n### 1e. And there is a live predicate disagreement\n\n`crates/stella-cli/src/agent.rs:259` computes presence one way:\n\n```rust\n// Whether this surface may ask the human mid-turn (ask_user, approvals).\nlet ask = format == OutputFormat::Text;\n```\n\n`crates/stella-cli/src/interactive.rs:96-102` computes it another:\n\n```rust\nif interactive_output && std::io::stdin().is_terminal() { TtyAskUserIo } else { HeadlessAskUserIo }\n```\n\nSo in `--output-format text` with stdin piped (`echo hi | stella …`), the rules/approval layer believes a human can approve while `ask_user` is already headless. One derived value fixes this for free; it is called out here rather than filed separately because this design consumes it.\n\n---\n\n## 2. What #639 already got right — and the exact boundary it drew\n\nThis design is an extension of a decision that already exists, not a reversal of it. Read `prompt.rs:11-37`. Both prompts used to open with a hand-maintained per-tool catalogue: ~1,240 tokens restating the generated schemas, and since `ToolRegistry::schemas` is serialized at **position 0 of the same cached prefix**, a ~46-tool session bought every description **twice on every call**. #639 deleted it, and `neither_prompt_re_enumerates_the_generated_tool_schemas` (`prompt.rs:861`) now fails if anyone reintroduces per-tool prose — it parses `- name: …` lines and resolves each name against `stella_tools::catalog::get`.\n\nSo **what a tool does is already generated**. What was deliberately kept is the residue, pinned by `both_prompts_keep_the_steering_the_schemas_cannot_carry` (`prompt.rs:878`): *\"composition rules and the availability model, neither of which a single tool's schema can express.\"*\n\nThat residue is **relational**. \"Read a definition by name with `read_symbol`; guessing `read_file` offsets after a `graph_query` is the round-trip it exists to remove\" is a fact about three tools together. \"A change touching several files is ONE `apply_edits` call, not a chain of `edit_file` calls\" is a fact about two. A schema is a description of one tool in isolation, so relational steering genuinely cannot live there — but that is an argument for putting it somewhere else *structured*, not for leaving it as prose in two constants. #639 moved the per-tool half from prose to generated; this issue moves the relational half.\n\n---\n\n## 3. The design\n\n### 3.1 Steering becomes declared data, keyed on a **set** of tools\n\nA steering rule is `{ tools: &[&str], text: &str, provenance }` — the tools it is a fact *about*, and the one sentence. Emission rule, and this is the whole point:\n\n> **A rule is emitted iff every tool it names is registered in this session.**\n\nThat single predicate structurally fixes §1a (no rule about `ask_user` can reach a session without `ask_user`), §1b (all four surfaces get the right text with no per-surface edit), and the withheld-`bash` case that `\"tools\": {\"bash\": \"off\"}` already creates today.\n\nRules whose text is true regardless of which tools exist — the availability model itself (*\"a tool you cannot see is not available in this session rather than nonexistent\"*, `tool_steering!()`) — declare `tools: &[]` and always emit. That is not a loophole; it is the correct answer for a rule that is *about the absence of tools*.\n\n**Where it lives — maintainer call, with a recommendation.** `crates/stella-tools/src/catalog.rs` is already *\"the canonical tool table — the one place a new built-in is declared\"*, already derives four downstream lists from one declaration precisely to stop them drifting, and already carries every session-layer tool including `ask_user` under `Availability::Session`. Three options:\n\n- **(a) A new column on `ToolEntry`.** Maximally coupled: adding a tool without steering is one edit away from impossible. But single-tool steering is exactly what #639 says belongs in the schema description, and relational rules do not have one owner, so a per-row column models the problem wrong.\n- **(b) `crates/stella-tools/src/steering.rs`** — a sibling table of rules keyed by tool-name sets, cross-checked against `CATALOG` in the same crate. **Recommended.** Names resolve against a table that is already the authority (`catalog::get` already backs the #639 guard), it keeps prose out of the hot flags table, it puts the text in the crate that owns the tools, and it takes ~all of the pressure off `prompt.rs` (#2985).\n- **(c) A steering module in `stella-cli`.** Keeps `stella-tools` free of prompt prose, but re-splits the tool and its steering across crates — the exact distance that let #3014 and #3020 land hours apart.\n\nInvariant #1 is satisfied by (a), (b) and (c) alike: `stella-core` imports none of them, and none is a concretion — a table of `&'static str` with no I/O, in the same crate as `catalog.rs`, which `stella-cli` already reads from `prompt.rs`'s own tests.\n\n**Explicit non-goal.** MCP and custom tools have no catalog row, so they get no steering. That is correct and should be stated in the module doc rather than discovered: the availability rule (`tools: &[]`) plus `tool_search` is what covers them, and inventing steering for tools we do not ship is how a table becomes a place to write essays.\n\n### 3.2 Emission: deterministic, sorted, in the stable prefix\n\n`assemble_system_prompt` (`prompt.rs:372`) gains one section, emitted after the resolved tool set is known and before any volatile content:\n\n```\n[persona opening]  →  [generated steering block]  →  [shared persona contracts]\n   →  [presence block]  →  [methodology / rules]  →  [session environment, memories]\n```\n\nOrdering is a decision, not an accident: the goldens record `cache_zone` per block (`make cache-correctness`, `.github/workflows/ci.yml:423`), so the block boundary is observable and must be chosen once. Emission iterates the rules in table order (deterministic and reviewable — the table reads top-to-bottom the way the prompt does) filtered by the resolved set. **Not** by hash-map order, ever.\n\n### 3.3 Byte-stability (invariant #7): state the objection and answer it\n\nThe objection that would kill this design if unanswered is \"invariant #7 forbids runtime assembly\". It does not. It forbids **nondeterminism**. `assemble_system_prompt` already appends computed content — the session environment, the exploration index, sorted workspace memories — and is byte-stable because each of those is a deterministic function of resolved inputs (`prompt.rs:1-8` states exactly this discipline). A sorted iteration over a resolved tool set is the same kind of thing: identical inputs, identical bytes, every run, forever.\n\nWhat actually varies is the **number of distinct prefixes**, and it varies with the resolved tool set — which it already does today, because `\"tools\": {\"bash\": \"off\"}` already changes the schema list at position 0 of the same prefix. Cost, argued rather than asserted:\n\n- The system prompt is ~0.9% of a trial's total input tokens (measured; the schemas at position 0 are the large half of the prefix, and they already vary with the tool set).\n- Distinct prefixes **coexist** in the provider cache rather than evicting each other; a session's tool set and presence are fixed for its life, so **no session ever sees more than one variant**.\n- Therefore the incremental cost is **one cold write per variant per session** — which is what a session already pays for its own prefix today. It is not a rebuild, and it is not per-call.\n\nThe one honest cost: extracting the `ask_user` clauses out of `action_care!()` and `tool_steering!()` changes the bytes of **both existing prompts**, so every session pays one cold write on the release that lands it. That is the unavoidable price of any prompt edit — #3020 paid it last week — and it is one-time.\n\n### 3.4 The mode axis: presence is an input to resolution, not a second prompt family\n\nHuman presence is what makes the tool set vary in the first place, so it belongs here as **one input**, not as its own design.\n\n**How many modes: two.** `Presence::Attended | Unattended`. The discriminating question is *\"can a mid-turn question be answered?\"*, which is a boolean. Everything a third mode would capture (CI, supervised-async, IDE host) is a different *way of answering that boolean*, not a third answer. Adding modes multiplies every guard in §3.5 for no behavioural difference.\n\n**What determines it: the calling surface declares it; nothing re-derives it.** This is the fix for §1e. One value, computed once where the tool stack is assembled, threaded to both the stack and the prompt:\n\n- plain REPL, Command Deck, `stella run`: `Attended` iff `output_format == Text && stdin().is_terminal()` — today's `default_ask_io` predicate (`interactive.rs:97`), promoted to the single source and reused by `enforce_workspace_rules` (`agent.rs:259-261`).\n- sub-agent lanes, fleet workers, pipeline candidates: `Unattended`, hard-coded. They already withhold the tool; this makes the prompt agree.\n- `stella-serve`: **out of scope for v1, and the reason is interesting** — serve already has an async human channel (`crates/stella-serve/src/pending.rs:116-118`, scope review, #1288), so presence there is a **wire capability the host declares**, not a TTY fact. Serve does not link `stella-cli`'s prompt at all (every model/tool call is remoted), so it cannot be fixed here. File it separately once this lands.\n- **An explicit override should exist** (`--unattended` / `--presence`), because inference cannot be right for a wrapper that pipes stdin with a human behind it (tmux, IDE host, a CI operator watching). Flag name and whether it ships in v1 is a maintainer call.\n\n**What differs between modes — deliberately short.** A long list means the modes are not really two things.\n\n1. **Escalation surface.** Attended: `ask_user` is registered, and its steering rule (*\"when the mandate is unclear, ask before acting\"*) emits because the tool is present. Unattended: the tool is absent, the rule does not emit, and a presence contract says instead: *there is nobody to ask — take the reversible option and state the assumption in your final report.* Note the asymmetry: (1) is mostly **not** a persona edit at all once §3.1 lands; it falls out of emission.\n2. **Hard-to-reverse actions.** Attended: stop and confirm. Unattended: the mandate plus workspace isolation is the authority — if the mandate does not cover it, do not do it and say so.\n3. **Who the task board is for.** `tool_steering!()`'s *\"The user watches your plan on screen the whole time you work\"* is false unattended. Either narrow the clause to be true in both (cheapest, no new variant of the largest contract) or split it. **Maintainer call.**\n\nExplicitly **not** mode-varying: verification, scope, measurement, faithful reporting, complexity, injection defense. In particular, **do not weaken the verification contract when unattended** — it is the substitute for the absent human gate, so relaxing it exactly where the human is missing is backwards. Stated here so nobody proposes it later as an efficiency.\n\n**Prompt-constant count.** With steering generated, the persona constants stay **two** (bare / pipeline) and presence contributes one appended block with two literals — not four persona constants. If a maintainer prefers four `concat!` constants, that is available and costs nothing but combinatorics in §3.5; the recommendation is two-plus-a-block, because the appended block is byte-stable for the same reason the environment block already is (§3.3), and because four constants makes every future contract a four-way edit.\n\n### 3.5 What replaces `parity.rs`'s guarantee — stronger, and mechanically checkable\n\n`parity.rs` fails by name when a contract reaches one prompt and not the other. Generated steering changes the failure it must guard against, and the replacements are strictly stronger because they check things convention could never reach:\n\n1. **Coverage.** Every `Availability::Always` catalog row either has ≥1 steering rule naming it, or declares an explicit gap citing an issue. The in-house exemplar is invariant #10's consumer ledger (`crates/stella-protocol/src/event/consumers.rs`): one row per wire tag, `Behavioral`/`Surfaced`/`RecordedOnly`/`Unclassified`, with `MAX_UNCLASSIFIED` a down-only ratchet on the unaudited backlog. Copy that shape — a declared, issue-cited gap, never a silence. This is the check that would have caught #3015 the hour #3014 merged.\n2. **Reference integrity.** Every tool name appearing in a rule's `text` resolves in `CATALOG` **and** appears in that rule's `tools` list. Reuse the #639 guard's technique (`prompt.rs:836-848` already scans prompt text for catalog names). This is what makes the emission predicate honest: a rule that mentions a tool it did not declare would leak past the filter.\n3. **No advertisement of an absent tool.** Property test: for a resolved set S (proptest over subsets of `CATALOG`), the emitted block mentions no catalog name outside S. This is the general form of the §1a bug and the one check that makes the whole design safe.\n4. **The persona residue keeps its existing guard.** Whatever prose stays hand-written stays under `parity.rs`'s source-derived scan — including the presence block, whose two literals must appear in exactly the prompts of their mode and neither of the others (a partition assertion, so a contract cannot land in three of four).\n5. **#639 stays enforced on the generated block too.** The new risk this design introduces is a steering table that grows back into a catalogue. `neither_prompt_re_enumerates_the_generated_tool_schemas` must run over the **assembled** prompt including the generated section, not just the constants.\n6. **Witness test** (fails on `main` today): a headless session's advertised schema list contains no `ask_user`, and its assembled prompt contains no occurrence of the string.\n7. **Golden fixtures** regenerated and **the diff read**, per AGENTS.md.\n\n### 3.6 Migration: the test that decides, not a list\n\nFor each sentence of existing prose, ask: **does removing tool T from the session make this sentence false or useless?**\n\n- **Yes, and it names exactly one tool** → it belongs in that tool's `description`. #639's rule, unchanged; most of these are already gone.\n- **Yes, and it names two or more** → relational steering. Move it to the table keyed on that set.\n- **No — true whatever is registered** (methodology order, scope discipline, faithful reporting, injection defense, reversibility posture) → persona prose. It stays, and `parity.rs` keeps guarding it.\n\nWorked examples: *\"guessing read_file offsets after a graph_query\"* → steering on `{read_symbol, graph_query, read_file}`. *\"ONE apply_edits call, not a chain of edit_file calls\"* → steering on `{apply_edits, edit_file}`. #3020's localization rule → steering on `{graph_query, semantic_code_search, grep, glob}`, which also makes it degrade correctly on a repo with no index, since the rule stops emitting when the tools are not there. *\"Never modify existing tests to make them pass\"* → persona; no tool named, true always.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Sequencing and dependencies\n\n**#2985 must land first, strictly.** It splits the persona literals out of `prompt.rs` (1486 lines against a hard 1500 ceiling) and its definition of done is *\"`SYSTEM_PROMPT` and `PIPELINE_SYSTEM_PROMPT` are byte-identical before and after\"* — which this work violates **by design**. Conflating a pure move with a byte-changing edit destroys the only check that makes the move reviewable. #2985 also has to repoint `parity.rs`'s `include_str!(\"../prompt.rs\")`, which this design then repoints again.\n\nSuggested slices (each independently mergeable, each with its own witness):\n\n- **A.** #2985 as written. Pure move, bytes identical.\n- **B.** `Presence` resolved once per surface; `ask_user` **not registered** when `Unattended` (drop the io arm rather than passing `HeadlessAskUserIo` — mirror `with_recall`/`with_skill_registry`); `discovery.rs`'s `DEFAULT_CORE_TOOLS` filtered by presence; the two predicates in §1e unified. Prose unchanged, so the prompt is *still* wrong — but the tool surface is right and the witness in §3.5(6) half-passes. Consider folding B into C if a reviewer objects to landing a known-inconsistent intermediate.\n- **C.** The steering table, the emission hook, and guards §3.5(1)(2)(3)(5). Migrate the relational prose out of `tool_steering!()` — including the `ask_user` clauses, which is what completes B.\n- **D.** The presence block and its partition guard §3.5(4); retire `subsession.rs:483`'s user-message workaround.\n\n**Do not run this concurrently with another prompt edit.** #3020 just landed; a fifth copy of the localization text appearing mid-migration is exactly how #450 happened.\n\nAlso update in the same PRs, because they are copies of one text and drift silently (#3015's finding): `docs/prompts/worker.md`, and `crates/stella-cli/README.md:155-179` which documents `build_system_prompt` / `assemble_system_prompt`.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Where a maintainer has to choose\n\n1. **Steering table location** — (a) column on `ToolEntry`, (b) `stella-tools/src/steering.rs` sibling table, (c) `stella-cli` module. Recommendation (b); trade-offs in §3.1.\n2. **Persona constants** — two + an appended presence block (recommended), or four `concat!` constants. §3.4.\n3. **The \"user watches your plan on screen\" clause** — narrow it to be true in both modes, or split `tool_steering!()` (the largest contract) into two variants. §3.4.\n4. **Explicit presence flag** — ship `--unattended` in v1 or infer only. §3.4.\n5. **Is a declared-gap ledger with a ratchet required** for coverage (§3.5(1)), or is coverage advisory until the table is populated? The invariant #10 precedent says required-with-a-ratchet; that is a real cost on every new tool.\n6. **`HeadlessAskUserIo`** — delete once nothing registers `ask_user` unattended, or keep as defence in depth. Keeping it means keeping a code path that can only be reached by a bug, which is arguably the point of defence in depth.\n\n## 6. Open, and honestly unresolved\n\n- **How much prose is genuinely relational?** §3.6 gives the decision procedure but not the answer; the count only comes from applying it sentence by sentence, and it decides whether this is a large change or a small one. Nobody should estimate this issue's size without doing that pass first.\n- **`stella-serve` presence** is a wire capability (#1288's channel already exists) and cannot be resolved from the CLI. Needs its own issue after this lands.\n- **Whether generated steering measurably changes behaviour** is unmeasured. The mechanism removes a proven waste (a turn spent calling a tool that errors) and a proven drift class; it does not follow that solve rate moves. If it is measured, it belongs as its own arm alongside #2995/#3020 rather than confounded with them — and a five-task solve rate cannot resolve anything this small.\n\n## 7. Definition of done (for the series, not one PR)\n\n- No assembled prompt, at any surface, names a tool that surface's registry does not advertise — enforced by property test, not review.\n- A new `Availability::Always` tool cannot merge with neither steering nor an issue-cited declared gap.\n- A relational rule cannot name a tool it did not declare, and cannot name a tool that does not exist.\n- Presence is one resolved value per session, consumed by the tool stack, the approval/rules layer, and the prompt; no second derivation anywhere.\n- `subsession.rs:483`'s user-message workaround is deleted, its content having a correct home.\n- Prompt-cache goldens regenerated **and the diff read**; `make gate` green with no new `scripts/file-size-baseline.txt` entry.\n\n---\n\nRelated: #639 (deleted the per-tool catalogue; drew the boundary this extends), #2231 (the parity guard, and the reason a derived check beats a listed one), #2985 (the `prompt.rs` ceiling — **blocks this**), #3015 / #3020 (a tool shipping hours ahead of its steering — the defect §3.5(1) makes impossible), #2995 (the measurement arm any behavioural claim here belongs in).\n"[m[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"number"[m[1;37m:[m 3031[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"title"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"Cross-tool steering should be data on the resolved tool set, not prose copied into two personas — and human presence is one input to that resolution"[m
  [1;37m}[m[1;37m,[m
