    [1;34m"body"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"## Problem\n\n`arenabench cloud fetch <run>` downloads only each trial's `results.json`\nunless `--artifacts` is passed. `arenabench assemble` (called at the end of\nfetch) needs the per-trial artifact trees — `<job>/ws/matches/*/jobs/*/<task>__*`\n— to link into the match, so a default fetch assembles **nothing** and prints:\n\n```\nassembled : 0 trial(s) into one match (…)\n  !! no trial artifacts under … — a results-only fetch carries no traces to\n     assemble. Re-fetch with `--artifacts`.\n```\n\nThat is honest, and it is still one wasted round trip: the operator learns\nafter the download that the download was not the one they wanted.\n\n## The tension\n\n`--artifacts` is opt-in on purpose (`cloud.py::fetch_results`): the full tree is\n\"events, recordings, workspaces\" and can be gigabytes. But every rule this\nrepository holds itself to says a bench conclusion comes from the trace, and\n`bench/trace_triage` — both the issue-filing path and the new postmortem — can\nread *nothing at all* from a results-only fetch. In practice a measured run is\nalways fetched with `--artifacts`; the default optimises for the case nobody\nuses.\n\n## Options\n\n1. Default `--artifacts` **on** for a fetch, with `--results-only` as the opt\n   out. Matches how the runs in `arenabench-cloud/` were actually fetched.\n2. Keep the default and refuse earlier: have `cloud fetch` say, **before**\n   downloading, that this fetch will not be assemblable or readable by the\n   trace tooling.\n3. Fetch results first and the artifact tree lazily, on the first thing that\n   needs it.\n\n## Files\n\n- `arenabench/arenabench/cloud.py` — `CloudExecutor.fetch_results`,\n  `_fetch_prefix`, `_cmd_cloud_fetch`, `_cmd_cloud_run`, `register_cli`\n- `arenabench/arenabench/assemble.py` — `discover_trials`, `assemble_run`\n- `arenabench/tests/test_assemble.py::test_a_results_only_fetch_says_so_instead_of_assembling_nothing`\n  pins today's behaviour and must be updated with whichever option lands.\n\n## Verify\n\n```sh\narenabench cloud fetch <run-id> --out /tmp/r && ls /tmp/r/matches/*/jobs/*/\n```\n\n## Done when\n\nA plain `arenabench cloud fetch` either produces an assemblable match or says\nso before spending the download, and the size tradeoff is stated in\n`cloud.py`'s module docstring rather than only in a flag's help text.\n"[m[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"number"[m[1;37m:[m 3025[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"title"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"arenabench: a default `cloud fetch` cannot be assembled or read by the trace tooling, and only says so after the download"[m
  [1;37m}[m[1;37m,[m
  [1;37m{[m
    [1;34m"body"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"## Problem\n\n`bench/trace_triage/postmortem.py` (added alongside `arenabench assemble`)\nrenders a run's trace probe onto the match assembled from it — but **nothing\nruns it automatically**. `arenabench cloud fetch` assembles the match and then\nprints nothing about the postmortem; an operator has to remember\n`make postmortem RUN=<dir>`. The stated purpose of the report is \"so we have\nthe probe into traces done in automation if we forget to read ourselves\", and\na step a human must remember is exactly the thing it was built to replace.\n\n## Why it is not already wired\n\nLayering, deliberately, and the tradeoff should be re-decided rather than\ninherited:\n\n- `arenabench/` is an installable, agent-agnostic package (it seats\n  `claude-code` and `stella` alike) and is being prepared for ejection into its\n  own repository (`.github/workflows/bench.yml` says so).\n- `bench/trace_triage/` is a set of flat stdlib modules with a `sys.path`\n  `conftest.py`, not importable as a package, and every one of its detectors\n  cites a **Stella crate path** (`crates/stella-pipeline/src/verify.rs`,\n  `crates/stella-core/src/driver.rs`, …). It is Stella-specific by construction.\n\nSo `arenabench` importing `trace_triage` would both break the ejection and put\nStella-specific analysis inside an agent-agnostic package. The fold and the\nprobe therefore meet through a **file**, not an import.\n\n## Options\n\n1. **A repo-level wrapper** — a `scripts/` shim or a Makefile target that runs\n   `arenabench cloud fetch` and then `postmortem.py`, so the two-step is one\n   command for a Stella operator without arenabench knowing trace_triage\n   exists. Cheapest, keeps every boundary.\n2. **`arenabench cloud fetch --postmortem CMD`** — a declared hook the operator\n   or a wrapper supplies. Keeps arenabench agnostic; costs a flag.\n3. **Make `trace_triage` an installable package** and let a Stella-only extra\n   depend on it. Largest change; revisit only if the probe grows other callers.\n\n## Files\n\n- `bench/trace_triage/postmortem.py` — `main`, `write_report`, `report_paths`\n- `arenabench/arenabench/assemble.py` — `assemble_run`, `register_cli`\n- `arenabench/arenabench/cloud.py` — `_assemble_fetched`, `_cmd_cloud_fetch`,\n  `_cmd_cloud_run`\n- `Makefile` — the `postmortem` target\n\n## Verify\n\n```sh\narenabench cloud fetch <run-id> --artifacts --out arenabench-cloud/<run-id>\nls arenabench-cloud/<run-id>/matches/*/postmortem.md\n```\n\n## Done when\n\nA single fetch of a run leaves `postmortem.md` and `postmortem.json` beside the\nassembled match without a second command, and `arenabench` still imports\nnothing under `bench/`. The chosen option is named in the PR description with\nthe boundary argument, because whichever one wins is a decision about the\nejection and not only about convenience.\n"[m[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"number"[m[1;37m:[m 3024[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"title"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"bench: the trace postmortem is not run automatically at fetch time, so it still depends on somebody remembering"[m
  [1;37m}[m[1;37m,[m
