    [1;34m"body"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"## Problem\n\n`cargo` writes its diagnostics with ANSI colour escapes when it believes it is talking to a terminal. The escape sequence comes **before** the word `error`, so a line that reads `error[E0624]: method is private` on screen is actually:\n\n```\n\\x1b[1m\\x1b[91merror[E0624]\\x1b[0m\\x1b[1m: method `record_touch` is private\\x1b[0m\n```\n\nA filter anchored at the start of the line therefore matches nothing:\n\n```sh\ncargo build -p stella-tools 2>&1 | rg '^error|^warning' | head -20   # prints nothing\n```\n\nThat is not \"no errors\" — it is \"the filter cannot see them\". The build failed.\n\nThis bit during the work on #2907/#2917/#2918 (PR #3005). A `cargo build --workspace` was declared clean on exactly that filter while `stella-cli` was failing to compile with six `documentation comments cannot be applied to function parameters` errors. The mistake survived two more commands before an exit code contradicted it.\n\nIt is the same family as the existing gotcha about piped `cargo` masking exit codes, and it is worse in one specific way: piping to `tail`/`head` at least leaves the output visible, while this filter *removes the evidence and prints a reassuring nothing*.\n\n## Why it is worth writing down\n\nEvery agent session in this repo greps build output — it is the cheapest way to get a signal out of a long compile — and the failure mode is silent and confidence-building rather than noisy. AGENTS.md already warns about pipes hiding the exit code; a reader who has internalised that lesson will still write this filter, because it *looks* like it respects it.\n\n## Reproduce\n\n```sh\n# in a worktree with any deliberate compile error, e.g.\n#   add `/// doc` above a function parameter\ncargo build -p stella-tools 2>&1 | rg -c '^error'    # -> 0, exit 1 from rg\necho \"${PIPESTATUS[0]}\"                              # -> 101, the build really failed\ncargo build -p stella-tools 2>&1 | rg -c 'error\\['   # -> the real count\n```\n\n## Fixes, in preference order\n\n1. **Read the exit code, never a grep count.** `cargo build ... > /tmp/b.txt 2>&1; echo \"EXIT=$?\"` then inspect the file. This is the only method that cannot be fooled, and it is what the existing \"piped cargo lies\" gotcha is already asking for.\n2. **Disable the colour** when the output is going to be filtered: `cargo build --color=never`, or `CARGO_TERM_COLOR=never`. Then `^error` behaves as written.\n3. **If you must filter coloured output**, anchor on something that survives: `rg 'error\\['` for rustc diagnostics, or `rg -o 'error(\\[E[0-9]+\\])?: .*'`. Do not anchor at `^`.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nA short entry in AGENTS.md § Gotchas, beside the existing note about piped `cargo` and exit codes, stating:\n\n- `cargo` colourises by default, so the escape precedes the word and `^error` never matches;\n- a grep over build output is not a build result — the exit code is;\n- use `--color=never` / `CARGO_TERM_COLOR=never` when filtering deliberately.\n\nDocs-only, so no witness test is required; `make gate`'s `docs-guards` path covers it. Two or three sentences is the right size — this is a gotcha entry, not a section.\n\n## Notes for whoever picks this up\n\n- The gotcha belongs in AGENTS.md rather than CLAUDE.md: it is a fact about the toolchain that applies to humans and agents alike, and CLAUDE.md defers to AGENTS.md for commands and gotchas.\n- Worth checking whether any committed script under `scripts/` filters cargo output this way — I did not audit them, and a guard that greps a build for `^error` would be the same defect with a much longer blast radius.\n"[m[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"number"[m[1;37m:[m 3006[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"title"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"A grep over cargo's output is not a build result: ANSI colour makes `rg '^error'` return a false green"[m
  [1;37m}[m[1;37m,[m
