    [1;34m"body"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"## Problem\n\nSemantic `graph_query` is opt-in. With nothing configured the tool falls back\nto `HashEmbedder`, which is a hashed-character-n-gram projection: deterministic,\noffline, and structurally incapable of mapping \"the code that handles telemetry\ndrains\" onto a file called `sink.rs`. It declares\n`SimilarityPosture::Surface` and every surface that reaches it says so — the\ndegradation is labelled, not hidden — but a user with no embedding key and no\nlocal model server gets name matching.\n\nThe semantic backend that shipped is `HttpEmbedder`: an OpenAI-shaped\n`POST {base}/embeddings` client, which covers Voyage (`voyage-code-3`), OpenAI,\nand — this is the offline half — a local Ollama / llama.cpp / TEI server via\n`STELLA_EMBED_URL`. That was chosen over an in-process model because it adds\n**no new third-party dependency at all**, which an inference runtime very much\ndoes. The trade was stated rather than hidden; this issue is the other half of\nit.\n\n## What to build\n\nAn `Embedder` implementation running a small model in-process — the standing\n\"R14\" plan (bge-small class, ~130 MB, checksum-pinned weights). It slots in\nbehind the existing seam with no changes above it.\n\n## Files\n\n- `crates/stella-embed/src/` — new module beside `hash.rs` and `http.rs`,\n  behind its own cargo feature, exactly as `http` is.\n- `crates/stella-embed/src/seam.rs` — the trait it implements; unchanged.\n- `crates/stella-embed/README.md` § \"Why the semantic backend is HTTP, not\n  in-process ONNX\" — the section this issue supersedes when it lands.\n- `crates/stella-tools/src/graph/semantic.rs` — resolution order: an\n  in-process model should be the default when nothing is configured, ahead of\n  `HashEmbedder`, and behind an explicit `STELLA_EMBED_URL`.\n\n## Constraints the implementer must clear first\n\n- **`deny.toml` is a real gate.** `cargo deny check advisories bans sources\n  licenses` runs in CI. A dependency carrying a restrictive licence gets\n  dropped, never allow-listed. Check the whole transitive tree of whatever\n  runtime is chosen (`ort` links onnxruntime; `candle` is pure Rust and\n  Apache-2/MIT but pulls a large tree) **before** writing code.\n- **Binary size and compile time.** The workspace gate compiles 22 crates; a\n  heavy inference tree is a cost every contributor pays on every `make gate`.\n  Measure both before and after and put the numbers in the PR.\n- **The weight fetch is on the indexing path.** It must be checksum-pinned,\n  cached under `~/.stella`, resumable, and must never make a first query hang\n  with no output. A failed fetch degrades to `HashEmbedder` with a labelled\n  note, the same shape `semantic.rs` already uses for a failed HTTP backend.\n- **Determinism.** The stored vector for identical input must be identical\n  across machines and across a compiler upgrade — the fingerprint's\n  `(content_hash, fingerprint)` skip depends on it.\n- **The posture is a claim with a number to defend.** See #2993: declaring\n  `Semantic` requires a measured admission floor.\n\n## Done looks like\n\n- `graph_query op=semantic` returns a genuine meaning match on a fresh clone\n  with no environment variables set.\n- A witness in the shape of\n  `crates/stella-graph/tests/semantic_recall.rs` — a query whose answer shares\n  no substring with the file's name — passing against the in-process model.\n- The size/compile-time numbers in the PR description."[m[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"number"[m[1;37m:[m 2994[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"title"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"stella-embed: in-process semantic embedder (ONNX/candle) so semantic search needs no configuration — the R14 follow-up"[m
  [1;37m}[m[1;37m,[m
