    [1;34m"body"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"## Problem\n\n#2935 gave adoption a mode replay (`crates/stella-cli/src/candidate_ws/modes.rs`).\nIt is deliberately **asymmetric**: it applies the candidate's permission bits only\nwhen the candidate's mode is one a git patch cannot encode (anything other than\n`0644`/`0755`). That is what keeps it safe — a tracked file enters the candidate\nthrough `git worktree add`, so its mode there is git's normalization and *not* the\nmode the user's own file carries, and forcing the candidate's mode onto every\ndelivered path would destroy a `0600` the user set on a file the candidate merely\nedited the contents of.\n\nThe residue is the other direction. If the real tree holds `0600` at a path and the\ncandidate *deliberately* relaxes it to `0644`, nothing carries that:\n\n- git sees both as `100644`, so the patch has no mode line;\n- the replay skips it, because `0644` is representable.\n\nThe delivered file keeps `0600`. A task whose deliverable is \"make this file\nworld-readable again\" therefore cannot be satisfied through the candidate path.\n\n## Why it was not fixed with #2935\n\nThe two cases are indistinguishable from the candidate worktree alone: \"the\ncandidate chose `0644`\" and \"git checked it out as `0644` and nobody touched it\"\nproduce the identical `st_mode`. Distinguishing them needs a third observation the\nworkspace does not currently make — most likely the mode of the same path in the\n**baseline commit's worktree at the moment of snapshot**, i.e. the real tree's mode\nrecorded at `create_workspace` time, so the replay can ask \"did the candidate change\nthis from what the real tree had?\" rather than \"is this mode unusual?\".\n\nThat is a real design step (it reintroduces a recorded description of a tree, which\n`modes.rs`'s doc argues against, so the recording has to be justified and bounded),\nand it wants its own witness.\n\n## Files\n\n- `crates/stella-cli/src/candidate_ws/modes.rs` — `is_git_representable`,\n  `replay_unrepresentable_modes`, and the doc section \"What is replayed, and what\n  deliberately is not\"\n- `crates/stella-cli/src/candidate_ws.rs` — `create_workspace` / `populate_snapshot`,\n  where a real-tree mode observation would have to be taken\n\n## Verify\n\n`crates/stella-cli/src/candidate_ws/tests.rs::a_mode_no_patch_can_encode_survives_adoption`\nis the existing witness for the tightening direction. The new case: scaffold a repo\nwhose tracked file is `0600` in the real tree, have the candidate `chmod 644` it and\nedit it, adopt, assert the delivered file is `0644`. It fails today.\n\n## Definition of done\n\n- Adoption delivers a deliberate relaxation, and still never clobbers a user-set mode\n  on a path the candidate only edited the contents of (both assertions in the suite).\n- Whatever recording the fix introduces is argued in `modes.rs`'s module doc against\n  the \"one description of the tree\" rule that doc currently states.\n\nRefs #2935."[m[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"number"[m[1;37m:[m 2988[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"title"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"candidate_ws: adoption cannot deliver a mode RELAXATION, only a tightening git could not express"[m
  [1;37m}[m[1;37m,[m
