    [1;34m"body"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"## Problem\n\nIn a measured head-to-head against Claude Code (2026-08-12, 10 Terminal-Bench-style tasks x 2\nattempts per arm, claude-sonnet-5 at effort high on Anthropic direct, 30-minute timeout, no caps),\nStella spent **24-36% of every tool call on its own verification apparatus** — witness authoring,\nwitness *debugging*, fail-then-pass control construction, `verify_done`, and `repo_status` /\n`repo_recover` / `repo_diff` bookkeeping. Claude Code spent **zero** calls on any of it and solved\n20/20.\n\nThe apparatus is not free and it is not currently visible as a cost anywhere.\n\n## The measurement that makes this the headline\n\nPartitioning every trial's calls into (A) verification apparatus and (B) residual task work — where\nA is every call at or after the first call touching `verify_done` or a witness artifact, plus every\n`repo_status` / `repo_recover` / `repo_diff` / `repo_history` / `ask_user` / `probe_capability` /\n`get_environment` / `project_overview` / `graph_query` call anywhere:\n\n| calls per trial (n=20 each) | careful prompt | decisive prompt | Claude Code |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| TOTAL tool calls | 32.45 | 27.15 | 12.45 |\n| A. verification apparatus | 11.75 | 6.40 | 0.00 |\n| B. residual task work | **20.70** | **20.75** | 12.45 |\n\nSolve rate: 18/20, 17/20, 20/20. Cost/trial: $0.787, $0.766, $0.370.\n\n**The two Stella arms do identical amounts of task work — 20.70 vs 20.75 calls — to two decimal\nplaces.** The entire 32.5 -> 27.1 call reduction attributed to the \"decisive prompt\" experiment was\nthe apparatus shrinking from 11.75 to 6.40. Nothing about how Stella solves the task changed. That\nalso explains the two results that looked contradictory at the time: cost stayed flat (the removed\ncalls were cheap short-output ones) and a solve was lost (the removed calls included real\nverification).\n\n14/20 careful trials and 8/20 decisive trials built a witness apparatus at all.\n\n## The apparatus is also where the debugging goes\n\n`dec1/sanitize-git-repo/sanitize-git-repo__zxiY4H6` (82 calls, **reward 0.0**) — Claude Code solved\nthe same task in 14 calls. Calls 1-27 are the task. Then:\n\n- call 28: `ask_user` with three options — returns **0 bytes**; there is no human in a bench run.\n- calls 42-56: author `tests/test_no_hardcoded_secrets.py`, then **fifteen calls debugging the\n  witness test's own subprocess invocation**, not the code under test. Three full edit-then-run\n  cycles on `fatal: ambiguous argument 'AKIA1234567890123456': unknown revision or path not in the\n  working tree` — a `git grep` argv bug in the harness Stella wrote.\n- calls 57-65: `verify_done`, `repo_status`, `repo_recover`, `.git/filter-repo/commit-map`\n  archaeology, then building a *contaminated control branch* (`_verify_control`) to prove the\n  witness fails on bad code — 3 more calls.\n\n38 of 82 calls after the task was done, on an instrument that then scored 0.\n\n## What to decide\n\nThis is a design question, not a bug report with an obvious patch, which is why it is filed rather\nthan fixed:\n\n1. **Is the apparatus load-bearing on the benchmark?** Careful (11.75 apparatus calls) scored 18/20;\n   decisive (6.40) scored 17/20. One trial is noise at n=20 — this comparison **cannot** resolve\n   whether verification buys solves. It needs repeats per task before anyone concludes either way.\n2. **`ask_user` in a headless run.** It returned 0 bytes twice per arm and consumed a call plus a\n   model round-trip. A tool that cannot be answered should not be offered when no interactive\n   surface is attached. (Cheap, separable, probably its own PR.)\n3. **Witness-harness debugging is uncapped.** 15 consecutive calls fixing a test harness Stella\n   wrote is the dominant single cost observed. Related: #2872 (a witness broken as an instrument\n   reads as a failing assertion), #2863 (`verify_done` has no rejection cap), #2559 (witness author\n   re-derives test conventions, 20 tool calls).\n4. **`repo_status` / `repo_recover` bookkeeping** fired 2.45-3.95 times per trial with no\n   task-visible consequence in the traces read.\n\n## Repro\n\nData is local, no S3 fetch needed:\n`/Users/macanderson/.claude/jobs/e286a51b/tmp/s5-mirror/{s5b2,dec1,ccFull}/<task>/<trial>/`\n(`events.jsonl` + `reward.txt` for Stella; `cc.txt` + `reward.txt` for Claude Code).\n\nCount tool calls from `tool_start` events only — `step_usage` is a completed model call and\n`num_turns` in the Claude Code stream is a conversation turn; **tool calls are the only unit\nrecorded identically on both sides**, and comparing the other two already produced one wrong\nconclusion in this investigation.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nNot \"make the number smaller\". Either (a) a measurement with repeats per task showing what the\napparatus buys in solve rate, so the cost is a justified trade a maintainer can see; or (b) a\ndesign change that keeps the guarantee at materially fewer calls, with the same measurement to\nprove it did not cost solves. Items 2 and 4 above are separable and can land first.\n\nRefs #2872, #2863, #2559, #2668\n"[m[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"number"[m[1;37m:[m 2983[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"title"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"bench: the verification apparatus is 24-36% of every tool call and Claude Code spends zero — and it is the ONLY thing the decisive-prompt experiment changed (residual task work identical to 2dp)"[m
  [1;37m}[m[1;37m,[m
