    [1;34m"body"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"## Problem\n\nImproving stella is already run as a discipline — preregister, pin the dataset,\nrun both arms, read the trace — but the discipline lives in prose\n(`CLAUDE.md`, `AGENTS.md`, `bench/terminal-bench-2.1-protocol.md`) and in\nhand-assembled directories under `bench/evidence/`. Nothing in the tree models\nthe objects themselves, so:\n\n- a hypothesis exists only as a GitHub issue and a commit message;\n- the link from *hypothesis → experiment → dataset → benchmark run → traces →\n  verdict* is reconstructed by a human reading directory names;\n- a run's identity digests are re-derived per analysis script rather than being\n  a queryable property (`bench/make_manifest.py`, `bench/compare_arms.py`,\n  `bench/score_dev_baseline.py` each re-do part of this);\n- \"which commit was this arm, and what was the control?\" is answerable only by\n  someone who was there.\n\nThis is the exact failure CLAUDE.md's bench rules were written against: the\n2026-08-07 session where every surface signal was wrong in a different\ndirection, and the 2026-08-09 session that dated a run from two signals present\non both sides of the commit in question. Those are both *lineage* failures —\nthe evidence could not distinguish the claim from its opposite because the\nobjects and their relationships were never recorded.\n\nThe vision microsite (`website/public/vision.html` §03) sells this plane as a\nstella 2.0 product. Today it is a practice, not a product — that gap is what\nthis issue closes.\n\n## What \"done\" looks like\n\nA durable, queryable experiment plane where each object has an identity and a\nparent link:\n\n- `hypothesis` — a falsifiable statement, its owner, its status, the issue it\n  came from.\n- `experiment` — a hypothesis plus the branch/commit under test and the control\n  commit. An experiment that names no control is rejected, not defaulted.\n- `dataset` — a pinned identifier plus content digest (the\n  `terminal-bench-2-1@sha256:…` shape already used in `bench/evidence/`).\n- `benchmark_run` — an (experiment, dataset) pair with its preregistration\n  timestamp, host, and per-arm SUT digests.\n- `trial` / `trace` — per-task rows joined to the run, pointing at the\n  `stella-events.jsonl` that is ground truth.\n- `verdict` — the conclusion drawn, with the query that produced it, so a\n  reader can re-run the reasoning rather than trust the sentence.\n\nPlus a CLI surface to create and query them, and one command that reproduces a\npublished number from the recorded lineage alone.\n\n## Constraints discovered\n\n- **Invariant #1 and #2** (`AGENTS.md` § \"Architecture: ports, not\n  concretions\"): no I/O in `stella-core`. Persistence belongs in\n  `stella-store`; the decision logic (is this experiment well-formed? does this\n  comparison have a valid control?) is pure functions and should be\n  property-tested.\n- **Invariant #9, tool-first single-purpose**: each verb is its own tool /\n  subcommand. `record_experiment(delete=true)` is two tools and gets split.\n- **Invariant #3, zero telemetry egress**: these rows describe *runs*, and a\n  run's prompts and diffs are content. Anything that could become exportable\n  must go through the reviewed allowlist in\n  `crates/stella-store/src/content_free.rs` — adding a hub column fails\n  `make gate` until a human edits the allowlist, which is the intended\n  friction. Do not widen it casually.\n- **Schema work**: `crates/stella-store/src/ddl.rs` declares no foreign keys;\n  `Store::prune` cascades explicitly across the tables keyed off\n  `executions.id` (`crates/stella-store/src/prune.rs`). Any new table keyed off\n  an execution must be added to that cascade in the same PR or retention\n  silently orphans rows.\n- **Invariant #10**: a new `AgentEvent` variant must declare its consumer in\n  `crates/stella-protocol/src/event/consumers.rs` or it is an `E0004` build\n  error.\n- **God files**: `crates/stella-store/src/lib.rs` and\n  `crates/stella-store/src/tests.rs` are both grandfathered over the 1500-line\n  ratchet and closed to growth (`scripts/file-size-baseline.txt`). New logic\n  lands in a sibling submodule — `crates/stella-core/src/driver/settlement.rs`\n  is the pattern.\n\n## How to verify\n\n- Witness test: a test that asserts a benchmark run can be reconstructed from\n  the store alone — same solve counts, same per-task verdicts as the committed\n  `bench/evidence/tb21-hh10-20260731/` directory. It must fail on `main`\n  (there is no such query today) and pass on the change.\n- Property test for the well-formedness rules (an experiment with no control,\n  a run whose two arms differ by more than the declared variable) — these are\n  pure predicates and belong in `stella-core` alongside the existing property\n  suites.\n- `make gate` green, including `file-size`, `typed-errors` and\n  `wire-schema` if any protocol type changes.\n\n## Scope note\n\nThis is deliberately the *plane*, not the UI. Surfacing it in\n`stella observe` (`crates/stella-observatory/`) is a follow-up, and should be\nfiled separately once the schema settles rather than bundled here.\n\n\n---\n\n## Alignment note — the cheap first cut lands first (2026-08-16)\n\nDesign: `doc:turn-loop-wrappers` §5. Refs #3381.\n\n#3381 adds **one column** to the executions table: the id of the wrapper variant\nthat ran, beside the door tag that is already there. That gives us\ncost/outcome/verified-rate per variant from a `GROUP BY variant` over\n`.stella/private/store.db`, with no new object model at all.\n\n**This is not a competing design.** It is the narrowest possible instance of\nthis epic's thesis — that lineage should be a queryable property rather than\nsomething a human reconstructs from directory names — applied to the one axis we\nare about to start iterating on hardest.\n\n**The ask: let #3381's column land first, then model it.**\n\nTwo reasons.\n\n1. **This epic gets real rows to model instead of imagined ones.** Right now the\n   object shapes here are derived from how bench runs were organised in the\n   past. A variant column produces a live, growing, in-tree example of\n   \"hypothesis → arm → run → verdict\" at the smallest scale, and modelling\n   something that exists is a much better test of a schema than modelling\n   something that does not.\n2. **It is the same honesty problem at one-tenth the size.** #3381 carries two\n   rules that are this epic's rules in miniature: a variant id is written only\n   when that manifest actually ran (never blank on a fallback path — a missing\n   measurement must not render as a negative one), and two variants compared\n   over different task sets are confounded and the query must say so. If those\n   two rules cannot be made to hold for one column, they will not hold for a\n   full experiment plane.\n\n**What that means for this epic's plan.** Nothing is dropped. Add the wrapper\nvariant as a named first-class dimension the plane must be able to express, and\ntreat #3381's column as the migration source rather than as a thing to replace.\n"[m[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"number"[m[1;37m:[m 2889[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"title"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"Experiment plane: make hypotheses, experiments, datasets, benchmark runs and traces first-class linked objects"[m
  [1;37m}[m[1;37m,[m
