    [1;34m"body"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"## Problem\n\n`check-no-scratch` (`scripts/check-no-scratch.sh`) cannot see the most common way session scratch\nreaches the remote, because its invariant is narrower than the rule it enforces.\n\nAGENTS.md states the rule without qualification: *\"**Session scratch must never reach the\nremote** (#448) — your reflections, plans, repro trees, and memory files stay on your disk.\"*\n\nThe guard's invariant is different: **if a path is ignored, it must not be tracked.** Its whole\nimplementation is one line —\n\n```sh\noffenders=\"$(git ls-files --cached --ignored --exclude-standard)\"\n```\n\n— so a scratch file only fails the gate if some `.gitignore` rule *already matches it*. Scratch\nunder a path this repository does not ignore is invisible to it. The script's own header explains\nthe design (\"deliberately general rather than a second copy of the scratch path list … catches\nevery future scratch directory the moment someone ignores it\"), which is a good property; the gap\nis that it is also the *only* property, so an unignored scratch path is unguarded.\n\n`.scratch/` is exactly that path. `git check-ignore -v .scratch/msg.txt` exits 1 — nothing ignores\nit — so a `.scratch/` file that gets committed passes `check-no-scratch`, `make gate`, and CI.\n\n## Repro (this actually happened, 2026-08-11)\n\nWorking on #2880 in a worktree. I wrote a commit message to `.scratch/msg.txt` (scratch, by\nconstruction), then amended with `git add -A`, which swept it into the commit, and pushed.\n\n```\n$ git ls-files .scratch\n.scratch/msg.txt\n$ git check-ignore -v .scratch/msg.txt ; echo \"exit:$?\"\nexit:1\n$ bash scripts/check-no-scratch.sh\ncheck-no-scratch: OK — no tracked file is gitignored.\n```\n\nGreen gate, scratch on the remote. It was public for about a minute before I noticed it in\n`git status` and removed it with `git rm --cached` + amend + force-push. Nothing in the toolchain\ntold me; I found it by eye.\n\nMinimal reproduction from a clean checkout:\n\n```sh\nmkdir -p .scratch && echo scratch > .scratch/note.md\ngit add .scratch/note.md\nbash scripts/check-no-scratch.sh   # prints OK, exit 0\n```\n\n`git add -A` is the aggravating factor and is a real hazard in a worktree where scratch lives\n*inside* the tree, but it is not the defect. The defect is a guard that cannot answer the question\nAGENTS.md asks.\n\n## Two candidate fixes — deliberately not choosing between them\n\n**(a) Ignore `.scratch/` repo-wide, so the existing guard covers it.** One line in `.gitignore`\nand the current invariant does the rest, including the \"already tracked\" case it was written for.\nCheap and consistent with the script's stated design. Costs: it only closes the paths someone\nthought to ignore, so the next unignored scratch convention (`.notes/`, `.plans/`, `tmp/`) is open\nagain; and per that same header, a too-broad ignore rule has its own failure mode — a file that\ngenuinely belongs under the path can then never be committed without `-f`.\n\n**(b) Teach the guard a set of known-scratch path names, independent of gitignore.** A short\nreviewed list (`.scratch/`, `.agent/`, `.notes/`, …) checked against `git ls-files` directly,\n*in addition to* the existing ignored-but-tracked check. Closes the case regardless of what\n`.gitignore` says. Costs: it is the \"second copy of the scratch path list\" the script's header\nargues against, so the list must be justified as a reviewed artifact rather than a pattern dump,\nand it needs an escape hatch for a repository that legitimately tracks such a path.\n\nThe trade — one general invariant that misses cases, versus a general invariant plus a named list\nthat drifts — is a maintainer's call, not a mechanical one, which is why both are stated here\nwithout a recommendation.\n\n## Done looks like\n\n- The repro above fails the gate, with a message naming the file and how to untrack it.\n- The existing ignored-but-tracked check still fails on its own case (do not replace it — #448's\n  original incident, and the `website/public/icons/` too-broad-rule case in the script's own\n  footer, both live there).\n- A witness: a test that fails before the change and passes after. `scripts/` has no Rust harness,\n  so the natural home is the shell harness `make self-driving-test` already runs, or a new\n  `scripts/`-level check with the same shape — say which you used in the PR.\n- `make gate` green.\n\n## Constraints already discovered\n\n- `scripts/check-no-scratch.sh` is the first step in `GATE_STEPS` \"because it costs milliseconds\"\n  — whatever lands must stay that cheap.\n- The script deliberately uses `git ls-files --cached --ignored` rather than `git check-ignore`,\n  because `check-ignore` consults the index and reports a *tracked* file as \"not ignored\", i.e. it\n  is blind to precisely the state the guard exists to find. Any new check must not reintroduce\n  that.\n- It traps `SIGPIPE` on the success path (#1815) so a reader closing the pipe cannot turn a green\n  verdict red; keep that.\n- It runs on a bare CI runner, POSIX tools only.\n- Adding a gate step means updating `GATE_STEPS` in the `Makefile` *and* the block in AGENTS.md\n  *and* CONTRIBUTING.md, or `check-gate-parity` fails. Extending the existing step avoids that.\n\nRelated: #448 (the original incident and the rule), #1815 (the SIGPIPE trap), #2880 (where this\nwas found).\n"[m[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"number"[m[1;37m:[m 2888[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"title"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"check-no-scratch cannot see unignored session scratch — .scratch/ reached the remote through a green gate"[m
  [1;37m}[m[1;37m,[m
