    [1;34m"body"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"## Problem\n\n#2545's supervisor refuses to arm for two match shapes, and warns on the seat rather than\nfailing silently (`reauth.supervision_blocked`):\n\n1. **No explicit task list** (`spec.tasks == ()`, i.e. run the whole dataset). The\n   re-dispatch set is \"the sweep's tasks minus the ones with a measured trial\", and a\n   `harbor run` with no `--include-task-name` filter has a task set the supervisor cannot\n   see. Arming anyway would make `Snapshot.unmeasured()` empty from the first tick, which\n   reads as `COMPLETE` — a supervisor that believes a running sweep is finished.\n2. **`attempts != 1`.** With `--n-attempts > 1`, \"this task has a measured trial\" stops\n   meaning \"this task is finished\", so the supervisor would release its watch early *and*\n   a re-dispatch would launch a whole fresh attempt set to recover one voided attempt.\n\nBoth refusals are deliberate and each is pinned by a test\n(`tests/test_reauth_wiring.py::TestTheSupervisorIsReached`). But they are gaps, not\ndecisions: a rotation during either shape still truncates the arm exactly as it did\nbefore #2545.\n\nThe planned 89-task head-to-head passes an explicit task list with one attempt, so it is\ncovered. A `arenabench run` over a whole dataset is not.\n\n## What each needs\n\n- **Whole-dataset**: the supervisor needs the task list Harbor would enumerate. The\n  registry already resolves a dataset to a local export (`Registry.local_run_path`), so\n  the names are obtainable without the network in the common case. Decide whether to\n  resolve them at launch and pass them to `reauth.supervise`, or to teach the supervisor a\n  \"task set unknown\" mode that can still act on voids without computing `unmeasured`.\n- **Multi-attempt**: `Snapshot.unmeasured` becomes per (task, attempt) rather than per\n  task, and a re-dispatch must ask Harbor for only the missing attempts. Check whether\n  `harbor run` can express that at all — if it cannot, the honest outcome may be to keep\n  refusing and say so in the docs.\n\n## Also here: the pause grace is not configurable\n\n`reauth.PAUSE_GRACE_SECONDS` (1800s) bounds how long a paused seat waits for a credential\nbefore the match gives up and reports the shortfall. It is a constructor parameter on\n`MatchReauth` and nothing sets it, so there is no way for an operator running a long\nunattended sweep to say \"wait longer\" or for CI to say \"never wait\". A field on\n`MatchSpec` (and the `arenabench.toml` template) is the obvious home.\n\n## Files\n\n- `arenabench/arenabench/reauth.py` — `supervision_blocked`, `PAUSE_GRACE_SECONDS`,\n  `MatchReauth.pause_grace`, `Snapshot.unmeasured`\n- `arenabench/arenabench/reauth_wiring.py` — `supervise_match`\n- `arenabench/arenabench/registry.py` — `local_run_path`, for enumerating a dataset\n- `arenabench/arenabench/model.py` — `MatchSpec`, if the grace becomes a template field\n- `arenabench/tests/test_reauth_wiring.py` — `TestTheSupervisorIsReached` pins the refusals\n\n## Definition of done\n\n- Either the shape is supervised, with a witness at the wiring layer, or the refusal is\n  documented as permanent with the reason — not left as an unexplained warning string.\n- The existing refusal tests are updated rather than deleted, and named in the PR.\n\n## Related\n\n- #2545 — the wiring that introduced both refusals.\n"[m[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"number"[m[1;37m:[m 2858[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"title"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"arenabench: whole-dataset and multi-attempt matches get no credential-rotation recovery"[m
  [1;37m}[m[1;37m,[m
