    [1;34m"body"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"Three declared, documented limitations of #2685's working-set restoration, filed as the logical next steps. Files: `crates/stella-core/src/restore.rs` (pure half), `crates/stella-core/src/driver/restore.rs` (engine half).\n\n(1) Only `read_file` tool calls enter the working set (`restore::READ_TOOL`): content the model obtained via `read_symbol` (which reads through the same tool at the stella-tools layer but calls it under its own name) or via `bash cat` is not tracked and not restored. Extending READ_TOOL to a set — or having read_symbol record a read_file-shaped call — needs a decision about input replay (read_symbol's input names a symbol, not a path).\n\n(2) Chained restoration parses embedded sections by fence lines (restore.rs: FILE_SECTION_OPEN/SKILL_SECTION_CLOSE, `fn embedded_sections`); a skill body containing a literal `--- end skill ---` line truncates its own chained copy in the NEXT summarization round (round one is unaffected). Documented in the fences doc comment; a content-derived delimiter or length-prefixed section would close it.\n\n(3) The budget-abort arm of `Engine::summarize_overflow_span` applies a paid summary without restoration (deliberate — the splice there only shrinks a dying turn's resumable context; rationale in driver/restore.rs module docs). If resumed sessions should reload a restored working set, that wants its own design at resume time, not at abort time.\n\nVerify each by reading the named docs/sites; witnesses for any fix should follow the existing test pattern in driver/restore.rs::tests (in-memory MapTools, base-runnable literals). Done: each limitation either implemented with witnesses or explicitly declined in the module docs with this issue closed.\n\nRefs #2685, #2682, #2787."[m[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"number"[m[1;37m:[m 2838[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"title"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"Working-set restoration follow-ups: non-read_file reads, fence-collision in chained skill bodies, budget-abort arm"[m
  [1;37m}[m[1;37m,[m
