    [1;34m"body"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"**Outcome:** the candidate-workspace (best-of-N) lifecycle in `stella-cli`/`stella-pipeline` never silently discards work and never leaves the graded tree or the user's real repo in a corrupted or half-repaired state.\n\nThree open issues share this outcome and no existing epic covers `candidate_ws` scope (#2773 \"verification ladder evidence integrity\" is scoped to the witness/verify stage's signals and evidence artifacts, not execute-stage worktree/ref lifecycle mechanics):\n\n- #2641 — a refused ref-escape names the recovery command but never runs it, leaving the graded tree's ref moved with no repair. Measured to zero a solvable TB2.1 trial.\n- #2651 — work done in the candidate is silently discarded when the turn budget ends the run before `seal()`; a solved fix-git run scores 0 and the candidate doubles wall-clock for nothing. Corroborated by a 12-trial / 4-SUT experiment (0/2 vs 5/5).\n- #1383 — replace the git-diff-and-adopt design with a container-native whole-tree copy-and-promote `CandidateWorkspacePort`, which removes the class of ref-mutation hazard #2641 and #2651 both hit.\n\n**Definition of done:** all three children closed — #1383's container-native redesign lands, and #2641/#2651 either become moot under it or are independently fixed if #1383 slips.\n\n**Children:** #2641, #2651, #1383\n\n\n## Children adopted in the 2026-08-12 triage sweep\n\nFive more, all measured on the 60-trial `main@554bb4f` run: the sandbox rewriting the worker's own deliverables (#2928), the string-match isolation guard a worker escaped (#2931), file modes that cannot ride a git patch (#2935), a wall-clock kill discarding the whole candidate (#2941), and the recovery surface that under-reports the orphaned commits (#2589).\n\n#2589 #2928 #2931 #2935 #2941\n\n\n\n---\n\n## Alignment note — outcome unchanged, landing site changes (2026-08-16)\n\nDesign: `doc:turn-loop-wrappers` §4. Refs #3380, #3381.\n\nThe candidate-workspace outcome stands exactly as written: never silently\ndiscard work, never leave the graded tree or the user's repo half-repaired.\n#2651 alone is a measured zero on a solvable task, so nothing here waits on\narchitecture.\n\nWhat changes is **where the eventual fix belongs**.\n\nThe staged pipeline becomes a plugin over a four-point wrapper contract (#3380)\nwith its stages declared in a manifest (#3381). The candidate workspace is\nmachinery the `execute` stage uses — so under that architecture it is a\n**capability the host lends a wrapper**, not something a wrapper owns. That\nmatters for #1383 in particular: a container-native `CandidateWorkspacePort`\ndesigned as a port the host implements is exactly the right shape and gets\neasier, not harder. A container-native design wired directly into\n`pipeline.rs`'s execute path would have to move again.\n\n**Two constraints for whoever picks up a child here**\n\n1. **Do not grow `crates/stella-pipeline/src/pipeline.rs`** — god-file list,\n   closed to growth, and being taken apart. Land in a sibling module or behind\n   the port.\n2. **Prefer the port.** #1383 already proposes replacing git-diff-and-adopt with\n   a whole-tree copy-and-promote port, and this epic's own definition of done\n   says #2641/#2651 may become moot under it. That is the alignment: the port is\n   the durable shape under the wrapper architecture too.\n\n**One cross-reference worth making explicit.** #2651's failure mode — the turn\nbudget ends the run before `seal()`, so finished work is discarded — is an\n*ending-ownership* bug in the same family as #3379. Today the pipeline decides\nwhen a run is over, and the promotion step is something it does on the way to\nthat decision, which is why a budget stop can cut in front of it. Under #3379\nthe engine's turn ends cleanly and the wrapper's `again?` runs afterward on a\ncompleted turn. That does not fix #2651 by itself, but it removes one of the\nraces, and whoever fixes #2651 should check whether the fix is simpler stated\nagainst the post-#3379 shape.\n"[m[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"number"[m[1;37m:[m 2815[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"title"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"Epic: candidate workspace (best-of-N) lifecycle correctness"[m
  [1;37m}[m[1;37m,[m
