    [1;34m"body"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"Split out of #2641. That issue's fix repairs an escaped shared ref at two\nseams — `seal_inner` and `remove()` — and both are **in-process**. This issue\ncovers the third case: the process is gone.\n\n## Problem\n\n`refs_at_create` (`crates/stella-cli/src/candidate_ws.rs`, on\n`GitCandidateWorkspace`) is the *only* record of what the shared ref namespace\nlooked like before a candidate could reach it, and it lives in process memory.\nIf the `stella` process is killed — SIGKILL, OOM, a container teardown, a\nlaptop lid — after a candidate moved `refs/heads/master` but before either\nrepair seam runs, the graded tree keeps the moved ref and **nothing on disk\nknows what it should be**. The next `stella run` in that repository records the\nalready-corrupted value as its own `refs_at_create` and treats the damage as\nthe baseline.\n\nThis is the residue of the #2641 fix, not a regression from it: before that\nfix the ref stayed moved in *every* case.\n\n## Sketch\n\nA durable per-candidate record — the shared-ref snapshot plus the candidate's\nworktree path and baseline — written at `create_workspace` time and removed on\na clean teardown. A later `stella` invocation sweeps leftover records and\noffers (or performs) the same compare-and-swap restore\n`GitCandidateWorkspace::restore` performs today.\n\n## Two constraints that dictate the design\n\n### 1. `cleanup()` destroys the natural home for the record\n\nThe obvious place to put a per-candidate record is\n`<toplevel>/.git/worktrees/<name>/` — git already keeps per-worktree state\nthere, and it is keyed by exactly the right thing. But `cleanup()`\n(`crates/stella-cli/src/candidate_ws.rs`, the `git worktree prune` at the end)\n**deletes that directory**:\n\n```rust\nasync fn cleanup(toplevel: &Path, dir: &Path) {\n    ... worktree remove --force ...\n    let _ = tokio::fs::remove_dir_all(dir).await;\n    let _ = SystemGitCli.run(toplevel, &[\"worktree\", \"prune\"]).await;   // <- removes .git/worktrees/<name>/\n}\n```\n\nSo either the record lives somewhere `prune` does not reach (e.g. under\n`.stella/private/`, which is already the home for owner-only generated local\nstate, `0700`/`0600`), or the sweep must read it **before** `cleanup` runs —\nwhich defeats the point, since the crash case is precisely the one where\nnothing runs. Put it outside `.git/worktrees/`.\n\nNote also that `prune` is what makes the record's *absence* ambiguous: a\npruned worktree and a never-created one look identical from the toplevel.\n\n### 2. Repairing a run you did not start is a consent question\n\nA sweep that silently rewrites refs on startup is a different contract from\n#2641's, and a worse one:\n\n- The record may belong to a **live** `stella` process in another terminal\n  working in the same repository. Restoring its refs mid-run is the damage\n  this whole area exists to prevent. A liveness check (pid + start time, or an\n  advisory lock) is required, not optional.\n- The record may be arbitrarily old, and the user may have since made the moved\n  value their own deliberately — the fix-git shape in #2651 is exactly a task\n  whose *correct answer* is a ref move.\n- #2641's repair is justified by immediacy: the candidate moved the ref\n  seconds ago and this process watched it happen. A cross-process sweep has\n  neither property, so the honest default is likely **report and offer**\n  (a named command, or a prompt) rather than an automatic write.\n\nWhatever is chosen, the compare-and-swap discipline from #2641 is the floor:\nname both the recorded value and the value found, never force, and report a\nlost CAS as \"still moved\" rather than overwriting.\n\n## Files\n\n- `crates/stella-cli/src/candidate_ws.rs` — `refs_at_create`,\n  `create_workspace` (where the record would be written), `cleanup` (the prune\n  described above), `restore_escaped_refs_inner` (the teardown seam)\n- `crates/stella-cli/src/candidate_ws/ref_escape.rs` —\n  `GitCandidateWorkspace::restore`, `restore_stash`, `refusal`; the\n  compare-and-swap and `refs/stash` handling a sweep would reuse. Note both are\n  methods on `GitCandidateWorkspace` and read `self.baseline` / `self.dir`, so\n  a sweep with no live workspace needs them refactored to free functions over\n  a record, or a reconstructed workspace value.\n- `crates/stella-cli/src/candidate_ws/tests.rs` — `scaffold()`, the dirty-repo\n  fixture the tests drive real git against\n\n## Also in scope: the teardown repair is silent\n\n`CandidateWorkspace::remove` returns `()`\n(`crates/stella-pipeline/src/ports.rs`), so\n`restore_escaped_refs_inner` — added by #2641 — repairs the graded tree with\n**no way to tell anyone**. `stella-cli` has no `tracing` dependency today. A\nuser whose branch was moved and put back during teardown currently learns\nnothing. Surfacing it (an `AgentEvent`, a port return value, or a CLI notice)\nbelongs with this work, and invariant #10 (\"every emitted signal names its\nconsumer\") applies to whatever is added.\n\n## Definition of done\n\n- A candidate that moved a shared ref and whose process died before any\n  teardown can be detected and repaired on a later invocation, with the\n  liveness and consent questions above answered explicitly in the PR.\n- The durable record survives `git worktree prune`.\n- A witness test: write a record, simulate the dead process, assert the sweep\n  restores (or offers to restore) the ref — failing on `main`.\n- A test that a record belonging to a live process is **not** swept.\n- The teardown repair is no longer silent.\n\n## Related\n\n- #2641 — the in-process repair this is the residue of\n- #2651 — the measured cost of the ref-escape wall on TB2.1 fix-git\n- #1383 — the structural fix (a substrate that cannot reach those refs at all)\n"[m[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"number"[m[1;37m:[m 2813[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"title"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"candidate_ws: a candidate killed before teardown leaves the graded tree's ref moved with no in-process record to repair it from"[m
  [1;37m}[m[1;37m,[m
  [1;37m{[m
    [1;34m"body"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"Split out of #2641, which fixed that issue's headline (a refused escape now\nrestores the graded tree's ref) and explicitly left these two **detection**\ngaps open. `crates/stella-cli/src/candidate_ws/ref_escape.rs`'s module doc\ncites this issue by number under \"Boundaries, accepted and documented\".\n\n## Problem\n\n`GitCandidateWorkspace::escaped_refs_inner` attributes a ref move to the\ncandidate by signing its **new value**: either the new value is an\nancestor-or-equal of the candidate's live `HEAD` (it committed onto the shared\nbranch), or the candidate's private baseline is an ancestor of the new value\n(its history was grafted in). Two shapes carry no such signature.\n\n### 1. A deleted ref is not detected\n\nA ref present in `refs_at_create` and absent at seal (`git branch -D`,\n`git tag -d`, executed from the candidate worktree) has **no new value to\nsign**, so the comparison in `escaped_refs_inner` — which iterates the refs\nthat exist *now* — never even visits it. The `refs_at_create` map already\nholds everything needed to notice the disappearance; a set difference in the\nother direction is a few lines.\n\nWhat is missing is the attribution, and it may genuinely not exist:\n\n- **Git deletes a ref's reflog along with the ref**, so the forensic record\n  that would say *who* moved it and *from what* is gone at exactly the moment\n  you want it. There is no forgery-resistant signature left to read.\n- A user deleting a merged branch in their own checkout mid-run is an ordinary\n  action and is indistinguishable from a candidate doing it.\n\nSo the honest outcome is probably **a distinct, non-failing warning** rather\nthan an abort — and almost certainly **not** a repair. Note that resurrecting\na deliberately-deleted branch is its own damage: `#2641`'s repair contract\n(\"put back exactly what this candidate moved, compare-and-swap, never force\")\ndoes not extend here, because the precondition it relies on (a value only this\ncandidate could have produced) is unavailable. A repair that recreates a branch\nthe user meant to delete is strictly worse than the silence it replaces.\n\n### 2. A rewind to an unrelated commit is not detected\n\nThe third probe deliberately screens out a ref that moved **backwards into\nhistory the graded tree already had**:\n\n```rust\nif let Some(before) = before\n    && is_ancestor(&self.toplevel, after, before).await\n{\n    continue;\n}\n```\n\nThat exists because `git reset --hard HEAD~1` in the user's own checkout is a\nmid-run user action, and the negative control\n`a_mid_run_user_rewind_is_not_a_ref_escape` pins it. But a candidate that\nrewinds a shared branch to an **unrelated** commit (one not an ancestor of\n`before`) also lands outside both positive signatures, so it escapes unnoticed\nby a different route.\n\n## Files\n\n- `crates/stella-cli/src/candidate_ws/ref_escape.rs` — `escaped_refs_inner`\n  (the signature and the three probes), the module doc's \"Boundaries\" section\n  which cites this issue, and `refusal()` if a new non-failing class is added\n- `crates/stella-cli/src/candidate_ws.rs` — `refs_at_create`, `seal_inner`\n  (the seam), `restore_escaped_refs_inner` (the teardown seam)\n\n## Reproduce\n\nBoth fixtures run from the candidate worktree, defeating git's interlock the\nway the observed #2541 escape did. Use `scaffold()` +\n`GitCandidateWorkspaces::create_workspace` exactly as the existing tests in\n`ref_escape.rs`'s test module do (they drive real git, no provider or network):\n\n```rust\n// (1) deleted ref\nscratch_git(&root, &[\"branch\", \"keepme\"]);            // before create_workspace\nlet ws = port(root.clone()).create_workspace().await.unwrap();\nscratch_git(ws.dir(), &[\"branch\", \"-D\", \"keepme\"]);\nws.seal().await;                                       // succeeds today — should not be silent\n\n// (2) rewind to an unrelated commit\n// build an unrelated commit (e.g. `git commit-tree` or an orphan branch), then:\nscratch_git(ws.dir(), &[\"branch\", \"-f\", \"--ignore-other-worktrees\", &branch, &unrelated]);\nws.seal().await;                                       // succeeds today\n```\n\n## Constraints already discovered\n\n- **The two negative controls must stay green**:\n  `a_mid_run_user_commit_is_not_a_ref_escape` and\n  `a_mid_run_user_rewind_is_not_a_ref_escape` (both in `ref_escape.rs`). #2641\n  strengthened both with an assertion that the user's branch still holds\n  *their* value after the seal **and** after `remove()` — a new detection that\n  also triggers a repair on either fixture is strictly worse than the current\n  silence.\n- **`#2641` made this guard write to the real repository.** Any new class must\n  say explicitly whether it repairs; the module doc's \"Detection, then repair\"\n  section is the normative statement and must be updated in the same PR.\n- **Best-effort is the module's contract**: every failed probe reports \"no\n  escape\" rather than failing a candidate on broken forensics. A new probe\n  must preserve that direction.\n- `refs/worktree/*` is per-worktree and invisible to `for-each-ref` in the main\n  worktree, which is what keeps `stella_tools::verify::WITNESS_BASELINE_WORKTREE_REF`\n  out of the comparison. A set-difference over `refs_at_create` does not widen\n  the enumeration, but re-check this if you change `shared_refs`.\n- `RefAudit::unexamined` already exists for \"moved but never attributed\"; a\n  deleted-ref warning likely wants its own field rather than reusing it, since\n  the two mean different things to a reader.\n\n## Definition of done\n\n- A candidate that deletes a shared ref produces a **named, distinct** outcome\n  (warning or abort — the PR must argue which, and the argument must address\n  that the reflog is gone with the ref), with a witness test that fails on\n  `main`.\n- A candidate that rewinds a shared branch to an unrelated commit is detected,\n  with a witness test that fails on `main`.\n- Both negative controls above still pass, including their post-`remove()`\n  assertions.\n- The module doc's \"Boundaries\" section no longer lists whichever gap was\n  closed, and this issue is unlinked from it.\n\n## Related\n\n- #2641 — the repair this was split out of\n- #2541 — the observed defect the guard was built for\n- #1383 — the structural fix (a substrate that cannot reach those refs at all)\n- #2540 — the sibling ref-namespace contamination\n"[m[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"number"[m[1;37m:[m 2812[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"title"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"candidate_ws/ref_escape: two shared-ref escapes the guard still cannot see — a deleted ref, and a rewind to an unrelated commit"[m
  [1;37m}[m[1;37m,[m
