    [1;34m"body"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"Problem found while building #2682's allowed-tools layer, with a committed proptest counterexample (`crates/stella-tools/proptest-regressions/skill_grant.txt`): folding `operator = {\"build\": on}` with `scope = {\"*\": off, \"custom\": on}` through `ToolPolicy::narrow_with` (`crates/stella-tools/src/policy.rs`) yields a policy that ALLOWS `verify_done` even though the scope denies every tool in the build group. Mechanism: narrow_with resolves each raw KEY through `allows()`, and `scope.allows(\"build\")` routes \"build\" through `catalog::group_for` — which treats it as an unknown TOOL name and answers with the dynamic \"custom\" group → true. The folded entry `{\"build\": true}` then covers every build-group tool. The narrowing guarantee (operator-denied stays denied) still holds; what breaks is intersection-exactness in the scope→fold direction whenever a group name doubles as a key and the other side grants \"custom\" or \"mcp\".\n\nExposure: any narrow_with caller whose scopes can carry group keys — e.g. the CLI `--tools` spec composition (`ToolPolicy::parse_spec` accepts group names; see policy.rs narrowing_tests). #2682's skill_grant sidesteps it by computing the intersection per concrete tool name (`skill_grant::effective_allows`) and documents why a folded form is not offered (`crates/stella-tools/src/skill_grant.rs` module docs, \"The intersection is computed per name\").\n\nRepro: run the seed in proptest-regressions/skill_grant.txt against a reintroduced folded form, or unit-test narrow_with directly with the pair above and assert on `verify_done`.\n\nDone: either narrow_with expands group keys to their member names (`catalog::names_in_group`) before resolving, or its docs + a pinned test state the constraint and every caller is audited for group-key scopes.\n\nRefs #2682, #2787."[m[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"number"[m[1;37m:[m 2800[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"title"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"ToolPolicy::narrow_with can widen relative to the per-name intersection when a scope carries group keys (group_for misreads a group key as an unknown tool name)"[m
  [1;37m}[m[1;37m,[m
  [1;37m{[m
    [1;34m"body"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"Problem: the blocking chains (tool.call.requested, file.*, command.started) and the new approval flow live in `ToolRegistry::execute` (`crates/stella-tools/src/registry.rs` + `registry/approval.rs`). MCP tools run through `stella-mcp`'s `McpToolSet` and custom TOML tools through `CustomToolSet` (`crates/stella-tools/src/custom.rs`); both wrap the registry as an inner executor and dispatch their own names WITHOUT the chains, so an extension policy (or a RequireApproval-producing hook) never sees those calls. The ToolPolicy on/off switch layer (stella-cli `PolicyToolSet`) does cover them — this gap is specifically the hook/approval plane. Pre-existing before #2676, but the approval flow makes the asymmetry user-visible: a gated built-in asks a human, an MCP tool with the same effect does not.\n\nFix direction: either route decorator dispatch through a shared gate entry (the #2676 `ApprovalBroker::resolve` + `resolve_precedence` seam in `crates/stella-tools/src/registry/approval.rs` is reusable and bus-parameterized), or have each decorator emit the same chains before executing. Verify: a blocking handler on tool.call.requested denying an MCP/custom tool name must actually block it. Done when the hook/approval plane sees every tool dispatch, not just built-ins.\n\nRefs #2676, #2787."[m[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"number"[m[1;37m:[m 2793[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"title"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"MCP and custom tools bypass the blocking policy chains and the #2676 approval flow"[m
  [1;37m}[m[1;37m,[m
