    [1;34m"body"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"## Problem\n\nStella's recall today is built from the current tree: the tree-sitter code\ngraph (`stella-graph`, `.stella/private/codegraph.db`) plus semantic and\nepisodic retrieval (`stella-context`, `.stella/private/context.db`). A\nrepository's **history** — commit messages, linked issues, and co-change\npatterns — is a large, free, already-local corpus that recall does not read at\nall.\n\nThe plausible hypothesis is that history helps most on exactly the tasks\nlocalization is hardest for: \"which file actually implements this behaviour\"\nand \"what else must change when this changes\". Files that historically change\ntogether are a cheap prior on blast radius; the commit that introduced a\nbehaviour usually explains it better than the code does.\n\nThe hypothesis is **plausible, not established**, and it has three specific\nways to be wrong:\n\n1. **Token cost.** History is enormous. A frame that pays for itself on a\n   localization task may be pure overhead on every other turn — and prompt\n   bloat is what the ingest-first context-record model exists to avoid.\n2. **Stale paths.** A commit from two years ago cites files that have since\n   moved or been deleted. Recall that surfaces them wastes turns.\n3. **False co-change correlation.** Two files that always changed together\n   because of a formatting sweep, a rename, or a vendored-dependency bump are\n   not coupled at all. Without filtering, co-change is mostly noise.\n\nIngesting history by default before answering these is the expensive mistake.\n\n## Proposal\n\nA **bounded, opt-in experiment** — not a default ingestion path — racing\ndigest-pinned, cited history frames against current graph/semantic recall on a\nlocalization-heavy held-out task set.\n\n- Frames are **digest-pinned** (they name the commit they came from) and\n  **cited** (a frame that steers a turn is attributable), so a wrong frame is\n  diagnosable rather than ambient.\n- Mined from commits, linked issues, and co-change history, with explicit\n  filters for the three failure modes above: a token budget per turn, a\n  path-liveness check against the current tree, and a co-change filter that\n  discards sweeps and renames.\n- Arms: current recall alone vs. current recall plus history frames, on the\n  same tasks, with repeats.\n\n## Definition of done\n\n1. The experiment can return a **null result** and that is a complete,\n   publishable outcome. It must not require lift to conclude.\n2. Nothing is ingested by default as a consequence of running it. Any default\n   change is a separate PR arguing from the measurement.\n3. **Losing evidence is retained** — a recorded null result, findable by the\n   next person who has this idea, so the hypothesis is not repeatedly funded.\n   This is the point of the issue as much as the lift is.\n4. Per-arm reporting includes token cost, not only verification rate: a frame\n   set that lifts resolve rate while doubling prompt size has not obviously\n   won, and a blended score would hide that.\n\n## Constraints and prior art\n\n- #2305 (paired with-records vs without arm on TB 2.1) is the closest existing\n  A/B and the natural template. Reuse its harness rather than building a second\n  one, or state why it does not fit.\n- Bench rules apply in full: read `stella-events.jsonl`, never a surface\n  signal; repeats per task; name and exclude operational aborts; no budget or\n  token cap on a bench arm.\n- Citation plumbing is known-broken in a way that will silently null this\n  experiment if not checked first: `cite_memory` is registered on every surface\n  and has never been called (#2195), and there is no end-to-end witness that a\n  cited memory reaches `memory_citations` (#2431). **Verify the citation path\n  fires before trusting any attribution this experiment produces** — otherwise\n  a dead arm scores as a clean null.\n- Recall lives in `stella-context` (retrieval, embeddings, episodic memory) and\n  the code graph in `stella-graph`; the two databases are deliberately separate\n  files and must stay that way (AGENTS.md gotchas).\n\n## Related\n\n#2305, #2431, #2195, #2123. Its result feeds the transfer/decay panel's\nretrieval-precision metric; its frames carry a provenance grade from the\nevidence-grade policy.\n\n---\n\n## Sibling issues — one backlog batch, filed together\n\nThese five were filed as one set and are meaningfully coupled; picking up any\none in isolation is fine, but read this list first so the seams are not\nreinvented.\n\n- **#2780** — enforced evolution-surface parity ledger (declares *what* may\n  evolve; its `evidence` column is defined by #2782)\n- **#2781** — longitudinal transfer and decay panel (measures whether an\n  artifact still helps elsewhere and later)\n- **#2782** — evidence-grade preservation and promotion policy (defines what\n  counts as evidence for each impact class)\n- **#2783** — corpus information-gain and saturation gate (decides whether a\n  training round is worth funding)\n- **#2784** — bounded historical repository-memory experiment (prove-or-null,\n  no default ingestion)\n"[m[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"number"[m[1;37m:[m 2784[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"title"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"context: bounded experiment — do digest-pinned history frames (commits, linked issues, co-change) beat current recall on localization?"[m
  [1;37m}[m[1;37m,[m
