    [1;34m"body"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"## Problem\n\nThe weight-space adapter track (#836, with its eval harness #876 and the bandit\nover prompts and routing #831) is designed to train on Stella's own trajectory\ncorpus. Nothing currently asks the question that decides whether training is\nworth doing at all: **does the corpus contain anything the model has not\nalready learned?**\n\nThe default failure mode is not a bad checkpoint — it is an expensive one that\nchanges nothing. A self-improvement loop that keeps solving tasks it already\nsolves generates a growing corpus of duplicate successes. Trained on, that\ncorpus produces a checkpoint indistinguishable from its base, and the eval\nharness dutifully reports no lift after the compute has been spent. The loop\nthen does it again, because nothing in it can tell \"we learned everything\navailable\" from \"we learned nothing this round\".\n\nThis is the corpus-side analogue of a guardrail this repository already\nenforces on the loop: `/fullauto`'s dry-streak termination exists precisely\nbecause a loop that cannot detect saturation runs forever.\n\n## Proposal\n\nA gate that runs **before** a training round is funded, measuring corpus delta\nrather than corpus size:\n\n- **normalized novelty** of new traces, lessons, and hypotheses against the\n  corpus already consumed (dedup at the semantic level, not the byte level —\n  the same task re-solved with different whitespace is not novelty)\n- **new failure-cluster coverage** — how many previously unrepresented failure\n  modes the generation added\n- **divergent outcome pairs** — the same task with materially different\n  trajectories and different verdicts, which is where preference data\n  (#1044's DPO pairs) actually lives\n- **held-out learning-curve lift** — does adding the new slice to the training\n  set move a held-out curve at all\n\nWhen a generation adds neither new clusters nor lift, the curriculum is\n**suspended or redirected**, not repeated.\n\n## Definition of done\n\n1. Duplicate successes cannot trigger a training round. A corpus consisting of\n   N re-solutions of already-solved tasks fails the gate.\n2. New **verified counterexamples** can trigger a round — the gate must be\n   permissive in the direction that matters, or it will suppress the learning\n   it exists to protect.\n3. Every training round records, in its ledger entry, the corpus delta that\n   justified it and the held-out evidence backing that delta. A round with no\n   recorded justification is a defect in the PR that ran it.\n4. A witness fixture proves both directions: a duplicate-heavy corpus is\n   rejected, a counterexample-bearing corpus of the same size is accepted.\n\n## Constraints and prior art\n\n- **#836 is closed but the gate belongs before its successor, not after it.**\n  Whoever picks this up should first establish what of #836/#831/#876 is\n  actually live today — CLAUDE.md is explicit that a closed issue is not\n  evidence of a shipped fix, and that a commit message is a claim, not a fact.\n  State what you verified and how.\n- The corpus plumbing has known holes that will distort any novelty measure\n  taken today: `stella dataset export` writes zero records for executions that\n  cannot prove their transcripts (#2123), and historical bench stores are not\n  in the usage hub (#2140). Measure on a corpus whose provenance you can state.\n- Novelty is a **trajectory abstraction** in the evidence-grade sense: it may\n  justify funding a training round, and it may not by itself justify\n  publishing the resulting artifact.\n\n## Related\n\n#836, #831, #876, #1044 (DPO preference pairs), #1069, #2123, #2140.\nConsumes the transfer/decay panel's held-out strata; gated by the\nevidence-grade policy.\n\n---\n\n## Sibling issues — one backlog batch, filed together\n\nThese five were filed as one set and are meaningfully coupled; picking up any\none in isolation is fine, but read this list first so the seams are not\nreinvented.\n\n- **#2780** — enforced evolution-surface parity ledger (declares *what* may\n  evolve; its `evidence` column is defined by #2782)\n- **#2781** — longitudinal transfer and decay panel (measures whether an\n  artifact still helps elsewhere and later)\n- **#2782** — evidence-grade preservation and promotion policy (defines what\n  counts as evidence for each impact class)\n- **#2783** — corpus information-gain and saturation gate (decides whether a\n  training round is worth funding)\n- **#2784** — bounded historical repository-memory experiment (prove-or-null,\n  no default ingestion)\n"[m[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"number"[m[1;37m:[m 2783[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"title"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"self-improvement: nothing measures corpus information gain — a duplicate-success corpus can fund a training round that cannot lift"[m
  [1;37m}[m[1;37m,[m
