    [1;34m"body"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"## Problem\n\nStella's self-improvement pipeline turns observations into artifacts through a\nlong chain — reflection → proposal → skill / rule / tool → campaign — and\n**the strength of the original evidence is not carried along the chain.** By the\ntime something is a published skill or an executable tool, the record that it\noriginated in a model's opinion rather than in a passing test is gone.\n\nThis is the same class of failure the repository already treats as its most\nserious: a claim whose evidence cannot distinguish it from its opposite\n(CLAUDE.md, \"Every factual claim ships with the evidence that establishes it\").\nApplied to artifacts instead of sentences, the concrete hazard is\n**laundering by aggregation**: several model critiques agreeing with each other\nis still zero deterministic evidence, but a promotion gate that counts votes\nwill read it as strong. #2569/#2570 is the live proof that a plausible-looking\nconsensus signal can be structurally worthless — ablating the verifier turned\nevery run into a PASS.\n\n## Proposal\n\nDefine an explicit, ordered provenance grade and carry it as a first-class\nfield through every derived artifact:\n\n| Grade | Source |\n|---|---|\n| **Deterministic proof** | a witness test that went fail → pass; a guard that fails the gate |\n| **Environment observation** | a command exit status, a build result, a measured number from `stella-events.jsonl` |\n| **Trajectory abstraction** | a pattern mined across runs — statistical, not proven |\n| **Model critique** | an LLM's judgement about a run |\n| **Human review** | a person read it and signed off |\n\nTwo rules make the grade load-bearing:\n\n1. **No aggregation promotes a grade.** N model critiques agreeing remain a\n   model critique. Only re-deriving the claim from a stronger source promotes\n   it.\n2. **Impact sets the required grade.** A prompt hint or a recall bias may be\n   trialled from trajectory evidence. A **blocking guard** or an **executable\n   tool** requires deterministic proof plus publication authority — because\n   those two can break a teammate's session, and the blast radius is the thing\n   the grade is rationing.\n\n## Definition of done\n\n1. Provenance survives every hop: reflection → proposal → skill/rule/tool →\n   campaign. A hop that drops it fails a test.\n2. No artifact can claim a grade stronger than its reproducible source. The\n   check is mechanical, not editorial: the grade is derived from the source\n   record, never asserted by the promoting code.\n3. The grade is exposed where a human decides — the Observatory, `stella\n   context explain` (#892), and any export path — so a reviewer sees \"model\n   critique\" and \"witness flip\" as visibly different things.\n4. A witness test proves rule 1 above: a fixture with several model critiques\n   agreeing must not reach a grade that authorises tool publication.\n\n## Constraints and prior art\n\n- Context records already have the closest thing to this: a governance mode, a\n  hash-chained promotions ledger (`.stella/rules/promotions.jsonl`), lifecycle\n  events for retirement and supersession (#2728), and CI re-verification via\n  `stella context validate`. **Extend that machinery outward rather than\n  building a parallel one** — and say in the PR description why any new store\n  was unavoidable.\n- Promotion tiers shipped in #2762 (residue #2761) are the nearest live\n  surface; read them before designing the grade enum.\n- `stella-diag` field values cannot hold a `String`, a `Path`, or model output\n  — that is a compile error, not a review question. A provenance grade emitted\n  as a diagnostic must be an enum.\n- Invariant #3 (zero telemetry egress) constrains what may leave the machine:\n  a provenance record is metadata, but the evidence it points at is content.\n  Anything crossing an egress encoder goes through\n  `crates/stella-store/src/content_free.rs`.\n\n## Related\n\n#892 (rule provenance in agent output), #2723 (ToolCtx provenance, `proof_ref`\ncitation), #2709/#2762 (promotion tiers), #2761, #2728, #2569/#2570 (why vote\ncounting is not evidence), #2023. Feeds the `evidence` column of the\nevolution-surface parity ledger.\n\n---\n\n## Sibling issues — one backlog batch, filed together\n\nThese five were filed as one set and are meaningfully coupled; picking up any\none in isolation is fine, but read this list first so the seams are not\nreinvented.\n\n- **#2780** — enforced evolution-surface parity ledger (declares *what* may\n  evolve; its `evidence` column is defined by #2782)\n- **#2781** — longitudinal transfer and decay panel (measures whether an\n  artifact still helps elsewhere and later)\n- **#2782** — evidence-grade preservation and promotion policy (defines what\n  counts as evidence for each impact class)\n- **#2783** — corpus information-gain and saturation gate (decides whether a\n  training round is worth funding)\n- **#2784** — bounded historical repository-memory experiment (prove-or-null,\n  no default ingestion)\n"[m[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"number"[m[1;37m:[m 2782[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"title"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"self-improvement: provenance is dropped between reflection and published artifact — agreeing model critiques can launder into executable evidence"[m
  [1;37m}[m[1;37m,[m
