    [1;34m"body"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"## Problem\n\nEvery promotion gate Stella has built so far measures an artifact **on the\nrepository and the model family that produced it**, at the moment it was\nproduced. Nothing measures whether it still helps somewhere else, or whether it\nstill helps here next month.\n\n- Skill promotion gates on measured lift (#1067) — lift on the panel it was\n  mined from.\n- Skill decay detection (#1068) was designed to demote skills that stop\n  helping, but the citation signal it needs never populates (#2195, #2431), so\n  in practice nothing retires.\n- The adapter eval harness (#876) A/Bs against a held-out task set, but a\n  single held-out set does not separate \"learned something general\" from\n  \"memorised this repository's layout\".\n\nThe failure mode this invites is specific and expensive: a skill or memory\nmined from one repository, at one dependency version, under one model family,\ngets published globally, and then **actively harms** unrelated work — while the\nnumber that promoted it still looks good, because it is still being measured on\nits source panel. A blended single score cannot detect this, because local gain\nand remote harm cancel.\n\n## Proposal\n\nA longitudinal panel that runs promoted artifacts — memories, skills, context\nrecords, policies, and eventually weights (#836) — across deliberately\nstratified axes:\n\n- **held-out repositories** (never seen during mining)\n- **held-out versions** of a repository seen during mining\n- **at least two model families** for any prompt-shaped artifact, since a\n  prompt artifact tuned on one family is the classic overfit\n- **language / build strata** (a Rust artifact tested on a Python task)\n- **a dependency-upgraded snapshot**, to catch advice that was true at one\n  version and is now wrong\n- **unrelated repositories**, as the harm control\n\nMeasured per stratum, never blended: verification rate, cost, steps, retrieval\nprecision, stale activation rate, and retirement rate.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nTwo guards exist and are separately reportable — a transfer guard and a decay\nguard — rather than one composite score:\n\n1. A source-specific skill that improves its **local** panel but harms the\n   **unrelated** panel is blocked from global publication. It may still be\n   published narrowly (scoped to its origin repository) — narrowing is the\n   correct outcome, not deletion.\n2. Advice that has gone stale because a dependency moved is **detected** and\n   either retired or narrowed, **without deleting its history** — the losing\n   evidence is retained so the same hypothesis is not re-funded later.\n3. The panel reports per-stratum numbers. A PR that improves the blended\n   average while regressing the unrelated-repository stratum is visibly a\n   regression.\n\n## Constraints and prior art\n\n- **This is bench work, and the bench rules are hard rules.** Read CLAUDE.md's\n  bench section before designing the panel: conclusions come from\n  `stella-events.jsonl`, never from a surface signal or `result.json`; a\n  five-task solve rate resolves nothing smaller than a catastrophe, so this\n  needs repeats per task and per stratum; operational aborts must be named and\n  excluded explicitly.\n- #2023 is a live warning about panels that silently degenerate: every\n  arenabench run to date is the control arm, with a witness authored in 1 of\n  51 trials. Design the null-arm detection into this panel from the start.\n- #2305 (paired with-records vs without arm on TB 2.1) is the nearest existing\n  measurement and is the natural first stratum — do not build a second harness\n  beside it without saying why.\n- Never run a bench arm with a budget or token cap; a capped arm scores a dead\n  arm as a win.\n\n## Related\n\n#1067, #1068, #2195, #2431 (promotion and decay machinery this measures),\n#876, #836 (adapter promotion gate), #2305, #2023 (bench integrity).\nDepends on the evolution-surface parity ledger for the list of surfaces to\ncover, and on the evidence-grade policy for what each stratum's result is\nallowed to justify.\n\n---\n\n## Sibling issues — one backlog batch, filed together\n\nThese five were filed as one set and are meaningfully coupled; picking up any\none in isolation is fine, but read this list first so the seams are not\nreinvented.\n\n- **#2780** — enforced evolution-surface parity ledger (declares *what* may\n  evolve; its `evidence` column is defined by #2782)\n- **#2781** — longitudinal transfer and decay panel (measures whether an\n  artifact still helps elsewhere and later)\n- **#2782** — evidence-grade preservation and promotion policy (defines what\n  counts as evidence for each impact class)\n- **#2783** — corpus information-gain and saturation gate (decides whether a\n  training round is worth funding)\n- **#2784** — bounded historical repository-memory experiment (prove-or-null,\n  no default ingestion)\n"[m[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"number"[m[1;37m:[m 2781[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"title"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"self-improvement: promotion measures an artifact only on the panel that produced it — no transfer or decay panel"[m
  [1;37m}[m[1;37m,[m
