    [1;34m"body"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"## Problem\n\nStella now has three enforced declaration ledgers, each born from the same\ndefect shape — a capability that shipped on one axis and silently missed\nanother, discovered by a bench run paying for it rather than by a test:\n\n- **Provider feature parity** — `crates/stella-model/src/provider_parity.rs`\n  (AGENTS.md invariant #8). Every provider id declares a posture on every\n  divergent axis and names the witness test proving it on the wire.\n- **Cross-surface capability parity** — `crates/stella-parity/src/lib.rs`\n  (`Capability`, `SurfacePosture`, `CAPABILITIES`, `UNWITNESSED_BASELINE`).\n  Absence is legal only as `Deferred` (naming what it waits on) or\n  `NotApplicable` (naming the design reason).\n- **Signal consumption** — `crates/stella-protocol/src/event/consumers.rs`\n  (invariant #10). Generated from the same table as `type_tag()`, so a variant\n  with no declared consumer is a build error (`E0004`), not a red test.\n\n**The self-improvement track has no equivalent, and it is the surface where the\ngap costs the most.** Stella evolves itself through at least six distinct object\nclasses — `framework` (prompts, policies, the loop itself), `memory`, `skill`,\n`tool`, `model` (weights/adapters), `workflow` — and each one currently answers\n\"when may this change itself, on what evidence, published by whom, and how is it\nrolled back?\" in a different file, or nowhere. Compare:\n\n- Skills promote on measured lift (#1067) and were meant to decay and retire\n  (#1068) — but efficacy-based retirement can never populate because\n  `cite_memory` was never called (#2195), and there is still no end-to-end\n  witness that a cited memory reaches `memory_citations` (#2431).\n- Context records have an explicit governance mode, a hash-chained promotions\n  ledger under `.stella/rules/`, and CI re-verification via\n  `stella context validate`.\n- Weight-space adapters (#836) and the bandit over prompts/routing (#831) were\n  designed with a promotion gate (#876) that nothing structurally enforces.\n\nNobody can answer \"what may Stella change about itself today, and what evidence\ndoes each change require?\" by reading one reviewed table. That is exactly the\nquestion the other three ledgers exist to make answerable.\n\n## Proposal\n\nAdd a code-owned evolution-surface matrix, following `stella-parity`'s shape\nrather than inventing a fourth idiom. One row per evolution object, each\ndeclaring:\n\n| Field | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| `surface` | `Framework`, `Memory`, `Skill`, `Tool`, `Model`, `Workflow` |\n| `posture` | `Shipped`, `Experimental`, `Planned(issue)`, `Prohibited(reason)` |\n| `timing` | when the change may take effect (in-turn, end-of-session, offline batch) |\n| `evidence` | the accepted evidence grade for a change to this surface |\n| `authority` | who or what may publish it (agent, local human, org policy) |\n| `rollback` | the named artifact that reverses it |\n| `witness` | the test proving the row, for any non-`Planned`/`Prohibited` posture |\n\nThe `evidence` column should reference the provenance grades from the\nevidence-grade policy issue rather than restating them, so the two ledgers\ncannot drift.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nEnforced from both sides, as the existing three are:\n\n1. A new evolution surface with no row **fails the build or the gate** —\n   prefer the `consumers.rs` treatment (generate the rows from the same table\n   as the surface enum, so omission is `E0004`) over a test that can be\n   forgotten.\n2. A `Shipped` or `Experimental` row whose named witness test no longer exists\n   fails a parity test, exactly as `stella-model`'s parity tests do today.\n3. A `Planned` row cites a GitHub issue; a `Prohibited` row cites a stable\n   design document by `id` (AGENTS.md: cite by id, never by path).\n4. If any currently-shipped surface cannot be witnessed yet, it is a\n   `ShippedUnwitnessed` row against a **down-only** baseline constant in the\n   style of `UNWITNESSED_BASELINE` — recording debt that already exists, never\n   granting new permission. Adding a row to that baseline to turn the gate\n   green is the expedient CLAUDE.md forbids.\n\n## Constraints for whoever picks this up\n\n- Decide crate placement first. `stella-parity` is the closest neighbour and is\n  currently a single `src/lib.rs`; a second matrix may belong beside it as a\n  sibling module, or in its own crate if it needs dependencies `stella-parity`\n  refuses. Name the exemplar you followed in the PR description.\n- `stella-protocol` is types-only (invariant #1) and `stella-core` does no I/O\n  (invariant #2) — a ledger that wants to read `.stella/` state belongs in\n  neither.\n- Read the \"God files\" table in AGENTS.md before planning where lines land.\n\n## Related\n\nLedger exemplars: invariants #8 and #10 in AGENTS.md; #2707 (pinning a\nwhitelist to the signal-consumer ledger with a parity test); #2730.\nEvolution surfaces this would cover: #836, #831, #876, #1067, #1068, #2195,\n#2431, #2378.\n\n---\n\n## Sibling issues — one backlog batch, filed together\n\nThese five were filed as one set and are meaningfully coupled; picking up any\none in isolation is fine, but read this list first so the seams are not\nreinvented.\n\n- **#2780** — enforced evolution-surface parity ledger (declares *what* may\n  evolve; its `evidence` column is defined by #2782)\n- **#2781** — longitudinal transfer and decay panel (measures whether an\n  artifact still helps elsewhere and later)\n- **#2782** — evidence-grade preservation and promotion policy (defines what\n  counts as evidence for each impact class)\n- **#2783** — corpus information-gain and saturation gate (decides whether a\n  training round is worth funding)\n- **#2784** — bounded historical repository-memory experiment (prove-or-null,\n  no default ingestion)\n"[m[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"number"[m[1;37m:[m 2780[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"title"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"self-improvement: no enforced evolution-surface ledger — six ways stella changes itself, none declaring evidence, authority, or rollback"[m
