    [1;34m"body"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"## Problem\n\nPR #2748's review (Vercel agent, comment on `crates/stella-model/src/zai/unary.rs`) caught that the unary-fallback **body read** (`response.text()`) mapped a read-timeout to retryable `Transport`, re-creating the #547 identical-request retry storm even after the `.send()` path was fixed. The suggested fix was applied at merge — on main, `crates/stella-model/src/zai/unary.rs` (`complete_unary_attempt`) now routes `response.text()` errors through `http::classify_unary_dispatch_error` — but **nothing tests that hunk**: the existing witness `a_unary_read_timeout_is_terminal_never_a_retry_storm` (`crates/stella-model/src/zai/tests/stream_fallback.rs`) pins only the `.send()` path, where wiremock's whole-response delay lands. A future refactor of `complete_unary_attempt` could quietly revert the body read to `ProviderError::transport` and no test would go red.\n\n## What to build\n\nA test in `crates/stella-model/src/zai/tests/stream_fallback.rs` that stalls the response BODY after the headers, so the timeout strikes inside `response.text()` rather than `send()`:\n\n- Reuse the hand-rolled std-TCP server pattern already in that file (`hang_streams_answer_unary` — wiremock cannot hold a body open): for a `\"stream\":false` request, write `HTTP/1.1 200 OK` + `content-type: application/json` + a `content-length` larger than what you send (or chunked encoding with no terminating chunk), write a few body bytes, then hold the socket.\n- Drive `complete_unary_attempt` end-to-end (arm the latch first via an empty `\"stream\":true` response, then call `complete` again), with the provider's unary client... note the 600s `UNARY_READ_TIMEOUT` is a const — either drive `dispatch`+`text()` with a tiny-read-timeout client like the existing send-path test does, or make the unary client injectable for tests (mirroring `with_first_byte_deadline`).\n- Assert the error is `ProviderError::Terminal` and `!is_retryable()`.\n\n## Constraints\n\n- `zai/tests.rs` is a god file — the test goes in `zai/tests/stream_fallback.rs`.\n- Flip check: revert the `map_err` in `complete_unary_attempt`'s `text()` call to `ProviderError::transport(e.to_string())` and the test must go red.\n\n## Done when\n\nThe new test fails on a tree with the body-read classification reverted and passes on main; `cargo test -p stella-model` + clippy `-D warnings` green.\n\nRefs #2686, #2748, #547"[m[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"number"[m[1;37m:[m 2756[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"title"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"Witness the unary body-read timeout classification the #2748 review applied"[m
  [1;37m}[m[1;37m,[m
  [1;37m{[m
    [1;34m"body"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"## Problem\n\nThe reactive overflow recovery (#2680, `crates/stella-core/src/driver/overflow_recovery.rs`) clamps the compaction budget and re-runs the step, relying on the existing machinery: pure passes first, then `Engine::summarize_overflow_span` (`crates/stella-core/src/driver.rs`). But the summarizer is itself a model call over the rendered span — if the span is enormous, that call can ALSO be rejected as a context overflow. Today that lands in `summarize_overflow_span`'s `AccountedCallError::Provider` arm: `SummarizerHealth::record_failure` counts toward the give-up latch, the context is left intact, and after the recovery ladder's two rungs the turn aborts — the one branch recovery cannot repair.\n\nThe comparator (zappy's reactive ladder, quoted in #2680) handles this: when the summarization call itself overflows, the head of the span is truncated wholesale and the summarizer retried, bounded at 3 attempts.\n\n## What to do\n\nIn `summarize_overflow_span` (or a sibling helper, driver.rs is a god file at its ceiling — put logic in `driver/overflow_recovery.rs` or a new sibling): on `AccountedCallError::Provider(ProviderError::ContextOverflow { .. })`, halve the rendered span (drop the older head, keep the newer tail) and retry the summarizer call, bounded (comparator uses 3 attempts). Each dropped-head retry must note in the spliced summary marker that older content was truncated unsummarized. Failures still count toward `SummarizerHealth`.\n\n## Verify\n\nA driver test with a scripted provider whose Summarization-role calls reject with `ContextOverflow` until the request shrinks below a threshold, asserting the span still gets folded and the turn completes; a bound test that the truncation retries are capped.\n\n## Constraints\n\n- Every attempt is billed (the `UsageIncomplete` observer inside `run_accounted_call` already handles this).\n- Transcript must stay well-paired; the splice bounds already walk off Tool messages — head truncation must respect the same rule.\n- `driver.rs` and `driver/tests.rs` are at their file-size ceilings; new logic and tests go in sibling modules (`driver/overflow_recovery.rs`, `driver/tests/context_overflow.rs`).\n\nRefs #2680."[m[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"number"[m[1;37m:[m 2751[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"title"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"Overflow recovery's last gap: a summarizer request that itself overflows never repairs — add the span head-truncation rung"[m
  [1;37m}[m[1;37m,[m
  [1;37m{[m
    [1;34m"body"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"## Problem\n\nPR #2680's reactive context-overflow recovery keys on `ProviderError::ContextOverflow`, classified per-dialect in `crates/stella-model/src/http.rs::is_context_overflow`. The parity matrix's third axis (`OVERFLOW_POSTURE` in `crates/stella-model/src/provider_parity.rs`) declares five rows as `Detected` with witnesses (anthropic, openai, gemini, vertex, bedrock) and five as `BestEffort` (zai, xai, deepseek, openrouter, local): their errors funnel through the shared classifier, so OpenAI-dialect overflow spellings are caught opportunistically, but the providers' real wire shapes were never verified — an unmatched spelling degrades to `Terminal` and the turn aborts without recovery.\n\n## What to do\n\nFor each BestEffort provider, provoke a real context overflow on the live wire (an oversized prompt against a small-window model), capture the status + body, and:\n- if the shared detector matches it: add a body-shape witness test in `crates/stella-model/src/http.rs` (mod tests) and flip the row to `Detected { signature, witness }`;\n- if it does not match: extend `is_context_overflow` with the provider's exact signature (keep phrases narrow — false positives make the engine compact and retry a request that failed for another reason), then witness it.\n\nOpenRouter needs per-upstream care: it forwards the routed vendor's own body, so verify at least an Anthropic-routed and an OpenAI-routed overflow.\n\n## Constraints\n\n- `provider_parity` tests enforce: every `Detected` witness must exist in the adapter sources (http.rs is included), and all three axes must cover the same provider ids.\n- `stella-cli` config tests enforce a row per seeded provider.\n- Detection precision guard: `an_unrelated_400_never_classifies_as_context_overflow` must stay green.\n\n## Done\n\nAll five rows are `Detected` with wire-verified signatures and named witnesses, or a row documents (in its note) a provider that genuinely has no distinguishable overflow shape.\n\nRefs #2680."[m[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"number"[m[1;37m:[m 2750[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"title"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"Verify the wire overflow signatures for zai/xai/deepseek/openrouter/local and upgrade their OverflowPosture rows to Detected"[m
  [1;37m}[m[1;37m,[m
  [1;37m{[m
    [1;34m"body"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"## Context\n\nThe #2686 fallback — a first-byte deadline plus a bounded per-session latch that re-issues a hung/empty stream's retried attempt as a unary request — shipped for the shared OpenAI-compatible chat-completions adapter only (`crates/stella-model/src/zai.rs` + `zai/stream.rs` + `zai/unary.rs`), which covers the `zai`, `openrouter`, `xai`, `deepseek`, `local`, and settings-defined gateway identities. The other three streaming dialects still burn their whole retry budget re-streaming into a broken pipe, and are declared as such on the parity matrix's third axis (`StreamFallbackPosture::StreamingOnly` in `crates/stella-model/src/provider_parity.rs`). Bedrock is `AlwaysUnary` and needs nothing.\n\n## What to build\n\nPer dialect, mirror the zai shape — the shared machinery already exists:\n\n- `crates/stella-model/src/stream_recovery.rs` — the `StreamRecovery` latch (Streaming → Probing → Confirmed, reverts on a failed probe) and `StreamFault { error, fallback_eligible }`. Reuse as-is.\n- `crates/stella-model/src/http.rs` — `FIRST_BYTE_TIMEOUT` (90s) and `next_stream_read` (the `StreamRead` enum that keeps Idle distinct from Failed). Reuse as-is.\n\nPer adapter:\n1. Bound the FIRST body read by `first_byte_deadline` (a provider field defaulting to `http::FIRST_BYTE_TIMEOUT`, test-shrinkable) and classify the two nothing-arrived shapes (idle before any accumulation; EOF-before-terminal with nothing accumulated) as `fallback_eligible` — every other error stays ineligible, especially a mid-stream death with salvage (its `attach_partial` accounting must be preserved).\n2. Add the unary request/parse path: `anthropic.rs` — Messages API with `stream: false` (single JSON message: content blocks, `stop_reason`, `usage`); `openai.rs` — Responses API non-streaming (`output` array + `usage`); `gemini.rs`/`vertex.rs` — `generateContent` instead of `streamGenerateContent` (same response shape as one streamed chunk; the two share the aggregator, so one implementation covers both). Dispatch through a `http::unary_client()` (600s read bound — a unary call has no first token to reset the clock, #547).\n3. Consult the latch per attempt; report every unary outcome; keep the fault error retryable Transport so the engine's retry ladder drives the switch and bills the discarded attempt via the existing `UsageIncomplete` observer.\n4. Flip the provider's row to `StreamFallbackPosture::UnaryFallback { mechanism, witness }` and write the wiremock witnesses (see `crates/stella-model/src/zai/tests/stream_fallback.rs` for the shapes: hung-first-byte via the hand-rolled std TCP server — wiremock cannot hold a body open — empty-stream via wiremock, a healthy-stream control, a salvage-stays-streaming control, and the failed-probe reversion). Note `openai.rs` is a god file at its ceiling: the new code must land in sibling submodules, following the `zai/stream.rs` + `zai/unary.rs` split.\n\n## Constraints discovered\n\n- The unary paths must reuse shared assembly helpers (the zai pair shares `tool_call_input`, `promote_reasoning_as_text`, `fold_usage`, `final_finish_reason` in `zai/stream.rs`) so streaming and unary results cannot drift.\n- The fallback is deliberately two attempts, never a hidden internal resend: accounting for the discarded attempt must ride the engine's retry observer.\n- Parity tests enforce witness existence from `provider_parity.rs::adapter_sources()` — a new test file must be added to that `include_str!` list.\n\n## Done when\n\nEach of `anthropic`, `openai`, `gemini`, `vertex` declares `UnaryFallback` with a passing witness that fails on the pre-change code (`git stash`-style flip check), and `cargo test -p stella-model` + clippy `-D warnings` are green.\n\nRefs #2686"[m[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"number"[m[1;37m:[m 2746[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"title"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"Extend the streaming→non-streaming fallback (#2686) to the anthropic, openai, gemini, and vertex dialects"[m
  [1;37m}[m[1;37m,[m
  [1;37m{[m
    [1;34m"body"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"## Problem\n\nThe #2677 rate-limit park honors the soft-stop latch per 30s chunk (`RateLimitPark::park_tick` in `crates/stella-core/src/driver/rate_limit.rs`): pressing Esc during a multi-minute wait ends the park promptly. But the park sits *inside* the model-call ladder, so aborting it surfaces the pending `RateLimited` error — the turn ends as `TurnOutcome::Aborted { kind: Failure }` with a `RetriesExhausted` + `Error` event pair, not the clean soft-stop exit a step-boundary Esc gets (transcript kept, no error).\n\nThis is livable (the user asked to stop, and prior completed steps are kept by the abort path), but the event stream records a provider failure for what was a user decision, which misclassifies the ending in telemetry.\n\n## Sketch\n\n`ParkDirective::Abort` could carry a reason, and `drive_attempt_ladder` (same file) could map a soft-stop abort to the soft-stop settlement instead of the error path — the mapping decision belongs in the driver, not `retry.rs` (which stays pure). Witness: TestSteering that latches mid-park; assert the outcome matches the step-boundary soft-stop shape and no `RetriesExhausted` is emitted.\n\n## Constraints\n- `driver.rs` and `driver/tests.rs` are god files at their ceilings; use `driver/rate_limit.rs` and `driver/tests/parked_wait.rs`.\n\nRefs #2677."[m[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"number"[m[1;37m:[m 2743[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"title"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"stella-core: a soft stop during a rate-limit park aborts the turn as a Failure instead of the graceful boundary soft-stop exit"[m
  [1;37m}[m[1;37m,[m
