    [1;34m"body"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"## Problem\n\n`classify_http_status` (`crates/stella-model/src/http.rs`) maps 5xx — explicitly including 529, which Anthropic and Z.ai use for 'overloaded' — to retryable `ProviderError::Transport`. The #2677 parked recovery (`crates/stella-core/src/retry.rs`) engages only for `ProviderError::RateLimited`, because rate limiting is the one failure whose recovery is a function of waiting. Result: a sustained 529 brownout still exhausts the inline ladder (~16s) and aborts the turn even with wall-clock budget left — the exact shape #2667 measured for 429s.\n\nThe comparator lineage retries 529 like 429 in its unattended mode (foreground request sources only; background classifiers bail — Stella's `RetryPolicy::deterministic()` already matches that half).\n\n## Sketch\n\nEither a distinct `ProviderError::Overloaded` variant (wire/protocol change: serde round-trip test, adapters updated), or a park-eligibility predicate on the error that adapters set at the source (L-M7: classification is never re-derived downstream). The park path in `retry_with_backoff_observed` then keys on that predicate instead of matching `RateLimited` only. Hint-less parks already back off 30s→5min, which fits 529 semantics.\n\n## Done looks like\nA wiremock-style or scripted-provider witness: a provider returning 529 for longer than the inline ladder, then 200, yields a completed step (parks) instead of `RetriesExhausted` — fails before the change, passes after. L-M4 preserved: `deterministic()` never parks.\n\n## Constraints\n- `crates/stella-core/src/driver.rs` is a god file at its ceiling — new logic goes in `driver/rate_limit.rs` or `retry.rs`.\n- A new `ProviderError` variant touches the wire schema (`make wire-schema`).\n\nRefs #2677, #2667."[m[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"number"[m[1;37m:[m 2742[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"title"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"stella-model/core: 529 (overloaded) classifies as Transport, so a sustained 529 brownout never reaches the rate-limit park"[m
  [1;37m}[m[1;37m,[m
  [1;37m{[m
    [1;34m"body"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"## Context\n\nPR #2677's fix (parked budget-aware retry, see the PR closing #2677/#2667) solved the engine half of #2667: sustained 429s now park within the wall-clock budget instead of aborting (`crates/stella-core/src/retry.rs` ParkSupervisor + `crates/stella-core/src/driver/rate_limit.rs`). Two runner-side asks from #2667 remain unbuilt:\n\n1. **Abort taxonomy**: a trial that still dies on rate limiting (e.g. deadline headroom genuinely exhausted, or an old binary) exits with `retries_exhausted` → `NonZeroAgentExitCodeError` and is scored as an agent loss. The abort taxonomy already excludes 401s as operational; a 429-driven `RetriesExhausted { retryable: true }` belongs beside it, classified `infrastructure`. The event now carries enough signal: `RetriesExhausted.reasons` strings contain \"rate limited\" and `retryable: true`.\n2. **Pressure source**: the 429s in run `turnloop-cert-panel1` came from the match's own concurrent trials. A shared token bucket (or per-provider concurrency ceiling) across a match's trials in the arenabench runner would degrade to slower trials instead of a thundering herd that the per-trial park then has to absorb.\n\n## Files\n- runner classification: arenabench runner (abort taxonomy where 401 exclusion lives)\n- evidence: `turnloop-cert-panel1` transcripts, `retries_exhausted` events\n\n## Done looks like\n- A 429-exhaustion abort is reported as infrastructure, not an agent loss, with a witness in the runner's classification tests.\n- A panel launched over the plan's concurrency ceiling produces slower trials, not dead ones (this is now mostly covered by the engine park; the bucket removes the self-inflicted pressure).\n\nRefs #2667, #2677."[m[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"number"[m[1;37m:[m 2741[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"title"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"bench/runner: classify a 429-retries_exhausted exit as infrastructure, and rate-limit the fan-out (the two #2667 halves not fixed by the core park)"[m
  [1;37m}[m[1;37m,[m
