    [1;34m"body"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"**Worker model: `claude-sonnet-5`** — this converts a prose rule into a structural guard, and the detection design has genuine judgment in it (what counts as \"volatile bytes\", which tools get exceptions). The registry shape is specified below, but a naive implementation produces either false greens (harness can't fail) or a flaky gate — both worse than the status quo.\n\nPart of epic #2701. This is the epic's converse case: not a produced signal with no consumer, but a **consumer (the loop detector) whose input contract is enforced by nothing**.\n\n## Problem\n\nThe loop detector keys on tool output bytes: `exact_repeat_threshold` counts identical `(name + input + output)` calls and the stagnation rung counts byte-identical outputs (`crates/stella-core/src/loop_detect.rs:119-158`). One timestamp or counter in a `ToolOutput` makes both rungs **permanently blind for that tool** — the epic's motivating \"loop detector firing on a tool's bug\" class, inverted: the tool's bytes silently disarm the detector. The rule exists only as prose (`crates/stella-tools/README.md:187-195`) plus scattered discipline: `verify_done` routes phase timings to the diagnostic plane (`crates/stella-tools/src/verify/phases.rs:300`, #2486), `read_file`'s volatile footer has a hand-maintained normalizer (`crates/stella-core/src/driver/loop_evidence.rs:372-381`) pinned by one cross-crate test, and `HALTED_TOOL_RESULT` is a fixed string for this exact reason (`crates/stella-core/src/driver/dispatch.rs:23`). Nothing stops the next tool PR from shipping an elapsed-time in its verdict. That is the same posture `content_free.rs` replaced for egress.\n\n## Design — a loop-comparability registry, same shape as `registered_encoders()`\n\nIn `stella-tools` (which owns the tools) with the normalizer contract pinned against `stella-core`:\n\n```rust\n/// How a tool's output relates to loop comparison. Every registered tool\n/// declares one. `VolatileWithNormalizer` is the read_file case: volatile\n/// bytes are permitted ONLY when comparable_output() strips them.\npub enum LoopComparability {\n    /// Byte-identical output for identical (input, workspace state).\n    Deterministic,\n    /// Output carries volatile bytes; names the normalizer seam in\n    /// stella-core that strips them, and the test pinning that contract.\n    VolatileWithNormalizer { normalizer: &'static str, pinned_by: &'static str },\n    /// Inherently volatile and deliberately exempt (e.g. bash — its output\n    /// IS world state). Must cite rationale; exemption list is pinned by a\n    /// test so widening it is a visible diff (the\n    /// native_drain_exclusions_are_deliberate pattern, content_free.rs:906).\n    ExemptWorldState { rationale: &'static str },\n}\n```\n\n- A registry fn mapping every tool the `ToolRegistry` registers to a posture; parity test: every registered built-in tool has exactly one row (walk the actual registry, not a hand list — `crates/stella-tools/src/registry.rs` is the enumeration; note it is a god file, put the registry + tests in a new sibling module).\n- **Sentinel harness** (the part that makes it a check, not a claim): for each `Deterministic` tool, a fixture invocation run **twice against the same fixture workspace** asserting byte-identical `comparable_output`. For `VolatileWithNormalizer`, run twice and assert the *normalized* outputs match while raw outputs differ — which also proves the fixture actually exercises the volatile bytes (the `fixtures_actually_carry_the_sentinels` discipline, `content_free.rs:1051`).\n- Negative control: a deliberately timing-stamped fake tool the harness must catch.\n\n## Constraints & honest limits\n\n- Tools whose double-run is side-effecting (edit, bash) can't be harness-run naively — that's what `ExemptWorldState` and per-tool fixtures are for; the design call of which tools land where is the PR's core judgment, and each exemption needs its rationale sentence.\n- This guards *built-in* tools. MCP tools are out of scope (their outputs are foreign); note that limit in the module doc rather than implying coverage.\n- The read_file footer contract already has its pin; this registry's row cites it rather than duplicating it.\n\n## Witness\n\nThe negative-control fake tool: harness catches it (passes with this PR), and stashing the harness makes nothing catch it. Also: add a timing to one real `Deterministic` tool's output locally and confirm the harness goes red before landing (state this check in the PR; don't commit it).\n\n## Definition of done\n\n- Every built-in tool has a declared, tested posture; adding a tool without one is a red test.\n- `crates/stella-tools/README.md:187-195` updated to point at the registry as the enforced home (prose follows the check, never the reverse).\n- `make gate` green; registry module is not a god file and adds no lines to `registry.rs`/`scripts.rs`.\n"[m[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"number"[m[1;37m:[m 2706[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"title"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"tools: loop-comparability registry — turn the 'no timings in ToolOutput' prose rule into a sentinel-tested check (worker: sonnet)"[m
  [1;37m}[m[1;37m,[m
