    [1;34m"body"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"## Thesis\n\nSignals are produced and not consumed — a repo-wide shape, and every instance so far was found by a bench run paying for it rather than a test going red:\n\n- `flip.json` written, nothing read it — #1536 (fixed; its capture record is still ignored, #2217).\n- `verify_done` confirmations tallied but not feeding the halt — fixed only yesterday (036755435, #2661), after `solved_then_timeout` hit 4× on the cert panel at 955s against a 900s ceiling.\n- The flip transition still emits **no durable event** (#2661 point 3, open) — we shipped a halt we cannot measure in the field.\n- 17 of ~41 `AgentEvent` variants have no behavioral consumer and are invisible to the observatory (which whitelists 8 types, `crates/stella-observatory/src/journal.rs:72-79`) — persisted blobs plus a TUI paint, nothing more.\n- The loop detector's input contract (\"no timings in `ToolOutput`\", `crates/stella-tools/README.md:187-195`) is prose-only — one timestamp in a tool verdict disarms two detection rungs silently.\n\nThe event architecture is rich; consumption is ad hoc. Each fix above was whack-a-mole. This epic makes the class **structural**, the way `crates/stella-store/src/content_free.rs` did for telemetry egress: a reviewed ledger where **every emitted signal names its consumer or cites the issue tracking why it doesn't**, enforced by parity tests, so an unconsumed signal is a red test at PR time instead of a field incident.\n\n## The exemplar being copied\n\n`content_free.rs`'s proven shape, element for element:\n\n| content-free registry | this epic's ledger |\n|---|---|\n| `ContentFreeEncoder` trait + `registered_encoders()` (`content_free.rs:727-736`) | `SIGNAL_CONSUMERS` const, one row per `AgentEvent` variant |\n| `DRAIN_FORMATS` with `Guarded` / `NotYetBuilt { issue }` (`:739-757`) | `Behavioral { site }` / `Surfaced { surfaces }` / `RecordedOnly { issue }` |\n| `every_built_drain_format_has_a_registered_encoder` (`:878-901`) | totality vs `KNOWN_TYPE_TAGS` (tag_table.rs), both directions |\n| negative-control tests (`:940-1010`) — the harness can fail | same, mandatory |\n\nHonest limit, inherited from the exemplar: the ledger makes a gap *declared and human-reviewed*, not mechanically proven consumed. That is the same epistemic position `DRAIN_FORMATS` holds, and it was enough to end that class.\n\n## Sub-issues and worker-model assignments\n\nModel policy: **cheapest agent that does not risk reference-grade quality.** Design and concurrency-adjacent work gets the judgment tier; everything downstream of a fixed design is data entry with the compiler as reviewer and goes to the cheapest tier. Specs carry the source code for exactly this reason.\n\n| # | Task | Worker model | Why this tier |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| #2702 | Signal-consumer ledger in `stella-protocol` + invariant #9 | `claude-opus-5` | New registry in the architecture crown; the design IS the deliverable |\n| #2703 | Populate all ~41 ledger rows + file the gap umbrella issues | `claude-haiku-4-5` | Pure data entry against the audit table in the spec; parity tests catch every mistake |\n| #2704 | Durable `HaltFired` event on the flip/verify_done transition (#2661 pt 3) | `claude-sonnet-5` | Event sender through a concurrency-sensitive closure; exhaustive-match fan-out |\n| #2705 | Witness the verify_done scoring closure; pin the marker cross-crate | `claude-sonnet-5` | Witness authorship with a real inert-declaration trap |\n| #2706 | Loop-comparability registry for tool outputs (prose rule → check) | `claude-sonnet-5` | Detection design has judgment (exemptions, side-effecting tools) |\n| #2707 | Observatory whitelist ↔ ledger parity test | `claude-haiku-4-5` | Small, fully specified, dep-direction pre-decided |\n\nOrdering: #2702 → {#2703, #2707}; #2704, #2705, #2706 are independent of the ledger's population but #2704's event must take a ledger row, so land #2702 first or coordinate.\n\nExisting related sub-issue: #2217 (flip.json capture record ignored by the scoreboard) — same class, already filed, attached below.\n\n## Freeze note\n\nPer the standing feature-freeze directive (#2374): this epic is **hardening/instrumentation/guard** work — the class the directive explicitly keeps in scope (tests, instrumentation, reliability). S3 additionally *enables* measurement the prove-value plan needs (halt latency in the field). If any sub-issue reads as new surface area rather than guarding existing surface, that call is Mac's — sequencing against Phase 0 is deliberately left to the maintainer.\n\n## Definition of done (epic)\n\n- Adding an `AgentEvent` variant without declaring its consumer is a red test.\n- Every currently-unconsumed signal is either wired to a consumer or cited to an open issue from a ledger row — zero silent gaps.\n- The loop detector's input contract and the observatory's projection are checks, not prose.\n- No baseline widened, no `#[allow]` without a why-here comment, every PR names its witness or argues its absence.\n\n\n## Children adopted in the 2026-08-12 triage sweep\n\nTwo more ledger-correctness issues: two rows citing #2703 (the meta-task) as their gap issue rather than a decision about the signal, and `FileChange` declared `Unclassified` when it is in fact surfaced — blocked on the `Surface` vocabulary having no terminal-surface variant.\n\n#2832 #2874\n\n\n\n---\n\n## Alignment note — an ending signal is about to move (2026-08-16)\n\nDesign: `doc:turn-loop-wrappers` §3. Refs #3379.\n\n#3379 changes who emits the terminal signal of a run, and this epic's ledger is\nwhere that change gets declared. Recording it here so the ledger work does not\nhave to be redone a week later.\n\n**What changes.** Today `AgentEvent::Complete` means two different things\ndepending on who emitted it: the engine's means \"this turn is over\", and the\npipeline's — which it emits after swallowing the engine's — means \"the whole job\nis over\". #3379 makes the engine always emit its own `Complete`, one per turn,\nand gives the wrapper's job-level ending a **separate tag**.\n\n**What this epic has to absorb**\n\n- One new wire tag in `crates/stella-protocol/src/event/tags.rs`, and therefore\n  one new row in `consumers.rs`. It must be `Behavioral` or `Surfaced` on day\n  one — this is the ending of a run, so an `Unclassified` row here would be the\n  exact defect this epic exists to prevent.\n- `Complete`'s existing row changes meaning without changing name. Its consumer\n  list should be re-read rather than carried over: a consumer that counted one\n  `Complete` per run now sees one per turn, and a consumer that *should* have\n  been reading the job-level ending is the interesting case.\n- The observatory whitelists 8 event types\n  (`crates/stella-observatory/src/journal.rs:72-79`). The new ending belongs on\n  that list, or its absence is a declared gap with an issue.\n\n**This is a good test of the ledger.** #3379 is a signal that changes hands\nbetween two crates. If the ledger catches it — if the PR cannot compile without\ndeclaring the new tag's consumer and cannot pass without the posture being\ncoherent — the mechanism this epic built has done exactly the job it was built\nfor. Worth saying so in the PR either way.\n"[m[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"number"[m[1;37m:[m 2701[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"title"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"epic: every emitted signal names its consumer — a ledger + parity tests, the content-free registry shape applied to signals"[m
  [1;37m}[m[1;37m,[m
