    [1;34m"body"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"## Directive (Mac, 2026-08-10)\n\nConvert Stella to a **tool-first architecture**. Everything the system can do is a tool, advertised to the agent. The thesis this exists to prove: **our tool shapes all** — the quality of an agent is the quality of its tool contracts, and a model given the right levers with the right I/O shapes outperforms a model orchestrated from outside. Do not lose the opportunity to prove it: this conversion IS the experiment.\n\n## Why now (measured, 2026-08-10 like-for-like transcripts)\n\nOn identical GLM-5.2, Claude Code's separating behaviors were all tool-shaped (task CRUD plan-keeping, web search, fetch-with-extraction), while Stella's pipeline planned AROUND the model: stage artifacts the worker never consumed (research subagents \"cancelled: the caller dropped\"), plan steps as prose the worker cannot update, `proven=0` on every TB trial ever. The pipeline's value over bare was +1 cell at 2–4× the steps. The architecture treats the model as a component to orchestrate; the winning shape is the model as orchestrator whose stages are tools.\n\n## The conversion\n\n**1. Capability inventory → tools.** Literally everything, advertised through the discovery layer:\n- Introspection: `list_mcp_servers`, `list_skills`, `list_agents`, `list_commands`, `list_context_records`\n- Skills: `create_skill`, `use_skill`, `call_skill_function`, `retire_skill`\n- Commands: `create_command`, `invoke_command`\n- Agents (fleet primitives in-loop): `create_agent_tool`, `use_agent_tool`, `retire_agent_tool`, `send_message`\n- Memory & adaptive context: `record_memory`, `get_context_records`, `promote_context_record`, `retire_context_record`\n- Plan/task CRUD (batched — #2511's round-trip-per-card lesson): `make_plan`, `create_task`/`update_task`/`complete_task`/`cancel_task` with array inputs, status enums, no tri-state booleans (the `flip_achieved` lesson)\n\n**2. State tools — the first-class environment object.** This is how we save state:\n- `save_state(key, source)` / `load_state(key)` / `invalidate_state(key)` / `list_state` over a session scratch store inside the tool-confinement boundary but outside the graded diff (`<workspace>/.stella/scratch/`, exported as `$STELLA_SCRATCH` — see #2670's measured motivation: a 48MB input re-parsed 12×)\n- The environment's other facets ride the same object: filesystem inventory N levels down/up with types (#2669), process registry with a survival contract (#2666), turn-0 capability probe (#2670). One owner for \"what exists here, what's running, what survives me.\"\n\n**3. Proof tools — verification the agent must *do*, not prose it must *say*.**\nRigor-through-prompt is the current failure mode; replace it with tools whose contracts force the proof:\n- A task-shape taxonomy at the architecture level — **creation / change / service** — with a proof-form tool per shape: `prove_change` (today's witness flip), `prove_creation` (deliverable-existence + behavior assertion valid when baseline lacks the artifact — kills the VACUOUS catch-22, #2668), `prove_service` (declared endpoint answers AFTER agent teardown semantics, #2666)\n- `declare_done(proof_ref)` — completion requires a proof reference or an explicit `unprovable(reason)`; the prove-it gate (#2663) becomes this tool's enforcement rather than a prose nudge\n- Deliverable-shape check (`check_deliverable`: filename case, path, format — the two one-line-diff losses in #2670)\n\n**4. The contract discipline (the thesis's own proof).** Every tool:\n- Fixed, versioned I/O shape; wire-schema'd like the protocol types (round-trip tested, serde-first)\n- **Token-optimized**: outputs designed for \"least context required to complete the highest quality of work\" — bounded lists with counts for the remainder, steady strings for repeated states (loop-detector safety), no volatile timings in ToolOutput, references (state keys, proof refs) instead of re-inlined payloads\n- Journal discipline: infrastructure-mutating tools (create_skill, create_agent_tool, promote_context_record) emit the same replayable events as file mutations — determinism and replayability survive the model editing its own capabilities\n- Discovery-layer routing: many tools shipped, a curated core surfaced, the rest discoverable — the always-visible schema mass stays flat (the real replacement for the no-new-tools posture)\n\n## Invariant compliance\n\nPorts, not concretions: each tool is a `Tool` impl in stella-tools over ports; no I/O enters stella-core — the *decisions* (what counts as proof per shape, when state invalidates) stay pure and property-testable. The pipeline does not disappear; its stages become tools the worker drives, and its verdict layer remains the independent grader of the proofs the worker files.\n\n## Acceptance / done\n\n- The capability inventory above exists as registered tools with wire-schema'd contracts and witnesses each.\n- A TB-style panel run shows: worker-held plans (task CRUD in traces), state saved/loaded instead of recomputed, `proven>0` via the per-shape proof tools, services surviving teardown.\n- Always-visible schema tokens measured flat vs today (discovery routing asserted in a test).\n- The thesis measurement: re-run the like-for-like GLM panel; the delta against CC-on-GLM 6/8 is the number this architecture answers to.\n\nSupersedes #2672 (its two waves become this epic's first milestones). Refs #2666, #2668, #2669, #2670, #2671, #2663, #2511.\n\n\n## Children adopted in the 2026-08-12 triage sweep\n\nThree more. #2738 is the P7 web-retrieval carve-out this epic's own \"why now\" section names as a separating Claude Code behavior. #2800 is a live `ToolPolicy::narrow_with` widening bug whose eventual home is #2716, the governance half of this epic. #2900 is `recall_context` reachable on exactly one of six surfaces.\n\n#2738 #2800 #2900\n\n\n\n---\n\n## Alignment with the turn-loop wrapper architecture (2026-08-16)\n\nMac's architecture review of the turn-loop deck (#3377) settles a boundary this\nepic left open. Design: `doc:turn-loop-wrappers`. Filed as #3379, #3380, #3381.\n\n**The conflict, stated plainly.** This epic's invariant-compliance section says:\n\n> The pipeline does not disappear; its stages become tools the worker drives,\n> and its verdict layer remains the independent grader of the proofs the worker\n> files.\n\nThe wrapper architecture says the pipeline's stages become **entries in a\ndeclarative manifest that a wrapper plugin runs** (#3381). Those are two\ndifferent destinations for the same stages, and shipping both would give us two\ncopies of the staged flow — the exact duplication #3380 exists to remove.\n\n**The resolution: the split is by who decides, not by which stage.**\n\n| Thing | Home | Why |\n|---|---|---|\n| Proof the **worker** files — `prove_change`, `prove_creation`, `prove_service`, `declare_done(proof_ref)`, `check_deliverable` | **tools**, as this epic specifies | The worker must be able to *do* verification mid-turn, on its own initiative. That is this epic's thesis and it stands. |\n| Stage **sequencing** — triage, plan, scope, witness authoring, verify, the revise decision | **manifest**, run by the wrapper (#3381) | Nobody is orchestrating the model here; this is the ceremony *around* the turn, and it must be editable without a recompile. |\n| The **grader** | `judge`, the wrapper's third point (#3380) | Unchanged from this epic's own wording: it remains independent and it calls no model (#2584). |\n\nSo both statements survive with one word changed: the worker's proof\n**capabilities** become tools; the pipeline's **stage sequencing** becomes a\nmanifest. A stage that is nothing but \"ask the worker to file a proof\" collapses\ninto a tool call and stops being a stage at all — which is a reduction this epic\nand #3381 both want.\n\n**What changes in this epic's plan**\n\n- Section 3 (proof tools) is unaffected and stays the highest-value part.\n- The sentence quoted above should be read as: *its stages become manifest\n  entries; its proof-filing verbs become tools; its verdict layer stays the\n  independent grader.*\n- Any child of this epic that plans to add a **stage** to `pipeline.rs` should\n  instead land as a manifest entry once #3381 exists, or wait. That file is on\n  the god-file list and closed to growth, so this is a hard constraint, not a\n  preference.\n- The `declare_done(proof_ref)` gate and the wrapper's `judge` point must not\n  both be able to end a run. `declare_done` files evidence; `judge` decides.\n  Whoever implements `declare_done` states that boundary in its tool doc.\n\nRefs #3379, #3380, #3381.\n"[m[1;37m,[m
