    [1;34m"body"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"## Problem\n\nThe runner entrypoint's dockerd-collision guard (added on the re-landed #2180 tail, PR #2656) refuses to start when the `DOCKER-BRIDGE` iptables chain exists in the host network namespace:\n\n```\n[runner] FATAL: another dockerd already owns this network namespace.\n```\n\nBut Batch runs trials with ECS `networkMode: host`, and **iptables chains created by a trial's dockerd persist in the host netns after that trial's container exits**. So the guard cannot distinguish \"a live dockerd owns this netns\" (the co-placement bug it was written against) from \"residue of a finished trial on a reused instance\". Any warm-instance reuse false-positives and kills the trial with exit 1.\n\nObserved live 2026-08-10, cloud run `turnloop-rerun-pipe`: all 3 trials failed in under 2 minutes because AWS Batch placed them on instances just freed by `turnloop-rerun-bare`'s finished trials (same 4-vCPU-per-m6i.xlarge exclusive topology — genuine co-placement is impossible by construction, so every firing on this topology is a false positive). Runs that fan out onto an all-cold fleet (16 jobs → 16 fresh instances, `turnloop-cert-panel1`) never hit it, which is why it survived validation.\n\n## Repro / verify\n\n1. Run any cloud match; wait for a trial to finish but before its instance scales in (~10 min window).\n2. Submit another trial; Batch reuses the warm instance; the entrypoint refuses with the FATAL above.\n3. Logs: `/arenabench/batch` log group, e.g. run `turnloop-rerun-pipe` (all 3 trials).\n\n## Fix directions (pick one)\n\n- **Treat the chain as residue when exclusivity is structural**: the job def requests all 4 vCPU of the pinned m6i.xlarge, so co-residency cannot happen; the entrypoint can flush stale `DOCKER-*` chains (and the orphaned `docker0`/veth devices) before starting dockerd, keeping the refusal only for genuinely unexpected state (e.g. a bridge with live veth peers).\n- **Give each dockerd its own netns** (`unshare --net` + slirp/bridge egress), which the guard's own message suggests, and which makes reuse and co-placement both safe.\n- **awsvpc network mode** for trial tasks — heavier infra change, each task gets its own ENI/netns.\n\nFiles: `arenabench/infra/runner/entrypoint.sh` (`iptables -w -t filter -n -L DOCKER-BRIDGE` check, ~line 48), `arenabench/infra/core.yaml` (TrialTopology).\n\n## Done looks like\n\nTwo consecutive cloud trials on the same instance (finish → reuse) both run to completion; a genuinely concurrent second dockerd on one instance is still refused (or made impossible by netns isolation). A regression test at the entrypoint level (chain exists + no live bridge peers → cleaned and started) would witness it.\n\n## Constraints\n\n- Do not weaken the guard for genuinely co-placed live dockerds if the CE ever allows larger instance types (the guard's original purpose, #2180 defect 1).\n- Operational workaround meanwhile: submit panels only onto a scaled-to-zero fleet and keep job count ≥ concurrent-instance count, or wait ~10-15 min between runs for scale-in.\n\nRefs #2656, #2179, #2180\n"[m[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"number"[m[1;37m:[m 2659[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"title"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"arenabench runner: dockerd netns guard false-positives on warm-instance reuse — DOCKER-BRIDGE residue outlives the trial that made it"[m
  [1;37m}[m[1;37m,[m
