    [1;34m"body"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"## Problem\n\nThe ref-escape guard landed for #2541 (`crates/stella-cli/src/candidate_ws/ref_escape.rs`)\n**detects and refuses**; it does not repair. When a candidate has moved a ref\nthe graded tree depends on, the seal fails with a message naming the ref, the\nvalue recorded at the candidate's creation, and the `git update-ref` that\nrestores it — and then the graded tree is left exactly as corrupted as it was.\n\nThat is deliberate and documented (the module doc's \"Boundaries\" section):\nnothing in `candidate_ws` writes to the real repository except the winner's\nadoption `git apply`, and silently rewriting a user's branch on a mis-attribution\nwould be far worse than the damage it repairs. But it means a `stella run` can\nend with the user's `master` still pointing at pipeline history and their index\nreading as a staged reversal, with recovery left as manual work.\n\nTwo further gaps in the same guard, both accepted at the time and both worth\nclosing in one pass:\n\n1. **A deleted ref is not detected.** `escaped_refs_inner` compares values and\n   attributes a *new* value to the candidate. A ref present at creation and\n   absent at seal (`git branch -D`, `git tag -d`) carries no new value to sign,\n   so it passes silently. The recorded `refs_at_create` map already contains\n   everything needed to notice the disappearance; what is missing is an\n   attribution signature that a mid-run *user* deletion cannot forge — which\n   may not exist, in which case the honest outcome is a distinct, non-failing\n   warning rather than an abort.\n2. **A rewind to an unrelated commit is not detected.** The third probe\n   deliberately excludes a ref that moved backwards into history the graded\n   tree already had, because `git reset --hard HEAD~1` in the user's own\n   checkout is indistinguishable from a candidate doing the same. A candidate\n   that rewinds a shared branch to an unrelated commit therefore escapes\n   unnoticed.\n\n## Files\n\n- `crates/stella-cli/src/candidate_ws/ref_escape.rs` — the guard, its signature,\n  and `refusal()`\n- `crates/stella-cli/src/candidate_ws.rs` — `seal_inner` (the seam), the\n  `refs_at_create` field, and the module doc's ref-namespace section\n- `crates/stella-pipeline/src/ports.rs` — `WorkspaceError::Seal`, the variant the\n  refusal travels in today\n\n## Constraints already discovered\n\n- The module doc states that every command run against the real repository is\n  read-only except the winner-adoption `git apply`. Adding a repair write\n  changes that invariant, so it must be restated in the same PR, not quietly\n  broken.\n- Restoring `refs/heads/X` to its creation value **can** drop a commit the user\n  made mid-run on that branch, if the candidate then built on top of it. Any\n  repair needs to establish that the recorded value is still an ancestor of the\n  current one, and to say what it is discarding when it is not.\n- The attribution signature must keep the two negative controls green:\n  `a_mid_run_user_commit_is_not_a_ref_escape` and\n  `a_mid_run_user_rewind_is_not_a_ref_escape`. A repair that fires on either is\n  strictly worse than the current refusal.\n- `refs/worktree/*` is per-worktree and invisible to `for-each-ref` in the main\n  worktree, which is what keeps `WITNESS_BASELINE_WORKTREE_REF` out of the\n  comparison. Do not widen the enumeration without re-checking that.\n\n## Verify\n\n`crates/stella-cli/src/candidate_ws/ref_escape.rs`'s tests already build the\nexact escape (`committing_onto_the_graded_trees_branch_refuses_the_seal`). Extend\nit to assert the graded tree's ref is back at its pre-run value after the\nrefusal — that assertion fails today. For the two gaps above, the fixtures are\n`git branch -D` and `git branch -f <branch> <unrelated>` executed from the\ncandidate worktree with `--ignore-other-worktrees`.\n\n## Done when\n\nEither the graded tree's refs are restored to their creation values whenever the\nguard fires — with a witness test asserting the restoration and the existing\nfalse-positive controls still green — or a maintainer records the decision that\nrefusal-without-repair is the intended contract, in which case this issue closes\nas `not planned` and the two detection gaps above move to their own issue.\n\n## Related\n\n- #2541 — the defect this guard was built for\n- #1383 — the structural fix (a substrate that cannot reach those refs at all)\n- #2540 — the sibling ref-namespace contamination, still open\n"[m[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"number"[m[1;37m:[m 2641[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"title"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"candidate_ws: a refused ref escape leaves the graded tree's ref moved — refusal names the recovery but never performs it"[m
  [1;37m}[m[1;37m,[m
