    [1;34m"body"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"## Problem\n\n`arenabench/arenabench/reauth.py` is on `main` (merged in #2531) with 22 passing tests, and **nothing calls it**. `arenabench/arenabench/runner.py` contains no reference to `reauth`, `Supervisor`, or `read_trials`.\n\nSo the OAuth-rotation resilience it implements is not in effect. A Claude Code seat still dies exactly as before.\n\n## Why it matters\n\nClaude Code rotates its OAuth credential whenever any local session refreshes, and rotation **revokes** the previous token. The token is baked into the seat's `harbor run` subprocess environment at `Popen` time and cannot be changed in flight. A rotation two hours into an 89-task sweep kills every remaining trial of that arm.\n\nThose trials void rather than score (`telemetry.VOID_CREDENTIALS`), so `solve_rate` stays honest — but an arm measured on 20 of 89 tasks has not been measured, and the shortfall is in the direction that flatters whichever arm was still running. This is a live blocker for the planned 89-task head-to-head.\n\n## What already exists\n\n`reauth.py` provides a pure decision half plus a thin I/O shell:\n\n- `decide(Snapshot) -> Step` — the state machine (`RUNNING` / `HEALING` / `PAUSED` / `COMPLETE` / `EXHAUSTED`), a pure function over an immutable snapshot.\n- `Supervisor` — `tick(trials, seat_running=...)` calls `decide`, then acts: stops the seat, launches the re-dispatch, tracks rounds. Takes `token_source`, `stop`, and `launch` as injected callables.\n- `read_trials(job_dir)` — reads real trial directories into `TrialView`s via `telemetry.MetricsReader`. Deliberately does **not** collapse attempts.\n- `credential_digest` — per-process salted digest, so change detection never handles or persists the token.\n\nTwo properties are pinned by tests and must survive wiring:\n\n1. The token never reaches a log, a reason string, or a `repr`.\n2. A trial that failed for any **non-credential** reason is never re-dispatched. Re-running a timeout to get a nicer number is the dishonesty this must not enable.\n\n## The wiring, and the one real design problem in it\n\n`MatchRunner.start` launches one `harbor run` per seat covering all tasks; `_await_completion` polls until no process is alive. The natural shape is:\n\n1. Build a `Supervisor` for each contestant declaring `CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN`. `claude_oauth.local_claude_token` is the `token_source`; `stop` mirrors `MatchRunner.cancel`'s `os.killpg(os.getpgid(pid), 15)` (the whole process group — Harbor spawns Docker children); `launch` re-invokes `_launch` for a task subset.\n2. Tick each supervisor inside `_await_completion`'s poll loop, feeding it `read_trials(run.job_dir)`.\n3. Change the loop's exit condition. It currently breaks when no process is alive — a **paused** seat has no live process but is not finished, so as written it would end the match the moment the supervisor stopped the seat.\n4. Surface `PAUSED` on the match snapshot so the operator learns a token is wanted, and provide a path for `Supervisor.provide_token` (the CLI drives the run, so an in-memory hand-off from the supervising process is enough; do not add a disk channel — `claude_oauth`'s contract is that the value never touches disk).\n\n**The design problem to solve before coding:** a re-dispatch needs a job directory. `Match.trial_dirs` reads a single `run.job_dir` and collapses attempts with `setdefault`, so the *first sorted* trial wins per task. If a retry writes `beta__RETRY` beside a voided `beta__abc`, the scoreboard may keep the void and the successful re-run becomes invisible — the arm would read worse than it performed, which is a correctness regression introduced by the feature. Either:\n\n- re-dispatch into the same job dir and make `trial_dirs` prefer a *measured* trial over a voided one (this is arguably a latent bug already, with `--n-attempts > 1`), or\n- give each retry round its own job dir and teach `Match.trial_dirs` / `metrics_for` to union across them.\n\nPick one deliberately and say which in the PR.\n\n## Definition of done\n\n- A match whose Claude Code seat loses its credential mid-run finishes with every task measured, or reports an explicit shortfall — never silently 20-of-89.\n- Auto-heal from the local login without operator involvement when the keychain has rotated (the common case).\n- The opposing arm is never paused. Pausing both to fix one is itself a confound: the arms would no longer have faced the same machine load.\n- A test that fails without the wiring. The existing 22 unit tests do not cover it — they exercise `decide`/`Supervisor` in isolation, which is exactly why this shipped unwired.\n- Retry rounds are visible in the match record, so a reader can tell a first-attempt pass from a re-dispatched one.\n\n## Constraints\n\n- `arenabench/arenabench/runner.py` is ~1100 lines. Keep the delta there small; put the glue in `reauth.py` or a sibling.\n- The re-dispatch must reuse `_launch` rather than re-implementing the Harbor command line — provenance, the seat manifest, and the SUT pin all hang off it.\n- Never persist the token; never log it.\n\n## Related\n\n- #2101 — Claude Code auth lifecycle.\n- #2531 — merged the module.\n"[m[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"number"[m[1;37m:[m 2545[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"title"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"Wire arenabench reauth into the runner — the OAuth-rotation supervisor is on main and nothing calls it"[m
  [1;37m}[m[1;37m,[m
