    [1;34m"body"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"## Problem\n\n#2486 asked for the measurement before the optimisation, on the grounds that\nthe last guess did not survive contact with data. The measurement now exists\n(`tools.verify_done.phases`, `crates/stella-tools/src/verify/phases.rs`), and\nit names the phase.\n\nOne `verify_done` call against this repository, warm, with a trivial shell\nwitness so both test runs are near-zero:\n\n| phase | ms | share of call |\n|---|---|---|\n| `worktree_add` | **368** | **65%** |\n| `cleanup` | **108** | **19%** |\n| `baseline` | 37 | 7% |\n| `prune` | 23 | 4% |\n| `setup` | 11 | 2% |\n| `working_tree_run` + `shadow_run` | 11 | 2% |\n| `machinery` | **550** | **98%** |\n| `total` | 562 | |\n\nSo the shadow worktree's **create and destroy** are 85% of a call, and\n#2424's estimate (\"the machinery is the cost\") was right about the total and\nsilent about which part. Neither test run matters at this size.\n\nThis is a single warm reading on one repository, not a distribution. It is\nenough to pick a direction; it is **not** enough to claim a p90 improvement,\nwhich is what the definition of done below is for.\n\n## Why the existing fixes do not cover this\n\n- #2208 (landed) memoises the shadow *observation*, so an identical repeat\n  inside one turn pays none of this. It does nothing for the first call —\n  which every proof pays — nor for a second call whose key legitimately\n  differs because the agent edited a test file in between.\n- #2071 pins the baseline; it changes which commit is checked out, not what\n  checking one out costs.\n\n## Candidate directions, with the hazard each carries\n\nDo not treat this list as a plan; it is what the measurement makes plausible.\n\n1. **A session-scoped shadow worktree, reset per call instead of created per\n   call.** Converts `worktree_add` + `cleanup` from per-call to once-per-\n   session. Hazards: resetting to a clean baseline tree needs `git checkout\n   -f` plus `git clean -xdff`, and on a large tree that clean may cost what\n   the create cost — measure before assuming. Concurrent `verify_done` calls\n   need distinct shadows (the current code already namespaces by pid +\n   counter for exactly this reason), so this becomes a small pool, not a\n   singleton. Correctness is load-bearing: a shadow that retains anything\n   from a previous call is a false verdict, which is worse than a slow one.\n2. **`git worktree add --no-checkout` plus a sparse checkout of what the\n   test command touches.** Hazard: nothing knows what the command touches,\n   and guessing wrong produces a `WITNESS_INCONCLUSIVE_BUILD_ERROR` at best\n   and a false `VACUOUS` at worst.\n3. **Attack `cleanup` alone** — it is 19% and is three operations (`git\n   worktree remove --force`, `remove_dir_all`, `git worktree prune`). The\n   `prune` is idempotent bookkeeping that may not need to be on the call's\n   critical path.\n\nDirection 1 is the one the numbers point at. Directions 2 and 3 are listed\nso a reader can rule them out on purpose rather than by omission.\n\n## Files\n\n- `crates/stella-tools/src/verify.rs` — `run_against_baseline` (the\n  `worktree add`, the staging, the run), `cleanup_shadow`, `ShadowDirGuard`\n- `crates/stella-tools/src/verify/phases.rs` — the measurement; a before/after\n  reads these fields and needs no new instrumentation\n- `crates/stella-tools/src/verify/memo.rs` — #2208's memo, whose key logic\n  already enumerates everything a shadow's identity depends on. A reused\n  shadow has to respect the same set.\n- `crates/stella-tools/src/registry.rs` is a god file at its ceiling; a\n  session-scoped shadow pool belongs in a sibling submodule\n  (AGENTS.md § \"God files\").\n\n## Verify\n\nThe instrument is already in the tree, so a before/after is a census of one\nfield rather than a new measurement rig:\n\n1. Run the same task twice, once per binary, with `--log-file <path>`.\n2. Census `tools.verify_done.phases` records for `worktree_add`, `cleanup`,\n   and `machinery` — median and p90 of each.\n3. Report `verdict` alongside: `not_done` and `error` calls never build a\n   shadow, and including their zeroes drags every percentile down.\n4. `shadow_reused: true` records are memo hits and must be excluded from a\n   shadow-cost percentile for the same reason.\n\n## Definition of done\n\n`machinery`'s median and p90 both fall on the same task, shown as a\nbefore/after census of the field above, with the reused and never-built\ncalls excluded and said to be excluded. No verdict changes: the existing\n`verify::tests` and `verify::phases::tests` suites all still pass, and in\nparticular a reused shadow must not be able to carry state between two calls\n(the false-`VACUOUS` failure mode).\n\n## Related\n\n- #2486 — the measurement this comes out of (this table is its result)\n- #2424 — the half-1 question that produced the estimate\n- #2208 — the shadow memo, landed\n- #2377 — the interactive loop's per-tool-call workspace probe, the other\n  fixed-cost-per-call finding\n"[m[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"number"[m[1;37m:[m 2505[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"title"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"tools: verify_done's shadow lifecycle is 85% of a call — worktree_add 65%, cleanup 19% (measured, #2486)"[m
  [1;37m}[m[1;37m,[m
