    [1;34m"body"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"## Problem\n\nA constant that was set by measurement carries nothing that says so to the\ncompiler, so a merge can revert it in total silence. This is not hypothetical —\nit happened on 2026-08-09 and shipped to `main`:\n\n- #2414 moved `triage_latency_ceiling` from 10s to 30s after measuring that the\n  10s bound sat **inside** the answering distribution (27 of 34 triage calls\n  burned the full 10,000ms and returned nothing; the 7 that answered took\n  4,684–8,587ms).\n- #2462, a large and otherwise good PR, rewrote the `PipelineConfig::default`\n  struct literal from a branch that predated it and carried `from_secs(10)` over\n  the top.\n- Git reported **no conflict**, because one side simply did not contain the\n  other's lines. No test failed, because no test asserted the value. The field's\n  own doc comment — which explains the move and names its 7-sample caveat — was\n  left describing a number the struct no longer had.\n- It was found only because someone tried to start the follow-up issue (#2429)\n  that depends on the 30s ceiling.\n\nThis is the same *shape* as the deleted-test problem `check-deleted-tests.sh`\nexists for (#1976, #1860) — a textually clean merge where one branch's change is\nsilently absent from the result — but for **values** rather than tests, and\nnothing guards it.\n\n#2492 fixes that one field and adds an assertion for it. This issue is the\ngeneral question that fix deliberately did not answer.\n\n## Why it matters more here than in most codebases\n\nThese constants are the tuning surface of the agent. A halved latency ceiling\ndoes not crash, does not fail a test, and does not show up in a diff review of an\nunrelated PR — it just quietly changes what fraction of triage calls answer, and\nevery bench number measured afterwards is measured on a different engine than the\none the last measurement described. CLAUDE.md's rule that a bench conclusion\ncomes from the trace assumes the trace was produced by the configuration you\nthink it was.\n\n## What to do — the design question\n\nOptions, roughly in increasing cost:\n\n1. **Assert each measured constant in a test** (what #2492 did for one field).\n   Cheap, no machinery, but it is opt-in and the next tuned constant will forget.\n2. **A marker plus a guard.** Give measured constants a recognisable form — a\n   `const` with a doc-comment convention, or a small attribute — and a\n   `scripts/check-*.sh` that fails when one has no corresponding assertion. This\n   is how `check-left-behind.sh` handles the analogous \"a marker with no issue\n   number\" case.\n3. **Move them out of the struct literal entirely**, into a named `const` block\n   with the measurement recorded beside each. Does not stop a revert by itself,\n   but makes a reverting diff legible instead of one line inside a 40-line\n   literal.\n\nI have no strong recommendation; option 2 matches the repository's existing\nguard idiom most closely, and option 1 is what is already deployed for one field.\n\n## Scope — what to audit\n\nStart with the values that were set by a measurement and would not fail a test if\nreverted:\n\n- `crates/stella-pipeline/src/pipeline.rs` — `PipelineConfig::default`:\n  `triage_latency_ceiling` (now asserted), `recall_latency_ceiling`,\n  `research_latency_ceiling`, `scope_thresholds`\n- `crates/stella-core/` — compaction, eviction, loop-detection and budget\n  thresholds\n- Model output caps and retry counts in `crates/stella-model/`\n\n**This audit is the deliverable even if no guard is built**: a list of tuned\nconstants with no assertion behind them is useful on its own, and is what sizes\noptions 1–3.\n\n## Also worth checking, and not done\n\nNobody has swept #2462 against the other PRs it raced. #2492 diffed **#2430's**\nadded lines against `main` and found three reverted (two values and one emit\nsite). The same check against every PR that merged near it has not been run, and\n`git` will not volunteer it.\n\nA cheap version, for whoever picks this up:\n\n```bash\n# lines PR X added that main no longer has\ngit show <X> --unified=0 | grep '^+' | grep -v '^+++' | sed 's/^+//' \\\n  | while IFS= read -r l; do\n      t=\"$(printf '%s' \"$l\" | sed 's/^[[:space:]]*//; s/[[:space:]]*$//')\"\n      [ ${#t} -lt 25 ] && continue\n      git grep -qF -- \"$t\" origin/main -- crates/ || printf '%s\\n' \"$t\"\n    done\n```\n\nIt has false positives (a line that was legitimately reworded reads as missing),\nso it is a triage list, not a verdict.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nEither a guard that fails when a measured constant loses its assertion, or a\nwritten decision that per-constant assertions are the policy — plus the audit\nlist above, so the decision is made against the real population rather than one\nexample.\n\n## Related\n\n- #2492 — restores the reverted values and asserts the one field\n- #2429 — the issue blocked by the revert\n- #2414, #2430 — where the measurement and the value came from\n- #2462 — the PR that reverted them, blamelessly\n- #1976, #1860 — the same merge shape, for deleted tests\n"[m[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"number"[m[1;37m:[m 2495[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"title"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"gate: a measurement-tuned constant carries no assertion, so a clean merge can revert it in silence (#2462 did)"[m
  [1;37m}[m[1;37m,[m
