    [1;34m"body"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"## Problem\n\nPR #2269's Harbor resolver provisions an exact-pinned Harbor into `~/.arenabench/harbor/<version>/`, staging the build in a sibling directory named by PID: `staging = target.with_name(f\"{target.name}.staging.{os.getpid()}\")` (`arenabench/arenabench/harbor.py:473-474`, lines as of that PR's head), preceded by an unconditional `shutil.rmtree(staging)`.\n\n`server.py:925` is a `ThreadingHTTPServer` and `runner.start` runs on request threads, so two matches provisioning the same Harbor version in one process share a PID and therefore share the staging path. An event-controlled repro produced, in a single run: thread A failing with \"installed harbor into … does not report a version\", thread B failing while writing into a path renamed away underneath it, and `target` published **half-built** with `bin/harbor` absent.\n\nTwo aggravating details:\n\n- The second `os.rename` (`harbor.py:485`) is unwrapped. A raw `OSError` there escapes `resolve_for_dataset` (which catches only `HarborUnavailableError`, :568) and `runner.start` (only the two harbor types, `runner.py:655`), so it surfaces as an unclassified crash at the start of a paid run.\n- `finally` does not run on SIGKILL or a default SIGTERM — and this rig has documented foreign SIGTERMs — so `0.20.0.staging.<pid>/` survives a kill. `parse_version(\"0.20.0.staging.99999\")` returns `(0,20,0)` and `_discovered` (`harbor.py:393`) iterates every child of `managed_root()`, so a probe had `resolve_for_dataset` **select the staging directory's binary** and report it as \"installed by arenabench\". Validation is `--version`-only, so a half-built venv whose console script answers `--version` would grade a paid run.\n\n## Definition of done\n\n- Staging directory is unique per attempt: `Path(tempfile.mkdtemp(dir=target.parent, prefix=f\"{target.name}.staging.\"))`, with the pre-`rmtree` dropped. The rename-wins design is preserved.\n- The second rename is wrapped, or `ready()` is re-verified after it, so a lost race cannot escape as an unclassified `OSError`.\n- `_discovered` admits only children matching `^[0-9]+(\\.[0-9]+)*$`, and dead-PID staging debris is GC'd at provision entry.\n- Witness tests: a forced two-thread interleaving that must not publish a half-built `target`, and a test that a staging-named directory is never selected by resolution.\n- The new resolver tests scrub `ARENABENCH_HARBOR*` (autouse fixture or `monkeypatch.delenv`). With that variable set — as the bench rig's own server launch env sets it — 5 of the 6 new tests fail spuriously, because `_pinned` wins over the monkeypatched PATH.\n\n## Verify\n\n`uv run --frozen pytest arenabench/tests/ -k harbor` with and without `ARENABENCH_HARBOR` set in the environment; both must pass. The interleaving witness must fail against the PID-named staging and pass with `mkdtemp`.\n\n## Constraints discovered\n\n- The brew `harbor` 0.6.1 at `/opt/homebrew/bin/harbor` must never be uninstalled, upgraded, shadowed, or written to — other suites depend on it. #2269 is clean on this axis (`uv venv --relocatable` + `uv pip install --python <venv>`, no PATH shim) and the fix must stay clean.\n- `provision` / `_install` / `_discovered`-versus-debris currently have zero tests, which is where both defects live.\n\nRefs #2269, #2098.\n"[m[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"number"[m[1;37m:[m 2313[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"title"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"arenabench: Harbor provisioning stages under a PID-shared directory — threads clobber each other, and stale staging dirs get selected as installed harbors"[m
  [1;37m}[m[1;37m,[m
  [1;37m{[m
    [1;34m"body"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"## Goal\n\nMeasure the adaptive-context end goal directly: **does learned context give Stella a better chance to solve a task, faster, for less money?** CI certifies the mechanics (selection precision, lifecycle, compactness — `crates/stella-core/src/records/tests.rs` certification suites; the trace-replay harness epic); this arm measures the outcome, and it is the only place that can.\n\n## Design\n\nA paired arm on the existing TB 2.1 rig (bench/harbor_adapter): identical SUT, identical tasks, identical seats — one arm with a curated `.stella/rules/` record corpus mounted into the task workspace, one arm without. Per protocol discipline:\n\n- Same-commit SUT for both arms (never >5 commits behind tip), repeats per task, conclusions from `stella-events.jsonl` never surface signals, operational aborts named and excluded from the denominator, `priced_cost` vs `total_cost` kept distinct.\n- Metrics per arm: solve rate, wall clock to verdict, priced cost, tokens in/out, and — the record-specific ones — records rendered per turn (from the receipts/ledger), citations (`^handle` attributions), and bytes of volatile channel injected.\n- **Contamination note, stated up front:** records hand-written against known task families certify steering value, not benchmark standing. Any published number from this arm must say the corpus was task-aware. The clean variant — records learned by the replay harness from *other* tasks' traces, then evaluated on held-out tasks — is the follow-on once the harness epic lands.\n\n## Done when\n\nOne paired calibration run (5-task panel, ≥3 repeats) reports the four metric families per arm from the trace, with the comparison written up in bench/evidence/ and the contamination caveat in the report.\n\nRefs the replay-harness epic, #2283. Constraints: AWS rig scale-to-zero (STOP instances after use), nohup-detached server, no heavy local builds during runs.\n"[m[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"number"[m[1;37m:[m 2305[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"title"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"bench: paired with-records vs without arm on TB 2.1 — measure whether learned context solves faster and cheaper"[m
  [1;37m}[m[1;37m,[m
