    [1;34m"body"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"## Problem\n\nPR #2282 killed the operator-attachment family of false cd-escape warnings (`cd /app; ls` parsing its target as `/app;`). The false-warning *class* survives in a different family: `cd_escape_target` (`crates/stella-tools/src/bash.rs:126-146`, line numbers as of #2282's head) scans **all** words via `windows(2)` with no notion of command position, so a `cd <path>` appearing as *data* still warns as if it were a command:\n\n- **Heredoc bodies** — `cat > setup.sh <<'EOF'` … `cd /workspace` … `EOF`: a very common agent shape for writing scripts; the heredoc's `cd` line triggers the workspace-escape warning even though nothing changed directory.\n- **Echoed text** — `echo cd /outside` or `printf 'cd /tmp\\n'`.\n- **Comments** — `# cd /old-path` inside a longer compound command.\n\nEach of these tells a (possibly paid) agent, falsely, that its work is invisible to verification — the exact defect class #2282 set out to kill, with the same cost per occurrence.\n\n## Also worth folding in (drift bait, no behavioral bug today)\n\nThere are now **three near-copies of \"what is an operator word\"** in `bash.rs`: the tokenizer's operator arm, the cd skip list (`\";\" | \"&\" | \"&&\" | \"|\" | \"||\"`), and the grep-boundary list at `bash.rs:161` — which omits `&` while the cd list includes it. A shared `is_operator_word` helper would close the gap before the copies diverge for real. Redirection tokens (`2>&1`, `>>`) are also outside the tokenizer's operator set; benign for both current consumers, worth one word in the splitter's doc comment.\n\n## Definition of done\n\n- `cd` occurrences inside heredoc bodies, quoted/echoed arguments, and comments no longer produce the workspace-escape note. Detection posture stays \"better a missed note than a wrong one\" (bash.rs:124-125) — err toward the miss side.\n- Witness tests for the three data shapes (fail on current code, pass with fix).\n- Operator-word predicate unified or the three copies deliberately documented.\n\n## Constraints discovered\n\n- The splitter deliberately preserves backslashes rather than interpreting them (`is_symbol_shaped`'s `\\|` test depends on it) — don't \"fix\" that while in there.\n- `shlex`/`shell-words` do not help: both do field splitting only; `;` is a parser metachar to them too, and both strip the backslashes the callers need (verified during #2282's review).\n\nRefs #2282.\n"[m[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"number"[m[1;37m:[m 2301[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"title"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"tools: cd-escape detector warns on cd appearing as data — heredoc bodies, echo args, comments still false-warn the worker"[m
  [1;37m}[m[1;37m,[m
