    [1;34m"body"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"## Problem\n\nPR #2265 (#2127) stages the **harbor adapter** so a running match survives its\nlaunch checkout being deleted. It does not stage `arenabench` itself, and that\npackage is resolved the same fragile way:\n\n```python\n# arenabench/arenabench/runner.py, _agent_environment\nroots = [str(Path(__file__).resolve().parent.parent)]\n```\n\n`__file__` points into whatever tree the server was launched from. If that tree\nis deleted mid-match — the exact `cc00894779ff` scenario, where the server was\nlaunched from a git worktree that was later removed — every Harbor subprocess\nstarted afterwards gets a `PYTHONPATH` entry naming a directory that no longer\nexists, and `import arenabench.harbor_agent` fails.\n\nThe server process itself keeps running: it imported `arenabench` long ago and\nholds it in memory. Only the *subprocesses* break, which is why this failure\nlooks like an agent problem from the dashboard rather than a server problem.\n\n## Why it was out of scope for #2265\n\nThe traceback observed in `cc00894779ff` named `stella_harbor`, not\n`arenabench`, so staging the adapter is what the evidence supported. In that\nmatch `arenabench` was importable by another route (installed into the\nenvironment running Harbor) while the adapter rode `PYTHONPATH` from the\nworktree. A deployment where both come from the launch tree would fail here\ninstead, and nothing currently prevents it.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nEither:\n\n1. **Stage it too.** `arenabench.adapter.stage_adapter` is written against a\n   package name (`PACKAGE_NAME = \"stella_harbor\"`) and a containing directory;\n   generalising it to stage `arenabench` as well is mostly parameterisation.\n   Note the self-reference: the module doing the staging lives in the package\n   being staged, so the digest must be computed before anything is copied and\n   the staged copy must never be re-staged from itself.\n2. **Or refuse at match start**, once, with a named error that\n   `INFRASTRUCTURE_FAILURES` already recognises — rather than per trial. #2127\n   explicitly offered this as an acceptable resolution, and it is much simpler:\n   check at `MatchRunner.create` that `Path(arenabench.__file__).parent` still\n   exists and contains `harbor_agent.py`, and abort the match if not.\n\nWhichever is chosen, the acceptance property is the same as #2127's: **a match\nwhose server tree is deleted mid-run must not produce agent-setup failures\ninside `solve_rate`'s denominator.**\n\n## Verify\n\nReproduce as in #2127:\n\n1. Launch `python -m arenabench serve` from worktree A; start a multi-trial match.\n2. After the first trial begins, `git worktree remove A` (or `rm -rf`).\n3. Assert every later trial is either launched successfully (option 1) or the\n   match aborts with an operational error (option 2), and that\n   `GET /api/matches/<id>` reports the affected trials under `infrastructure`\n   rather than `judged`.\n\nA regression test can fake this without Docker the way\n`arenabench/tests/test_adapter.py::test_a_staged_adapter_outlives_the_checkout_it_was_copied_from`\ndoes — stage, delete the tree, then assert the next launch still resolves.\n\n## Files\n\n- `arenabench/arenabench/runner.py` — `_agent_environment`, the `roots` list\n- `arenabench/arenabench/adapter.py` — `stage_adapter`, `PACKAGE_NAME`\n- `arenabench/arenabench/telemetry.py` — `INFRASTRUCTURE_FAILURES`, `VOID_SETUP`\n- `arenabench/tests/test_adapter.py` — the harness to extend\n\n## Related\n\n- #2127 / #2192 — the adapter half, fixed in PR #2265\n- #2098 — the same family: launch-time resolution against a moving source tree\n"[m[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"number"[m[1;37m:[m 2266[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"title"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"arenabench: the arenabench package itself is still resolved from __file__, so a deleted launch tree strands every later Harbor subprocess"[m
  [1;37m}[m[1;37m,[m
