    [1;34m"body"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"## Problem\n\n`stella_runtime::seed_calibration` (`crates/stella-runtime/src/parts.rs`) queries `store.drift_samples(provider_id, model_id, …)` with the CONFIGURED model id and seeds the `CalibrationMap` under that same id. But the telemetry rows it queries persist `StepUsage.model` — the provider-REPORTED `result.model` (recorded by `Engine::handle_committed_result` in `crates/stella-core/src/driver.rs`; queried by `Store::drift_samples` in `crates/stella-store/src/telemetry.rs`).\n\nWhenever the reported string differs from the configured id — model aliases resolving to dated ids, gateway/router variants, Bedrock ARNs — two silent failures follow:\n\n1. The seed query matches zero rows, so every session re-warms from scratch (three fresh samples before any correction, per `CALIBRATION_MIN_SAMPLES` in `crates/stella-core/src/estimator.rs`). After #2133's latch fix this costs about three steps per session instead of the whole turn, but the cross-session warm-up the seed exists for never happens.\n2. Even when the query DOES match, `CalibrationMap::seed(model_id, …)` keys the entry under the configured id while live `record(&result.model, …)` keys under the reported string — two map entries, so the `factor(None)` single-entry fallback that serves the pre-first-result reads of a session goes dead (`estimator.rs`, `CalibrationMap::factor`).\n\nNote `crates/stella-core/src/estimator.rs` already documents the intent: 'keyed by the model string the provider reports … the provider dimension is handled where samples are persisted and re-loaded'. The seed side does not hold up that contract when the two strings diverge.\n\n## How to verify\n\nRun one turn against a provider whose reported model differs from the configured id (or a wiremock adapter test reporting a variant string), then inspect `.stella/private/store.db` `telemetry.model` versus the configured id, and assert what `CalibrationMap::report()` contains after `seed_calibration`.\n\n## Possible fixes (pick one, with a witness)\n\n- Seed under the reported strings actually present in the store: group `drift_samples` rows by their stored `model` and seed each group under its own key (query by provider only, or by both ids).\n- Or normalize the join key at persist time so the stored string equals the configured id — but that erases the reported-model truth the row also serves.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nA witness proving a session seeded from rows recorded under a reported string that differs from the configured id starts corrected (factor != 1.0 before the first live sample), failing on today's join. Refs #2133."[m[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"number"[m[1;37m:[m 2156[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"title"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"runtime/store: calibration seeding joins on the configured model id while telemetry rows carry the provider-reported model string"[m
  [1;37m}[m[1;37m,[m
  [1;37m{[m
    [1;34m"body"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"## Problem\n\nBench trials now export their container's `.stella/private/` telemetry to the host (adapter commit on PR #2136: `agent/telemetry/store.db`, `reflections.jsonl`, `context.db` per trial), but nothing on the Stella side can ingest a *foreign* store into the local telemetry surfaces:\n\n- `stella usage sync` replicates only the current workspace's `.stella/private/store.db` into `~/.stella/usage.db` — an exported bench store in a job directory is invisible to it.\n- `stella observe` reads the workspace store / hub, so bench runs never appear in the Observatory.\n- Skill mining consumes the workspace's `reflections.jsonl`; exported bench reflections sit unread.\n- Historical runs (before the export existed) left only `stella-events.jsonl` journals — no store at all.\n\n## Fix (two rungs)\n\n1. **`stella usage sync --from <store.db> [--label <text>]`** — ingest an exported store into the hub under a distinct bench workspace identity (`workspace_id` derived from the match/trial so re-syncs are idempotent via the existing cursor machinery). This makes every *future* bench trial visible to `stella usage report` and the Observatory's hub-backed views. Design constraint: the hub's schema already scopes by `org_id`/`workspace_id`/`repo_id` (`crates/stella-store/src/usage.rs` hub tables), so this is a read-attach-replicate of an extra source path, not new schema.\n2. **Journal backfill for historical runs** — a `stella usage import-journal <stella-events.jsonl>` that synthesizes one execution row plus `step_usage`-derived child rows from the journal. Lower fidelity (the journal is a projection); label rows as imported so they are never confused with first-party telemetry. This unlocks the ~20 matches of existing traces under `~/.arenabench/matches/*/jobs/*/*/agent/`.\n\nReflections: once (1) lands, a small host-side sweep can also append exported `reflections.jsonl` files into a bench-scoped reflections log for skill mining — but decide deliberately whether bench reflections *should* feed the same skill pool as interactive sessions (a bench container's lessons may not transfer; consider a `bench` domain tag via `.stella/domains.toml` instead of unconditional merging).\n\n## Verify / definition of done\n\n- `stella usage sync --from <exported store>` followed by `stella usage report` shows the bench rows under their bench workspace identity; running it twice adds nothing (cursor idempotence test).\n- Witness test in `stella-cli`'s usage command tests: sync from a fixture store, assert hub rows and idempotence — fails on main where the flag does not exist.\n- The Observatory renders the bench workspace in its hub-backed views.\n\nConstraints for the implementor: invariant 3 (zero telemetry egress) is untouched — this is local-to-local; `stella-store` owns all SQLite (invariant 1); the hub never reads project stores at query time (AGENTS.md `~/.stella/usage.db` contract) — ingest replicates, it does not attach live.\n\nRefs #2136 (the adapter export this consumes), #2089.\n"[m[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"number"[m[1;37m:[m 2140[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"title"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"telemetry: ingest exported bench stores into the usage hub (usage sync --from) + journal backfill for historical runs"[m
  [1;37m}[m[1;37m,[m
