    [1;34m"body"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"## Problem\n\n#2083 (PR pending) tightened `stella dataset export`'s default filter: a record is accepted only when every recorded model call reconstructs digest-verified (`Reconstruction::is_verified`), with at least one such call. That is the right default — an unvouched transcript must never reach a training set silently — but it has a stated consequence the issue's own wording anticipates: it says records are excluded *\"under the default filter\"*, implying a non-default path exists. None does.\n\nConcretely: a store whose history predates the receipts plane (no `step_receipt` rows), or whose journals mismatch benignly (the `JournalEra::CompactionUnjournaled` era — see `crates/stella-store/src/reconstruct.rs`), now exports **zero** records. The manifest counts them (`filter.executions_transcripts_unverified`) and the summary says why, so nothing is hidden — but there is no way to export those turns' outcome/tool-call/change trajectories at all, even though the v1 exporter could and the journal fold still can.\n\n## Fix shape\n\n- Add an explicit opt-in flag to `DatasetCmd::Export` in `crates/stella-cli/src/dataset_cmd.rs` (e.g. `--include-unverified-transcripts`) that admits otherwise-accepted executions whose transcripts do not verify. For those records `calls` must state the truth rather than fake it — either empty with a per-record marker (e.g. `transcript_verified: false`), or per-call `prompt_messages` withheld — never unverified bytes presented as verified ones.\n- The manifest's `acceptance_predicate` must state the loosened rule verbatim (same named-predicate discipline as today), and `executions_transcripts_unverified` should keep counting so the two modes stay comparable.\n- Consider whether `MismatchSeverity::Compaction` (benign, legacy-era) deserves different treatment from `Integrity` here — `Reconstruction::mismatch_severity()` is the one place that distinction lives; do not re-derive it.\n\n## Verify\n\n- Extend `crates/stella-cli/tests/dataset_export_cli.rs`: the seeded `unvouched` execution (the fixture already builds one — a receipt citing a block with no journal preimage) is absent by default and present under the flag, with its transcript honestly marked; the manifest states the loosened predicate verbatim; byte-determinism preserved.\n\n## Constraints discovered in #2083\n\n- `DATASET_SCHEMA_VERSION` is 2; if the record shape changes (a marker field), bump it and say so in the manifest.\n- Every string leaf passes through `redact_after_assembly` after assembly — a new field is covered automatically, but keep the no-`skip_serializing_if` discipline on `DatasetRecord`.\n- The reward policy is resolved from settings (`Settings::reward_policy()`); tests pin it in project scope so labels cannot drift with user-scope settings.\n\nRefs #2083, #2081, #872.\n\n## Done when\n\nThe flag exists, the default is unchanged, both modes are witnessed in `dataset_export_cli.rs`, and the manifest states whichever predicate actually ran."[m[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"number"[m[1;37m:[m 2123[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"title"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"stella dataset export: a non-default path for executions that cannot prove their transcripts (pre-receipts stores export zero records)"[m
  [1;37m}[m[1;37m,[m
  [1;37m{[m
    [1;34m"body"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"## Problem\n\nPR for #2099 ships `arenabench cloud run` (module `arenabench/arenabench/cloud.py`, tests `arenabench/tests/test_cloud.py`). Its submit contract — env names, queue selection, per-trial fan-out sizing, credential refusal, CodeBuild fallback, progress derivation — is proven against recorded-call fakes and pure-function tests, because the authoring session had no AWS access. What no fake can prove is the issue's operational half: **a 144-trial submission completing on the quota-limited account (on-demand vCPU 64) by queueing**, with results and artifacts actually landing in S3 and DynamoDB status rows written by the runner.\n\n## What to run\n\nOn a machine with AWS credentials for account 578673726240 (us-east-1), stack `arenabench-core` deployed (see `arenabench/infra/README.md` § Runbook), and SSM credentials populated under `/arenabench/`:\n\n1. Smoke: a 1-task, 1-seat match — `arenabench cloud run match.toml --ref main` (install with `pip install 'arenabench[cloud]'`). Verify: submit output names the run id; `runs/<run-id>/jobs.json`, `runs/<run-id>/match.toml` and the trial slice exist in `s3://arenabench-artifacts-578673726240/`; the job appears on queue `arenabench-measure`; progress lines stream; `results.json` downloads; DynamoDB table `arenabench` has `PK=RUN#<run-id>` rows.\n2. Scale: a 12-task x 12-seat (or repeated-seat) match → 144 trials. Verify the executor reports the queued backlog explicitly (the RUNNABLE note), Batch drains it under the 64-vCPU quota without any operator action, and every trial settles.\n\n## Constraints already discovered\n\n- Measured comparisons go to `arenabench-measure` only; `--burst` (spot) is a confound for measurement (#2099).\n- In-container pickup of the staged SUT binary rides on the sut_ref wiring fixes in #2082/#2098 — until those land, trials may fail to locate the staged binary even though staging (`SUT_S3_URI`/`SUT_COMMIT`) is correct. Re-check those issues first.\n- Costs real money; EC2 scale-to-zero means idle cost is nil, but 144 trials is hours of on-demand compute.\n- Claude Code seats additionally need #2101's durable credential.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nThe two runs above completed and their run ids recorded here; any contract drift found between the fakes and real AWS fixed in `cloud.py` with a matching fake-side test, so the recorded-call suite stays an honest mirror.\n\nRefs #2099, #2082, #2098, #2101"[m[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"number"[m[1;37m:[m 2112[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"title"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"arenabench cloud: first real-AWS acceptance run of the Batch executor (144-trial queue-through)"[m
  [1;37m}[m[1;37m,[m
  [1;37m{[m
    [1;34m"body"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"## Problem\n\nCloud trials read provider credentials from SSM SecureStrings under\n`/arenabench/` (exported by `arenabench/infra/runner/entrypoint.sh`). The\nClaude Code seat is currently fed by `/arenabench/claude_code_oauth_token`,\nseeded from the operator's local `~/.arenabench/credentials.env` — but OAuth\ntokens rotate, and #1980 already documents a CC seat dying mid-match when\nthe local token rotates. In the cloud the failure is worse: the SSM copy\ngoes stale silently and every subsequent CC trial fails at auth, which a\nscoreboard then records as agent losses (the operational-abort vs. loss\ndistinction the bench rules require).\n\n## What to do\n\n1. Prefer a non-rotating credential for the cloud CC arm: support\n   `/arenabench/anthropic_api_key` (API billing) as the primary CC seat\n   credential; keep the OAuth token as a fallback with a loud staleness\n   warning.\n2. At submit time (cloud executor) and at trial start (entrypoint), preflight\n   the credential — a cheap authenticated call — and refuse/flag the seat\n   before burning a trial container, extending #1827's refuse-at-launch\n   direction and #1980's mid-match detection to the cloud path.\n3. Mark auth-failure trials as operational aborts, not losses, in results.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nWith a deliberately invalid token in SSM, a submitted CC trial is refused\n(or voided as an operational abort) with a message naming the parameter;\nwith a valid `anthropic_api_key`, the CC seat completes a task. Witness:\nentrypoint/executor test covering the preflight refusal.\n"[m[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"number"[m[1;37m:[m 2101[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"title"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"arenabench cloud: Claude Code seat needs a durable credential — SSM OAuth token rotates stale"[m
  [1;37m}[m[1;37m,[m
  [1;37m{[m
    [1;34m"body"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"## Problem\n\n#2032 added a SUT freshness guard, but it blocks at exactly two chokepoints:\n\n- `preflight()` in `bench/evidence/run/env.sh` — the Terminal-Bench evidence runbook\n- `sut.unpinned_problem()` in `arenabench/arenabench/sut.py` — an unpinned arena match\n\nBoth are *launch-time, host-side* checks, deliberately mirroring\n`assert_portable_binary`: one message beats a wasted run. Neither is reached by a\ncaller who drives Harbor directly with the Stella adapter and never sources\n`env.sh` — for example a hand-assembled `harbor run` during debugging, a future\nwrapper, or a CI job that sets `STELLA_BINARY` itself. Such a run today produces\nscoreable trials from a binary of any age, which is the residual half of the\nfailure mode #2032 was filed against (\"a pinned-but-ancient build reached by a\npath nobody anticipated\").\n\nThe adapter *already* establishes the binary's identity per trial:\n`StellaAgent.install` in `bench/harbor_adapter/stella_harbor/__init__.py` parses\nthe embedded commit out of `stella --version` (`_embedded_source_commit`),\nverifies it against `STELLA_SOURCE_COMMIT`, and stores it as `_source_commit` /\n`_source_commit_verified`, which flow into trial metadata as\n`stella_source_commit` and `stella_source_commit_verified_in_binary`. So the\ncommit is known at install; only the *distance* is not.\n\n## What to do\n\nDecide, and implement, one of:\n\n1. **Record, do not block.** Measure the distance once on the host at adapter\n   construction and carry it into trial metadata alongside\n   `stella_source_commit`, so a result set can be audited after the fact even\n   when nothing refused. Cheapest, and keeps the blocking check where it costs\n   one message instead of one run.\n2. **Block at install.** Refuse in `install()` when the distance exceeds the\n   limit. Catches every caller, but pays per trial and needs a git checkout\n   reachable from wherever Harbor runs — measure once and cache, not per trial,\n   and note that `measure_distance` deliberately never fetches.\n\nOption 1 is the better default; #2032's blocking checks already cover both real\nlaunch paths, and a per-trial refusal fires after the cost it was meant to save.\nWhichever is chosen, say so in `bench/evidence/run/README.md` beside the\nexisting \"The binary ran, and it was the wrong code\" section, so the split\nbetween recorded and refused is documented rather than inferred.\n\n## Files\n\n- `bench/harbor_adapter/stella_harbor/__init__.py` — `StellaAgent.install`\n  (around the `_embedded_source_commit` / `STELLA_SOURCE_COMMIT` comparison),\n  and the trial-metadata dict carrying `stella_source_commit`\n- `bench/harbor_adapter/stella_harbor/freshness.py` — `measure_distance`,\n  `check_freshness`, `DEFAULT_MAX_BEHIND`\n- `bench/harbor_adapter/tests/test_adapter.py`\n- `bench/evidence/run/README.md`\n\n## Verify\n\nPoint `STELLA_BINARY` at a binary stamped with a commit far behind\n`origin/main`, drive a trial through the adapter *without* sourcing\n`bench/evidence/run/env.sh`, and confirm the chosen behaviour: the distance\nappears in trial metadata (option 1) or the install refuses naming the commit\nand the distance (option 2). Today neither happens.\n\n`bench/harbor_adapter/tests/test_freshness.py` shows how to synthesize a stamped\nbinary (`_stamp` writes the NUL-delimited `<version>-dev.<sha>` literal\n`crates/stella-cli/build.rs` emits) and a repository with a chosen distance\n(`_seed`), so no real 32 MiB artifact is needed.\n\n## Constraints\n\n- `bench/harbor_adapter/stella_harbor/__init__.py` is at its\n  `scripts/file-size-baseline.txt` ceiling and is closed to growth — the logic\n  belongs in `freshness.py`, with `__init__.py` holding only the call.\n- `measure_distance` never fetches, on purpose: a rig may be offline mid-run and\n  a guard that reaches the network fails for reasons unrelated to what it\n  measures. Keep that property; state the reference as-of-this-checkout rather\n  than making it fresh.\n- Do not make an unstamped binary fatal here. A plain `cargo build --release`\n  carries no stamp, and the adapter is used in the local development loop.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nA run driven through the adapter without `env.sh` either records the binary's\ndistance from `origin/main` in its trial metadata or refuses, and a test proves\nwhich — failing on the current code and passing on the new.\n\nRelated: #2032, #2016, #2020.\n"[m[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"number"[m[1;37m:[m 2051[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"title"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"bench: a run driven through the adapter without env.sh still measures a binary of any age"[m
  [1;37m}[m[1;37m,[m
  [1;37m{[m
    [1;34m"body"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"## Problem\n\nThree separate functions now read the same thing — the compile-time\n`-dev.<40-hex>` commit `crates/stella-cli/build.rs` stamps into the Stella\nbinary — from the same byte shape, with the same 1 MiB chunk-and-overlap\nstreaming, and the same regex:\n\n| Function | File | Returns |\n|---|---|---|\n| `_binary_version_source_commits` | `bench/harbor_adapter/stella_harbor/secure_launcher.py` | `set[str]` |\n| `embedded_source_commits` | `bench/harbor_adapter/stella_harbor/freshness.py` | `tuple[str, ...]`, lowercased |\n| `embedded_commit` | `arenabench/arenabench/sut.py` | `str`, `\"\"` when not exactly one |\n\nThe first two are in the *same package* and should be one function. The third is\na deliberate copy and is not the subject of this issue — `arenabench` is\nApache-2.0 and slated for ejection (see `.github/workflows/bench.yml`'s\n\"arenabench — pytest (until ejection)\" step), so it must not import from the\nAGPL adapter tree. Note that fact here so a later reader does not \"fix\" it by\nadding the coupling.\n\nWhy it matters beyond tidiness: this reader is what makes a binary's identity\n*intrinsic* rather than a claim in a sidecar, which is the whole basis of the\nfreshness guard (#2032) and of the secure launcher's artifact attestation. Two\nimplementations can disagree about an edge case — a stamp at EOF, a stamp\nstraddling a chunk boundary, case normalisation — and the failure mode is that\none surface says a binary is identifiable and the other says it is not.\n\n## What to do\n\nMove the reader into `freshness.py` (or a small shared module) and have\n`secure_launcher.py` call it, keeping `_VERSION_COMMIT_BYTES_RE` in exactly one\nplace. Preserve each caller's current contract:\n\n- The secure launcher compares against `{source_commit}` as a *set* and must\n  keep failing closed when more than one stamp is present.\n- `freshness.read_identity` needs the full tuple to report \"carries N different\n  compile-time commit stamps\" in its refusal.\n\n## Files\n\n- `bench/harbor_adapter/stella_harbor/secure_launcher.py` —\n  `_binary_version_source_commits`, `_VERSION_COMMIT_BYTES_RE`, and the\n  `embedded_commits != {source_commit}` check in `_validate_claim_environment`\n- `bench/harbor_adapter/stella_harbor/freshness.py` — `embedded_source_commits`\n- `bench/harbor_adapter/tests/test_freshness.py` — already covers EOF stamps,\n  chunk-boundary straddling, case, and repeated stamps; reuse these\n- `bench/harbor_adapter/tests/test_secure_launcher.py`\n\n## A second, smaller copy: the staleness limit\n\n`freshness.DEFAULT_MAX_BEHIND` (25) and `arenabench.sut.MAX_BEHIND_UNPINNED`\n(25) are two copies of one number, documented as matching but not enforced to.\nCross-tree enforcement is the wrong fix for the ejection reason above. Decide\nexplicitly whether they *should* stay equal after ejection — they answer\nslightly different questions (a published evidence run versus an interactive\narena match) and may legitimately diverge. If they should stay equal, the\nhonest mechanism is a test in each tree asserting its own value with a comment\nnaming the other, not a shared constant.\n\n## Verify\n\n```console\n$ rg -n \"dev\\\\\\\\.\" bench/harbor_adapter/stella_harbor arenabench/arenabench\n$ cd bench/harbor_adapter && uv run --no-sync pytest -q\n$ cd arenabench && uv run --with pytest --no-project pytest -q\n```\n\n## Constraints\n\n- `secure_launcher.py` is at its `scripts/file-size-baseline.txt` ceiling;\n  removing the function should *lower* it, so run `make file-size-update` and\n  commit the baseline diff.\n- Any new module under `bench/harbor_adapter/stella_harbor/` must be added to\n  `_FIXED_ADAPTER_SOURCE_PATHS` in `secure_launcher.py`. That tuple is\n  enumerated by hand on purpose — the paid launcher byte-compares the running\n  adapter against the published commit, so a globbed file would execute without\n  ever being compared. Forgetting it fails closed with \"public adapter tree hash\n  differs from runtime identity\".\n\n## Definition of done\n\nOne implementation of the embedded-commit reader inside `stella_harbor`, both\ncallers' fail-closed behaviour unchanged and still witnessed by tests, and a\nrecorded decision on whether the two staleness limits stay coupled.\n\nRelated: #2032 (introduced `freshness.py`), #2016, #2020.\n"[m[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"number"[m[1;37m:[m 2050[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"title"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"bench: two copies of the embedded-commit reader inside stella_harbor can disagree about a binary's identity"[m
  [1;37m}[m[1;37m,[m
  [1;37m{[m
    [1;34m"body"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"## Problem\n\n`crates/stella-cli/src/trace.rs::assemble` records a call's reconstruction failure as one free-text field:\n\n```rust\nformat!(\n    \"unresolved: [{}]; digest mismatches ({}): [{}]\",\n    reconstruction.unresolved.join(\", \"),\n    crate::inspect::severity_tag(reconstruction.mismatch_severity()),\n    reconstruction.digest_mismatches.join(\", \")\n)\n```\n\n`#1981` added the severity word to that string so a trace read long after the run says whether a digest mismatch was routine compaction or a real integrity signal. That is an improvement on silence, but it leaves the one field a consumer would filter on buried in prose it has to parse with a regex.\n\nEvery other surface got structured fields in the same PR:\n\n- `stella inspect --format json` emits `journal_era` and `digest_mismatch_severity`.\n- `/api/execution-context` emits the same two keys.\n\nThe trace record is the only one that did not, and it is the surface most likely to be consumed by a machine — it is an append-only JSONL artifact whose entire purpose is later analysis.\n\n## Files\n\n- `crates/stella-cli/src/trace.rs` — `assemble`, the `TraceCall` struct, and the `error` field.\n- `crates/stella-cli/src/inspect.rs` — `severity_tag` / `era_tag`, the shared spellings.\n\n## What \"done\" looks like\n\n`TraceCall` carries the verdict as data — e.g. `journal_era: &'static str` and `digest_mismatch_severity: &'static str` beside `verified` — using the same three severity words and two era words the other two surfaces emit, so one grep over the JSONL answers \"did any call in this run raise a real integrity signal\". `error` may keep the human sentence; it must stop being the only place the answer lives.\n\n## Verify\n\n`crates/stella-cli/src/trace.rs`'s test module builds a `TraceRecord` from a seeded `Store`. Add a case with one mismatching block on an execution stamped `journal_era = 1` and assert the emitted record exposes `integrity` as a field rather than as a substring; it fails today.\n\n## Constraints\n\n- `TraceRecord` is a serialized artifact read by tooling outside this repo — add fields, never rename or remove existing ones.\n- The severity words are pinned on both sides of the observatory boundary today (`crates/stella-cli/src/inspect.rs::tests::the_json_verdict_words_match_the_observatorys` and `crates/stella-observatory/tests/journal_era.rs`). A third emitter joins that convention rather than inventing a fourth spelling.\n\nRefs #1981"[m[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"number"[m[1;37m:[m 2030[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"title"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"stella trace records reconstruction failures as an unstructured prose string, so no consumer can act on the severity"[m
  [1;37m}[m[1;37m,[m
  [1;37m{[m
    [1;34m"body"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"## Evidence\n\nMatch `bbc9ea944037`, task `portfolio-optimization` (`~/.arenabench/matches/bbc9ea944037/jobs/bbc9ea944037-stella/portfolio-optimization__o9T24nP/agent/stella-events.jsonl`):\n\n- **1,089 seconds spent inside `sleep`** out of 1,548s of total tool time — against a 900s agent budget. Model time was 204s. The trial spent 5.3x longer sleeping than thinking.\n- The exact calls: `sleep 300; echo done` ×3, `sleep 120` ×1, `sleep 60` ×1.\n- Fleet-wide, **6 of 51 trials** slept more than 30s; the top five: portfolio-optimization 1,089s, crack-7z-hash 307s, make-mips-interpreter 114s, git-multibranch 73s, sqlite-with-gcov 47s.\n\nThe shape is always the same: the agent starts a long job with `start_process`, then blocks on `sleep N` in `bash` instead of polling `read_output`. It already *has* the right tool — the same trial calls `read_output` five times — it just interleaves useless sleeps between the polls.\n\n## Why nothing catches it\n\n- **Loop detection doesn't fire.** `LoopConfig` defaults are `exact_repeat_threshold: 3`, but the sleeps are separated by `read_output` spacers, so they land on the *interleaved-repeat* rung, which is guarded by `window_is_progressing` — and each poll returns different bytes, so the window looks like it is progressing. Correct by its own definition; wrong for this shape.\n- **Budget doesn't fire.** The budget guard is spend-based; sleeping costs $0.\n- **Nothing distinguishes waiting from working.** To every metric, 300s inside `bash` is indistinguishable from a 300s compile.\n\n## What to do (pick one or more, this is a design question)\n\n1. **Make the shape visible first.** Attribute sleep time in the trial's telemetry — a `wasted_elapsed`-style field already exists for replay (`TrialMetrics.wasted_elapsed` in arenabench); a \"blocked on sleep\" number would make this measurable before it is policed.\n2. **Guidance rung**: when accumulated sleep in a turn crosses a threshold, inject guidance naming `read_output`/`wait_for` as the correct instrument. This fits the existing distress-guidance loop rather than adding a new mechanism.\n3. **Tool-level**: have `bash` refuse or clamp a *bare* `sleep` above some ceiling (a sleep inside a real command — `sleep 2 && curl` retry loops — must stay legal; the pathological form is `sleep N; echo done` as the whole command).\n\nOption 1 is the honest first step and is a prerequisite for judging 2 and 3 — do not skip it.\n\n## Files\n\n- `crates/stella-core/src/loop_detect.rs` — the rungs and `window_is_progressing`\n- `crates/stella-tools/src/` — the bash tool, if the clamp route is chosen\n- `crates/stella-core/src/driver.rs` — where guidance is injected (**a god file: land new logic in a sibling submodule**)\n\n## Done when\n\nA trial that sleeps away its budget is either prevented, steered, or at minimum *reported as having done so*, with a witness test over a synthetic event stream carrying the portfolio-optimization shape (three `sleep 300` calls separated by polls).\n"[m[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"number"[m[1;37m:[m 2022[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"title"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"agent loop: a bare `sleep` as a polling strategy burns the whole trial budget and no guard sees it"[m
  [1;37m}[m[1;37m,[m
