    [1;34m"body"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"## Problem\n\nFrontier-Bench is now registered and selectable in the arena GUI\n(`arenabench/arenabench/registry.py::FRONTIER_BENCH`, 74 tasks enumerated from the\nexport at `~/.arenabench/datasets/frontier-bench/`). The task picker lists all 74 with\nno indication that a given host cannot run a large fraction of them.\n\nTwo distinct hazards, and neither surfaces as an error:\n\n1. **Four tasks declare `gpus = 1`** (`exam-pdf-eval`, `fp8-rmsnorm-gemm`,\n   `jax-speedrun-gpu`, `math-eval-grader`). On a host with no GPU the container comes\n   up, the work is impossible, and the verifier returns 0.0. The row is\n   **arithmetically identical to a genuine miss**. Terminal-Bench 2.1 has no such tasks,\n   so this hazard is new with this dataset.\n2. **Memory ceilings exceed what the Docker VM can hand out.** Frontier-Bench tasks ask\n   for 2–32 GB. `memory_mb` is a cap Docker accepts on a smaller VM, so an 8 GB task on\n   a 10 GB daemon *starts*, then competes with the daemon, swaps, and gets OOM-killed\n   partway in — arriving as another reward-0 row. On the current Mac rig (Colima, ~10 GB)\n   only **48 of 74** are runnable; 18 of the 22 memory-excluded tasks want exactly 8192 MB,\n   so raising Docker to ~12 GB takes it to 66, and 70 is the local ceiling on 16 GB physical.\n\nAn arena match runs **N contestant containers per task slot**, so the ceiling that\nmatters is the Docker allocation divided by N — a number only the operator knows.\n\n## Prior art in this repo — do not reinvent it\n\n`bench/evidence/frontier/plan.py` already solves exactly this: it reads each task's\ndeclared resources, compares them against the host's actual Docker allocation\n(`FB_MEMORY_HEADROOM_MB`), honours `FB_ALLOW_GPU` as a tri-state, and emits a plan that\n**names every excluded task**. `bench/evidence/frontier/env.sh` documents the reasoning\nin full. `primary.sh` prints a `posture=` line naming every reason a run is not\nsubmittable.\n\nThe arena has none of this. The right fix is almost certainly to lift `plan.py`'s\ncapacity logic into `arenabench/` and have the picker consume it, not to write a second\none — two definitions of \"can this host run this task\" is the drift class this repo\ntreats as a defect.\n\n## Files\n\n- `arenabench/arenabench/registry.py` — `Task` already parses `memory_mb`, `cpus`; it does\n  **not** parse `gpus`. `Task.heavy` is a TB2.1-calibrated proxy (`memory_mb > 4096 or cpus > 1`)\n  and is not a capacity check.\n- `arenabench/ui/components/setup/task-picker.tsx` — `TaskFilters` has `lightOnly`; no\n  host-capacity awareness.\n- `arenabench/arenabench/server.py` — `ArenaServer.task_sample` takes `max_memory_mb`, so\n  half the plumbing exists but the operator must supply the number by hand.\n- `bench/evidence/frontier/plan.py`, `bench/evidence/frontier/env.sh` — the logic to lift.\n\n## Repro\n\n1. Start the arena with Frontier-Bench available (see the `ARENABENCH_HARBOR_FRONTIER_BENCH`\n   note in `arenabench/arenabench/harbor.py`).\n2. Pick `frontier-bench` in the wizard; all 74 tasks are offered.\n3. Select `jax-speedrun-gpu` on a machine with no GPU and launch.\n4. The trial completes and reports reward 0.0, with nothing distinguishing it from a task\n   the agent genuinely failed.\n\n## Definition of done\n\n- `Task` parses `gpus` from `task.toml` and it reaches the picker.\n- The picker knows the host's real capacity (Docker allocation ÷ contestant count) and\n  visibly marks tasks this host cannot run — and either refuses to select them or\n  requires an explicit override.\n- A match that ran an unrunnable task says so in its own record, so the denominator is\n  auditable after the fact. Excluding a task and *saying so* is the honest version;\n  silently absorbing four guaranteed zeros is not.\n- The capacity rule has exactly one implementation shared with `bench/evidence/frontier/`.\n\n## Constraints\n\n- Do not raise `Task.heavy`'s TB2.1 calibration to cover this; it is a different question\n  (`heavy` = \"forces concurrency to 1\", capacity = \"can run at all\").\n- `arenabench/ui` has no test harness (#1751), so the UI half needs its assertions in the\n  Python layer or that harness lands first."[m[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"number"[m[1;37m:[m 1983[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"title"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"arenabench: Frontier-Bench task picker offers tasks this host cannot run, and a GPU task scores 0.0 like a real failure"[m
  [1;37m}[m[1;37m,[m
  [1;37m{[m
    [1;34m"body"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"## Problem (medium confidence — external-API half unverified)\n\n`crates/stella-model/src/zai.rs:1100-1110` sends `cache_control` + `session_id` as **top-level request fields** when serving OpenRouter. `to_zai_messages` (:903-921) emits plain string content for every message — no parts array, so no per-part `cache_control` anywhere in the body. OpenRouter's documented Anthropic caching is engaged by `cache_control` on message content **parts**; if the root-level field is not honored for Anthropic-routed models, Claude via OpenRouter runs at 0% cache while the parity matrix (provider_parity.rs:83-92) reports OptIn satisfied — the exact silent-drop shape invariant 8 was born from (its witness only asserts the field reaches the wire, which cannot prove the gateway honors it).\n\n## To do\n\n1. Verify against a live OpenRouter Claude route: send two identical requests and read the cache-discount/usage fields OpenRouter returns.\n2. If root-level is dead: switch to parts-form content for system (at minimum) with per-part cache_control, mirroring the native Anthropic adapter's placement.\n3. Extend the parity witness to assert the part-level placement.\n\n## Constraints\n\nThe user's OpenRouter posture memory and #1386 (attested cost lookup) are adjacent; a live check needs an API key — coordinate with the bench rig rather than burning ad-hoc credits."[m[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"number"[m[1;37m:[m 1854[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"title"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"model: OpenRouter cache opt-in rides the request root, but Anthropic-routed models read cache_control on message content parts — needs live verification"[m
  [1;37m}[m[1;37m,[m
