    [1;34m"body"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"## What is wrong\n\n`bench/evidence/judge-evidence-demand-1295/run.sh` is the preregistered two-arm run\nscript for #1295. It selects its arm with an environment variable:\n\n```sh\n# run.sh:68\nSTELLA_JUDGE_EVIDENCE_DEMAND=\"$demand\" scrubbed harbor run ...\n```\n\nThe #1394 rename moved that name in the adapter without an alias:\n\n```python\n# bench/harbor_adapter/stella_harbor/posture.py:115\n_VERIFIER_EVIDENCE_DEMAND_ENV = \"STELLA_VERIFIER_EVIDENCE_DEMAND\"\n```\n\n`STELLA_JUDGE_EVIDENCE_DEMAND` is now unregistered. The adapter's ambient-env check\n**fails closed** on unregistered `STELLA_*` names — see the comment at\n`bench/harbor_adapter/stella_harbor/__init__.py:246`, which records that an\nunlisted `STELLA_TURN_BUDGET` \"killed all ten trials of a run.\"\n\n**So the script cannot run as written.** It fails loudly rather than silently\nmis-arming, which is the gate working — but the experiment is scripted and\n**unrun** (`bench/evidence/README.md` records it as \"scripted but **unrun** (no\nfunded credential)\"), so the first person to fund it hits a refusal.\n\n## Why this needs a decision rather than a patch\n\nThree options, and the right one is a judgment call about a preregistered artifact:\n\n1. **Add a read alias in `posture.py`** so `STELLA_JUDGE_EVIDENCE_DEMAND` still\n   selects the arm. Matches the additive-alias rule #1394 applied to every wire\n   type. Costs: a second live name for one knob, indefinitely.\n2. **Edit `run.sh` + the directory README** to the new name. `bench/evidence/` is\n   frozen *at the vocabulary each run was recorded in* — but this run has no\n   recorded results yet, so there may be nothing to protect. Costs: establishes\n   that a frozen preregistration can be edited, which is exactly the norm the\n   freeze exists to prevent.\n3. **Leave it and document the substitution** in the directory README, so whoever\n   funds the run knows to export the new name.\n\nMy read is (2) is defensible *only* because the run never happened, and that\ndistinction should be written down wherever it lands.\n\n## Verifying\n\nThe arm is supposed to be a property of the digest — the directory README says\n\"The arm is `STELLA_JUDGE_EVIDENCE_DEMAND`, which lands in the hashed posture, so\na trial's arm is a property of its digest.\" Whatever fix is chosen, the check is:\nthe two arms must produce **different** posture digests.\n\n```python\nimport importlib.util, sys\nspec = importlib.util.spec_from_file_location(\n    \"posture\", \"bench/harbor_adapter/stella_harbor/posture.py\")\nm = importlib.util.module_from_spec(spec); sys.modules[\"posture\"] = m\nspec.loader.exec_module(m)\n# with the selector on vs off, these must differ\nprint(m._benchmark_engine_posture(\"openrouter/z-ai/glm-5.1\",\n                                  verifier_evidence_demand=True)[2][:8])\nprint(m._benchmark_engine_posture(\"openrouter/z-ai/glm-5.1\",\n                                  verifier_evidence_demand=False)[2][:8])\n```\n\nIf a fix makes both arms produce the same digest, the arm is not on the record and\nthe run is worthless — that is the failure to test for.\n\n## Context\n\n- Found while carrying #1394's rename through the bench records (#1417, #1429, and\n  the PR that links here).\n- `bench/READINESS.md` §8.4.4 documents the digest-rename boundary and is the right\n  place to note this if option 3 is chosen.\n- Related: #1432 (no guard pins non-Rust role-name producers to the Rust enum —\n  the root cause of this whole class).\n"[m[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"number"[m[1;37m:[m 1446[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"title"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"bench: the #1295 two-arm run script can no longer select its own arm"[m
  [1;37m}[m[1;37m,[m
