    [1;34m"body"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"## Problem\n\n`GitCandidateWorkspaces` is the only `CandidateWorkspacePort` implementation. It isolates a candidate as a detached git shadow worktree and promotes the winner by diffing the snapshot against its seal and `git apply`-ing the result onto the real tree.\n\nThat is the right design **when the real tree is precious** — a user's working copy that must not be clobbered and whose index and stash are shared machine state. It is the wrong design when the tree is disposable, and it fails in two distinct ways there.\n\n### 1. The snapshot is a git *view*, not a copy\n\n`populate_snapshot` carries HEAD, the tracked delta, and `git ls-files --others --exclude-standard` — which excludes ignored paths by construction (`candidate_ws.rs`). So every candidate is missing everything in `.gitignore`: `node_modules/`, `.venv/`, downloaded datasets, compiled artifacts the tests exec.\n\nIn a benchmark container that state is routinely load-bearing and pre-installed by task setup, so the candidate solves a materially different tree than the grader inspects. In an ordinary project it means best-of-N is unusable for most JS and Python repos: every candidate fails the project's own test command on a missing dependency.\n\n(Partially mitigated by the `omitted_paths` disclosure — the candidate is now *told* what is absent and can reinstall. That converts a silent failure into a recoverable one; it does not make the snapshot faithful.)\n\n### 2. Adoption is overhead-only where the container is already the isolation\n\n`adopt` is all-or-nothing and requires the shadow to still be byte-identical to its seal. Its failure modes — seal mismatch, `git apply` conflict — are live in a container while the guarantee it buys (don't clobber the user's tree) is already provided by the container being disposable. Pure risk, no benefit.\n\n## Proposal\n\nA second adapter behind the same `CandidateWorkspacePort`, selected explicitly (never auto-detected — it is destructive by design):\n\n- **Isolate** by copying the whole tree, ignored files included. No git query decides what belongs; the directory is the truth.\n- **Promote** by replacing the target tree's contents with the winner's. No diff, no patch, no seal, no conflict class.\n- **Require** an explicit opt-in, because whole-tree promotion is destructive and is only safe where the tree is disposable.\n\nSame fan-out semantics, same port, no pipeline changes. Cost is disk (N copies) rather than risk, which is the correct trade where the tree is a container layer.\n\n## Why it matters beyond benchmarks\n\n#1044 mines best-of-N `CandidateScore` for DPO chosen/rejected pairs. Candidates that lose work to an unfaithful snapshot or a failed adoption would poison those pairs — so this is a precondition for the distillation roadmap, not only for the bench board.\n\n## Blocks\n\n- #1289 — deciding retries/parallel attempts per benchmark task needs a trustworthy `candidates>1` first.\n- #1044 — DPO pairs are only as good as the candidate outcomes they are mined from.\n\n## Context\n\nBest-of-N had never run outside tests until `STELLA_CANDIDATES` became selectable (#1211 §6.8) — `candidates` had been written exactly once in the tree, to `None`. These are first-contact defects in a path that was previously unreachable, not regressions."[m[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"number"[m[1;37m:[m 1383[1;37m,[m
    [1;34m"title"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"Container-native CandidateWorkspacePort: whole-tree copy and promotion instead of git-diff-and-adopt"[m
  [1;37m}[m[1;37m,[m
