    [1;34m"body"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"## What's going on\n\nThe Terminal-Bench 2.1 leaderboard's top entry is **Claude Code with Fable 5: 83.8% (plus or minus 1.2%), costing $552.67**.\n\nOur best published number is **58 of 89 tasks, or 65.2%** — a single attempt, using GLM-5.2. On that same model Claude Code scored 49.4%, so our harness clearly wins on equal footing. But we are still more than 18 points below the top of the board, and our number is not a leaderboard submission.\n\n## Why it matters\n\nRight now we can say \"we beat Claude Code on the same model\". We cannot say \"we are competitive with the best published result\", because we have never run the configuration that would test it or submitted under the rules the leaderboard requires.\n\n## What a real submission requires\n\n- All 89 tasks, at least 5 attempts each (not one)\n- The official dataset reference, not a local copy\n- Publicly available run transcripts\n- Review by the benchmark maintainers\n- The configuration written down **before** the run, not chosen afterwards\n\nThat last point is the one that protects us. We already did it once for the GLM-5.1 claim, and the machinery to keep it honest — the checks that confirm the settings we published are the settings that ran — was unblocked in #1209.\n\n## What has to happen first\n\nThis is the last step, not the next one. It depends on:\n\n- #1211 §6.2 — the Fable 5 limits (**done**, shipped in #1277)\n- the Fable wait-time check (its own issue)\n- the independent checker measurement (its own issue)\n- the token cost work (its own issue) — at Fable prices this decides whether a full run costs $550 or $4,000\n\n## Done when\n\n- 89 tasks x 5 or more attempts have run on the official dataset\n- The configuration was published before the run started\n- Transcripts are public and maintainers have reviewed them\n- The result is recorded in `bench/evidence/`\n\n## Background\n\n#1211 §6.9.\n"[m[1;37m,[m
