    [1;34m"body"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"## What's going on\n\nWe just approved two new limits for benchmark runs on Claude Fable 5:\n\n- the model may write up to **128,000 tokens** in a single step (it was 64,000)\n- we will wait up to **1,572 seconds** for the model to send us something before we give up on that step\n\nThe first number is real — it is what Anthropic says the model can produce.\n\n**The second number is a guess.** We worked it out on paper: a step we actually measured on another model produced 64,000 tokens in 756 seconds, which is about 85 tokens per second. At that speed, 128,000 tokens would take about 1,512 seconds. We added a 60-second cushion and got 1,572.\n\nNobody has watched Fable 5 run at this limit.\n\n## Why it matters\n\nThat wait time is our own stopwatch. If we set it too low, we kill steps that were working fine and score them as failures. The results then say \"the model could not solve this task\" when what really happened is \"we stopped it\".\n\nThis is the same mistake we have already made three times. The token limit went 16,384 → 32,000 → 64,000, and each time we raised one limit while leaving the other alone, so the failure just moved from one limit to the other and the scores did not improve.\n\n## What to do\n\n1. Run a **small** Fable 5 benchmark — a handful of tasks, not all 89. This is about timing, not scores.\n2. For every step that succeeded, record how long it took.\n3. If the longest successful step ran longer than 1,512 seconds, our guess is too tight. Replace 1,572 with **(longest successful step) + 60 seconds**, which is the same rule we used to set the old 816-second value.\n4. If nothing came close, the guess stands and we can say so with evidence.\n\n## Done when\n\n- A short Fable 5 run has finished and its step timings are saved under `bench/evidence/`\n- The 1,572 number is either confirmed or replaced, in `bench/harbor_adapter/stella_harbor/posture.py` (`_MODEL_TIMEOUT_BY_SLUG`)\n- The memo at `bench/evidence/glm52-h2h-postmortem/PROPOSAL-fable-128k-posture.md` says the number is measured instead of derived\n- If the number changed, the recorded fingerprints in that memo are updated too (a test pins them, so it will tell you)\n\n## Background\n\nApproved as part of #1211 §6.2. The ceilings themselves shipped in #1277.\n"[m[1;37m,[m
